window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("revelation", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"Revelation","chapters":"22","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"The Lamb Has Overcome, and He Is Coming Soon","paragraphs":["Picture an old man in exile on a rocky island in the Aegean Sea. He is the last of the twelve apostles still living. The churches he loves on the mainland are small and pressured: some are being slandered and imprisoned, some are drifting into compromise, some have grown comfortable and lukewarm. The empire around them is vast and confident, its emperor honoured as lord and god, its cities full of temples. On a Sunday, while he is worshipping, the old man hears a voice behind him like a trumpet — and turns to see the risen Jesus.","That is how the last book of the Bible begins: “This is the Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave him to show to his servants the things which must happen soon” (Revelation 1:1). The word ‘Revelation’ is ἀποκάλυψις, an unveiling — the drawing back of a curtain. It is first a revelation of Jesus Christ Himself: the Son of Man among the lampstands, the Lamb who was slain and is standing, the Rider called Faithful and True. Then it unveils what is really happening behind the events of history: a throne in heaven that no earthly empire can shake, a war with the dragon already decided at the cross, and a future in which God dwells with His people in a new creation.","Revelation is written in a style called apocalyptic, full of symbols drawn from the Old Testament — lampstands, seals, trumpets, beasts, a woman, a city, a bride. The symbols are not a puzzle code for the clever; they are pictures that show the true nature of things. Rome looks like a glittering city; Revelation shows it as a prostitute drunk on the blood of the saints. The church looks small and weak; Revelation shows it as a vast multitude in white robes before the throne. Jesus looks defeated, crucified by Rome; Revelation shows Him as the Lion of Judah who has overcome — by being the slain Lamb.","The book says it was written by John (1:1, 4, 9; 22:8), and the early church identified him with the apostle John, the author of the Gospel and letters, writing from Patmos near the end of the first century, probably under the emperor Domitian (about AD 95). Some scholars think it was a different John, or date it earlier, under Nero; the book itself names only ‘John’. Christians have also read it in different ways: as describing events in the first century, as a map of church history, as mainly about the final events before Christ's return, or as a picture of the timeless battle between God and evil. This study will read each passage in the way that best fits its own words, and say honestly where faithful Christians disagree."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"1 John","intro":"1 John ended by pointing ahead to 2 John and 3 John. Those two short letters are waiting for a full copy of their text, so this study now moves on to the last book written by the same John, and the last book of the Bible. The threads of 1 John run straight into Revelation.","points":[{"title":"From ‘You Have Overcome’ to ‘To Him Who Overcomes’","body":"John wrote to the young men, “you have overcome the evil one” (1 John 2:13), and said, “This is the victory that has overcome the world: your faith” (1 John 5:4). In Revelation the same verb, νικάω, closes each of the seven letters: “To him who overcomes I will give to eat from the tree of life, which is in the Paradise of my God” (Revelation 2:7). And the source of every victory is the Lamb: “Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has overcome” (Revelation 5:5)."},{"title":"From Antichrists to the Beast","body":"1 John warned that “even now many antichrists have arisen” (1 John 2:18), and spoke of “the spirit of the Antichrist, of whom you have heard that it comes” (1 John 4:3). Revelation unveils the full shape of that opposition: a dragon, a beast from the sea and a false prophet from the land, who deceive the world and demand its worship. The test is the same in both books: who do you confess Jesus to be, and whom do you worship?"},{"title":"From ‘Keep Yourselves from Idols’ to ‘Worship God’","body":"1 John ended, “Little children, keep yourselves from idols” (1 John 5:21). Revelation is a book about worship: the beast demands it, and the saints refuse it, even to death. When John himself falls at an angel's feet, he is told, “See you don’t do it! I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers, the prophets, and with those who keep the words of this book. Worship God” (Revelation 22:9). And the first of the seven churches addressed is Ephesus, the city of Artemis (2:1)."},{"title":"From ‘We Will See Him’ to ‘They Will See His Face’","body":"John promised, “when he is revealed, we will be like him; for we will see him just as he is” (1 John 3:2). Revelation ends with that promise fulfilled: “They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads” (Revelation 22:4). The true God of 1 John 5:20 is the One whose words are “faithful and true” (Revelation 21:5), and the eternal life John wrote about becomes a river of the water of life flowing from the throne."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"The Lamb on the Throne — Overcome, for He Has Overcome","anchorRef":"Revelation 5:5-6; 11:15; 21:5","paragraphs":["At the centre of Revelation is a throne. In chapter 4 John is taken up into heaven and sees God seated in majesty, surrounded by worship: “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty, who was and who is and who is to come!” (Revelation 4:8). Whatever is happening on earth — persecution, war, plague, deception — the throne is not empty and is not shaken. History is not out of control.","And at the centre of the throne is a Lamb. John is told to look for a Lion, and he turns and sees “a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain” (Revelation 5:6). This is the heart of the book: God conquers evil not by brute force but by the self-giving death of His Son. The Lamb is worthy to open the scroll of history because He was slain and bought people for God by His blood. Everything that follows — seals, trumpets, bowls — flows from His hand.","So the call to the church is to overcome in the same way the Lamb did: by faithful witness, even to death. “They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death” (Revelation 12:11). The book promises that this costly faithfulness will be vindicated: “The kingdom of the world has become the Kingdom of our Lord, and of his Christ. He will reign forever and ever!” (Revelation 11:15).","And it ends with the goal of the whole Bible: “Behold, God’s dwelling is with people, and he will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God” (Revelation 21:3). The story that began in a garden with a tree of life ends in a city with the tree of life, where there is no curse, no death, no tears — and the One on the throne says, “Behold, I am making all things new” (Revelation 21:5)."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 22 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"Jesus Is Lord of History","body":"Revelation opens with a vision of the risen Christ holding the stars in His hand and the keys of death in His power: “I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever. Amen. I have the keys of Death and of Hades” (Revelation 1:18). No emperor, no beast, no disaster is outside His rule. Whatever your fear, it is not on the throne."},{"title":"Faithful Witness Will Cost Something — and Win","body":"Revelation never promises that believers will escape suffering. It promises that suffering faithfulness is victory. The Lamb's followers overcome “because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony” (Revelation 12:11). Compromise with the world's idols looks safe but loses everything; faithfulness looks costly but inherits everything."},{"title":"He Is Coming, and All Things Will Be Made New","body":"The book is not mainly about charts and dates but about a Person who is coming: “Behold, he is coming with the clouds, and every eye will see him” (Revelation 1:7). Evil will be judged, Babylon will fall, the devil will be thrown down, death will be destroyed, and God will dwell with His people forever. The church's answer is the Bible's last prayer: ‘Come, Lord Jesus.’"}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Read for Worship, Not for Curiosity","body":"Revelation promises a blessing: “Blessed is he who reads and those who hear the words of the prophecy, and keep the things that are written in it, for the time is at hand” (Revelation 1:3). The blessing is not for those who solve every symbol, but for those who keep its words. Ask the Spirit to show you Jesus, to lead you into worship, and to make you faithful."},{"title":"Where Is Your Lampstand?","body":"Jesus walks among the seven churches and knows each one: their love, their compromise, their suffering, their sleep. Before you begin, ask yourself which of the seven you most resemble — and invite the One with eyes like a flame of fire to speak to you as He spoke to them."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Lord Jesus Christ, faithful witness, firstborn of the dead and ruler of the kings of the earth, You love me and have freed me from my sins by Your blood, and made me part of Your kingdom and a priest to Your God and Father. To You be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. As I open the Revelation You gave to Your servant John, unveil Yourself to me. Let me see You walking among Your churches with eyes like fire, and let me fall at Your feet and hear You say, ‘Don’t be afraid.’ Lift my eyes to the throne in heaven, and let me see the Lamb who was slain and is worthy. Where I have left my first love, call me back. Where I have compromised with idols, make me faithful. Where I am lukewarm, set me on fire. Give me courage to overcome by Your blood and by the word of my testimony, even when it costs me. Keep me from the deceptions of the beast and the glitter of Babylon. Fill me with hope for the new heaven and the new earth, where You will wipe away every tear. Holy Spirit, let me hear what You are saying to the churches. Amen. Come, Lord Jesus.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"The Revelation of Jesus Christ and the Son of Man Among the Lampstands","themeTitle":"“I Am the First and the Last, and the Living One”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"This is the Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave him to show to his servants the things which must happen soon, which he sent and made known by his angel to his servant, John,","understanding":"Ἀποκάλυψις Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “This is the Revelation of Jesus Christ” — ἀποκάλυψις, from the appear family: an unveiling, the drawing back of a curtain. It is both from Jesus and about Jesus. ἣν ἔδωκεν αὐτῷ ὁ θεὸς δεῖξαι τοῖς δούλοις αὐτοῦ, “which God gave him to show to his servants” — δοῦλος, from the servant family: a bondservant, owned by and devoted to a master. ἃ δεῖ γενέσθαι ἐν τάχει, “the things which must happen soon” — δεῖ, it is necessary: God's plan will certainly be done. καὶ ἐσήμανεν, “and made known” — σημαίνω, to signify, to show by signs: the book will speak in pictures.","words":[{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"Revelation"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G5034","hebrew":"τάχος","translit":"táchos","english":"soon"},{"strongs":"G4591","hebrew":"σημαίνω","translit":"sēmaínō","english":"made known"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2491","hebrew":"Ἰωάννης","translit":"Iōánnēs","english":"John"}]},{"v":2,"text":"who testified to God’s word, and of the testimony of Jesus Christ, about everything that he saw.","understanding":"ὃς ἐμαρτύρησεν τὸν λόγον τοῦ θεοῦ, “who testified to God’s word” — μαρτυρέω, from the witness family; λόγος, from the word family. καὶ τὴν μαρτυρίαν Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “and of the testimony of Jesus Christ” — μαρτυρία, also from the witness family: the testimony Jesus gave, and the testimony about Him. Two members of one family in one verse: John testifies to the testimony. ὅσα εἶδεν, “about everything that he saw” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family): this book is an eyewitness report of a vision.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testified"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Blessed is he who reads and those who hear the words of the prophecy, and keep the things that are written in it, for the time is at hand.","understanding":"μακάριος ὁ ἀναγινώσκων καὶ οἱ ἀκούοντες, “Blessed is he who reads and those who hear the words of the prophecy” — λόγος, from the word family. This is the first of seven blessings in Revelation (1:3; 14:13; 16:15; 19:9; 20:6; 22:7, 14). One person reads aloud in the church; the congregation listens. καὶ τηροῦντες τὰ ἐν αὐτῇ γεγραμμένα, “and keep the things that are written in it” — τηρέω, from the hold family: to guard and obey. The blessing is not for decoding but for keeping. ὁ γὰρ καιρὸς ἐγγύς, “for the time is at hand” — καιρός, from the time family: the decisive season, not mere χρόνος, clock-time.","words":[{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G314","hebrew":"ἀναγινώσκω","translit":"anaginṓskō","english":"reads"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G1451","hebrew":"ἐγγύς","translit":"engýs","english":"at hand"}]},{"v":4,"text":"John, to the seven assemblies that are in Asia: Grace to you and peace, from God, who is and who was and who is to come; and from the seven Spirits who are before his throne;","understanding":"Ἰωάννης ταῖς ἑπτὰ ἐκκλησίαις ταῖς ἐν τῇ Ἀσίᾳ, “John, to the seven assemblies that are in Asia” — the Roman province of Asia, in modern western Turkey. Seven is the number of completeness: these seven real churches also stand for the whole church. χάρις ὑμῖν καὶ εἰρήνη, “Grace to you and peace” — Paul's greeting. ἀπὸ ὁ ὢν καὶ ὁ ἦν καὶ ὁ ἐρχόμενος, “from God, who is and who was and who is to come” — echoing God's name revealed to Moses, ‘I AM’. καὶ ἀπὸ τῶν ἑπτὰ πνευμάτων, “and from the seven Spirits who are before his throne” — the strongest reading is the Holy Spirit in His fullness (Zechariah 4:2–10 speaks of seven lamps as the eyes of the Lord); others think of seven angels. θρόνος, throne, appears over forty times in this book.","words":[{"strongs":"G2491","hebrew":"Ἰωάννης","translit":"Iōánnēs","english":"John"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and from Jesus Christ, the faithful witness, the firstborn of the dead, and the ruler of the kings of the earth. To him who loves us, and washed us from our sins by his blood;","understanding":"καὶ ἀπὸ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, ὁ μάρτυς ὁ πιστός, “and from Jesus Christ, the faithful witness” — μάρτυς, from the witness family: the one who testifies; by the end of the first century the word was coming to mean one who dies for the testimony. ὁ πρωτότοκος τῶν νεκρῶν, “the firstborn of the dead” — first to rise to unending life, and heir. καὶ ὁ ἄρχων τῶν βασιλέων τῆς γῆς, “and the ruler of the kings of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. Three titles: faithful in death, risen from death, reigning over all. Τῷ ἀγαπῶντι ἡμᾶς, “To him who loves us” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family, present tense: He keeps on loving us. καὶ λούσαντι ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ τῶν ἁμαρτιῶν ἡμῶν ἐν τῷ αἵματι αὐτοῦ, “and washed us from our sins by his blood” — ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. The WEB follows manuscripts reading λούσαντι, ‘washed’; the oldest read λύσαντι, ‘freed’ or ‘loosed’ — one letter's difference; both are true.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witness"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G4416","hebrew":"πρωτότοκος","translit":"prōtótokos","english":"firstborn"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G758","hebrew":"ἄρχων","translit":"árchōn","english":"ruler"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loves"},{"strongs":"G3068","hebrew":"λούω","translit":"loúō","english":"washed"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"}]},{"v":6,"text":"and he made us to be a Kingdom, priests to his God and Father; to him be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. Amen.","understanding":"καὶ ἐποίησεν ἡμᾶς βασιλείαν, ἱερεῖς τῷ θεῷ καὶ πατρὶ αὐτοῦ, “and he made us to be a Kingdom, priests to his God and Father” — God's words to Israel at Sinai, ‘a kingdom of priests’, are now fulfilled in the church: every believer has access to God and represents Him to the world. αὐτῷ ἡ δόξα καὶ τὸ κράτος εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “to him be the glory and the dominion forever and ever” — κράτος, from the power family: ruling might, power that prevails, as distinct from δύναμις, ability; αἰών, from the time family and the world family, doubled: ‘to the ages of the ages’.","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"made"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priests"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"dominion"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Behold, he is coming with the clouds, and every eye will see him, including those who pierced him. All the tribes of the earth will mourn over him. Even so, Amen.","understanding":"Ἰδοὺ ἔρχεται μετὰ τῶν νεφελῶν, “Behold, he is coming with the clouds” — Daniel saw ‘one like a son of man’ coming with the clouds of heaven to receive a kingdom; Jesus applied it to Himself. καὶ ὄψεται αὐτὸν πᾶς ὀφθαλμὸς καὶ οἵτινες αὐτὸν ἐξεκέντησαν, “and every eye will see him, including those who pierced him” — ὁράω, from the see family; and ‘all the tribes of the earth’ — γῆ, from the world family; ἐκκεντέω, to pierce through, from Zechariah: “they will look to me whom they have pierced” (Zechariah 12:10). κόπτω, to beat the breast in mourning: the whole earth will mourn when He appears. ναί, ἀμήν, “Even so, Amen” — Greek and Hebrew ‘yes’ side by side.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"clouds"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eye"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G1574","hebrew":"ἐκκεντέω","translit":"ekkentéō","english":"pierced"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribes"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2875","hebrew":"κόπτω","translit":"kóptō","english":"mourn"}]},{"v":8,"text":"“I am the Alpha and the Omega,” says the Lord God, “who is and who was and who is to come, the Almighty.”","understanding":"Ἐγώ εἰμι τὸ Ἄλφα καὶ τὸ Ὦ, “I am the Alpha and the Omega” — the first and last letters of the Greek alphabet: God is the beginning and the end of everything, and everything between. λέγει κύριος ὁ θεός, “says the Lord God” — one of only two places in Revelation where God the Father speaks directly (with 21:5–8). ὁ παντοκράτωρ, “the Almighty” — παντοκράτωρ, the all-ruling, from πᾶς and κράτος: used nine times in Revelation and only once elsewhere in the New Testament. Caesar claimed to rule the world; God alone holds all things.","words":[{"strongs":"G1473","hebrew":"ἐγώ","translit":"egṓ","english":"I"},{"strongs":"G1","hebrew":"Α","translit":"A","english":"Alpha"},{"strongs":"G5598","hebrew":"Ω","translit":"Ō","english":"Omega"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"to come"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"}]},{"v":9,"text":"I John, your brother and partner with you in the oppression, Kingdom, and perseverance in Christ Jesus, was on the isle that is called Patmos because of God’s Word and the testimony of Jesus Christ.","understanding":"Ἐγὼ Ἰωάννης, ὁ ἀδελφὸς ὑμῶν καὶ συγκοινωνὸς ἐν τῇ θλίψει καὶ βασιλείᾳ καὶ ὑπομονῇ ἐν Ἰησοῦ, “I John, your brother and partner with you in the oppression, Kingdom, and perseverance in Christ Jesus” — not a superior but a fellow-sufferer. θλῖψις, pressure, crushing affliction; βασιλεία, the reign of God; ὑπομονή, from the endurance family: remaining under the load. All three belong together, held by one Greek article: the kingdom is entered through pressure and endurance. ἐν τῇ νήσῳ τῇ καλουμένῃ Πάτμῳ διὰ τὸν λόγον τοῦ θεοῦ, “on the isle that is called Patmos because of God’s Word” — λόγος, from the word family. Patmos was a small rocky island used for banishment. καὶ τὴν μαρτυρίαν Ἰησοῦ, “the testimony of Jesus Christ” — μαρτυρία, from the witness family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2491","hebrew":"Ἰωάννης","translit":"Iōánnēs","english":"John"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G4791","hebrew":"συγκοινωνός","translit":"synkoinōnós","english":"partner"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"oppression"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G3520","hebrew":"νῆσος","translit":"nēsos","english":"isle"},{"strongs":"G3963","hebrew":"Πάτμος","translit":"Pátmos","english":"Patmos"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"Word"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"}]},{"v":10,"text":"I was in the Spirit on the Lord’s day, and I heard behind me a loud voice, like a trumpet","understanding":"ἐγενόμην ἐν πνεύματι, “I was in the Spirit” — caught up by the Holy Spirit into a state of vision, like Ezekiel. ἐν τῇ κυριακῇ ἡμέρᾳ, “on the Lord’s day” — κυριακός, belonging to the Lord; ἡμέρα, from the time family. The strongest reading is Sunday, the day of the resurrection, when Christians met for worship; Caesar had his ‘imperial day’, but the church had the Lord's. καὶ ἤκουσα ὀπίσω μου φωνὴν μεγάλην ὡς σάλπιγγος, “and I heard behind me a loud voice, like a trumpet” — the trumpet sounded at Sinai when God came down.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2960","hebrew":"κυριακός","translit":"kyriakós","english":"Lord’s"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpet"}]},{"v":11,"text":"saying, “What you see, write in a book and send to the seven assemblies: to Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia, and to Laodicea.”","understanding":"λεγούσης· ὃ βλέπεις γράψον εἰς βιβλίον, “saying, ‘What you see, write in a book’” — βλέπω, from the see family: to look at, present tense — keep watching and keep writing. βιβλίον, from the book family: a scroll, as distinct from βίβλος, a volume or record (3:5). καὶ πέμψον ταῖς ἑπτὰ ἐκκλησίαις, “and send to the seven assemblies” — the seven cities lie on a circular road, in the order a messenger would travel: Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea.","words":[{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G3992","hebrew":"πέμπω","translit":"pémpō","english":"send"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"}]},{"v":12,"text":"I turned to see the voice that spoke with me. Having turned, I saw seven golden lamp stands.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐπέστρεψα βλέπειν τὴν φωνὴν ἥτις ἐλάλει μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ, “I turned to see the voice that spoke with me” — βλέπω, from the see family. καὶ ἐπιστρέψας εἶδον ἑπτὰ λυχνίας χρυσᾶς, “Having turned, I saw seven golden lamp stands” — εἴδω, also from the see family (and the know family): βλέπω is the act of looking, εἶδον what he actually saw. λυχνία, a lampstand: in the tabernacle there was one lampstand with seven lamps; Zechariah saw “a lamp stand all of gold, with its bowl on the top of it” (Zechariah 4:2). Now there are seven lampstands — the churches, carrying the light of God into the world (1:20).","words":[{"strongs":"G1994","hebrew":"ἐπιστρέφω","translit":"epistréphō","english":"turned"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stands"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"}]},{"v":13,"text":"And among the lamp stands was one like a son of man, clothed with a robe reaching down to his feet, and with a golden sash around his chest.","understanding":"καὶ ἐν μέσῳ τῶν λυχνιῶν ὅμοιον υἱὸν ἀνθρώπου, “And among the lamp stands was one like a son of man” — Daniel's heavenly figure (‘one like a son of man’), and Jesus' favourite title for Himself. He is not far off; He walks among His churches. ἐνδεδυμένον ποδήρη, “clothed with a robe reaching down to his feet” — ποδήρης, the long robe of a priest. καὶ περιεζωσμένον πρὸς τοῖς μαστοῖς ζώνην χρυσᾶν, “and with a golden sash around his chest” — royal and priestly dress: He is King and High Priest.","words":[{"strongs":"G3319","hebrew":"μέσος","translit":"mésos","english":"among"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stands"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G1746","hebrew":"ἐνδύω","translit":"endýō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G4158","hebrew":"ποδήρης","translit":"podḗrēs","english":"robe"},{"strongs":"G2223","hebrew":"ζώνη","translit":"zṓnē","english":"sash"}]},{"v":14,"text":"His head and his hair were white as white wool, like snow. His eyes were like a flame of fire.","understanding":"ἡ δὲ κεφαλὴ αὐτοῦ καὶ αἱ τρίχες λευκαὶ ὡς ἔριον λευκόν ὡς χιών, “His head and his hair were white as white wool, like snow” — in Daniel's vision the Ancient of Days had hair ‘like pure wool’. What Daniel saw of God, John sees in Jesus: eternity, wisdom and purity. καὶ οἱ ὀφθαλμοὶ αὐτοῦ ὡς φλὸξ πυρός, “His eyes were like a flame of fire” — φλόξ, a blaze: eyes that see through every pretence (2:18, 23).","words":[{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G2359","hebrew":"θρίξ","translit":"thríx","english":"hair"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2053","hebrew":"ἔριον","translit":"érion","english":"wool"},{"strongs":"G5510","hebrew":"χιών","translit":"chiṓn","english":"snow"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G5395","hebrew":"φλόξ","translit":"phlóx","english":"flame"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]},{"v":15,"text":"His feet were like burnished brass, as if it had been refined in a furnace. His voice was like the voice of many waters.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ πόδες αὐτοῦ ὅμοιοι χαλκολιβάνῳ ὡς ἐν καμίνῳ πεπυρωμένης, “His feet were like burnished brass, as if it had been refined in a furnace” — χαλκολίβανον, a rare word for a glowing metal alloy; Thyatira, a city of metal-workers' guilds, would hear it (2:18). Feet of strength and stability, refined by fire. καὶ ἡ φωνὴ αὐτοῦ ὡς φωνὴ ὑδάτων πολλῶν, “His voice was like the voice of many waters” — Ezekiel heard God's voice like the sound of many waters; on Patmos the surf crashed on every side.","words":[{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G5474","hebrew":"χαλκολίβανον","translit":"chalkolíbanon","english":"burnished brass"},{"strongs":"G2575","hebrew":"κάμινος","translit":"káminos","english":"furnace"},{"strongs":"G4448","hebrew":"πυρόω","translit":"pyróō","english":"refined"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"}]},{"v":16,"text":"He had seven stars in his right hand. Out of his mouth proceeded a sharp two-edged sword. His face was like the sun shining at its brightest.","understanding":"καὶ ἔχων ἐν τῇ δεξιᾷ χειρὶ αὐτοῦ ἀστέρας ἑπτά, “He had seven stars in his right hand” — ἀστήρ: Roman coins showed Domitian's dead infant son as a god holding seven stars. The real Lord of the stars is Christ, who holds His churches' messengers in His hand. καὶ ἐκ τοῦ στόματος αὐτοῦ ῥομφαία δίστομος ὀξεῖα ἐκπορευομένη, “Out of his mouth proceeded a sharp two-edged sword” — ῥομφαία, from the sword family: the long, broad sword, as distinct from μάχαιρα, the short sword (6:4). His word judges and conquers (2:12, 16; 19:15). καὶ ἡ ὄψις αὐτοῦ ὡς ὁ ἥλιος φαίνει ἐν τῇ δυνάμει αὐτοῦ, “His face was like the sun shining at its brightest” — δύναμις, from the power family: literally ‘in its power’, its full inherent strength; the WEB renders it ‘brightest’. φαίνω, from the appear family: to shine, to give light. This is the face Peter, James and John saw on the mountain.","words":[{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G4501","hebrew":"ῥομφαία","translit":"rhomphaía","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G3691","hebrew":"ὀξύς","translit":"oxýs","english":"sharp"},{"strongs":"G3799","hebrew":"ὄψις","translit":"ópsis","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"shining"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"brightest"}]},{"v":17,"text":"When I saw him, I fell at his feet like a dead man. He laid his right hand on me, saying, “Don’t be afraid. I am the first and the last,","understanding":"Καὶ ὅτε εἶδον αὐτόν, ἔπεσα πρὸς τοὺς πόδας αὐτοῦ ὡς νεκρός, “When I saw him, I fell at his feet like a dead man” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). The disciple who once leaned on Jesus' chest now collapses before His glory. καὶ ἔθηκεν τὴν δεξιὰν αὐτοῦ ἐπ᾽ ἐμὲ λέγων· μὴ φοβοῦ, “He laid his right hand on me, saying, ‘Don’t be afraid’” — the same hand that holds the stars touches the trembling disciple. φοβέομαι, to be afraid. ἐγώ εἰμι ὁ πρῶτος καὶ ὁ ἔσχατος, “I am the first and the last” — God's title in Isaiah (‘I am the first, and I am the last’), now spoken by Jesus.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"afraid"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"}]},{"v":18,"text":"and the Living one. I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever. Amen. I have the keys of Death and of Hades.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ ζῶν, “and the Living one” — ζάω, from the life family: not merely one who lives, but the source of life. καὶ ἐγενόμην νεκρὸς καὶ ἰδοὺ ζῶν εἰμι εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever” — ζάω again; αἰών, from the time family and the world family: the resurrection in one sentence. καὶ ἔχω τὰς κλεῖς τοῦ θανάτου καὶ τοῦ ᾅδου, “I have the keys of Death and of Hades” — κλείς, a key: authority to open and shut. ᾅδης, the realm of the dead. The believer facing a Roman sword need not fear: Jesus holds the keys.","words":[{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"Living"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"},{"strongs":"G2807","hebrew":"κλείς","translit":"kleís","english":"keys"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G86","hebrew":"ᾅδης","translit":"háidēs","english":"Hades"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Write therefore the things which you have seen, and the things which are, and the things which will happen hereafter;","understanding":"γράψον οὖν ἃ εἶδες καὶ ἃ εἰσὶν καὶ ἃ μέλλει γενέσθαι μετὰ ταῦτα, “Write therefore the things which you have seen, and the things which are, and the things which will happen hereafter” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); μέλλω, to be about to. Many read this as an outline of the book: the vision just seen (ch. 1), the present state of the churches (chs. 2–3), and what follows (chs. 4–22). Others take it as saying that the whole vision concerns both present and future.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"Write"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"will happen"},{"strongs":"G3326","hebrew":"μετά","translit":"metá","english":"hereafter"}]},{"v":20,"text":"the mystery of the seven stars which you saw in my right hand, and the seven golden lamp stands. The seven stars are the angels of the seven assemblies. The seven lamp stands are seven assemblies.","understanding":"τὸ μυστήριον τῶν ἑπτὰ ἀστέρων, “the mystery of the seven stars” — μυστήριον, a secret now revealed. οἱ ἑπτὰ ἀστέρες ἄγγελοι τῶν ἑπτὰ ἐκκλησιῶν εἰσιν, “The seven stars are the angels of the seven assemblies” — ἄγγελος means both ‘angel’ and ‘messenger’. Some think of guardian angels of the churches; others of the churches' leaders or messengers; others of the heavenly side of each church. καὶ αἱ λυχνίαι αἱ ἑπτὰ ἑπτὰ ἐκκλησίαι εἰσίν, “The seven lamp stands are seven assemblies” — the church exists to hold up the light.","words":[{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stands"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Voice Behind You","verseRef":"v. 9-12","image":"Picture an old man on a bare, rocky island, the sea on every side. He is in exile for preaching Jesus. It is Sunday; far away his churches are gathering without him. He bows to pray — and suddenly a voice like a trumpet blasts behind him. He turns around.","meaning":"“I John, your brother and partner with you in the oppression, Kingdom, and perseverance in Christ Jesus, was on the isle that is called Patmos because of God’s Word and the testimony of Jesus Christ” (v. 9). “I was in the Spirit on the Lord’s day, and I heard behind me a loud voice, like a trumpet” (v. 10). The loneliest places of faithfulness are often where Jesus reveals Himself most clearly."},{"title":"The Son of Man Among the Lampstands","verseRef":"v. 12-16","image":"Picture seven tall golden lampstands, their flames flickering. Walking among them is a figure in a long robe with a golden sash, hair white as snow, eyes like blazing fire, feet glowing like metal in a furnace, a sword coming from His mouth, His face shining like the noonday sun.","meaning":"“And among the lamp stands was one like a son of man, clothed with a robe reaching down to his feet, and with a golden sash around his chest” (v. 13). Jesus is not absent from His church. He walks among the lampstands, sees everything, tends the flames, and speaks with a word sharper than any sword."},{"title":"The Right Hand","verseRef":"v. 16-17","image":"Picture seven bright stars held in a strong right hand. Then see John collapse on the ground like a corpse — and the same right hand reach down and rest gently on his shoulder.","meaning":"“When I saw him, I fell at his feet like a dead man. He laid his right hand on me, saying, ‘Don’t be afraid. I am the first and the last’” (v. 17). The hand that holds the stars is the hand that holds you. His glory would crush you; His grace lifts you."},{"title":"The Keys","verseRef":"v. 18","image":"Picture a great ring of iron keys, the keys to the prison of death and the grave. For all of human history they have hung on the enemy's belt. Now see them in the hand of the risen Jesus, scarred by the nails.","meaning":"“I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever. Amen. I have the keys of Death and of Hades” (v. 18). The worst that any persecutor can do is send a believer through a door whose key Jesus holds. Death is not the end; it is a door He opens."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Prologue and Blessing (v. 1-3)","body":"The revelation comes from God, through Jesus Christ and His angel, to John and to Christ's servants; a blessing is promised to those who read, hear and keep it, for the time is near."},{"label":"Greeting and Doxology (v. 4-8)","body":"John greets the seven churches with grace and peace from the eternal God, the sevenfold Spirit and Jesus Christ the faithful witness; he praises the One who loves us, freed us by His blood and made us a kingdom of priests; Christ is coming with the clouds, and God declares Himself the Alpha and Omega, the Almighty."},{"label":"The Vision of the Son of Man (v. 9-16)","body":"On Patmos, on the Lord's day, John hears a trumpet voice telling him to write to the seven churches; he turns and sees seven golden lampstands and among them one like a son of man, robed as priest and king, with white hair, fiery eyes, glowing feet, a voice like waters, seven stars in His hand, a sword from His mouth and a face like the sun."},{"label":"The Commission (v. 17-20)","body":"John falls as if dead; Christ touches him, tells him not to fear, and declares Himself the First and Last, the Living One who was dead and holds the keys of death and Hades; John must write what he has seen, what is and what will be; the stars are the angels of the churches and the lampstands are the churches."}],"sections":[{"heading":"How to Read Revelation","paragraphs":["The first verse tells us what kind of book this is. It is an ‘apocalypse’, an unveiling: “This is the Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave him to show to his servants the things which must happen soon” (v. 1). It is also a ‘prophecy’: “Blessed is he who reads and those who hear the words of the prophecy” (v. 3). And it is a letter, addressed to seven real churches (v. 4). So Revelation must be read in three ways at once: as a picture-vision full of symbols, as God's word calling for a response, and as a message that first-century Christians could understand.","The word ‘made known’ in verse 1 is σημαίνω, ‘to show by signs’. Revelation tells us plainly that it speaks in symbols, and often explains them: the stars are angels, the lampstands are churches (v. 20). Most of its pictures come from the Old Testament — Daniel, Ezekiel, Zechariah, Isaiah, Exodus. A reader who knows those books will find Revelation far less strange.","Christians have read Revelation in four main ways. Some see most of it fulfilled in the first centuries (the preterist view); some see it as a map of church history (the historicist view); many see chapters 4–22 as mainly describing the events just before Christ's return (the futurist view); and others read it as a picture of the battle between Christ and evil in every age (the idealist view). Each has something true: Revelation spoke to its first readers, it describes realities that recur through history, and it looks forward to a real final coming of Christ. This study will take each vision in the way its own words suggest."]},{"heading":"The Faithful Witness Who Loves Us","paragraphs":["John's greeting is Trinitarian: grace and peace come “from God, who is and who was and who is to come; and from the seven Spirits who are before his throne; and from Jesus Christ” (vv. 4–5). Jesus is given three titles: “the faithful witness, the firstborn of the dead, and the ruler of the kings of the earth” (v. 5). He was faithful to death, He rose first from death, and He now rules over every king — including Caesar. For believers facing a hostile empire, this is the place to start.","Then John bursts into praise: “To him who loves us, and washed us from our sins by his blood; and he made us to be a Kingdom, priests to his God and Father” (vv. 5–6). The order matters: He loves us (present), freed us (past), and made us a kingdom of priests (our new identity). The church is small and persecuted, but it is a royal priesthood — the true people of God.","And He is coming: “Behold, he is coming with the clouds, and every eye will see him, including those who pierced him” (v. 7). John combines Daniel's son of man coming with the clouds and Zechariah's prophecy, “they will look to me whom they have pierced” (Zechariah 12:10). The One who was pierced will return in glory, and every eye will see Him."]},{"heading":"One Like a Son of Man","paragraphs":["John's vision of Jesus draws together Old Testament pictures of God Himself. The hair “white as white wool, like snow” (v. 14) is what Daniel saw of the Ancient of Days; the voice “like the voice of many waters” (v. 15) is how Ezekiel heard the glory of God. The title “I am the first and the last” (v. 17) is what God says in Isaiah. John is not seeing an angel or a great prophet; he is seeing the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.","Each detail of the vision will reappear in the letters to the churches. To Ephesus He is the one who holds the seven stars (2:1); to Smyrna the first and last, who was dead and came to life (2:8); to Pergamum the one with the sharp two-edged sword (2:12); to Thyatira the one with eyes like fire and feet like brass (2:18). The portrait of Christ is the medicine for each church's disease.","John's response is to fall as though dead (v. 17). The beloved disciple who once rested against Jesus' side cannot stand before His glory. But Jesus puts His right hand on him and says, “Don’t be afraid. I am the first and the last, and the Living one. I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever. Amen. I have the keys of Death and of Hades” (vv. 17–18). That is the word every suffering believer needs: the One who holds the keys of death is the One who loves us."]},{"heading":"Stars and Lampstands","paragraphs":["Jesus explains two symbols: “The seven stars are the angels of the seven assemblies. The seven lamp stands are seven assemblies” (v. 20). The churches are lampstands, not lamps: they do not produce the light, they hold it up. Their purpose is to shine in a dark world. And Jesus walks among them — present, watching, caring, correcting.","Who are the ‘angels’ of the churches? The Greek word ἄγγελος means messenger as well as angel. Some think each church has a guardian angel; others think of the church's leaders or messengers; others think the ‘angel’ represents the church's heavenly identity. What is clear is that Christ holds them in His right hand. The church is never out of His grasp.","John is told to write “the things which you have seen, and the things which are, and the things which will happen hereafter” (v. 19). Many see here a simple outline of the whole book. Whether or not that is its purpose, it reminds us that Revelation speaks to the present as well as the future: what Jesus says to the churches is what ‘is’, and it is addressed to us."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Son of Man, Seen in Glory","paragraphs":["Jesus called Himself the Son of Man and, at His trial, applied Daniel's vision to Himself: “you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of Power, and coming with the clouds of the sky” (Mark 14:62). At His ascension the angels said, “This Jesus, who was received up from you into the sky, will come back in the same way as you saw him going into the sky” (Acts 1:11). Revelation 1:7 picks up both.","John had seen this glory once before. On the mountain of transfiguration, “He was transfigured before them. His face shone like the sun, and his garments became as white as the light” (Matthew 17:2), and the disciples “fell on their faces, and were very afraid” (Matthew 17:6). Then, as on Patmos, “Jesus came and touched them and said, ‘Get up, and don’t be afraid’” (Matthew 17:7).","John's Gospel also records the piercing: “However one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out” (John 19:34), and adds, “Again another Scripture says, ‘They will look on him whom they pierced’” (John 19:37). Hebrews says of Him that “he lives forever to make intercession for them” (Hebrews 7:25) — the One John now sees robed as a priest, alive forever and ever."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Revelation","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","strongs":"G602 (made known σημαίνω, sēmaínō, G4591)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “disclosure.” ἀποκάλυψις is from ἀπό (away from) and καλύπτω (to cover): the removing of a cover, an unveiling. It gives the book its name in English (‘Apocalypse’). In popular speech ‘apocalypse’ means catastrophe; in the Bible it means disclosure — God drawing back the curtain to show what is really true about Jesus, about history and about the future. Everything in the book is ‘the Revelation of Jesus Christ’."},{"english":"Witness","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","strongs":"G3144 (testimony μαρτυρία, martyría, G3141; testified μαρτυρέω, martyréō, G3140)","verses":"v. 2, 5, 9","insight":"Strong's: “a witness (literally (judicially) or figuratively (genitive case)); by analogy, a \"martyr\".” Revelation is a book of witness: John testifies (v. 2), Jesus is the faithful witness (v. 5), John is on Patmos for the testimony of Jesus (v. 9), Antipas is ‘my faithful witness’ who was killed (2:13). By the end of the first century the word was moving from ‘one who testifies’ to ‘one who dies for the testimony’. Jesus is the first faithful martyr; His people follow Him."},{"english":"Almighty","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","strongs":"G3841 (dominion κράτος, krátos, G2904)","verses":"v. 8; 4:8; 19:6","insight":"Strong's: “the all-ruling, i.e. God (as absolute and universal sovereign).” παντοκράτωρ joins πᾶς (all) and κράτος (ruling power, from the power family). It appears nine times in Revelation and only once elsewhere in the New Testament (2 Corinthians 6:18). It translates the Old Testament ‘LORD of Armies’. In a world where Caesar claimed universal rule, Revelation insists that only one is the All-Ruler."},{"english":"Oppression / tribulation","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","strongs":"G2347 (perseverance ὑπομονή, hypomonḗ, G5281)","verses":"v. 9; 2:9-10; 7:14","insight":"Strong's: “pressure (literally or figuratively).” θλῖψις is from θλίβω, to press, squeeze or crush, as grapes in a winepress. John shares it with his readers now (v. 9); Smyrna will face it (2:10); the multitude in white come ‘out of the great tribulation’ (7:14). In verse 9 θλῖψις, βασιλεία and ὑπομονή are bound together by one article: suffering, the kingdom and patient endurance are one package for the follower of Jesus."},{"english":"Lampstand","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","strongs":"G3087 (stars ἀστήρ, astḗr, G792)","verses":"v. 12, 13, 20; 2:1, 5","insight":"Strong's: “a lamp-stand (literally or figuratively).” λυχνία, from λύχνος (lamp), is a stand that holds a lamp up so that its light can be seen. The tabernacle had one seven-branched lampstand; Zechariah saw a golden lampstand fed by olive trees, a picture of God's people shining by His Spirit. Revelation's seven lampstands are the churches (1:20), and two lampstands are the two witnesses (11:4). Jesus warns Ephesus that He can remove its lampstand (2:5): a church that stops shining loses its reason to exist."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this opening chapter the Holy Spirit wants to show you Jesus as He is now, and to change how you face your own trials.","items":[{"title":"Your Patmos Can Be Your Place of Vision","body":"John was in exile “because of God’s Word and the testimony of Jesus Christ” (v. 9) — and there he saw the glory of Christ. The Spirit wants you to know that your hardest, loneliest place of faithfulness is not wasted. Worship there. Listen there. The voice may be just behind you."},{"title":"Don't Be Afraid","body":"The One whose eyes are like fire and whose face shines like the sun lays His right hand on John and says, “Don’t be afraid” (v. 17). The Spirit brings you that same word today. Whatever you fear — death, loss, failure, the future — Jesus holds the keys. He was dead and is alive forever."},{"title":"He Walks Among the Lampstands","body":"Jesus is not absent from His church. He walks among the lampstands and sees everything (v. 13). The Spirit wants you to take your church — its weakness, its faults, its faithfulness — and see it as Jesus sees it: precious, held in His right hand, and called to shine."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Vision Check","body":"When you picture Jesus, what do you see? A gentle teacher? A distant figure? Compare your picture with verses 13–18. What part of His glory have you lost sight of?"},{"title":"The Fear Check","body":"What are you most afraid of? Bring it to the One who holds the keys of Death and of Hades (v. 18). What changes if He truly holds them?"},{"title":"The Keeping Check","body":"The blessing of verse 3 is for those who ‘keep’ what is written. Are you reading the Bible for information or for obedience?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Worship on the Lord's Day","body":"This Sunday, before you go to church, spend fifteen minutes alone reading Revelation 1:4–8 aloud as praise. Then, during the service, look around the room and remember: Jesus walks among the lampstands. Pray for your church as His."},{"title":"Write What You See","body":"Each day this week, read one part of the vision (vv. 12–18) and write down one thing it shows you about Jesus and one way it answers a fear or need in your life. At the end of the week, pray through your list with thanks."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus Christ, You are the faithful witness, the firstborn of the dead and the ruler of the kings of the earth. You love me, and You freed me from my sins by Your blood. You have made me part of Your kingdom and a priest to Your God and Father. To You be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. I believe You are coming with the clouds, and every eye will see You. You are the Alpha and the Omega, the Almighty. When I am pressed and alone, let me hear Your voice behind me. Let me see You as You are — walking among Your churches, eyes like fire, face shining like the sun. I fall at Your feet. Lay Your right hand on me and tell me not to be afraid. You were dead, and You are alive forever and ever, and You hold the keys of death and Hades. Hold my church in Your right hand, and make us shine like a lampstand in the dark. Make me one who hears and keeps Your word. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Transfiguration","ref":"Matthew 17:1-8","relatedVerses":"v. 13-17","summary":"Jesus took Peter, James and John up a high mountain, and was transfigured before them: “His face shone like the sun, and his garments became as white as the light” (Matthew 17:2). A bright cloud covered them and the Father's voice spoke. The disciples fell on their faces in terror, “Jesus came and touched them and said, ‘Get up, and don’t be afraid’” (Matthew 17:7). Sixty years later on Patmos, John saw that same face shining like the sun, fell again, and felt again the touch of the same hand."},{"title":"Daniel's Vision of the Son of Man","ref":"Daniel 7:9-14; 10:4-12","relatedVerses":"v. 7, 13-17","summary":"In exile in Babylon, Daniel saw thrones set up, the Ancient of Days with hair ‘like pure wool’, and ‘one like a son of man’ coming with the clouds to receive an everlasting kingdom. Later by the river Tigris he saw a man whose face was like lightning and eyes like flaming torches; Daniel fell on his face, and a hand touched him with the words, ‘Don’t be afraid.’ John, in exile on Patmos, receives a vision shaped by Daniel's — and sees that the Son of Man is Jesus."},{"title":"Zechariah's Golden Lampstand","ref":"Zechariah 4:1-14","relatedVerses":"v. 12-13, 20","summary":"After the exile, the prophet Zechariah saw “a lamp stand all of gold, with its bowl on the top of it” (Zechariah 4:2), fed by two olive trees. The message was for the small, weak community rebuilding the temple: “Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit” (Zechariah 4:6). Revelation's lampstands carry the same message to small, weak churches in a mighty empire: they shine not by their own power but by the Spirit of the One who walks among them."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Letters to Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum and Thyatira","themeTitle":"“To Him Who Overcomes”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"“To the angel of the assembly in Ephesus write: “He who holds the seven stars in his right hand, he who walks among the seven golden lamp stands says these things:","understanding":"Τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Ἐφέσῳ ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “To the angel of the assembly in Ephesus write” — Ephesus was the greatest city of Asia, a port and home of the vast temple of Artemis; Paul spent three years there (Acts 20:31), and tradition places John there in old age. Τάδε λέγει ὁ κρατῶν τοὺς ἑπτὰ ἀστέρας ἐν τῇ δεξιᾷ αὐτοῦ, “He who holds the seven stars in his right hand” — κρατέω, from the hold family: to grip firmly, to hold with strength. ὁ περιπατῶν ἐν μέσῳ τῶν ἑπτὰ λυχνιῶν τῶν χρυσῶν, “he who walks among the seven golden lamp stands” — each letter begins with a detail from the vision of chapter 1.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G2181","hebrew":"Ἔφεσος","translit":"Éphesos","english":"Ephesus"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"holds"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walks"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stands"}]},{"v":2,"text":"“I know your works, and your toil and perseverance, and that you can’t tolerate evil men, and have tested those who call themselves apostles, and they are not, and found them false.","understanding":"Οἶδα τὰ ἔργα σου καὶ τὸν κόπον καὶ τὴν ὑπομονήν σου, “I know your works, and your toil and perseverance” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family: Christ's full knowledge, which opens five of the seven letters. ἔργον and κόπος, both from the work family: ἔργον is the deed done, κόπος the exhausting labour behind it. ὑπομονή, from the endurance family. καὶ ὅτι οὐ δύνῃ βαστάσαι κακούς, “and that you can’t tolerate evil men” — κακός, from the evil family: bad, worthless. καὶ ἐπείρασας τοὺς λέγοντας ἑαυτοὺς ἀποστόλους, “and have tested those who call themselves apostles” — πειράζω, from the test family: they examined claims to authority. καὶ εὗρες αὐτοὺς ψευδεῖς, “and found them false.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2873","hebrew":"κόπος","translit":"kópos","english":"toil"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G941","hebrew":"βαστάζω","translit":"bastázō","english":"tolerate"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tested"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostles"},{"strongs":"G5571","hebrew":"ψευδής","translit":"pseudḗs","english":"false"}]},{"v":3,"text":"You have perseverance and have endured for my name’s sake, and have not grown weary.","understanding":"καὶ ὑπομονὴν ἔχεις, “You have perseverance” — ὑπομονή, from the endurance family, again. καὶ ἐβάστασας διὰ τὸ ὄνομά μου, “and have endured for my name’s sake” — βαστάζω, to carry a burden: the same word as ‘tolerate’ in verse 2. They could not carry evil men, but they carried suffering for Christ. καὶ οὐ κεκοπίακες, “and have not grown weary” — κοπιάω, from the work family: the verb of κόπος in verse 2. A play on words: they toiled, but did not tire.","words":[{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G941","hebrew":"βαστάζω","translit":"bastázō","english":"endured"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name’s"},{"strongs":"G2872","hebrew":"κοπιάω","translit":"kopiáō","english":"grown weary"}]},{"v":4,"text":"But I have this against you, that you left your first love.","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ ἔχω κατὰ σοῦ ὅτι τὴν ἀγάπην σου τὴν πρώτην ἀφῆκες, “But I have this against you, that you left your first love” — ἀγάπη, from the love family; πρῶτος, first in time and in rank. ἀφίημι, to let go, abandon. Their doctrine was sound and their work tireless, but the love that once burned — for Christ and for one another — had cooled. Orthodoxy without love is not enough.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"left"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Remember therefore from where you have fallen, and repent and do the first works; or else I am coming to you swiftly, and will move your lamp stand out of its place, unless you repent.","understanding":"μνημόνευε οὖν πόθεν πέπτωκας, “Remember therefore from where you have fallen” — μνημονεύω, to call to mind. καὶ μετανόησον καὶ τὰ πρῶτα ἔργα ποίησον, “and repent and do the first works” — μετανοέω, to change one's mind; ἔργον, from the work family. Three steps: remember, repent, return. εἰ δὲ μή, ἔρχομαί σοι καὶ κινήσω τὴν λυχνίαν σου ἐκ τοῦ τόπου αὐτῆς, “or else I am coming to you swiftly, and will move your lamp stand out of its place” — a church that loses its love loses its light.","words":[{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"Remember"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fallen"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G2795","hebrew":"κινέω","translit":"kinéō","english":"move"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stand"}]},{"v":6,"text":"But this you have, that you hate the works of the Nicolaitans, which I also hate.","understanding":"ἀλλὰ τοῦτο ἔχεις, ὅτι μισεῖς τὰ ἔργα τῶν Νικολαϊτῶν, “But this you have, that you hate the works of the Nicolaitans” — ἔργον, from the work family. The Nicolaitans are unknown outside Revelation; from 2:14–15 they seem to have taught that Christians could join in idol feasts and sexual immorality. ἃ κἀγὼ μισῶ, “which I also hate” — Jesus hates the works, not the people.","words":[{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hate"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G3531","hebrew":"Νικολαΐτης","translit":"Nikolaḯtēs","english":"Nicolaitans"}]},{"v":7,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies. To him who overcomes I will give to eat from the tree of life, which is in the Paradise of my God.","understanding":"Ὁ ἔχων οὖς ἀκουσάτω τί τὸ πνεῦμα λέγει ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις, “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies” — plural: every letter is for every church. Τῷ νικῶντι δώσω αὐτῷ φαγεῖν ἐκ τοῦ ξύλου τῆς ζωῆς, “To him who overcomes I will give to eat from the tree of life” — νικάω, to conquer; ζωή, from the life family. ξύλον, a tree (also used of the cross). The tree of Eden, barred after the fall, is opened again (22:2). ὅ ἐστιν ἐν τῷ παραδείσῳ τοῦ θεοῦ, “which is in the Paradise of my God” — παράδεισος, a Persian word for a royal garden.","words":[{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"tree"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G3857","hebrew":"παράδεισος","translit":"parádeisos","english":"Paradise"}]},{"v":8,"text":"“To the angel of the assembly in Smyrna write: “The first and the last, who was dead, and has come to life says these things:","understanding":"Καὶ τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Σμύρνῃ ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “To the angel of the assembly in Smyrna write” — Smyrna (modern İzmir) was proud, beautiful and fiercely loyal to Rome. Its name means ‘myrrh’, a perfume used in burial. Τάδε λέγει ὁ πρῶτος καὶ ὁ ἔσχατος, ὃς ἐγένετο νεκρὸς καὶ ἔζησεν, “The first and the last, who was dead, and has come to life” — ζάω, from the life family. To a church facing death, He speaks as the One who has been through death and out the other side.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4667","hebrew":"Σμύρνα","translit":"Smýrna","english":"Smyrna"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"come to life"}]},{"v":9,"text":"“I know your works, oppression, and your poverty (but you are rich), and the blasphemy of those who say they are Jews, and they are not, but are a synagogue of Satan.","understanding":"Οἶδά σου τὴν θλῖψιν καὶ τὴν πτωχείαν, ἀλλὰ πλούσιος εἶ, “I know your works, oppression, and your poverty (but you are rich)” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family (most early manuscripts lack ‘your works’ here). θλῖψις, crushing pressure; πτωχεία, beggarly poverty, perhaps from plundered property. Poor in the world's eyes, rich in God's. καὶ τὴν βλασφημίαν ἐκ τῶν λεγόντων Ἰουδαίους εἶναι ἑαυτούς, “and the blasphemy of those who say they are Jews” — βλασφημία, slander. Some in the local synagogue were denouncing Christians to the authorities; Jesus says they had become ‘a synagogue of Satan’ — the accuser's assembly. This is not a statement about Jewish people in general, but about those who had turned informer.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"oppression"},{"strongs":"G4432","hebrew":"πτωχεία","translit":"ptōcheía","english":"poverty"},{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"blasphemy"},{"strongs":"G2453","hebrew":"Ἰουδαῖος","translit":"Ioudaîos","english":"Jews"},{"strongs":"G4864","hebrew":"συναγωγή","translit":"synagōgḗ","english":"synagogue"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Don’t be afraid of the things which you are about to suffer. Behold, the devil is about to throw some of you into prison, that you may be tested; and you will have oppression for ten days. Be faithful to death, and I will give you the crown of life.","understanding":"μηδὲν φοβοῦ ἃ μέλλεις πάσχειν, “Don’t be afraid of the things which you are about to suffer” — πάσχω, from the suffer family. Jesus does not promise escape. ἰδοὺ μέλλει βάλλειν ὁ διάβολος ἐξ ὑμῶν εἰς φυλακὴν ἵνα πειρασθῆτε, “Behold, the devil is about to throw some of you into prison, that you may be tested” — πειράζω, from the test family: the devil means it to break them; God uses it to prove them. καὶ ἕξετε θλῖψιν ἡμερῶν δέκα, “and you will have oppression for ten days” — a short, limited time. γίνου πιστὸς ἄχρι θανάτου, καὶ δώσω σοι τὸν στέφανον τῆς ζωῆς, “Be faithful to death, and I will give you the crown of life” — στέφανος, from the crown family: the victor's wreath, not the king's διάδημα; ζωή, from the life family. Smyrna was famous for its ‘crown’ of buildings on the hill. About sixty years later Polycarp, bishop of Smyrna, was burned for refusing to call Caesar lord.","words":[{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"afraid"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffer"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G5438","hebrew":"φυλακή","translit":"phylakḗ","english":"prison"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"tested"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"oppression"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"}]},{"v":11,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies. He who overcomes won’t be harmed by the second death.","understanding":"Ὁ νικῶν οὐ μὴ ἀδικηθῇ ἐκ τοῦ θανάτου τοῦ δευτέρου, “He who overcomes won’t be harmed by the second death” — νικάω, to conquer; ἀδικέω, to harm. The double negative οὐ μή means ‘never, not at all’. The first death may come by a Roman sword; the second death, the lake of fire (20:14), can never touch Christ's faithful ones.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"harmed"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":12,"text":"“To the angel of the assembly in Pergamum write: “He who has the sharp two-edged sword says these things:","understanding":"Καὶ τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Περγάμῳ ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “To the angel of the assembly in Pergamum write” — Pergamum was the old capital of the province, home of the Roman governor who held ‘the right of the sword’, the power of execution. Τάδε λέγει ὁ ἔχων τὴν ῥομφαίαν τὴν δίστομον τὴν ὀξεῖαν, “He who has the sharp two-edged sword” — ῥομφαία, from the sword family: the long, broad sword. The governor's sword can kill the body; Christ's sword is the final word.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4010","hebrew":"Πέργαμος","translit":"Pérgamos","english":"Pergamum"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G4501","hebrew":"ῥομφαία","translit":"rhomphaía","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G3691","hebrew":"ὀξύς","translit":"oxýs","english":"sharp"}]},{"v":13,"text":"“I know your works and where you dwell, where Satan’s throne is. You hold firmly to my name, and didn’t deny my faith in the days of Antipas my witness, my faithful one, who was killed among you, where Satan dwells.","understanding":"Οἶδα ποῦ κατοικεῖς· ὅπου ὁ θρόνος τοῦ Σατανᾶ, “I know your works and where you dwell, where Satan’s throne is” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family. Pergamum had a great altar of Zeus on its acropolis, a temple of Asclepius (whose symbol was a serpent), and the first temple of emperor worship in Asia. Any or all of these made it ‘Satan's throne’. καὶ κρατεῖς τὸ ὄνομά μου, “You hold firmly to my name” — κρατέω, from the hold family. ἐν ταῖς ἡμέραις Ἀντιπᾶς ὁ μάρτυς μου ὁ πιστός μου, “in the days of Antipas my witness, my faithful one” — μάρτυς, from the witness family: the same title as Jesus in 1:5. ὃς ἀπεκτάνθη, “who was killed among you” — here μάρτυς is very close to our ‘martyr’.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan’s"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"hold"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"deny"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G493","hebrew":"Ἀντίπας","translit":"Antípas","english":"Antipas"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witness"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"}]},{"v":14,"text":"But I have a few things against you, because you have there some who hold the teaching of Balaam, who taught Balak to throw a stumbling block before the children of Israel, to eat things sacrificed to idols, and to commit sexual immorality.","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ ἔχω κατὰ σοῦ ὀλίγα, ὅτι ἔχεις ἐκεῖ κρατοῦντας τὴν διδαχὴν Βαλαάμ, “But I have a few things against you, because you have there some who hold the teaching of Balaam” — κρατέω, from the hold family: the same verb as verse 13. Some held firmly to Christ's name; others held firmly to Balaam's teaching. Balaam could not curse Israel, so he advised Balak to lure them into idolatry and immorality (Numbers 25; 31:16). ὃς ἐδίδασκεν τῷ Βαλὰκ βαλεῖν σκάνδαλον, “who taught Balak to throw a stumbling block” — σκάνδαλον, the trigger of a trap. φαγεῖν εἰδωλόθυτα καὶ πορνεῦσαι, “to eat things sacrificed to idols, and to commit sexual immorality” — trade-guild feasts in pagan temples often involved both.","words":[{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"hold"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G903","hebrew":"Βαλαάμ","translit":"Balaám","english":"Balaam"},{"strongs":"G4625","hebrew":"σκάνδαλον","translit":"skándalon","english":"stumbling block"},{"strongs":"G1494","hebrew":"εἰδωλόθυτον","translit":"eidōlóthyton","english":"things sacrificed to idols"},{"strongs":"G4203","hebrew":"πορνεύω","translit":"porneúō","english":"commit sexual immorality"}]},{"v":15,"text":"So you also have some who hold to the teaching of the Nicolaitans likewise.","understanding":"οὕτως ἔχεις καὶ σὺ κρατοῦντας τὴν διδαχὴν τῶν Νικολαϊτῶν ὁμοίως, “So you also have some who hold to the teaching of the Nicolaitans likewise” — κρατέω, from the hold family, a third time. Ephesus hated the Nicolaitans' works (v. 6); Pergamum tolerated their teaching. The Nicolaitans seem to have been a Christian version of Balaam's compromise.","words":[{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"hold"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G3531","hebrew":"Νικολαΐτης","translit":"Nikolaḯtēs","english":"Nicolaitans"}]},{"v":16,"text":"Repent therefore, or else I am coming to you quickly, and I will make war against them with the sword of my mouth.","understanding":"μετανόησον οὖν, “Repent therefore” — μετανοέω, addressed to the whole church, not only to the false teachers: tolerating them is itself a sin. εἰ δὲ μή, ἔρχομαί σοι ταχύ, “or else I am coming to you quickly” — a coming in judgement on this church, before the final coming. καὶ πολεμήσω μετ᾽ αὐτῶν ἐν τῇ ῥομφαίᾳ τοῦ στόματός μου, “and I will make war against them with the sword of my mouth” — πολεμέω; ῥομφαία, from the sword family: Balaam himself died by the sword (Numbers 31:8); now the sword is Christ's word.","words":[{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"Repent"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"quickly"},{"strongs":"G4170","hebrew":"πολεμέω","translit":"poleméō","english":"make war"},{"strongs":"G4501","hebrew":"ῥομφαία","translit":"rhomphaía","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"}]},{"v":17,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies. To him who overcomes, to him I will give of the hidden manna, and I will give him a white stone, and on the stone a new name written, which no one knows but he who receives it.","understanding":"Τῷ νικῶντι δώσω αὐτῷ τοῦ μάννα τοῦ κεκρυμμένου, “To him who overcomes, to him I will give of the hidden manna” — νικάω; μάννα, the bread from heaven (John 6:31–35). Those who refuse the idol feasts will eat Christ's food. καὶ δώσω αὐτῷ ψῆφον λευκήν, “and I will give him a white stone” — ψῆφος, a pebble: used as a vote of acquittal, a token of admission to a feast, or an amulet. καὶ ἐπὶ τὴν ψῆφον ὄνομα καινὸν γεγραμμένον, “and on the stone a new name written” — καινός, from the new family: new in kind, not merely recent. ὃ οὐδεὶς οἶδεν εἰ μὴ ὁ λαμβάνων, “which no one knows but he who receives it” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family: an intimate identity given by Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G3131","hebrew":"μάννα","translit":"mánna","english":"manna"},{"strongs":"G2928","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G5586","hebrew":"ψῆφος","translit":"psēphos","english":"stone"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receives"}]},{"v":18,"text":"“To the angel of the assembly in Thyatira write: “The Son of God, who has his eyes like a flame of fire, and his feet are like burnished brass, says these things:","understanding":"Καὶ τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Θυατείροις ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “To the angel of the assembly in Thyatira write” — the smallest of the seven cities, but the longest letter; a city of trade guilds — dyers, potters, bronze-workers. Lydia, the seller of purple, came from here (Acts 16:14). Τάδε λέγει ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, “The Son of God” — the only time this title appears in Revelation; Thyatira's local god was a son of Zeus. ὁ ἔχων τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς αὐτοῦ ὡς φλόγα πυρός, “who has his eyes like a flame of fire” — He sees through every pretence (v. 23).","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2363","hebrew":"Θυάτειρα","translit":"Thyáteira","english":"Thyatira"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G5395","hebrew":"φλόξ","translit":"phlóx","english":"flame"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G5474","hebrew":"χαλκολίβανον","translit":"chalkolíbanon","english":"burnished brass"}]},{"v":19,"text":"“I know your works, your love, faith, service, patient endurance, and that your last works are more than the first.","understanding":"Οἶδά σου τὰ ἔργα καὶ τὴν ἀγάπην καὶ τὴν πίστιν καὶ τὴν διακονίαν καὶ τὴν ὑπομονήν σου, “I know your works, your love, faith, service, patient endurance” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family; ἔργον, from the work family; ἀγάπη, from the love family; διακονία, from the servant family: practical service; ὑπομονή, from the endurance family. Where Ephesus had lost its first love, Thyatira's love was growing: καὶ τὰ ἔργα σου τὰ ἔσχατα πλείονα τῶν πρώτων, “and that your last works are more than the first.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1248","hebrew":"διακονία","translit":"diakonía","english":"service"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"patient endurance"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"}]},{"v":20,"text":"But I have this against you, that you tolerate your woman, Jezebel, who calls herself a prophetess. She teaches and seduces my servants to commit sexual immorality, and to eat things sacrificed to idols.","understanding":"ἀλλὰ ἔχω κατὰ σοῦ ὅτι ἀφεῖς τὴν γυναῖκα Ἰεζάβελ, “But I have this against you, that you tolerate your woman, Jezebel” — the nickname of a real woman in the church, recalling Ahab's queen who led Israel into Baal worship. ἡ λέγουσα ἑαυτὴν προφῆτιν, “who calls herself a prophetess” — she claimed prophetic authority. καὶ διδάσκει καὶ πλανᾷ τοὺς ἐμοὺς δούλους, “She teaches and seduces my servants” — πλανάω, to lead astray; δοῦλος, from the servant family. πορνεῦσαι καὶ φαγεῖν εἰδωλόθυτα, “to commit sexual immorality, and to eat things sacrificed to idols” — the same compromise as Pergamum. Love that tolerates false teaching is not love.","words":[{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"tolerate"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G2403","hebrew":"Ἰεζαβήλ","translit":"Iezabḗl","english":"Jezebel"},{"strongs":"G4398","hebrew":"προφῆτις","translit":"prophētis","english":"prophetess"},{"strongs":"G1321","hebrew":"διδάσκω","translit":"didáskō","english":"teaches"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"seduces"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G4203","hebrew":"πορνεύω","translit":"porneúō","english":"commit sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G1494","hebrew":"εἰδωλόθυτον","translit":"eidōlóthyton","english":"things sacrificed to idols"}]},{"v":21,"text":"I gave her time to repent, but she refuses to repent of her sexual immorality.","understanding":"καὶ ἔδωκα αὐτῇ χρόνον ἵνα μετανοήσῃ, “I gave her time to repent” — χρόνος, from the time family: a span of time, as distinct from καιρός, the decisive moment (1:3). God's patience gives room to turn. καὶ οὐ θέλει μετανοῆσαι ἐκ τῆς πορνείας αὐτῆς, “but she refuses to repent of her sexual immorality” — θέλω, to will: the problem is not ignorance but refusal.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"refuses"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Behold, I will throw her into a bed, and those who commit adultery with her into great oppression, unless they repent of her works.","understanding":"ἰδοὺ βάλλω αὐτὴν εἰς κλίνην, “Behold, I will throw her into a bed” — the bed of her sin becomes a bed of sickness: a grim irony. καὶ τοὺς μοιχεύοντας μετ᾽ αὐτῆς εἰς θλῖψιν μεγάλην, “and those who commit adultery with her into great oppression” — μοιχεύω, adultery, often a picture of spiritual unfaithfulness. ἐὰν μὴ μετανοήσωσιν ἐκ τῶν ἔργων αὐτῆς, “unless they repent of her works” — ἔργον, from the work family. Even now the door of repentance is open.","words":[{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"throw"},{"strongs":"G2825","hebrew":"κλίνη","translit":"klínē","english":"bed"},{"strongs":"G3431","hebrew":"μοιχεύω","translit":"moicheúō","english":"commit adultery"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"oppression"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":23,"text":"I will kill her children with Death, and all the assemblies will know that I am he who searches the minds and hearts. I will give to each one of you according to your deeds.","understanding":"καὶ τὰ τέκνα αὐτῆς ἀποκτενῶ ἐν θανάτῳ, “I will kill her children with Death” — τέκνον, from the children family: her followers, her spiritual offspring. καὶ γνώσονται πᾶσαι αἱ ἐκκλησίαι, “and all the assemblies will know” — γινώσκω, from the know family: learn by experience. ὅτι ἐγώ εἰμι ὁ ἐραυνῶν νεφροὺς καὶ καρδίας, “that I am he who searches the minds and hearts” — νεφρός, literally the kidneys, seat of the emotions; καρδία, the heart, seat of the will — a phrase used of God in Jeremiah. καὶ δώσω ὑμῖν ἑκάστῳ κατὰ τὰ ἔργα ὑμῶν, “I will give to each one of you according to your deeds” — ἔργον, from the work family.","words":[{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"kill"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2045","hebrew":"ἐρευνάω","translit":"ereunáō","english":"searches"},{"strongs":"G3510","hebrew":"νεφρός","translit":"nephrós","english":"minds"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"}]},{"v":24,"text":"But to you I say, to the rest who are in Thyatira, as many as don’t have this teaching, who don’t know what some call ‘the deep things of Satan,’ to you I say, I am not putting any other burden on you.","understanding":"ὑμῖν δὲ λέγω τοῖς λοιποῖς τοῖς ἐν Θυατείροις, “But to you I say, to the rest who are in Thyatira” — the faithful remnant. οἵτινες οὐκ ἔγνωσαν τὰ βαθέα τοῦ Σατανᾶ, “who don’t know what some call ‘the deep things of Satan’” — γινώσκω, from the know family. The false teachers apparently claimed that the spiritually advanced could explore evil without harm — ‘the deep things’. Jesus calls them what they are. οὐ βάλλω ἐφ᾽ ὑμᾶς ἄλλο βάρος, “I am not putting any other burden on you” — βάρος, weight: no new rules, just faithfulness.","words":[{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G2363","hebrew":"Θυάτειρα","translit":"Thyáteira","english":"Thyatira"},{"strongs":"G1322","hebrew":"διδαχή","translit":"didachḗ","english":"teaching"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G899","hebrew":"βάθος","translit":"báthos","english":"deep things"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G922","hebrew":"βάρος","translit":"báros","english":"burden"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Nevertheless, hold that which you have firmly until I come.","understanding":"πλὴν ὃ ἔχετε κρατήσατε ἄχρι οὗ ἂν ἥξω, “Nevertheless, hold that which you have firmly until I come” — κρατέω, from the hold family: grip it hard; ἥκω, to arrive and be present. The command for the faithful is simple: keep holding on.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"hold"},{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"}]},{"v":26,"text":"He who overcomes, and he who keeps my works to the end, to him I will give authority over the nations.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ νικῶν καὶ ὁ τηρῶν ἄχρι τέλους τὰ ἔργα μου, “He who overcomes, and he who keeps my works to the end” — νικάω; τηρέω, from the hold family, guarding and obeying, as distinct from κρατέω in verse 25, gripping; ἔργον, from the work family: not Jezebel's works (v. 22) but Christ's. ἄχρι τέλους, ‘to the end’ — τέλος, from the perfect family: the goal, the finish line. δώσω αὐτῷ ἐξουσίαν ἐπὶ τῶν ἐθνῶν, “to him I will give authority over the nations” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: delegated authority, the right to act, as distinct from δύναμις, ability.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"}]},{"v":27,"text":"He will rule them with a rod of iron, shattering them like clay pots; as I also have received of my Father:","understanding":"καὶ ποιμανεῖ αὐτοὺς ἐν ῥάβδῳ σιδηρᾷ, “He will rule them with a rod of iron” — ποιμαίνω, to shepherd: from Psalm 2, where God promises His anointed King that He will rule the nations ‘with a rod of iron’. ὡς τὰ σκεύη τὰ κεραμικὰ συντρίβεται, “shattering them like clay pots” — Thyatira's potters would understand. ὡς κἀγὼ εἴληφα παρὰ τοῦ πατρός μου, “as I also have received of my Father” — the Messiah's reign is shared with His faithful people.","words":[{"strongs":"G4165","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"rod"},{"strongs":"G4603","hebrew":"σιδήρεος","translit":"sidḗreos","english":"iron"},{"strongs":"G2764","hebrew":"κεραμικός","translit":"keramikós","english":"clay"},{"strongs":"G4632","hebrew":"σκεῦος","translit":"skeûos","english":"pots"},{"strongs":"G4937","hebrew":"συντρίβω","translit":"syntríbō","english":"shattering"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":28,"text":"and I will give him the morning star.","understanding":"καὶ δώσω αὐτῷ τὸν ἀστέρα τὸν πρωϊνόν, “and I will give him the morning star” — ἀστήρ; πρωϊνός, of the early morning. Jesus later says, “I am the root and the offspring of David; the Bright and Morning Star” (Revelation 22:16). The greatest reward is Christ Himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"star"},{"strongs":"G4407","hebrew":"πρωϊνός","translit":"prōïnós","english":"morning"}]},{"v":29,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.","understanding":"Ὁ ἔχων οὖς ἀκουσάτω τί τὸ πνεῦμα λέγει ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις, “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies” — from the fourth letter onward this call comes after the promise to the overcomer, not before it. The words of Jesus to one church are the words of the Spirit to every church.","words":[{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Fading Flame","verseRef":"v. 2-5","image":"Picture a busy church in a great city. The doors are always open, the programmes are full, the doctrine is sharp, false teachers are spotted at once. But look closely at the lampstand at the front: the flame is burning low, flickering, nearly out.","meaning":"“I know your works, and your toil and perseverance” (v. 2). “But I have this against you, that you left your first love” (v. 4). “Remember therefore from where you have fallen, and repent and do the first works” (v. 5). Activity is not the same as love. Jesus wants the flame, not just the stand."},{"title":"Ten Days and a Crown","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture a prison cell in Smyrna, damp and dark. A handful of believers sit chained together; their property has been seized and their neighbours have informed on them. Through the bars they can see the hill of their city, crowned with temples. And they hear a voice say, ‘Be faithful.’","meaning":"“Don’t be afraid of the things which you are about to suffer” (v. 10). “Be faithful to death, and I will give you the crown of life” (v. 10). Their suffering has a limit — ‘ten days’ — and an end beyond it that no prison can take away."},{"title":"Two Kinds of Holding","verseRef":"v. 13-16","image":"Picture two hands in the same congregation in Pergamum. One grips a name — the name of Jesus — and will not let go though the sword is raised. The other grips a different teaching: a little compromise at the temple feast, a little freedom in the body.","meaning":"“You hold firmly to my name, and didn’t deny my faith” (v. 13). “But I have a few things against you, because you have there some who hold the teaching of Balaam” (v. 14). The same word, κρατέω, describes both grips. What are you holding on to?"},{"title":"Eyes Like Fire","verseRef":"v. 18-23","image":"Picture a church meeting where everyone looks devoted, loving and busy. A confident woman stands up and speaks smooth words about freedom and ‘deep things’. Then picture eyes like a flame of fire looking past every face, into every heart.","meaning":"“I am he who searches the minds and hearts. I will give to each one of you according to your deeds” (v. 23). Christ sees through every pretence. To the faithful He says, “hold that which you have firmly until I come” (v. 25)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Ephesus: Lost Love (v. 1-7)","body":"Christ, who holds the stars and walks among the lampstands, praises Ephesus for its labour, endurance and testing of false apostles, but rebukes it for leaving its first love; it must remember, repent and do the first works or lose its lampstand; the overcomer will eat from the tree of life."},{"label":"Smyrna: Faithful in Suffering (v. 8-11)","body":"The First and Last, who died and lives, knows Smyrna's affliction, poverty and slander; He warns of prison and ten days of affliction and calls them to be faithful to death for the crown of life; the overcomer will not be harmed by the second death."},{"label":"Pergamum: Compromise (v. 12-17)","body":"The One with the sharp sword knows that Pergamum lives where Satan's throne is and has held fast His name even when Antipas was killed, but rebukes it for tolerating the teaching of Balaam and the Nicolaitans; it must repent; the overcomer will receive hidden manna and a white stone with a new name."},{"label":"Thyatira: Tolerated Corruption (v. 18-29)","body":"The Son of God with eyes of fire praises Thyatira's growing love, faith, service and endurance, but rebukes it for tolerating ‘Jezebel’, who leads His servants into immorality and idolatry; she will be judged and all the churches will know He searches hearts; the faithful must hold fast until He comes; the overcomer will share His authority and receive the morning star."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Shape of the Seven Letters","paragraphs":["The seven letters of chapters 2–3 follow a pattern: an address to the church's angel; a title of Christ taken from the vision of chapter 1; ‘I know’ — His assessment of the church; praise, rebuke, or both; a call to repent or hold fast; the refrain “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies” (v. 7); and a promise “To him who overcomes” (v. 7). The promises point forward to the new creation of chapters 21–22: the tree of life, escape from the second death, a new name, authority with Christ, the morning star.","The letters were written to seven real churches, and they fit what we know of those cities remarkably well. But the refrain is plural — ‘the assemblies’ — so every church is meant to hear every letter. Some have read the seven churches as seven ages of church history; there is no sign of this in the text itself, and the more natural reading is that they show the kinds of strength and weakness found in churches in every age.","Every letter calls for ‘overcoming’. The verb νικάω does not mean winning a political or military victory; it means staying faithful to Jesus to the end, whatever the cost. The model is the Lamb Himself, who “has overcome” (5:5) by being slain. The first letter of John said the same: “This is the victory that has overcome the world: your faith” (1 John 5:4)."]},{"heading":"Ephesus and Smyrna: Love and Faithfulness","paragraphs":["Ephesus was doing almost everything right. Jesus says, “I know your works, and your toil and perseverance, and that you can’t tolerate evil men, and have tested those who call themselves apostles, and they are not, and found them false” (v. 2). Paul had warned the Ephesian elders that ‘vicious wolves’ would come in (Acts 20:29), and they had guarded the flock well. But something essential had gone: “But I have this against you, that you left your first love” (v. 4). Whether this is love for Christ, love for one another, or both, the result was a church busy and correct but cold.","The cure is in three verbs: “Remember therefore from where you have fallen, and repent and do the first works” (v. 5). Love is not restored by feelings alone but by returning to the ‘first works’ — the prayer, worship, care and witness that once flowed from a heart full of Christ. If not, Jesus will remove their lampstand: the church will cease to be a church.","Smyrna receives no rebuke at all. It is poor, slandered and about to be imprisoned. Jesus speaks to it as “The first and the last, who was dead, and has come to life” (v. 8). He does not promise escape but faithfulness: “Be faithful to death, and I will give you the crown of life” (v. 10). The ‘ten days’ suggests a limited time under God's control. The second death cannot touch those who are faithful through the first."]},{"heading":"Pergamum and Thyatira: Compromise from Within","paragraphs":["Pergamum lived where Satan's throne was — perhaps the great altar of Zeus, perhaps the temple of the emperor. Yet it held firm under persecution: “You hold firmly to my name, and didn’t deny my faith in the days of Antipas my witness, my faithful one, who was killed among you” (v. 13). Antipas is called by the same title as Jesus: ‘faithful witness’. The church that survived the sword from outside was being weakened by compromise inside.","That compromise is called “the teaching of Balaam” (v. 14). In Numbers, Balaam could not curse Israel, but he could advise Balak to seduce them into idolatry and immorality. In first-century cities, trade guilds held feasts in pagan temples with food offered to idols, often followed by immorality. To refuse could cost a Christian his job and his place in society. The Nicolaitans apparently taught that it did not matter. Jesus says it does.","Thyatira's love and service were growing (v. 19), but it tolerated a woman Jesus calls ‘Jezebel’, who claimed to be a prophetess and taught His servants the same compromise (v. 20). She and her followers boasted of knowing ‘the deep things’; Jesus calls them “the deep things of Satan” (v. 24). Her judgement will show all the churches that Christ “searches the minds and hearts” (v. 23). To the faithful remnant He gives no new burden, only this: “hold that which you have firmly until I come” (v. 25)."]},{"heading":"The Promises to the Overcomer","paragraphs":["Each promise answers the church's situation. Ephesus, which has lost its first love, is offered a return to the beginning: the tree of life in God's paradise (v. 7). Smyrna, facing death, is promised the crown of life and safety from the second death (vv. 10–11). Pergamum, tempted by idol feasts, is promised hidden manna — the true bread from heaven — and a white stone with a new name (v. 17). Thyatira, under pressure from a seductive teacher, is promised a share in Christ's own rule over the nations, and the morning star (vv. 26–28).","The ‘crown of life’ (v. 10) is a στέφανος, a victor's wreath, not a king's διάδημα. It is the prize given to the athlete who finishes the race, as James promised: “Blessed is the man who endures temptation, for when he has been approved, he will receive the crown of life, which the Lord promised to those who love him” (James 1:12).","And the final promise is Christ Himself: “I will give him the morning star” (v. 28). At the end of the book Jesus says, “I, Jesus, have sent my angel to testify these things to you for the assemblies. I am the root and the offspring of David; the Bright and Morning Star” (Revelation 22:16). Every reward points to the Rewarder."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"From Paul's Ephesus to John's","paragraphs":["Paul spent three years in Ephesus, and when he said goodbye to its elders he warned them: “I know that after my departure, vicious wolves will enter in among you, not sparing the flock” (Acts 20:29). Forty years later, Jesus praises them for testing false apostles (v. 2). But Paul had also prayed that the Ephesians would “know Christ’s love which surpasses knowledge” (Ephesians 3:19), and he closed his letter to them, “Grace be with all those who love our Lord Jesus Christ with incorruptible love” (Ephesians 6:24). That love is what they had lost.","Paul faced the question of idol food in Corinth. He taught that idols are nothing, but warned, “But I say that the things which the Gentiles sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons, and not to God, and I don’t desire that you would have fellowship with demons” (1 Corinthians 10:20), and “Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry” (1 Corinthians 10:14). The Nicolaitans and ‘Jezebel’ taught the opposite.","Jesus Himself promised His suffering followers, “In the world you have oppression; but cheer up! I have overcome the world” (John 16:33). And He said of the bread from heaven, “I am the bread of life. He who comes to me will not be hungry” (John 6:35) — the true ‘hidden manna’ of verse 17."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Overcome / conquer","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","strongs":"G3528 (victory νίκη, níkē, G3529)","verses":"v. 7, 11, 17, 26; 3:5, 12, 21; 5:5; 12:11; 21:7","insight":"Strong's: “to subdue (literally or figuratively).” νικάω, from νίκη (victory), is the key verb of Revelation — every one of the seven letters ends with a promise to ‘him who overcomes’. But the victory is of a surprising kind. The Lamb ‘has overcome’ by being slain (5:5–6); the saints overcome the dragon ‘because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony’ (12:11). Even the beast is said to ‘overcome’ the saints by killing them (13:7) — yet in God's eyes they are the conquerors. To overcome is to remain faithful to Jesus to the end."},{"english":"Crown","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","strongs":"G4735 (crowns διάδημα, diádēma, G1238)","verses":"v. 10; 3:11; 4:4, 10","insight":"Strong's: “a chaplet (as a badge of royalty, a prize in the public games or a symbol of honor generally; but more conspicuous and elaborate than the simple fillet, G1238 (διάδημα)), literally or figuratively.” στέφανος, from the crown family, is the victor's wreath of olive or laurel, the prize of the games and the honour of the faithful. Smyrna is promised ‘the crown of life’ — the wreath that consists of life itself. The twenty-four elders cast their στέφανοι before the throne (4:10). In contrast the dragon and the beast wear διαδήματα (12:3; 13:1), royal crowns — but Christ wears ‘many’ (19:12)."},{"english":"Repent","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","strongs":"G3340 (works ἔργον, érgon, G2041)","verses":"v. 5, 16, 21, 22; 3:3, 19","insight":"Strong's: “to think differently or afterwards, i.e. reconsider (morally, feel compunction).” μετανοέω appears eight times in chapters 2–3 — five of the seven churches are told to repent. It is not just regret but a change of mind that leads to a change of direction: Ephesus must ‘do the first works’. Repentance is offered even to ‘Jezebel’ (v. 21), but she ‘refuses’. The tragedy of judgement in Revelation is not that God refuses mercy, but that people refuse to repent (9:20–21; 16:9, 11)."},{"english":"White stone","hebrew":"ψῆφος","translit":"psēphos","strongs":"G5586 (manna μάννα, mánna, G3131)","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “a pebble (as worn smooth by handling), i.e. (by implication, of use as a counter or ballot) a verdict (of acquittal) or ticket (of admission); a vote.” ψῆφος had many uses in the ancient world. A white stone was cast by a juror to vote ‘not guilty’; a stone token could serve as a ticket of admission to a banquet. Both fit: the overcomer is acquitted, and invited to the feast of the hidden manna. And on the stone is a new name, known only to the one who receives it — a personal identity given by Christ Himself."},{"english":"Time","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","strongs":"G5550 (time καιρός, kairós, G2540)","verses":"v. 21; 6:11; 10:6; 20:3","insight":"Strong's: “a space of time (in general, and thus properly distinguished from G2540 (καιρός), which designates a fixed or special occasion; and from G165 (αἰών), which denotes a particular period) or interval; by extension, an individual opportunity; by implication, delay.” χρόνος, from the time family, is a span of time; καιρός is the decisive season. In 1:3 ‘the time (καιρός) is at hand’ — the moment of decision is near. In 2:21 God ‘gave her time (χρόνος) to repent’ — a span of patience. And in 10:6 the angel swears that ‘there will no longer be delay’ (χρόνος): God's patience is long, but not endless."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In these four letters the Holy Spirit is speaking to your church — and to you.","items":[{"title":"Return to Your First Love","body":"Jesus' words to Ephesus may be the hardest to hear because they come to the busy and the faithful: “you left your first love” (v. 4). The Spirit asks you to remember when Jesus was your joy, not just your duty. What did you do then that you no longer do? Go back and do it."},{"title":"Fear Not What You Are About to Suffer","body":"To Smyrna Jesus says, “Don’t be afraid of the things which you are about to suffer” (v. 10). The Spirit does not promise you an easy road, but He promises that your suffering is limited, seen and rewarded. If you are facing loss for Christ today, hear Him: be faithful, and the crown of life is yours."},{"title":"What Are You Tolerating?","body":"Pergamum and Thyatira were strong in many ways but tolerated teaching that made sin acceptable. The Spirit asks gently but clearly: is there a compromise in your life that you have called freedom? Is there a teaching you have accepted because it was comfortable? He searches the heart — so let Him."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Love Check","body":"Is your love for Jesus warmer, the same, or colder than when you first believed (v. 4)? What evidence do you see?"},{"title":"The Courage Check","body":"Where are you under pressure to deny Christ's name — at work, in your family, online (v. 13)? Are you holding firmly?"},{"title":"The Compromise Check","body":"Is there a ‘feast’ you join — a habit, a group, a form of entertainment — that draws you into what Christ hates (vv. 14, 20)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Do the First Works","body":"Write down three things you did when your faith was fresh — perhaps early-morning prayer, telling others about Jesus, serving someone in need. Choose one and do it every day this week (v. 5)."},{"title":"Hold Fast with a Friend","body":"Find one believer who is under pressure for their faith — at home, in your church, or in the persecuted church overseas. Pray for them by name daily this week, and send them a word of encouragement from verse 10."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You hold the stars in Your right hand and walk among the lampstands. You know my works. Where my love has cooled, call me back; help me remember, repent and do the first works, so that my lampstand keeps shining. You are the First and the Last, who was dead and came to life. When I suffer, keep me from fear; make me faithful to death, and I will trust You for the crown of life. You hold the sharp two-edged sword. Keep me holding firmly to Your name, and cut away every compromise in me. You are the Son of God with eyes like fire. Search my mind and heart, and expose every ‘deep thing’ that is not of You. Help me hold fast what I have until You come. Let me overcome, by Your grace, and give me the tree of life, the hidden manna, a new name, and Yourself, the Bright Morning Star. I have an ear; let me hear what the Spirit says to the churches. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Paul's Farewell to the Ephesian Elders","ref":"Acts 20:17-38","relatedVerses":"v. 1-7","summary":"On his way to Jerusalem, Paul called the elders of Ephesus to Miletus. He reminded them how he had served among them with tears and taught them publicly and from house to house. He warned that wolves would come in and that some from among them would distort the truth: “Therefore watch, remembering that for a period of three years I didn’t cease to admonish everyone night and day with tears” (Acts 20:31). They wept and kissed him, knowing they would not see his face again. A generation later, Jesus praises the same church for guarding the truth — and calls them back to the love that once filled those tears."},{"title":"Balaam and Balak","ref":"Numbers 22–25; 31:16","relatedVerses":"v. 14-16","summary":"Balak, king of Moab, hired the prophet Balaam to curse Israel. Each time Balaam opened his mouth, God turned the curse into a blessing. But Balaam found another way: he advised Balak to invite Israel to the feasts of Baal of Peor, where they ate sacrifices to idols and committed immorality with the women of Moab. A plague followed, and Balaam later died by the sword. Pergamum's false teachers used the same strategy: if the church cannot be destroyed from outside, seduce it from within."},{"title":"Ahab and Jezebel","ref":"1 Kings 16:29-33; 18; 21","relatedVerses":"v. 20-23","summary":"Jezebel, daughter of the king of Sidon, married Ahab king of Israel and brought the worship of Baal into the heart of the nation. She fed hundreds of prophets of Baal, killed the prophets of the Lord, and arranged the murder of Naboth for his vineyard. Elijah stood against her on Mount Carmel. Her name became a byword for a powerful, seductive leader who leads God's people into idolatry. Jesus uses it for the false prophetess of Thyatira — and promises the same certain judgement unless she repents."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Letters to Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea","themeTitle":"“Behold, I Stand at the Door and Knock”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"“And to the angel of the assembly in Sardis write: “He who has the seven Spirits of God, and the seven stars says these things: “I know your works, that you have a reputation of being alive, but you are dead.","understanding":"Καὶ τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Σάρδεσιν ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “And to the angel of the assembly in Sardis write” — Sardis had been the rich capital of King Croesus, built on a high hill thought impregnable, yet twice captured because its guards were not watching. Τάδε λέγει ὁ ἔχων τὰ ἑπτὰ πνεύματα τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τοὺς ἑπτὰ ἀστέρας, “He who has the seven Spirits of God, and the seven stars” — the Spirit who gives life to a dead church. Οἶδά σου τὰ ἔργα, “I know your works” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family; ἔργον, from the work family. ὅτι ὄνομα ἔχεις ὅτι ζῇς, καὶ νεκρὸς εἶ, “that you have a reputation of being alive, but you are dead” — ζάω, from the life family. ὄνομα, a name, a reputation: Sardis looked alive.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assembly"},{"strongs":"G4554","hebrew":"Σάρδεις","translit":"Sárdeis","english":"Sardis"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirits"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"reputation"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"alive"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Wake up, and keep the things that remain, which you were about to throw away, for I have found no works of yours perfected before my God.","understanding":"γίνου γρηγορῶν, “Wake up” — γρηγορεύω, from the sleep family: to keep awake, be watchful — the very thing Sardis's guards had failed to do. καὶ στήρισον τὰ λοιπὰ ἃ ἔμελλον ἀποθανεῖν, “and keep the things that remain, which you were about to throw away” — στηρίζω, to strengthen, make firm: what little life is left is dying. οὐ γὰρ εὕρηκά σου ἔργα πεπληρωμένα ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ μου, “for I have found no works of yours perfected before my God” — ἔργον, from the work family; πληρόω, from the perfect family: filled up, completed. They started things they never finished.","words":[{"strongs":"G1127","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","english":"Wake up"},{"strongs":"G4741","hebrew":"στηρίζω","translit":"stērízō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"remain"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"perfected"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Remember therefore how you have received and heard. Keep it, and repent. If therefore you won’t watch, I will come as a thief, and you won’t know what hour I will come upon you.","understanding":"μνημόνευε οὖν πῶς εἴληφας καὶ ἤκουσας, “Remember therefore how you have received and heard” — the gospel they once received. καὶ τήρει καὶ μετανόησον, “Keep it, and repent” — τηρέω, from the hold family. ἐὰν οὖν μὴ γρηγορήσῃς, ἥξω ὡς κλέπτης, “If therefore you won’t watch, I will come as a thief” — γρηγορεύω, from the sleep family, again; κλέπτης, a thief: Jesus' own picture (Matthew 24:43). καὶ οὐ μὴ γνῷς ποίαν ὥραν ἥξω ἐπὶ σέ, “and you won’t know what hour I will come upon you” — γινώσκω, from the know family; ὥρα, from the time family: the appointed hour, unknown to the sleeper.","words":[{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"Remember"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"Keep"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G1127","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","english":"watch"},{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2812","hebrew":"κλέπτης","translit":"kléptēs","english":"thief"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Nevertheless you have a few names in Sardis that did not defile their garments. They will walk with me in white, for they are worthy.","understanding":"ἀλλὰ ἔχεις ὀλίγα ὀνόματα ἐν Σάρδεσιν, “Nevertheless you have a few names in Sardis” — a faithful remnant, known by name. ἃ οὐκ ἐμόλυναν τὰ ἱμάτια αὐτῶν, “that did not defile their garments” — μολύνω, to stain: Sardis was a centre of wool-dyeing; their clothes were clean of compromise. καὶ περιπατήσουσιν μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ ἐν λευκοῖς, “They will walk with me in white” — white is the colour of purity and victory throughout Revelation. ὅτι ἄξιοί εἰσιν, “for they are worthy” — worthy not by merit, but because they have kept faith with the One who is worthy (5:9).","words":[{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G4554","hebrew":"Σάρδεις","translit":"Sárdeis","english":"Sardis"},{"strongs":"G3435","hebrew":"μολύνω","translit":"molýnō","english":"defile"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garments"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"}]},{"v":5,"text":"He who overcomes will be arrayed in white garments, and I will in no way blot his name out of the book of life, and I will confess his name before my Father, and before his angels.","understanding":"Ὁ νικῶν οὕτως περιβαλεῖται ἐν ἱματίοις λευκοῖς, “He who overcomes will be arrayed in white garments” — νικάω. καὶ οὐ μὴ ἐξαλείψω τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ ἐκ τῆς βίβλου τῆς ζωῆς, “and I will in no way blot his name out of the book of life” — βίβλος, from the book family: the register of citizens; ζωή, from the life family. Cities struck traitors' names from their rolls; Christ will never strike out His faithful ones. οὐ μή, a strong double negative. καὶ ὁμολογήσω τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ ἐνώπιον τοῦ πατρός μου, “and I will confess his name before my Father” — the promise of Jesus in the Gospels (Matthew 10:32).","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"arrayed"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garments"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G1813","hebrew":"ἐξαλείφω","translit":"exaleíphō","english":"blot"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G976","hebrew":"βίβλος","translit":"bíblos","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G3670","hebrew":"ὁμολογέω","translit":"homologéō","english":"confess"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":6,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.","understanding":"Ὁ ἔχων οὖς ἀκουσάτω, “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies” — a sleeping church needs ears opened before anything else.","words":[{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"}]},{"v":7,"text":"“To the angel of the assembly in Philadelphia write: “He who is holy, he who is true, he who has the key of David, he who opens and no one can shut, and who shuts and no one opens, says these things:","understanding":"Καὶ τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Φιλαδελφείᾳ ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “To the angel of the assembly in Philadelphia write” — a young city on a frontier, called ‘the gateway to the East’, often shaken by earthquakes. Τάδε λέγει ὁ ἅγιος, ὁ ἀληθινός, “He who is holy, he who is true” — ἀληθινός, genuine, real. ὁ ἔχων τὴν κλεῖν Δαυίδ, “he who has the key of David” — κλείς: from Isaiah 22, where Eliakim is given the key of David's house. Jesus has full authority over the house of God and the kingdom. ὁ ἀνοίγων καὶ οὐδεὶς κλείσει, “he who opens and no one can shut” — ἀνοίγω, κλείω. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G5359","hebrew":"Φιλαδέλφεια","translit":"Philadélpheia","english":"Philadelphia"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G2807","hebrew":"κλείς","translit":"kleís","english":"key"},{"strongs":"G1138","hebrew":"Δαβίδ","translit":"Dabíd","english":"David"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opens"},{"strongs":"G2808","hebrew":"κλείω","translit":"kleíō","english":"shut"}]},{"v":8,"text":"“I know your works (behold, I have set before you an open door, which no one can shut), that you have a little power, and kept my word, and didn’t deny my name.","understanding":"Οἶδά σου τὰ ἔργα, “I know your works” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family; ἔργον, from the work family. ἰδοὺ δέδωκα ἐνώπιόν σου θύραν ἠνεῳγμένην, “behold, I have set before you an open door, which no one can shut” — θύρα: an open door into the kingdom (shut to them by the synagogue), and an open door for mission. ὅτι μικρὰν ἔχεις δύναμιν, “that you have a little power” — δύναμις, from the power family: little inherent strength. καὶ ἐτήρησάς μου τὸν λόγον, “and kept my word” — τηρέω, from the hold family; λόγος, from the word family. Small in power, great in faithfulness.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2374","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","english":"door"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G2808","hebrew":"κλείω","translit":"kleíō","english":"shut"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"little"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"kept"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"deny"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Behold, I give some of the synagogue of Satan, of those who say they are Jews, and they are not, but lie. Behold, I will make them to come and worship before your feet, and to know that I have loved you.","understanding":"ἰδοὺ διδῶ ἐκ τῆς συναγωγῆς τοῦ Σατανᾶ, “Behold, I give some of the synagogue of Satan” — as in Smyrna (2:9), some in the synagogue were accusing the Christians. ἰδοὺ ποιήσω αὐτοὺς ἵνα ἥξουσιν καὶ προσκυνήσουσιν ἐνώπιον τῶν ποδῶν σου, “Behold, I will make them to come and worship before your feet” — Isaiah promised that the Gentiles would bow before Israel; now the opponents will bow before the despised church. καὶ γνῶσιν ὅτι ἐγὼ ἠγάπησά σε, “and to know that I have loved you” — γινώσκω, from the know family; ἀγαπάω, from the love family: the love of choice and commitment.","words":[{"strongs":"G4864","hebrew":"συναγωγή","translit":"synagōgḗ","english":"synagogue"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G2453","hebrew":"Ἰουδαῖος","translit":"Ioudaîos","english":"Jews"},{"strongs":"G5574","hebrew":"ψεύδομαι","translit":"pseúdomai","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Because you kept my command to endure, I also will keep you from the hour of testing, which is to come on the whole world, to test those who dwell on the earth.","understanding":"ὅτι ἐτήρησας τὸν λόγον τῆς ὑπομονῆς μου, “Because you kept my command to endure” — τηρέω, from the hold family; λόγος, from the word family: literally ‘the word of my endurance’; ὑπομονή, from the endurance family. κἀγώ σε τηρήσω ἐκ τῆς ὥρας τοῦ πειρασμοῦ, “I also will keep you from the hour of testing” — τηρέω again: a play on words — you kept My word, I will keep you. ὥρα, from the time family; πειρασμός, from the test family. Some read ‘keep you from’ as removal before the trial; others as protection through it, as Jesus prayed, ‘keep them from the evil one’ (John 17:15). τῆς μελλούσης ἔρχεσθαι ἐπὶ τῆς οἰκουμένης ὅλης, “which is to come on the whole world” — οἰκουμένη, from the world family: the inhabited earth. πειράσαι τοὺς κατοικοῦντας ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “to test those who dwell on the earth” — πειράζω, from the test family; γῆ, from the world family. ‘Those who dwell on the earth’ is Revelation's phrase for unbelievers (6:10; 13:8).","words":[{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"kept"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"command"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"endure"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"testing"},{"strongs":"G3625","hebrew":"οἰκουμένη","translit":"oikouménē","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G3985","hebrew":"πειράζω","translit":"peirázō","english":"test"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":11,"text":"I am coming quickly! Hold firmly that which you have, so that no one takes your crown.","understanding":"ἔρχομαι ταχύ, “I am coming quickly!” — ταχύ, soon, suddenly. κράτει ὃ ἔχεις, “Hold firmly that which you have” — κρατέω, from the hold family, as in 2:25. ἵνα μηδεὶς λάβῃ τὸν στέφανόν σου, “so that no one takes your crown” — στέφανος, from the crown family: the victor's wreath. They already have it; they must not let it be snatched away.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"quickly"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"Hold"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"takes"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"}]},{"v":12,"text":"He who overcomes, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God, and he will go out from there no more. I will write on him the name of my God, and the name of the city of my God, the new Jerusalem, which comes down out of heaven from my God, and my own new name.","understanding":"Ὁ νικῶν ποιήσω αὐτὸν στῦλον ἐν τῷ ναῷ τοῦ θεοῦ μου, “He who overcomes, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God” — στῦλος: in a city where earthquakes often left only the pillars of buildings standing, a promise of permanence. καὶ ἔξω οὐ μὴ ἐξέλθῃ ἔτι, “and he will go out from there no more” — after earthquakes the citizens fled the city; the overcomer will be secure forever. καὶ γράψω ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν τὸ ὄνομα τοῦ θεοῦ μου καὶ τὸ ὄνομα τῆς πόλεως τοῦ θεοῦ μου, τῆς καινῆς Ἰερουσαλήμ, “I will write on him the name of my God, and the name of the city of my God, the new Jerusalem” — καινός, from the new family: new in kind. καὶ τὸ ὄνομά μου τὸ καινόν, “and my own new name” — καινός again: three names, God's, the city's and Christ's.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G4769","hebrew":"στῦλος","translit":"stŷlos","english":"pillar"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G2419","hebrew":"Ἱερουσαλήμ","translit":"Hierousalḗm","english":"Jerusalem"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"}]},{"v":13,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.","understanding":"Ὁ ἔχων οὖς ἀκουσάτω, “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies” — Philadelphia, like Smyrna, receives no rebuke.","words":[{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"}]},{"v":14,"text":"“To the angel of the assembly in Laodicea write: “The Amen, the Faithful and True Witness, the Beginning of God’s creation, says these things:","understanding":"Καὶ τῷ ἀγγέλῳ τῆς ἐν Λαοδικείᾳ ἐκκλησίας γράψον, “To the angel of the assembly in Laodicea write” — a wealthy banking centre, famous for black wool and an eye ointment, so rich it refused imperial aid after an earthquake in AD 60. Τάδε λέγει ὁ Ἀμήν, “The Amen” — the One who is God's ‘yes’ (2 Corinthians 1:20). ὁ μάρτυς ὁ πιστὸς καὶ ἀληθινός, “the Faithful and True Witness” — μάρτυς, from the witness family. ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κτίσεως τοῦ θεοῦ, “the Beginning of God’s creation” — ἀρχή, from the power family: it means beginning, but also first cause, ruler, source. Not the first thing created, but the origin and ruler of creation (Colossians 1:15–18).","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2993","hebrew":"Λαοδίκεια","translit":"Laodíkeia","english":"Laodicea"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"Witness"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"Faithful"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"True"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"Beginning"},{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"creation"}]},{"v":15,"text":"“I know your works, that you are neither cold nor hot. I wish you were cold or hot.","understanding":"Οἶδά σου τὰ ἔργα, “I know your works” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family; ἔργον, from the work family. ὅτι οὔτε ψυχρὸς εἶ οὔτε ζεστός, “that you are neither cold nor hot” — ψυχρός, cold; ζεστός, boiling hot. Hierapolis nearby had hot healing springs; Colossae had cold, pure water. Laodicea's water came by aqueduct and arrived lukewarm. ὄφελον ψυχρὸς ἦς ἢ ζεστός, “I wish you were cold or hot” — both hot and cold water are useful; lukewarm is useless.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G5593","hebrew":"ψυχρός","translit":"psychrós","english":"cold"},{"strongs":"G2200","hebrew":"ζεστός","translit":"zestós","english":"hot"}]},{"v":16,"text":"So, because you are lukewarm, and neither hot nor cold, I will vomit you out of my mouth.","understanding":"οὕτως ὅτι χλιαρὸς εἶ, “So, because you are lukewarm” — χλιαρός, tepid. Not ‘cold’ as in hostile and ‘hot’ as in zealous; rather useless, like water that neither heals nor refreshes. μέλλω σε ἐμέσαι ἐκ τοῦ στόματός μου, “I will vomit you out of my mouth” — ἐμέω, to vomit: Laodicea's lukewarm, mineral-laden water was known to make people sick.","words":[{"strongs":"G5513","hebrew":"χλιαρός","translit":"chliarós","english":"lukewarm"},{"strongs":"G2200","hebrew":"ζεστός","translit":"zestós","english":"hot"},{"strongs":"G5593","hebrew":"ψυχρός","translit":"psychrós","english":"cold"},{"strongs":"G1692","hebrew":"ἐμέω","translit":"eméō","english":"vomit"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Because you say, ‘I am rich, and have gotten riches, and have need of nothing;’ and don’t know that you are the wretched one, miserable, poor, blind, and naked;","understanding":"ὅτι λέγεις ὅτι πλούσιός εἰμι καὶ πεπλούτηκα καὶ οὐδὲν χρείαν ἔχω, “Because you say, ‘I am rich, and have gotten riches, and have need of nothing’” — the self-sufficiency of a city that needed no help from Rome, and of a church that felt no need of Christ. καὶ οὐκ οἶδας ὅτι σὺ εἶ ὁ ταλαίπωρος καὶ ἐλεεινὸς καὶ πτωχὸς καὶ τυφλὸς καὶ γυμνός, “and don’t know that you are the wretched one, miserable, poor, blind, and naked” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family. Five words that turn their three boasts upside down: rich in banks, poor before God; famed for eye ointment, blind; famed for wool, naked.","words":[{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"gotten riches"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G5005","hebrew":"ταλαίπωρος","translit":"talaípōros","english":"wretched"},{"strongs":"G1652","hebrew":"ἐλεεινός","translit":"eleeinós","english":"miserable"},{"strongs":"G4434","hebrew":"πτωχός","translit":"ptōchós","english":"poor"},{"strongs":"G5185","hebrew":"τυφλός","translit":"typhlós","english":"blind"},{"strongs":"G1131","hebrew":"γυμνός","translit":"gymnós","english":"naked"}]},{"v":18,"text":"I counsel you to buy from me gold refined by fire, that you may become rich; and white garments, that you may clothe yourself, and that the shame of your nakedness may not be revealed; and eye salve to anoint your eyes, that you may see.","understanding":"συμβουλεύω σοι ἀγοράσαι παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ χρυσίον πεπυρωμένον ἐκ πυρός, “I counsel you to buy from me gold refined by fire” — ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family: to buy in the market. The ‘buying’ is without money (Isaiah 55:1): what Christ offers must be received from Him. καὶ ἱμάτια λευκά, “and white garments” — not the city's black wool. καὶ μὴ φανερωθῇ ἡ αἰσχύνη τῆς γυμνότητός σου, “and that the shame of your nakedness may not be revealed” — φανερόω, from the appear family: made visible. καὶ κολλούριον ἐγχρῖσαι τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς σου ἵνα βλέπῃς, “and eye salve to anoint your eyes, that you may see” — βλέπω, from the see family. Laodicea's famous eye powder could not heal spiritual blindness.","words":[{"strongs":"G4823","hebrew":"συμβουλεύω","translit":"symbouleúō","english":"counsel"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"buy"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G4448","hebrew":"πυρόω","translit":"pyróō","english":"refined"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"become rich"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garments"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G152","hebrew":"αἰσχύνη","translit":"aischýnē","english":"shame"},{"strongs":"G1132","hebrew":"γυμνότης","translit":"gymnótēs","english":"nakedness"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G2854","hebrew":"κολλούριον","translit":"kolloúrion","english":"eye salve"},{"strongs":"G1472","hebrew":"ἐγχρίω","translit":"enchríō","english":"anoint"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"}]},{"v":19,"text":"As many as I love, I reprove and chasten. Be zealous therefore, and repent.","understanding":"ἐγὼ ὅσους ἐὰν φιλῶ ἐλέγχω καὶ παιδεύω, “As many as I love, I reprove and chasten” — φιλέω, from the love family: warm, personal affection, the love of a friend, as distinct from ἀγαπάω in verse 9, the love of committed choice. To the coldest church Jesus uses the warmest word. ἐλέγχω and παιδεύω, both from the discipline family: ἐλέγχω, to expose and convict; παιδεύω, to train a child. Rebuke is a sign of love (Hebrews 12:6). ζήλευε οὖν καὶ μετανόησον, “Be zealous therefore, and repent” — ζηλεύω, to be hot, boiling: the cure for lukewarmness.","words":[{"strongs":"G5368","hebrew":"φιλέω","translit":"philéō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1651","hebrew":"ἐλέγχω","translit":"elénchō","english":"reprove"},{"strongs":"G3811","hebrew":"παιδεύω","translit":"paideúō","english":"chasten"},{"strongs":"G2206","hebrew":"ζηλόω","translit":"zēlóō","english":"zealous"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, then I will come in to him, and will dine with him, and he with me.","understanding":"ἰδοὺ ἕστηκα ἐπὶ τὴν θύραν καὶ κρούω, “Behold, I stand at the door and knock” — θύρα; κρούω, to knock: perfect tense, ‘I have taken my stand’. Jesus is outside the door of His own church. ἐάν τις ἀκούσῃ τῆς φωνῆς μου καὶ ἀνοίξῃ τὴν θύραν, “If anyone hears my voice and opens the door” — τις, anyone: the call is personal. καὶ εἰσελεύσομαι πρὸς αὐτὸν καὶ δειπνήσω μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ καὶ αὐτὸς μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ, “then I will come in to him, and will dine with him, and he with me” — δειπνέω, to eat the main evening meal: close fellowship. Written to a church, it applies to every heart that has shut Christ out.","words":[{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stand"},{"strongs":"G2374","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","english":"door"},{"strongs":"G2925","hebrew":"κρούω","translit":"kroúō","english":"knock"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hears"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opens"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"come in"},{"strongs":"G1172","hebrew":"δειπνέω","translit":"deipnéō","english":"dine"}]},{"v":21,"text":"He who overcomes, I will give to him to sit down with me on my throne, as I also overcame, and sat down with my Father on his throne.","understanding":"Ὁ νικῶν δώσω αὐτῷ καθίσαι μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ ἐν τῷ θρόνῳ μου, “He who overcomes, I will give to him to sit down with me on my throne” — the lukewarm church is offered the highest promise of all. ὡς κἀγὼ ἐνίκησα καὶ ἐκάθισα μετὰ τοῦ πατρός μου ἐν τῷ θρόνῳ αὐτοῦ, “as I also overcame, and sat down with my Father on his throne” — νικάω twice: Christ's victory (by the cross and resurrection) is the pattern and the ground of ours. This verse leads straight into the throne room of chapter 4.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G2523","hebrew":"καθίζω","translit":"kathízō","english":"sit down"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"}]},{"v":22,"text":"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.”","understanding":"Ὁ ἔχων οὖς ἀκουσάτω τί τὸ πνεῦμα λέγει ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις, “He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies” — the seventh and last time. Seven letters, one Spirit, one Lord, one call: listen.","words":[{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Unwatched Wall","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture the great hill-fortress of Sardis, sheer cliffs on every side. The guards are asleep at their posts, certain no one can climb. In the night an enemy soldier finds a crack in the rock and climbs it, and by morning the city has fallen without a fight.","meaning":"“I know your works, that you have a reputation of being alive, but you are dead. Wake up” (vv. 1–2). “If therefore you won’t watch, I will come as a thief, and you won’t know what hour I will come upon you” (v. 3). A church can fall not by attack but by sleep."},{"title":"The Open Door","verseRef":"v. 7-8","image":"Picture a small group of believers standing before a huge door that others have slammed in their face. Then picture a hand holding a great key — the key of David — turning it, and the door swinging open. No one on earth can close it again.","meaning":"“He who opens and no one can shut, and who shuts and no one opens” (v. 7). “Behold, I have set before you an open door, which no one can shut” (v. 8). Little power, kept word, open door: what Christ opens for the faithful, no one can shut."},{"title":"The Lukewarm Cup","verseRef":"v. 15-17","image":"Picture a traveller arriving thirsty in Laodicea. He is handed a cup of water from the aqueduct — neither cold and refreshing nor hot and healing, but tepid and chalky. He takes one mouthful and spits it out.","meaning":"“I know your works, that you are neither cold nor hot. I wish you were cold or hot” (v. 15). “So, because you are lukewarm, and neither hot nor cold, I will vomit you out of my mouth” (v. 16). Comfortable, self-sufficient religion is useless to Christ."},{"title":"The Knock","verseRef":"v. 20","image":"Picture a warm, well-furnished house full of people eating and laughing. Outside in the cold, at the door, stands the One whose house it really is, knocking — patiently, repeatedly — waiting for someone to hear His voice.","meaning":"“Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, then I will come in to him, and will dine with him, and he with me” (v. 20). Jesus does not break the door down. He knocks — and promises fellowship to anyone who opens."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Sardis: Asleep (v. 1-6)","body":"Christ, who has the seven Spirits and the seven stars, tells Sardis that it has a name for being alive but is dead; it must wake up, strengthen what remains, remember, keep and repent, or He will come like a thief; a few have not soiled their garments and will walk with Him in white; the overcomer will not be blotted out of the book of life."},{"label":"Philadelphia: Faithful with Little Power (v. 7-13)","body":"The Holy and True One with the key of David has set before Philadelphia an open door; though they have little power they have kept His word; their opponents will bow and know He loves them; He will keep them from the hour of testing; they must hold fast; the overcomer will be a pillar in God's temple, bearing God's name, the new Jerusalem's name and Christ's new name."},{"label":"Laodicea: Lukewarm (v. 14-22)","body":"The Amen, the faithful and true witness, the ruler of God's creation, finds Laodicea lukewarm and will spit it out; it thinks it is rich and needs nothing, but it is wretched, poor, blind and naked; it must buy refined gold, white clothes and eye salve from Him; He disciplines those He loves; He stands at the door and knocks; the overcomer will sit with Him on His throne."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Sardis: A Name for Being Alive","paragraphs":["Sardis had a glorious past and a dead present. Jesus says, “I know your works, that you have a reputation of being alive, but you are dead” (v. 1). There is no mention of persecution or false teaching; Sardis was too inoffensive to be persecuted and too sleepy to be tempted by heresy. It had the forms of church life without the life. It is the most frightening of the letters because a church in that condition rarely knows it.","The city's history made the warning vivid. Twice its ‘impregnable’ citadel had fallen to surprise attack because no one was keeping watch. So Jesus says, “Wake up, and keep the things that remain” (v. 2), and “If therefore you won’t watch, I will come as a thief” (v. 3). He found no works “perfected before my God” (v. 2): πληρόω, filled up, completed. Sardis was a church of good beginnings and unfinished business.","Yet even here there is a remnant: “Nevertheless you have a few names in Sardis that did not defile their garments. They will walk with me in white” (v. 4). And the promise to the overcomer is that Christ will never blot their names “out of the book of life” (v. 5). Whatever the church's condition, Christ knows His faithful ones by name."]},{"heading":"Philadelphia: The Open Door","paragraphs":["Philadelphia, like Smyrna, receives no rebuke. Jesus speaks to it as “he who is holy, he who is true, he who has the key of David, he who opens and no one can shut, and who shuts and no one opens” (v. 7). The local synagogue may have shut the Christians out, but Christ has the key to the true house of God: “Behold, I have set before you an open door, which no one can shut” (v. 8). The ‘open door’ is entry into the kingdom, and probably also an opportunity for the gospel.","The church was not impressive: “you have a little power” (v. 8). But it had “kept my word, and didn’t deny my name” (v. 8). God measures faithfulness, not size. And Jesus promises vindication: their accusers will come and bow and “know that I have loved you” (v. 9).","Verse 10 contains a promise much discussed: “Because you kept my command to endure, I also will keep you from the hour of testing, which is to come on the whole world.” Some take ‘keep you from’ to mean removal before a final period of trial; others take it to mean protection through it, as Jesus prayed for His disciples: “I pray not that you would take them from the world, but that you would keep them from the evil one” (John 17:15) — the same verb and preposition. Either way, the promise is that the faithful will be kept by Christ."]},{"heading":"Laodicea: Lukewarm and Self-Satisfied","paragraphs":["Laodicea is the only church of the seven that receives no praise at all. Jesus says, “I know your works, that you are neither cold nor hot. I wish you were cold or hot” (v. 15). The picture is drawn from the city's water. Hierapolis, six miles away, had hot springs used for healing; Colossae, ten miles away, had cold, refreshing water. Laodicea's water came through a long aqueduct and arrived lukewarm and full of minerals. ‘Hot’ and ‘cold’ are both good; ‘lukewarm’ is useless. The church was not helping anyone.","Its problem was self-sufficiency: “Because you say, ‘I am rich, and have gotten riches, and have need of nothing;’ and don’t know that you are the wretched one, miserable, poor, blind, and naked” (v. 17). The city was famous for banking, black wool and an eye ointment. Jesus offers them true gold, white garments and real eye salve (v. 18) — everything they thought they already had.","And yet this is a love letter: “As many as I love, I reprove and chasten. Be zealous therefore, and repent” (v. 19). The verb for ‘love’ is φιλέω, the warm affection of a friend. Then comes the best-known verse of the seven letters: “Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, then I will come in to him, and will dine with him, and he with me” (v. 20). Jesus is outside His own church, asking to be let in. The offer is to anyone — and the reward for the lukewarm who repent is to sit with Christ on His throne (v. 21)."]},{"heading":"What the Seven Letters Teach Every Church","paragraphs":["Only two of the seven churches, Smyrna and Philadelphia, receive no rebuke — and both are poor, small and persecuted. The two most severely rebuked, Sardis and Laodicea, are comfortable and unthreatened. That pattern is a warning to every church at ease in its culture.","The letters show the main dangers a church faces: loss of love (Ephesus), fear of suffering (Smyrna), compromise with idolatry and immorality (Pergamum and Thyatira), spiritual deadness (Sardis), and self-satisfied lukewarmness (Laodicea). They also show what Christ values: love, faithfulness under pressure, sound teaching, holiness, perseverance and wholehearted zeal.","Above all, the letters show Christ Himself: He knows each church perfectly, speaks to each one personally, warns before He judges, offers repentance to the worst, and makes extravagant promises to everyone who overcomes. The last promise is the greatest: “He who overcomes, I will give to him to sit down with me on my throne, as I also overcame, and sat down with my Father on his throne” (v. 21). That throne is where the vision now goes."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Thief, the Door and the Discipline of Love","paragraphs":["Jesus warned, “But know this, that if the master of the house had known in what watch of the night the thief was coming, he would have watched, and would not have allowed his house to be broken into” (Matthew 24:43). Paul wrote, “For you yourselves know well that the day of the Lord comes like a thief in the night” (1 Thessalonians 5:2), and added, “so then let’s not sleep, as the rest do, but let’s watch and be sober” (1 Thessalonians 5:6).","Jesus promised, “Everyone therefore who confesses me before men, him I will also confess before my Father who is in heaven” (Matthew 10:32) — the promise of verse 5. And Paul wrote to nearby Colossae, a letter he wanted read in Laodicea too (Colossians 4:16), that Christ is the one “who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead; that in all things he might have the preeminence” (Colossians 1:18) — the ‘Beginning of God’s creation’ of verse 14.","Hebrews explains the loving rebuke of verse 19: “For whom the Lord loves, he chastens, and scourges every son whom he receives” (Hebrews 12:6). And the promise of fellowship in verse 20 echoes Jesus' words, “If a man loves me, he will keep my word. My Father will love him, and we will come to him, and make our home with him” (John 14:23)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Lukewarm","hebrew":"χλιαρός","translit":"chliarós","strongs":"G5513 (cold ψυχρός, psychrós, G5593; hot ζεστός, zestós, G2200)","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “tepid.” χλιαρός appears only here in the Bible. It is not a word about temperature of feeling — as if ‘cold’ meant hostile and ‘hot’ meant enthusiastic — but about usefulness. Hot water heals, cold water refreshes; lukewarm water does neither and makes you sick. Laodicea's water supply, piped in from a distance, arrived exactly like that. A lukewarm church is one that gives the world nothing it needs."},{"english":"Love (affection)","hebrew":"φιλέω","translit":"philéō","strongs":"G5368 (loved ἀγαπάω, agapáō, G25)","verses":"v. 19","insight":"Strong's: “to be a friend to (fond of (an individual or an object)), i.e. have affection for (denoting personal attachment, as a matter of sentiment or feeling; while G25 (ἀγαπάω) is wider, embracing especially the judgment and the deliberate assent of the will as a matter of principle, duty and propriety.” This chapter uses both words from the love family. To Philadelphia Jesus says, ‘I have loved (ἀγαπάω) you’ (v. 9) — the love of deliberate choice. To Laodicea, the coldest church, He says, ‘As many as I love (φιλέω)’ (v. 19) — the warm affection of a friend. His rebuke comes from the heart."},{"english":"Door","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","strongs":"G2374 (knock κρούω, kroúō, G2925)","verses":"v. 8, 20; 4:1","insight":"Strong's: “a portal or entrance (the opening or the closure, literally or figuratively).” Three doors stand in chapters 3–4. To Philadelphia, Christ sets an open door that no one can shut (3:8) — He opens. To Laodicea, He stands at a shut door and knocks (3:20) — He asks. To John, a door stands open in heaven (4:1) — He reveals. The One who has the key of David holds every door; but the door of the heart He opens only from the inside, when we hear His voice."},{"english":"Beginning","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","strongs":"G746 (creation κτίσις, ktísis, G2937)","verses":"v. 14; 21:6; 22:13","insight":"Strong's: “(properly abstract) a commencement, or (concretely) chief (in various applications of order, time, place, or rank).” ἀρχή, from the power family, means both ‘beginning’ and ‘ruler’ — the source and the authority. Calling Jesus ‘the Beginning of God’s creation’ does not make Him the first thing God made; it makes Him the origin and ruler of all that was made. Later God calls Himself ‘the Beginning and the End’ (21:6), and Jesus says the same of Himself (22:13)."},{"english":"Wake up / watch","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","strongs":"G1127 (thief κλέπτης, kléptēs, G2812)","verses":"v. 2, 3; 16:15","insight":"Strong's: “to keep awake, i.e. watch (literally or figuratively).” γρηγορεύω, from the sleep family, is the opposite of sleep: alert, watchful, ready. It was Jesus' word in Gethsemane: ‘Watch and pray’. Sardis was a city that fell twice because its watchmen slept; its church was falling the same way. Revelation 16:15 repeats the warning: ‘Behold, I come like a thief. Blessed is he who watches.’"}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In these last three letters the Holy Spirit searches for life, faithfulness and fire.","items":[{"title":"Reputation Is Not Life","body":"Sardis had a name for being alive but was dead (v. 1). The Spirit asks whether your faith is real life or only reputation — what others think of you. Wake up. Finish what you started. Strengthen what remains."},{"title":"Little Power Is Enough","body":"Philadelphia had “a little power” but kept Christ's word (v. 8), and He set before them an open door. The Spirit wants you to stop measuring yourself by size or strength and to be faithful with what you have. He holds the key; He opens doors no one can shut."},{"title":"Open the Door","body":"Jesus stands at the door and knocks (v. 20). He will not force His way in. The Spirit asks: is there a room of your life — your time, your money, your relationships, your thoughts — where Christ is still outside? Hear His voice, and open the door."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Sleep Check","body":"Are there areas of your spiritual life that look alive on the outside but are dead inside (v. 1)? What have you started and never finished (v. 2)?"},{"title":"The Door Check","body":"What open door has Christ set before you — an opportunity to serve, to witness, to grow (v. 8)? Are you walking through it?"},{"title":"The Temperature Check","body":"Where have you become comfortable, self-sufficient and lukewarm (vv. 15–17)? What do you think you have that you really need from Christ?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Finish One Thing","body":"Identify one spiritual commitment you began and left unfinished — a Bible reading plan, a reconciliation, a ministry, a promise to God. This week, take the next concrete step toward completing it (v. 2)."},{"title":"Dine with Jesus","body":"Take one evening meal this week alone with Christ. Read verse 20 aloud before you eat. As you eat, talk with Him as with a friend, and listen. Ask Him what He wants to say to your ‘church’ — your life."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You have the seven Spirits of God and the seven stars. Wake me up where I am sleeping, and strengthen what remains in me. Let me not have a name for being alive while I am dead. Keep my garments clean, and let me walk with You in white. Thank You that You will never blot my name out of the book of life. You are holy and true, and You hold the key of David. Thank You for the open doors You set before me. Though I have little power, help me keep Your word and not deny Your name. Keep me in the hour of testing, and let no one take my crown. You are the Amen, the faithful and true Witness, the ruler of God's creation. Where I am lukewarm, set me on fire. Where I think I am rich, show me my need. I buy from You gold refined in fire, white garments and eye salve, that I may see. Thank You that You rebuke me because You love me. I hear Your knock. I open the door. Come in and eat with me, Lord. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Eliakim and the Key of David","ref":"Isaiah 22:15-25","relatedVerses":"v. 7-8","summary":"In the days of King Hezekiah, the steward Shebna abused his office, so God removed him and gave his place to Eliakim. God said He would clothe Eliakim with Shebna's robe and lay ‘the key of David’s house’ on his shoulder: what he opened no one would shut, and what he shut no one would open. He would be ‘a father’ to Jerusalem and like a peg fixed in a sure place. Jesus takes this promise to Himself: He is the true and final steward of God's house, with all authority to open and shut."},{"title":"The Watchman Who Slept","ref":"Matthew 24:42-51","relatedVerses":"v. 2-3","summary":"Jesus told His disciples to watch, “for you don’t know in what hour your Lord comes” (Matthew 24:42). If the master of the house had known when the thief was coming, he would have stayed awake. He then told of a servant left in charge who said, ‘My lord is delaying his coming,’ and began to beat his fellow servants and to eat and drink with the drunken — until his master arrived on a day he did not expect. Sardis was that servant: asleep in its comfort, unaware that the Lord could come at any hour."},{"title":"Zacchaeus Opens His Door","ref":"Luke 19:1-10","relatedVerses":"v. 20","summary":"In Jericho a rich chief tax collector named Zacchaeus climbed a tree to see Jesus pass by. Jesus stopped, looked up, and said, “Zacchaeus, hurry and come down, for today I must stay at your house” (Luke 19:5). Zacchaeus hurried down and received Him joyfully. Over that meal his life was turned upside down: he gave half his goods to the poor and repaid those he had cheated fourfold. Jesus said, “Today, salvation has come to this house” (Luke 19:9). This is what happens when the One at the door is let in."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"The Throne in Heaven","themeTitle":"“Holy, Holy, Holy Is the Lord God, the Almighty”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"After these things I looked and saw a door opened in heaven, and the first voice that I heard, like a trumpet speaking with me, was one saying, “Come up here, and I will show you the things which must happen after this.”","understanding":"Μετὰ ταῦτα εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ θύρα ἠνεῳγμένη ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “After these things I looked and saw a door opened in heaven” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). After the door of 3:20 at which Jesus knocks, here is a door into heaven itself. καὶ ἡ φωνὴ ἡ πρώτη ἣν ἤκουσα ὡς σάλπιγγος, “and the first voice that I heard, like a trumpet” — the voice of 1:10. Ἀνάβα ὧδε, “Come up here” — ἀναβαίνω, to go up. Some see in this a picture of the church being taken up before the judgements that follow; most read it simply as John being called into the vision. καὶ δείξω σοι ἃ δεῖ γενέσθαι μετὰ ταῦτα, “and I will show you the things which must happen after this” — δεῖ, it must: history is in God's hands.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2374","hebrew":"θύρα","translit":"thýra","english":"door"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpet"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"Come up"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"must"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Immediately I was in the Spirit. Behold, there was a throne set in heaven, and one sitting on the throne","understanding":"εὐθέως ἐγενόμην ἐν πνεύματι, “Immediately I was in the Spirit” — as in 1:10, now at a deeper level. καὶ ἰδοὺ θρόνος ἔκειτο ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “Behold, there was a throne set in heaven” — θρόνος: the word appears seventeen times in chapters 4–5 alone. This is the centre of everything that follows. καὶ ἐπὶ τὸν θρόνον καθήμενος, “and one sitting on the throne” — κάθημαι, to sit: God is not named; John describes only light and colour, as Ezekiel did. The throne is occupied — whatever happens on earth.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sitting"}]},{"v":3,"text":"that looked like a jasper stone and a sardius. There was a rainbow around the throne, like an emerald to look at.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ καθήμενος ὅμοιος ὁράσει λίθῳ ἰάσπιδι καὶ σαρδίῳ, “that looked like a jasper stone and a sardius” — ὅρασις, appearance. Jasper (perhaps a clear, brilliant stone like diamond; see 21:11) and sardius (deep red) were the first and last stones on the high priest's breastplate. καὶ ἶρις κυκλόθεν τοῦ θρόνου ὅμοιος ὁράσει σμαραγδίνῳ, “There was a rainbow around the throne, like an emerald to look at” — ἶρις, a rainbow: the sign of God's covenant mercy after the flood. Judgement is coming, but the throne is circled with mercy.","words":[{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sitting"},{"strongs":"G3706","hebrew":"ὅρασις","translit":"hórasis","english":"looked"},{"strongs":"G2393","hebrew":"ἴασπις","translit":"íaspis","english":"jasper"},{"strongs":"G4555","hebrew":"σάρδινος","translit":"sárdinos","english":"sardius"},{"strongs":"G2463","hebrew":"ἶρις","translit":"îris","english":"rainbow"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G4664","hebrew":"σμαράγδινος","translit":"smarágdinos","english":"emerald"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Around the throne were twenty-four thrones. On the thrones were twenty-four elders sitting, dressed in white garments, with crowns of gold on their heads.","understanding":"καὶ κυκλόθεν τοῦ θρόνου θρόνοι εἴκοσι τέσσαρες, “Around the throne were twenty-four thrones” — lesser thrones around the great throne. καὶ ἐπὶ τοὺς θρόνους εἴκοσι τέσσαρας πρεσβυτέρους καθημένους, “On the thrones were twenty-four elders sitting” — πρεσβύτερος, elder. Twenty-four may combine the twelve tribes and the twelve apostles (both named on the new Jerusalem, 21:12–14): the whole people of God; or twenty-four orders of priests (1 Chronicles 24). περιβεβλημένους ἐν ἱματίοις λευκοῖς, καὶ ἐπὶ τὰς κεφαλὰς αὐτῶν στεφάνους χρυσοῦς, “dressed in white garments, with crowns of gold on their heads” — στέφανος, from the crown family: victors' wreaths, as promised to the overcomers (2:10; 3:11).","words":[{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"thrones"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sitting"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"dressed"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garments"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crowns"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Out of the throne proceed lightnings, sounds, and thunders. There were seven lamps of fire burning before his throne, which are the seven Spirits of God.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐκ τοῦ θρόνου ἐκπορεύονται ἀστραπαὶ καὶ φωναὶ καὶ βρονταί, “Out of the throne proceed lightnings, sounds, and thunders” — as at Sinai when God came down. This storm returns, growing, at the end of each series of judgements (8:5; 11:19; 16:18). καὶ ἑπτὰ λαμπάδες πυρὸς καιόμεναι ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου, ἅ εἰσιν τὰ ἑπτὰ πνεύματα τοῦ θεοῦ, “There were seven lamps of fire burning before his throne, which are the seven Spirits of God” — λαμπάς, a torch: the Holy Spirit in His fullness (1:4; Zechariah 4).","words":[{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G796","hebrew":"ἀστραπή","translit":"astrapḗ","english":"lightnings"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sounds"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunders"},{"strongs":"G2985","hebrew":"λαμπάς","translit":"lampás","english":"lamps"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirits"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Before the throne was something like a sea of glass, similar to crystal. In the middle of the throne, and around the throne were four living creatures full of eyes before and behind.","understanding":"καὶ ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου ὡς θάλασσα ὑαλίνη ὁμοία κρυστάλλῳ, “Before the throne was something like a sea of glass, similar to crystal” — θάλασσα: in Scripture the sea is often a picture of chaos and evil (13:1); before God's throne it is calm, transparent and still. In the new creation ‘the sea is no more’ (21:1). καὶ ἐν μέσῳ τοῦ θρόνου καὶ κύκλῳ τοῦ θρόνου τέσσαρα ζῷα, “In the middle of the throne, and around the throne were four living creatures” — ζῷον, a living being, not θηρίον, a wild beast (13:1). γέμοντα ὀφθαλμῶν ἔμπροσθεν καὶ ὄπισθεν, “full of eyes before and behind” — total awareness: nothing escapes God's notice.","words":[{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G5193","hebrew":"ὑάλινος","translit":"hyálinos","english":"glass"},{"strongs":"G2930","hebrew":"κρύσταλλος","translit":"krýstallos","english":"crystal"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"}]},{"v":7,"text":"The first creature was like a lion, and the second creature like a calf, and the third creature had a face like a man, and the fourth was like a flying eagle.","understanding":"καὶ τὸ ζῷον τὸ πρῶτον ὅμοιον λέοντι, “The first creature was like a lion” — ζῷον. καὶ τὸ δεύτερον ζῷον ὅμοιον μόσχῳ, “and the second creature like a calf” — μόσχος, a young ox. καὶ τὸ τρίτον ζῷον ἔχων τὸ πρόσωπον ὡς ἀνθρώπου, “and the third creature had a face like a man” — καὶ τὸ τέταρτον ζῷον ὅμοιον ἀετῷ πετομένῳ, “and the fourth was like a flying eagle.” Ezekiel's cherubim each had all four faces. Together they may represent the noblest of all creation — wild animals, tame animals, humanity, birds — worshipping God. Later Christians linked them with the four Gospels.","words":[{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"creature"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lion"},{"strongs":"G3448","hebrew":"μόσχος","translit":"móschos","english":"calf"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G105","hebrew":"ἀετός","translit":"aetós","english":"eagle"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The four living creatures, each one of them having six wings, are full of eyes around and within. They have no rest day and night, saying, “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty, who was and who is and who is to come!”","understanding":"καὶ τὰ τέσσαρα ζῷα, ἓν καθ᾽ ἓν αὐτῶν ἔχων ἀνὰ πτέρυγας ἕξ, “The four living creatures, each one of them having six wings” — like Isaiah's seraphim. καὶ ἀνάπαυσιν οὐκ ἔχουσιν ἡμέρας καὶ νυκτός, “They have no rest day and night” — ἀνάπαυσις, from the rest family: a pause, a break; ἡμέρα, from the time family. Their worship never pauses. Later the worshippers of the beast ‘have no rest day and night’ either (14:11) — but from torment. Ἅγιος ἅγιος ἅγιος κύριος ὁ θεὸς ὁ παντοκράτωρ, “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty” — Isaiah's ‘Holy, holy, holy’, with παντοκράτωρ, the All-Ruler. ὁ ἦν καὶ ὁ ὢν καὶ ὁ ἐρχόμενος, “who was and who is and who is to come!” ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4420","hebrew":"πτέρυξ","translit":"ptéryx","english":"wings"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G372","hebrew":"ἀνάπαυσις","translit":"anápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"to come"}]},{"v":9,"text":"When the living creatures give glory, honor, and thanks to him who sits on the throne, to him who lives forever and ever,","understanding":"Καὶ ὅταν δώσουσιν τὰ ζῷα δόξαν καὶ τιμὴν καὶ εὐχαριστίαν, “When the living creatures give glory, honor, and thanks” — δόξα, glory; τιμή, honour; εὐχαριστία, from the pray family: thanksgiving. τῷ καθημένῳ ἐπὶ τῷ θρόνῳ, τῷ ζῶντι εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “to him who sits on the throne, to him who lives forever and ever” — ζάω, from the life family; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. Caesar would die; this King lives forever.","words":[{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"thanks"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":10,"text":"the twenty-four elders fall down before him who sits on the throne, and worship him who lives forever and ever, and throw their crowns before the throne, saying,","understanding":"πεσοῦνται οἱ εἴκοσι τέσσαρες πρεσβύτεροι ἐνώπιον τοῦ καθημένου ἐπὶ τοῦ θρόνου, “the twenty-four elders fall down before him who sits on the throne” — πίπτω. καὶ προσκυνήσουσιν τῷ ζῶντι εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “and worship him who lives forever and ever” — προσκυνέω, to bow down in homage; ζάω, from the life family; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. καὶ βαλοῦσιν τοὺς στεφάνους αὐτῶν ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου, “and throw their crowns before the throne” — στέφανος, from the crown family: conquered kings laid their crowns before the emperor; these victors lay theirs before God. Every reward is given back to the Giver.","words":[{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fall down"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"throw"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crowns"}]},{"v":11,"text":"“Worthy are you, our Lord and God, the Holy One, to receive the glory, the honor, and the power, for you created all things, and because of your desire they existed, and were created!”","understanding":"Ἄξιος εἶ, ὁ κύριος καὶ ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶν, “Worthy are you, our Lord and God” — ἄξιος, deserving: the hymn opens with a title Domitian demanded for himself, ‘our Lord and God’. Only One is worthy of it. λαβεῖν τὴν δόξαν καὶ τὴν τιμὴν καὶ τὴν δύναμιν, “to receive the glory, the honor, and the power” — δύναμις, from the power family. ὅτι σὺ ἔκτισας τὰ πάντα, “for you created all things” — κτίζω: worship of God as Creator comes first (ch. 4), then worship of the Lamb as Redeemer (ch. 5). καὶ διὰ τὸ θέλημά σου ἦσαν καὶ ἐκτίσθησαν, “and because of your desire they existed, and were created!” — θέλημα, will: all things exist because God willed them.","words":[{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"Worthy"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2936","hebrew":"κτίζω","translit":"ktízō","english":"created"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"desire"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Open Door in Heaven","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture John on his barren island looking up and seeing, in the sky, a door standing open. Through it comes the trumpet voice: ‘Come up here.’ And in a moment he is standing in the throne room of the universe.","meaning":"“After these things I looked and saw a door opened in heaven” (v. 1). “Behold, there was a throne set in heaven, and one sitting on the throne” (v. 2). Before John sees anything of the troubles coming on earth, he sees the throne. Every disaster in the book must be read from here."},{"title":"The Rainbow Around the Throne","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture a throne blazing like a jewel, red and brilliant white, with lightning and thunder flashing out of it like the storm at Sinai. And circling it all, a rainbow glowing green like emerald.","meaning":"“There was a rainbow around the throne, like an emerald to look at” (v. 3). “Out of the throne proceed lightnings, sounds, and thunders” (v. 5). God's holiness is terrifying; but His throne is ringed with the sign of His covenant promise. Judgement and mercy surround Him together."},{"title":"The Sea of Glass","verseRef":"v. 6","image":"Picture the stormy Aegean that surrounded John on Patmos, grey and heaving. Then see, before God's throne, a sea as flat and clear as crystal, perfectly still, reflecting the light of the throne.","meaning":"“Before the throne was something like a sea of glass, similar to crystal” (v. 6). The chaos that rages on earth is calm before God. Nothing that frightens you is out of control in His presence."},{"title":"Crowns Thrown Down","verseRef":"v. 9-11","image":"Picture twenty-four elders on golden thrones, wearing golden victory wreaths. As the creatures cry ‘Holy, holy, holy’, they rise, fall on their faces, take off their crowns and throw them at the foot of the throne.","meaning":"“The twenty-four elders fall down before him who sits on the throne, and worship him who lives forever and ever, and throw their crowns before the throne” (v. 10). “Worthy are you, our Lord and God, the Holy One, to receive the glory, the honor, and the power” (v. 11). Whatever honour we receive is only something to lay at His feet."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Open Door and the Throne (v. 1-3)","body":"John sees a door open in heaven, is called up, and in the Spirit sees a throne with One seated on it, shining like jasper and sardius, surrounded by an emerald rainbow."},{"label":"Those Around the Throne (v. 4-8a)","body":"Around the throne are twenty-four elders on thrones, in white with golden crowns; from the throne come lightning and thunder; seven torches burn before it, the seven Spirits; before it is a sea of glass; around it are four living creatures full of eyes, like a lion, an ox, a man and an eagle, each with six wings."},{"label":"The Unceasing Worship (v. 8b-11)","body":"The creatures never stop crying ‘Holy, holy, holy’ to the Lord God Almighty; whenever they give glory, the elders fall down, worship and cast their crowns before the throne, declaring God worthy because He created all things by His will."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Centre of Everything","paragraphs":["Chapters 4–5 are the hinge of Revelation. Before any seal is opened or any trumpet blown, John is shown the throne room of heaven. “Behold, there was a throne set in heaven, and one sitting on the throne” (v. 2). The word ‘throne’ appears seventeen times in these two chapters. Everything that happens afterward — the seals, trumpets and bowls — comes out from this throne (6:1; 8:2; 16:1). The point is unmistakable: history is not random, and evil is not sovereign. God reigns.","For the first readers this was a direct challenge. The emperor sat on a throne in Rome, surrounded by courtiers who praised him. Domitian liked to be called ‘our Lord and God’. Revelation draws back the curtain and shows the true throne room, where the elders cry, “Worthy are you, our Lord and God” (v. 11) — to the only One who deserves it.","John describes the One on the throne only by colours and light: jasper, sardius, emerald (v. 3). Like Ezekiel, he does not try to describe God's form. And around the throne is a rainbow, the sign of God's covenant with Noah that He would never again destroy the earth by flood. The judgements that follow are real, but they come from a God who keeps His promises."]},{"heading":"Elders, Torches, Sea and Living Creatures","paragraphs":["The twenty-four elders sit on thrones, dressed in white and wearing golden crowns (v. 4). They seem to represent the whole people of God: perhaps the twelve tribes and the twelve apostles, whose names are both written on the new Jerusalem (21:12–14). They wear the white garments and victor's crowns promised to the overcomers (3:5, 11). Some think they are angels; but either way, they show the redeemed sharing in God's rule and leading creation in worship.","Before the throne burn “seven lamps of fire... which are the seven Spirits of God” (v. 5) — the Holy Spirit in His fullness. And before the throne is “something like a sea of glass, similar to crystal” (v. 6). In the Bible the sea is often the place of chaos and the home of monsters; the beast will rise from it (13:1). Before God it is perfectly calm.","The four living creatures (vv. 6–8) combine Ezekiel's cherubim and Isaiah's seraphim. Full of eyes, they see everything; like a lion, an ox, a man and an eagle, they represent the whole created order — the strongest, the most patient, the wisest and the swiftest. Their constant song is Isaiah's: “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty, who was and who is and who is to come!” (v. 8)."]},{"heading":"Worship of the Creator","paragraphs":["The worship of chapter 4 is centred on God as Creator: “Worthy are you, our Lord and God, the Holy One, to receive the glory, the honor, and the power, for you created all things, and because of your desire they existed, and were created!” (v. 11). Everything that exists exists because God willed it. That is why everything owes Him worship, and why the beast's demand for worship (13:4) is so wicked.","Notice the rhythm: the living creatures give glory, and then the elders fall down and throw their crowns before the throne (vv. 9–10). Their crowns are real — they have won them — but they will not keep them. Conquered kings in the ancient world laid their crowns before the emperor as a sign of submission. In heaven the victors themselves acknowledge that every victory came from God.","Chapter 4 prepares for chapter 5. God is worshipped as Creator; next, the Lamb will be worshipped as Redeemer. And at the end of chapter 5 the two songs join: “To him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb be the blessing, the honor, the glory, and the dominion, forever and ever! Amen!” (5:13)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Throne of Grace","paragraphs":["Jesus promised His disciples a place in His Father's house: “In my Father’s house are many homes. If it weren’t so, I would have told you. I am going to prepare a place for you” (John 14:2). Revelation 4 lets John see into that house.","Stephen, as he was stoned, “looked up steadfastly into heaven, and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing on the right hand of God” (Acts 7:55). Like John on Patmos, the suffering witness is shown the throne. And Hebrews invites every believer there now: “Let us therefore draw near with boldness to the throne of grace, that we may receive mercy, and may find grace for help in time of need” (Hebrews 4:16).","The worship of God as Creator in verse 11 is the worship of the Son too, for “All things were made through him. Without him was not anything made that has been made” (John 1:3), and “For by him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, things visible and things invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers” (Colossians 1:16)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Throne","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","strongs":"G2362 (sitting κάθημαι, káthēmai, G2521)","verses":"v. 2-6, 9-10; 5:1, 6-7, 11, 13","insight":"Strong's: “a stately seat (\"throne\"); by implication, power or (concretely) a potentate.” θρόνος appears more than forty times in Revelation — more than in the rest of the New Testament put together. Satan has a throne (2:13), the beast has a throne (13:2; 16:10), and the overcomer is promised a seat on Christ's throne (3:21). But there is only one throne at the centre, and in the end it is ‘the throne of God and of the Lamb’ (22:1, 3). The question of the whole book is: whose throne do you worship before?"},{"english":"Living creature","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","strongs":"G2226 (wings πτέρυξ, ptéryx, G4420)","verses":"v. 6-9; 5:6, 8, 14; 6:1","insight":"Strong's: “a live thing, i.e. an animal.” ζῷον, from ζάω (to live), is a different word from θηρίον, the wild beast of chapter 13 (the King James Version called both ‘beasts’). The living creatures are the highest order of heavenly beings, like Ezekiel's cherubim and Isaiah's seraphim. They lead heaven's worship, and they summon the four horsemen (6:1–7). Life itself, in its fullest created form, exists to worship."},{"english":"Elder","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","strongs":"G4245 (crowns στέφανος, stéphanos, G4735)","verses":"v. 4, 10; 5:5-14; 7:11-14","insight":"Strong's: “older; as noun, a senior; specially, an Israelite Sanhedrist (also figuratively, member of the celestial council) or Christian \"presbyter\".” πρεσβύτερος is the word for church elders (Titus 1:5) and for Israel's leaders. The twenty-four elders are a heavenly council, sitting on thrones, wearing white and golden wreaths, holding harps and bowls of incense (5:8). One of them comforts John (5:5) and another explains the vision of the white-robed multitude (7:13–14). They are the people of God, Old Testament and New, at rest and in worship."},{"english":"Rest","hebrew":"ἀνάπαυσις","translit":"anápausis","strongs":"G372 (night νύξ, nýx, G3571)","verses":"v. 8; 14:11","insight":"Strong's: “intermission; by implication, recreation.” ἀνάπαυσις, from the rest family, is a pause, a break from labour. The living creatures ‘have no rest day and night’ (v. 8) — their worship never stops, not as a burden but as a joy. The same phrase returns in 14:11 for the worshippers of the beast, who ‘have no rest day and night’ — from torment. Everyone worships without ceasing; the only question is whom. Those who die in the Lord, by contrast, ‘rest from their labors’ (14:13)."},{"english":"Worthy","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","strongs":"G514 (glory δόξα, dóxa, G1391)","verses":"v. 11; 5:2, 4, 9, 12","insight":"Strong's: “deserving, comparable or suitable (as if drawing praise).” ἄξιος comes from a root meaning ‘to weigh’: what balances the scale. In Roman cities crowds acclaimed the emperor with shouts of ‘Worthy!’. In heaven that word is given to God as Creator (4:11) and to the Lamb as Redeemer (5:9, 12). In 5:2–4 no one in all creation is found worthy to open the scroll — until the slain Lamb steps forward. Worth in heaven is measured by the cross."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit lifts your eyes from earth to the throne.","items":[{"title":"The Throne Is Occupied","body":"Before John sees anything of the coming troubles, he sees “a throne set in heaven, and one sitting on the throne” (v. 2). The Spirit wants you to begin every day, every crisis and every fear here. Whatever is happening, God is on His throne. Nothing has slipped out of His hands."},{"title":"Worship Is the Answer","body":"Heaven's response to God is not anxiety or argument but worship: “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty” (v. 8). The Spirit invites you to join the song of heaven now. Worship does not ignore the troubles of earth; it puts them in their proper place."},{"title":"Lay Down Your Crowns","body":"The elders throw their crowns before the throne (v. 10). The Spirit asks: what are your crowns — your achievements, gifts, reputation, successes? Lay them down before Him. Everything good you have is a gift; give Him the glory."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Throne Check","body":"When you face trouble, where do your eyes go first — to the problem, or to the throne (v. 2)?"},{"title":"The Worship Check","body":"Is your worship mainly about what God has given you, or about who God is (vv. 8, 11)?"},{"title":"The Crown Check","body":"What achievement or gift do you find hardest to lay at God's feet (v. 10)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Begin at the Throne","body":"For the next seven days, before you look at your phone or the news, read Revelation 4:8–11 aloud. Pray: ‘You are on the throne.’ Then bring the day's concerns to Him."},{"title":"Cast a Crown","body":"Write down your greatest achievement or strength. Then write beside it: ‘Worthy are you, our Lord and God, to receive the glory.’ Find a way this week to use that strength to serve someone else, quietly, for His glory alone."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty, who was and who is and who is to come! You sit on the throne of heaven, shining in light, surrounded by the rainbow of Your promise. Thunder and lightning come from Your throne, and Your Spirit burns before it like seven torches. Before You the sea is still as glass. With the living creatures I give You glory, honour and thanks, You who live forever and ever. With the elders I fall down before You, and I lay at Your feet every crown, every success, every gift You have given me. Worthy are You, our Lord and God, to receive the glory and the honour and the power, for You created all things, and by Your will they exist. When the world shakes, lift my eyes to Your throne. Open a door in heaven for me, and call me to come up and see. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Isaiah's Vision in the Temple","ref":"Isaiah 6:1-8","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 8","summary":"In the year King Uzziah died, Isaiah saw the Lord sitting on a throne, high and lifted up, His robe filling the temple. Seraphim with six wings called to one another, ‘Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of Armies! The whole earth is full of his glory!’ The temple shook and filled with smoke. Isaiah cried, ‘Woe is me!’ — until a seraph touched his lips with a burning coal and his sin was atoned for. Then he heard God ask, ‘Whom shall I send?’ and answered, ‘Here I am. Send me!’ John hears the same song and sees the same throne."},{"title":"Ezekiel by the River Chebar","ref":"Ezekiel 1:1-28","relatedVerses":"v. 3-7","summary":"Among the exiles in Babylon, the priest Ezekiel saw a storm wind from the north, a great cloud with flashing fire, and in it four living creatures, each with four faces — a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle — and wheels full of eyes. Above them was a dome like crystal, and on it a throne like sapphire, and on the throne a figure like a man, surrounded by brightness like a rainbow. Ezekiel called it ‘the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD’ and fell on his face. John's vision draws on Ezekiel's: in exile, God's people see that His throne is not confined to Jerusalem — it rules everywhere."},{"title":"The Covenant Rainbow","ref":"Genesis 9:8-17","relatedVerses":"v. 3","summary":"After the flood, God made a covenant with Noah and every living creature: never again would a flood destroy the earth. As a sign He set His bow in the clouds, saying that when He saw it He would remember His covenant. The rainbow around the throne in Revelation 4 reminds John that the God who is about to judge the earth is the same God who bound Himself by promise to preserve it. Judgement and mercy come from the same throne."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"The Scroll and the Lamb","themeTitle":"“Worthy Is the Lamb Who Has Been Killed”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw, in the right hand of him who sat on the throne, a book written inside and outside, sealed shut with seven seals.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἐπὶ τὴν δεξιὰν τοῦ καθημένου ἐπὶ τοῦ θρόνου βιβλίον, “I saw, in the right hand of him who sat on the throne, a book” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); βιβλίον, from the book family: a scroll, as distinct from βίβλος (3:5). It holds God's plan for history and its completion. γεγραμμένον ἔσωθεν καὶ ὄπισθεν, “written inside and outside” — full, with nothing to add, like Ezekiel's scroll of lament. κατεσφραγισμένον σφραγῖσιν ἑπτά, “sealed shut with seven seals” — κατασφραγίζω, to seal up completely; σφραγίς, a seal: a Roman will was sealed with seven seals by seven witnesses and could only be opened by the rightful heir.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G2696","hebrew":"κατασφραγίζω","translit":"katasphragízō","english":"sealed shut"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seals"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I saw a mighty angel proclaiming with a loud voice, “Who is worthy to open the book, and to break its seals?”","understanding":"καὶ εἶδον ἄγγελον ἰσχυρὸν κηρύσσοντα ἐν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ, “I saw a mighty angel proclaiming with a loud voice” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἰσχυρός, from the power family: strong in physical force, as distinct from δυνατός, able. Even a mighty angel cannot open the scroll. Τίς ἄξιος ἀνοῖξαι τὸ βιβλίον καὶ λῦσαι τὰς σφραγῖδας αὐτοῦ; “Who is worthy to open the book, and to break its seals?” — ἄξιος, worthy; βιβλίον, from the book family. The question is not who is strong enough, but who is worthy.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"proclaiming"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"break"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seals"}]},{"v":3,"text":"No one in heaven above, or on the earth, or under the earth, was able to open the book, or to look in it.","understanding":"καὶ οὐδεὶς ἐδύνατο ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ οὐδὲ ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς οὐδὲ ὑποκάτω τῆς γῆς, “No one in heaven above, or on the earth, or under the earth, was able” — δύναμαι, from the power family: to have the ability; γῆ, from the world family. The three-storey universe: angels, the living, the dead — none can do it. ἀνοῖξαι τὸ βιβλίον οὔτε βλέπειν αὐτό, “to open the book, or to look in it” — βιβλίον, from the book family; βλέπω, from the see family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"look"}]},{"v":4,"text":"And I wept much, because no one was found worthy to open the book, or to look in it.","understanding":"καὶ ἔκλαιον πολύ, “And I wept much” — κλαίω, to weep aloud, imperfect tense: he kept on weeping. If the scroll stays sealed, God's purposes for history — justice for the martyrs, the end of evil, the renewal of creation — will never be carried out. ὅτι οὐδεὶς ἄξιος εὑρέθη ἀνοῖξαι τὸ βιβλίον οὔτε βλέπειν αὐτό, “because no one was found worthy to open the book, or to look in it” — ἄξιος; βιβλίον, from the book family; βλέπω, from the see family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"wept"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"look"}]},{"v":5,"text":"One of the elders said to me, “Don’t weep. Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has overcome; he who opens the book and its seven seals.”","understanding":"καὶ εἷς ἐκ τῶν πρεσβυτέρων λέγει μοι· Μὴ κλαῖε, “One of the elders said to me, ‘Don’t weep’” — κλαίω. ἰδοὺ ἐνίκησεν ὁ λέων ὁ ἐκ τῆς φυλῆς Ἰούδα, ἡ ῥίζα Δαυίδ, “Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has overcome” — two Messianic titles: Jacob blessed Judah as a lion's cub from whom the sceptre would not depart; Isaiah spoke of a shoot from the root of Jesse. νικάω, aorist: the victory is already won. ὁ ἀνοίγων τὸ βιβλίον καὶ τὰς ἑπτὰ σφραγῖδας αὐτοῦ, “he who opens the book and its seven seals” — βιβλίον, from the book family.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weep"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"Lion"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G2455","hebrew":"Ἰούδας","translit":"Ioúdas","english":"Judah"},{"strongs":"G4491","hebrew":"ῥίζα","translit":"rhíza","english":"Root"},{"strongs":"G1138","hebrew":"Δαβίδ","translit":"Dabíd","english":"David"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opens"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seals"}]},{"v":6,"text":"I saw in the middle of the throne and of the four living creatures, and in the middle of the elders, a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain, having seven horns, and seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of God, sent out into all the earth.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἐν μέσῳ τοῦ θρόνου... ἀρνίον ἑστηκὸς ὡς ἐσφαγμένον, “I saw in the middle of the throne... a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: Revelation's word for Christ, as distinct from ἀμνός, the sacrificial lamb of John 1:29. John hears ‘Lion’ and turns to see a Lamb: the Lion conquers by being the slain Lamb. ἑστηκός, standing — risen; ἐσφαγμένον, slaughtered — still bearing the marks. ἔχων κέρατα ἑπτὰ καὶ ὀφθαλμοὺς ἑπτά, “having seven horns, and seven eyes” — horns are power, eyes are knowledge: seven means fullness — all-powerful, all-seeing. ἀπεσταλμένοι εἰς πᾶσαν τὴν γῆν, “sent out into all the earth” — γῆ, from the world family: the Spirit goes out into the whole world.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G3319","hebrew":"μέσος","translit":"mésos","english":"middle"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"slain"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirits"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent out"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Then he came, and he took it out of the right hand of him who sat on the throne.","understanding":"καὶ ἦλθεν καὶ εἴληφεν ἐκ τῆς δεξιᾶς τοῦ καθημένου ἐπὶ τοῦ θρόνου, “Then he came, and he took it out of the right hand of him who sat on the throne” — εἴληφεν, perfect tense: He has taken it and holds it. This is Daniel's vision fulfilled: the son of man comes to the Ancient of Days and receives dominion. The ascended Christ receives authority over all history.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"took"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Now when he had taken the book, the four living creatures and the twenty-four elders fell down before the Lamb, each one having a harp, and golden bowls full of incense, which are the prayers of the saints.","understanding":"καὶ ὅτε ἔλαβεν τὸ βιβλίον, “Now when he had taken the book” — βιβλίον, from the book family. τὰ τέσσαρα ζῷα καὶ οἱ εἴκοσι τέσσαρες πρεσβύτεροι ἔπεσαν ἐνώπιον τοῦ ἀρνίου, “the four living creatures and the twenty-four elders fell down before the Lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: the worship given to God in 4:10 is now given to the Lamb. ἔχοντες ἕκαστος κιθάραν καὶ φιάλας χρυσᾶς γεμούσας θυμιαμάτων, “each one having a harp, and golden bowls full of incense” — κιθάρα, a lyre; φιάλη, a wide bowl. αἵ εἰσιν αἱ προσευχαὶ τῶν ἁγίων, “which are the prayers of the saints” — προσευχή, from the pray family: prayer to God. The prayers of suffering believers are not lost; they are kept in golden bowls before the throne, and they will be answered (8:3–5). ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"taken"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell down"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G2788","hebrew":"κιθάρα","translit":"kithára","english":"harp"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowls"},{"strongs":"G2368","hebrew":"θυμίαμα","translit":"thymíama","english":"incense"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayers"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":9,"text":"They sang a new song, saying, “You are worthy to take the book, and to open its seals: for you were killed, and bought us for God with your blood, out of every tribe, language, people, and nation,","understanding":"καὶ ᾄδουσιν ᾠδὴν καινήν, “They sang a new song” — καινός, from the new family: new in kind, a song that could not be sung before the cross. ᾠδή, any song. Ἄξιος εἶ λαβεῖν τὸ βιβλίον, “You are worthy to take the book” — ἄξιος; βιβλίον, from the book family. ὅτι ἐσφάγης, “for you were killed” — σφάζω, to slaughter as a sacrifice. καὶ ἠγόρασας τῷ θεῷ ἐν τῷ αἵματί σου, “and bought us for God with your blood” — ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family: to buy in the market, as slaves were bought. ἐκ πάσης φυλῆς καὶ γλώσσης καὶ λαοῦ καὶ ἔθνους, “out of every tribe, language, people, and nation” — the fourfold formula of Revelation for all humanity.","words":[{"strongs":"G103","hebrew":"ᾄδω","translit":"áidō","english":"sang"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G5603","hebrew":"ᾠδή","translit":"ōidḗ","english":"song"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"worthy"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seals"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"bought"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nation"}]},{"v":10,"text":"and made us kings and priests to our God, and we will reign on the earth.”","understanding":"καὶ ἐποίησας αὐτοὺς τῷ θεῷ ἡμῶν βασιλείαν καὶ ἱερεῖς, “and made us kings and priests to our God” — the WEB follows manuscripts reading ‘kings’; the oldest read βασιλείαν, ‘a kingdom’ (as in 1:6). καὶ βασιλεύσουσιν ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “and we will reign on the earth” — βασιλεύω, to reign; γῆ, from the world family. Many manuscripts read ‘they will reign’. The despised believers of Asia will share Christ's rule over a renewed earth (22:5).","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"made"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priests"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reign"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":11,"text":"I saw, and I heard something like a voice of many angels around the throne, the living creatures, and the elders; and the number of them was ten thousands of ten thousands, and thousands of thousands;","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἤκουσα φωνὴν ἀγγέλων πολλῶν κύκλῳ τοῦ θρόνου, “I saw, and I heard something like a voice of many angels around the throne” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). The circle of worship widens. καὶ ἦν ὁ ἀριθμὸς αὐτῶν μυριάδες μυριάδων καὶ χιλιάδες χιλιάδων, “and the number of them was ten thousands of ten thousands, and thousands of thousands” — μυριάς, ten thousand, the largest Greek number word: an uncountable host, as in Daniel's vision of the Ancient of Days.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G3461","hebrew":"μυριάς","translit":"myriás","english":"ten thousands"},{"strongs":"G5505","hebrew":"χιλιάς","translit":"chiliás","english":"thousands"}]},{"v":12,"text":"saying with a loud voice, “Worthy is the Lamb who has been killed to receive the power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honor, glory, and blessing!”","understanding":"λέγοντες φωνῇ μεγάλῃ· Ἄξιόν ἐστιν τὸ ἀρνίον τὸ ἐσφαγμένον, “saying with a loud voice, ‘Worthy is the Lamb who has been killed’” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family; σφάζω. λαβεῖν τὴν δύναμιν καὶ πλοῦτον καὶ σοφίαν καὶ ἰσχὺν καὶ τιμὴν καὶ δόξαν καὶ εὐλογίαν, “to receive the power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honor, glory, and blessing!” — seven gifts, joined by one article: a complete offering. δύναμις and ἰσχύς both come from the power family: δύναμις is inherent ability, ἰσχύς forceful strength. σοφία, from the wisdom family. The Lamb who was weak, poor and shamed on the cross receives all power, wealth and honour.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"Worthy"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"wealth"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G2479","hebrew":"ἰσχύς","translit":"ischýs","english":"strength"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I heard every created thing which is in heaven, on the earth, under the earth, on the sea, and everything in them, saying, “To him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb be the blessing, the honor, the glory, and the dominion, forever and ever! Amen!”","understanding":"καὶ πᾶν κτίσμα ὃ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς καὶ ὑποκάτω τῆς γῆς καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς θαλάσσης, “I heard every created thing which is in heaven, on the earth, under the earth, on the sea” — κτίσμα, a created thing; γῆ, from the world family. The circle widens to all creation: the whole universe that no one could open the scroll for (v. 3) now worships. Τῷ καθημένῳ ἐπὶ τῷ θρόνῳ καὶ τῷ ἀρνίῳ, “To him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: God and the Lamb receive the same worship. ἡ εὐλογία καὶ ἡ τιμὴ καὶ ἡ δόξα καὶ τὸ κράτος εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “be the blessing, the honor, the glory, and the dominion, forever and ever!” — κράτος, from the power family: ruling might; αἰών, from the time family and the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2938","hebrew":"κτίσμα","translit":"ktísma","english":"created thing"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"dominion"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The four living creatures said, “Amen!” Then the elders fell down and worshiped.","understanding":"καὶ τὰ τέσσαρα ζῷα ἔλεγον· Ἀμήν, “The four living creatures said, ‘Amen!’” — ἀμήν, let it be so. καὶ οἱ πρεσβύτεροι ἔπεσαν καὶ προσεκύνησαν, “Then the elders fell down and worshiped” — προσκυνέω. Chapters 4 and 5 end where all history will end: in worship of God and the Lamb.","words":[{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell down"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Sealed Scroll","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture a great scroll in the right hand of God, written on both sides, bound with seven wax seals. A mighty angel's voice booms through heaven, earth and the world of the dead: ‘Who is worthy to open it?’ Silence. And an old man begins to weep.","meaning":"“Who is worthy to open the book, and to break its seals?” (v. 2). “And I wept much, because no one was found worthy to open the book” (v. 4). Without a worthy one, God's purposes of justice and renewal would stay sealed forever. Every human hope falls silent here."},{"title":"Lion and Lamb","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture John lifting his tear-stained face because an elder has told him to look for a Lion. He turns — and sees, in the very centre of the throne, a small Lamb, standing, with the wounds of slaughter still on it.","meaning":"“Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has overcome” (v. 5). “I saw... a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain” (v. 6). God's power is revealed in self-giving love. The cross is not Christ's defeat; it is His victory."},{"title":"Golden Bowls of Prayer","verseRef":"v. 8","image":"Picture the elders before the Lamb, each holding a golden bowl filled with incense. The fragrant smoke rises before the throne — and you realise that it is made of prayers: the whispered prayers of persecuted believers, prayers you prayed and thought were forgotten.","meaning":"“Golden bowls full of incense, which are the prayers of the saints” (v. 8). No prayer prayed in faith is wasted. Each one is kept in heaven, precious to God, waiting for its answer."},{"title":"The Widening Circle","verseRef":"v. 9-14","image":"Picture concentric circles of worship around the Lamb: first the four living creatures and twenty-four elders singing a new song; then millions upon millions of angels; then every creature in heaven, on earth, under the earth and in the sea — all one roaring song. At the end the four creatures say, ‘Amen,’ and the elders fall on their faces.","meaning":"“Worthy is the Lamb who has been killed to receive the power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honor, glory, and blessing!” (v. 12). “To him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb be the blessing, the honor, the glory, and the dominion, forever and ever!” (v. 13). This is where history is going: the whole universe at the feet of the Lamb."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Sealed Scroll and the Search (v. 1-4)","body":"In the right hand of God is a scroll sealed with seven seals; a mighty angel asks who is worthy to open it; no one in heaven, on earth or under the earth is able, and John weeps."},{"label":"The Lion Who Is a Lamb (v. 5-7)","body":"An elder tells John that the Lion of Judah, the Root of David, has conquered and can open the scroll; John sees a Lamb standing as though slain, with seven horns and seven eyes, who takes the scroll from the right hand of God."},{"label":"The New Song (v. 8-10)","body":"The living creatures and elders fall before the Lamb with harps and bowls of incense, the prayers of the saints, and sing a new song: He is worthy because He was slain and purchased people for God from every nation, and made them a kingdom and priests who will reign on the earth."},{"label":"The Universal Doxology (v. 11-14)","body":"Countless angels proclaim the Lamb worthy of power, wealth, wisdom, might, honour, glory and blessing; then every creature in all creation gives blessing, honour, glory and dominion to the One on the throne and to the Lamb; the creatures say ‘Amen’ and the elders worship."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Who Is Worthy?","paragraphs":["In God's right hand is a scroll, “written inside and outside, sealed shut with seven seals” (v. 1). As the following chapters show, it contains God's plan to judge evil, vindicate His people and bring in His kingdom. As long as it is sealed, that plan cannot go forward. So the angel's question is the question of history: “Who is worthy to open the book, and to break its seals?” (v. 2).","The answer is silence: “No one in heaven above, or on the earth, or under the earth, was able to open the book, or to look in it” (v. 3). No angel, no king, no prophet, no philosopher, no emperor. John weeps (v. 4). His tears are not self-pity; they are the grief of one who longs to see God's justice done and His world put right, and sees no one able to do it.","Notice the question is not ‘who is strong enough?’ — a mighty angel asks it — but ‘who is worthy?’ The opening of history's final chapter requires not just power but moral right: one who has paid the price and earned the authority."]},{"heading":"The Lion Who Is a Lamb","paragraphs":["An elder tells John to stop weeping: “Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has overcome; he who opens the book and its seven seals” (v. 5). These are the great Messianic titles: the lion of Judah from Jacob's blessing, the Root of David from Isaiah. John hears of a conquering Lion. Then he turns to look — and sees “a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain” (v. 6).","This may be the most important moment in the book. Everything Israel hoped for in a conquering Messiah is fulfilled — but by a slain Lamb. The Lion conquers by being the Lamb. God's power is shown not in crushing His enemies but in dying for them. The Lamb stands (He is risen), yet still bears the marks of slaughter (His death is eternally effective). He has seven horns (all power) and seven eyes (all knowledge, the Spirit sent into all the earth).","The Lamb takes the scroll from the hand of the One on the throne (v. 7). This is Daniel's vision of the son of man coming to the Ancient of Days to receive an everlasting kingdom, fulfilled in the ascension of Christ. From this moment, all history is in His hands."]},{"heading":"The New Song","paragraphs":["Heaven responds with worship. The creatures and elders fall before the Lamb as they fell before God (4:10; 5:8): the Lamb receives divine worship. They hold harps and “golden bowls full of incense, which are the prayers of the saints” (v. 8). The prayers of persecuted believers on earth are treasured in heaven; in chapter 8 they will be poured out on the earth in answer.","Their song is new because its subject is new: “You are worthy to take the book, and to open its seals: for you were killed, and bought us for God with your blood, out of every tribe, language, people, and nation” (v. 9). The Lamb's worthiness rests on the cross. His blood was the purchase price, and the people He bought come from everywhere. The church is not one nation or race but a people gathered from all of them.","And they are given a destiny: “and made us kings and priests to our God, and we will reign on the earth” (v. 10). The small, persecuted churches of Asia are told who they really are: a royal priesthood with a future on a renewed earth."]},{"heading":"Every Creature Worships","paragraphs":["The worship spreads outward. First the creatures and elders, then “ten thousands of ten thousands, and thousands of thousands” of angels (v. 11), crying, “Worthy is the Lamb who has been killed to receive the power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honor, glory, and blessing!” (v. 12). The seven gifts form one complete offering. The Lamb who on earth was weak, poor, mocked as foolish, shamed and cursed receives power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honour, glory and blessing.","Then the whole universe joins: “every created thing which is in heaven, on the earth, under the earth, on the sea” (v. 13). The same regions where no one was found worthy (v. 3) now praise the One who is. And the praise is given equally “To him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb” (v. 13). The Lamb shares the honour of God. In a book that forbids the worship of anyone but God (19:10; 22:9), this is a clear declaration that Jesus is God.","Chapter 5 ends with ‘Amen’ and worship (v. 14). Whatever terrors the following chapters describe, this is the end of the story: the universe in worship before God and the Lamb."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Behold, the Lamb of God","paragraphs":["John the Baptist first pointed to Jesus with the words, “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” (John 1:29). Paul wrote, “For indeed Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place” (1 Corinthians 5:7). And Peter: “knowing that you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold, from the useless way of life handed down from your fathers, but with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (1 Peter 1:18–19). Revelation shows that same Lamb on the throne.","The new song's ‘kings and priests’ (v. 10) echoes Peter's words: “But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession” (1 Peter 2:9).","And the universal worship of verse 13 fulfils Paul's hymn: “that at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of those in heaven, those on earth, and those under the earth, and that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father” (Philippians 2:10–11)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Lamb","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","strongs":"G721 (slain σφάζω, spházō, G4969)","verses":"v. 6, 8, 12, 13; 7:9-17; 14:1-4; 22:1-3","insight":"Strong's: “a lambkin.” ἀρνίον, from the lamb family, is Revelation's title for Christ — twenty-eight times, and almost nowhere else in the New Testament. Its form is a diminutive, ‘little lamb’, the most defenceless of creatures. Yet this Lamb has seven horns (v. 6), unleashes the seals (6:1), is the object of the wrath that the kings of the earth fear (6:16), conquers the beast's armies (17:14), marries His bride (19:7), and shares the throne of God (22:1). The weakest of animals is the Lord of all."},{"english":"Seal","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","strongs":"G4973 (sealed shut κατασφραγίζω, katasphragízō, G2696)","verses":"v. 1, 2, 5, 9; 6:1-12; 7:2-8","insight":"Strong's: “a signet (as fencing in or protecting from misappropriation); by implication, the stamp impressed (as a mark of privacy, or genuineness), literally or figuratively.” σφραγίς is both the wax seal that closes a document and the signet ring that makes the impression. The scroll's seven seals keep its contents secret until the rightful one breaks them. In chapter 7 God's servants are ‘sealed’ on their foreheads — marked as His, protected, genuine. The same word that hides God's plan also protects God's people."},{"english":"Slain / killed","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","strongs":"G4969 (Lamb ἀρνίον, arníon, G721)","verses":"v. 6, 9, 12; 6:9; 13:3, 8","insight":"Strong's: “to butcher (especially an animal for food or in sacrifice) or (generally) to slaughter, or (specially), to maim (violently).” σφάζω is the word for a sacrificial slaughter. The Lamb is ‘slain’ (v. 6), the martyrs under the altar were ‘killed’ (6:9) — and the beast mimics Christ with a head ‘as if wounded to death’ (13:3), the same word. Revelation's counterfeit trinity copies even the cross. But only one was truly slain and truly lives forever."},{"english":"Strength","hebrew":"ἰσχύς","translit":"ischýs","strongs":"G2479 (power δύναμις, dýnamis, G1411)","verses":"v. 12; 7:12; 18:2","insight":"Strong's: “compare , a form of G2192 (ἔχω)); forcefulness (literally or figuratively).” ἰσχύς, from the power family, is forceful strength, might in action — as distinct from δύναμις, which is inherent ability or capacity, also in verse 12. The seven-fold praise gives the Lamb both: every kind of power belongs to Him. The ‘mighty’ angel of verse 2 (ἰσχυρός, from the same root) could not open the scroll; only the Lamb, who laid His strength down, receives all strength."},{"english":"Song","hebrew":"ᾠδή","translit":"ōidḗ","strongs":"G5603 (new καινός, kainós, G2537)","verses":"v. 9; 14:3; 15:3","insight":"Strong's: “a chant or \"ode\" (the general term for any words sung; while G5215 (ὕμνος) denotes especially a religious metrical composition, and G5568 (ψαλμός) still more specially, a Hebrew cantillation).” ᾠδή is any song. The Psalms call for ‘a new song’ when God does a new act of salvation. The ‘new song’ of verse 9 is καινή, from the new family — new in kind, not just recent: it is the song of redemption by the blood of the Lamb, which no one could sing before the cross. In 14:3 only the redeemed can learn it."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you the slain Lamb at the centre of the throne — and the centre of history.","items":[{"title":"Stop Weeping","body":"John wept because no one was worthy, until he heard, “Don’t weep. Behold, the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah... has overcome” (v. 5). The Spirit speaks the same to you. Whatever seems hopelessly sealed — injustice, grief, a broken world, a broken life — the Lamb holds the scroll. He has already overcome."},{"title":"Your Prayers Are Kept","body":"The prayers of the saints fill golden bowls before the throne (v. 8). The Spirit wants you to know that not one of your faithful prayers has been lost. They rise like incense, and God will answer them in His time."},{"title":"Join the New Song","body":"You were bought for God with the Lamb's blood, from your tribe and language and people and nation (v. 9). The Spirit invites you to take your place in the widening circle of worship — now, in your home and church, and one day before the throne."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Power Check","body":"Do you expect God to work mainly through strength and success, or through the way of the Lamb — sacrifice, faithfulness and love (vv. 5–6)?"},{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"Have you stopped praying about something because it seemed unanswered? Remember the golden bowls (v. 8)."},{"title":"The Nations Check","body":"Does your picture of the church include every tribe, language, people and nation (v. 9)? How does that shape the way you treat people unlike you?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Sing the Song","body":"Read verses 9–14 aloud each day this week, then sing or say a song of worship to Jesus. On one day, pray verse 12 slowly, thanking the Lamb for each of the seven gifts He deserves and asking how you can give Him each one."},{"title":"Pray for Every Nation","body":"Choose one people group or nation where Christians are few or suffering. Learn something about it this week, and pray daily that the Lamb would gather worshippers from it (v. 9)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, Lion of the tribe of Judah, Root of David — You have overcome. When no one in heaven or on earth or under the earth could open the scroll, You stepped forward, the Lamb standing as though slain. You took the scroll from the right hand of the Father, and all history is in Your hands. You are worthy, for You were slain, and with Your blood You bought me for God, and people from every tribe and language and people and nation. You have made us a kingdom and priests to our God, and we will reign on the earth. Thank You that my prayers rise before You like incense in golden bowls. With the living creatures, the elders and countless angels I cry: Worthy is the Lamb who was slain, to receive power and wealth and wisdom and strength and honour and glory and blessing! To Him who sits on the throne and to the Lamb be blessing and honour and glory and dominion forever and ever. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Passover Lamb","ref":"Exodus 12:1-13","relatedVerses":"v. 6, 9","summary":"On the night God judged Egypt, each Israelite household took a lamb without defect, killed it at twilight, and put its blood on the doorposts. God said, ‘When I see the blood, I will pass over you.’ That night the firstborn of Egypt died, but the people under the blood were spared and walked out of slavery. The Lamb of Revelation 5 was slain like the Passover lamb, and by His blood He ‘bought’ a people for God out of every nation — a greater exodus. The plagues that follow in Revelation echo the plagues of Egypt."},{"title":"Jacob Blesses Judah","ref":"Genesis 49:8-12","relatedVerses":"v. 5","summary":"On his deathbed, Jacob blessed his sons. To Judah he said, ‘Judah is a lion’s cub... The sceptre will not depart from Judah, nor the ruler’s staff from between his feet, until he comes to whom it belongs. To him will the obedience of the peoples be.’ From Judah came David, and from David's line came Jesus. The elder's words, ‘the Lion who is of the tribe of Judah’, announce that the one to whom the sceptre belongs has come — though He conquers as a Lamb."},{"title":"The Son of Man Before the Ancient of Days","ref":"Daniel 7:9-14","relatedVerses":"v. 7, 11","summary":"Daniel saw thrones set in place and the Ancient of Days take His seat; thousands of thousands served Him, and ten thousand times ten thousand stood before Him. Then ‘one like a son of man’ came with the clouds of heaven to the Ancient of Days and was given dominion, glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations and languages should serve Him; His dominion would never pass away. Revelation 5 is the fulfilment: the Lamb comes to the throne, takes the scroll, and receives the worship of all creation."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":6,"chapterTitle":"The Lamb Opens the Six Seals","themeTitle":"“How Long, Master, the Holy and True?”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw that the Lamb opened one of the seven seals, and I heard one of the four living creatures saying, as with a voice of thunder, “Come and see!”","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ὅτε ἤνοιξεν τὸ ἀρνίον μίαν ἐκ τῶν ἑπτὰ σφραγίδων, “I saw that the Lamb opened one of the seven seals” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. Every judgement that follows is released by the Lamb: nothing happens outside His control. καὶ ἤκουσα ἑνὸς ἐκ τῶν τεσσάρων ζῴων λέγοντος ὡς φωνὴ βροντῆς· Ἔρχου, “and I heard one of the four living creatures saying, as with a voice of thunder, ‘Come and see!’” — ἔρχου, ‘Come!’ is addressed to the rider; ‘and see’ (βλέπε, from the see family) is found in later manuscripts.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seals"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunder"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"Come"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"}]},{"v":2,"text":"And behold, a white horse, and he who sat on it had a bow. A crown was given to him, and he came out conquering, and to conquer.","understanding":"καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ ἵππος λευκός, “And behold, a white horse” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). Zechariah saw horses of different colours sent through the earth (Zechariah 1:8; 6:2–3). καὶ ὁ καθήμενος ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν ἔχων τόξον, “and he who sat on it had a bow” — τόξον, the weapon of the feared Parthian horsemen on Rome's eastern frontier. καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ στέφανος, “A crown was given to him” — στέφανος, from the crown family: a victor's wreath. καὶ ἐξῆλθεν νικῶν καὶ ἵνα νικήσῃ, “and he came out conquering, and to conquer” — νικάω. Some see this rider as Christ or the gospel going out (19:11); but since the other three horsemen bring war, famine and death, the strongest reading is conquest and military aggression — perhaps a counterfeit of Christ, white on the outside, conquering by force.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"behold"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G5115","hebrew":"τόξον","translit":"tóxon","english":"bow"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"conquering"}]},{"v":3,"text":"When he opened the second seal, I heard the second living creature saying, “Come!”","understanding":"Καὶ ὅτε ἤνοιξεν τὴν σφραγῖδα τὴν δευτέραν, “When he opened the second seal” — σφραγίς. ἤκουσα τοῦ δευτέρου ζῴου λέγοντος· Ἔρχου, “I heard the second living creature saying, ‘Come!’” — each creature in turn summons a rider. Heaven commands; the horsemen obey.","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creature"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"Come"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Another came out, a red horse. To him who sat on it was given power to take peace from the earth, and that they should kill one another. There was given to him a great sword.","understanding":"καὶ ἐξῆλθεν ἄλλος ἵππος πυρρός, “Another came out, a red horse” — πυρρός, fiery red, the colour of blood. καὶ τῷ καθημένῳ ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν ἐδόθη αὐτῷ λαβεῖν τὴν εἰρήνην ἐκ τῆς γῆς, “To him who sat on it was given power to take peace from the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. The Pax Romana, Rome's boasted peace, can be taken away in a moment. καὶ ἵνα ἀλλήλους σφάξουσιν, “and that they should kill one another” — σφάζω, the word used of the slain Lamb (5:6): civil war, mutual slaughter. καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ μάχαιρα μεγάλη, “There was given to him a great sword” — μάχαιρα, from the sword family: the short sword of soldiers and executions, as distinct from the ῥομφαία of Christ's mouth (1:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"},{"strongs":"G4450","hebrew":"πυῤῥός","translit":"pyrrhós","english":"red"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"take"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"kill"},{"strongs":"G3162","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":5,"text":"When he opened the third seal, I heard the third living creature saying, “Come and see!” And behold, a black horse, and he who sat on it had a balance in his hand.","understanding":"Καὶ ὅτε ἤνοιξεν τὴν σφραγῖδα τὴν τρίτην, “When he opened the third seal” — σφραγίς. καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ ἵππος μέλας, “And behold, a black horse” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). μέλας, black: the colour of mourning and famine. καὶ ὁ καθήμενος ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν ἔχων ζυγὸν ἐν τῇ χειρὶ αὐτοῦ, “and he who sat on it had a balance in his hand” — ζυγός, a pair of scales: food is being weighed out and rationed.","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creature"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"behold"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"},{"strongs":"G3189","hebrew":"μέλας","translit":"mélas","english":"black"},{"strongs":"G2218","hebrew":"ζυγός","translit":"zygós","english":"balance"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"}]},{"v":6,"text":"I heard a voice in the middle of the four living creatures saying, “A choenix of wheat for a denarius, and three choenix of barley for a denarius! Don’t damage the oil and the wine!”","understanding":"Χοῖνιξ σίτου δηναρίου, “A choenix of wheat for a denarius” — a χοῖνιξ, about a litre, was one man's daily ration of grain; a denarius was a full day's wage. A day's work buys only enough bread for one person: famine prices. καὶ τρεῖς χοίνικες κριθῆς δηναρίου, “and three choenix of barley for a denarius!” — barley was the poor man's grain, enough for a small family. καὶ τὸ ἔλαιον καὶ τὸν οἶνον μὴ ἀδικήσῃς, “Don’t damage the oil and the wine!” — ἀδικέω, to harm. Olive trees and vines have deep roots and survive drought; or perhaps the luxuries of the rich are untouched while the poor go hungry. The judgement is limited.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3319","hebrew":"μέσος","translit":"mésos","english":"middle"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G5518","hebrew":"χοῖνιξ","translit":"choînix","english":"choenix"},{"strongs":"G4621","hebrew":"σῖτος","translit":"sîtos","english":"wheat"},{"strongs":"G1220","hebrew":"δηνάριον","translit":"dēnárion","english":"denarius"},{"strongs":"G2915","hebrew":"κριθή","translit":"krithḗ","english":"barley"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"damage"},{"strongs":"G1637","hebrew":"ἔλαιον","translit":"élaion","english":"oil"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"}]},{"v":7,"text":"When he opened the fourth seal, I heard the fourth living creature saying, “Come and see!”","understanding":"Καὶ ὅτε ἤνοιξεν τὴν σφραγῖδα τὴν τετάρτην, “When he opened the fourth seal” — σφραγίς. ἤκουσα φωνὴν τοῦ τετάρτου ζῴου λέγοντος· Ἔρχου, “I heard the fourth living creature saying, ‘Come and see!’” — ἔρχου; ‘and see’ (βλέπε, from the see family) appears in later manuscripts.","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G5067","hebrew":"τέταρτος","translit":"tétartos","english":"fourth"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creature"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"Come"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"}]},{"v":8,"text":"And behold, a pale horse, and he who sat on it, his name was Death. Hades followed with him. Authority over one fourth of the earth, to kill with the sword, with famine, with death, and by the wild animals of the earth was given to him.","understanding":"καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ ἵππος χλωρός, “And behold, a pale horse” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). χλωρός, the yellowish-green of a corpse. καὶ ὁ καθήμενος ἐπάνω αὐτοῦ, ὄνομα αὐτῷ ὁ Θάνατος, καὶ ὁ ᾅδης ἠκολούθει μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ, “and he who sat on it, his name was Death. Hades followed with him” — death takes the body, Hades the soul. But Christ holds their keys (1:18). καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς ἐξουσία ἐπὶ τὸ τέταρτον τῆς γῆς, “Authority over one fourth of the earth” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: delegated authority — given, and limited; γῆ, from the world family. ἀποκτεῖναι ἐν ῥομφαίᾳ καὶ ἐν λιμῷ καὶ ἐν θανάτῳ καὶ ὑπὸ τῶν θηρίων τῆς γῆς, “to kill with the sword, with famine, with death, and by the wild animals of the earth” — ῥομφαία, from the sword family; θάνατος here means plague. Ezekiel's ‘four severe judgements’: sword, famine, wild beasts and plague.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"behold"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"},{"strongs":"G5515","hebrew":"χλωρός","translit":"chlōrós","english":"pale"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G86","hebrew":"ᾅδης","translit":"háidēs","english":"Hades"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"Authority"},{"strongs":"G5067","hebrew":"τέταρτος","translit":"tétartos","english":"fourth"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"kill"},{"strongs":"G4501","hebrew":"ῥομφαία","translit":"rhomphaía","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G3042","hebrew":"λιμός","translit":"limós","english":"famine"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"wild animals"}]},{"v":9,"text":"When he opened the fifth seal, I saw underneath the altar the souls of those who had been killed for the Word of God, and for the testimony of the Lamb which they had.","understanding":"Καὶ ὅτε ἤνοιξεν τὴν πέμπτην σφραγῖδα, εἶδον ὑποκάτω τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου, “When he opened the fifth seal, I saw underneath the altar” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); θυσιαστήριον, the altar: in the temple the blood of sacrifices was poured out at the base of the altar. τὰς ψυχὰς τῶν ἐσφαγμένων διὰ τὸν λόγον τοῦ θεοῦ, “the souls of those who had been killed for the Word of God” — ψυχή, from the life family: the life, the person; σφάζω, the word used of the Lamb (5:6): they died as He died; λόγος, from the word family. καὶ διὰ τὴν μαρτυρίαν ἣν εἶχον, “and for the testimony of the Lamb which they had” — μαρτυρία, from the witness family.","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G3991","hebrew":"πέμπτος","translit":"pémptos","english":"fifth"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"Word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"}]},{"v":10,"text":"They cried with a loud voice, saying, “How long, Master, the holy and true, until you judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?”","understanding":"καὶ ἔκραξαν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ λέγοντες, “They cried with a loud voice, saying” — κράζω, to cry out. Ἕως πότε, ὁ δεσπότης ὁ ἅγιος καὶ ἀληθινός, “How long, Master, the holy and true” — the cry of the psalms. δεσπότης, absolute master, the owner of slaves; ἅγιος and ἀληθινός, the titles of Christ in 3:7. οὐ κρίνεις καὶ ἐκδικεῖς τὸ αἷμα ἡμῶν, “until you judge and avenge our blood” — κρίνω, from the judge family: to try and decide; ἐκδικέω, to vindicate, to do justice. Not personal revenge, but a plea for God to put things right publicly. ἐκ τῶν κατοικούντων ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “on those who dwell on the earth” — γῆ, from the world family: Revelation's phrase for those who oppose God. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"Master"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G1556","hebrew":"ἐκδικέω","translit":"ekdikéō","english":"avenge"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":11,"text":"A long white robe was given to each of them. They were told that they should rest yet for a while, until their fellow servants and their brothers, who would also be killed even as they were, should complete their course.","understanding":"καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς ἑκάστῳ στολὴ λευκή, “A long white robe was given to each of them” — στολή, the long robe of honour: the white garments promised to the overcomers (3:5). They are already vindicated in heaven. καὶ ἐρρέθη αὐτοῖς ἵνα ἀναπαύσονται ἔτι χρόνον μικρόν, “They were told that they should rest yet for a while” — ἀναπαύω, from the rest family; χρόνος, from the time family: a short span of time. ἕως πληρωθῶσιν καὶ οἱ σύνδουλοι αὐτῶν, “until their fellow servants and their brothers... should complete their course” — πληρόω, from the perfect family: to be made full, completed; σύνδουλος, a fellow slave. God has an appointed number; His patience is not neglect.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4749","hebrew":"στολή","translit":"stolḗ","english":"robe"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G373","hebrew":"ἀναπαύω","translit":"anapaúō","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"while"},{"strongs":"G4889","hebrew":"σύνδουλος","translit":"sýndoulos","english":"fellow servants"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G4137","hebrew":"πληρόω","translit":"plēróō","english":"complete"}]},{"v":12,"text":"I saw when he opened the sixth seal, and there was a great earthquake. The sun became black as sackcloth made of hair, and the whole moon became as blood.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ὅτε ἤνοιξεν τὴν σφραγῖδα τὴν ἕκτην, “I saw when he opened the sixth seal” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). καὶ σεισμὸς μέγας ἐγένετο, “and there was a great earthquake” — σεισμός: the cities of Asia knew earthquakes well (Sardis and Philadelphia were devastated in AD 17). καὶ ὁ ἥλιος ἐγένετο μέλας ὡς σάκκος τρίχινος, “The sun became black as sackcloth made of hair” — σάκκος, the rough black cloth of mourning. καὶ ἡ σελήνη ὅλη ἐγένετο ὡς αἷμα, “and the whole moon became as blood” — the prophets' language for the day of the Lord, which Peter quoted at Pentecost (Acts 2:20).","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G1623","hebrew":"ἕκτος","translit":"héktos","english":"sixth"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G4578","hebrew":"σεισμός","translit":"seismós","english":"earthquake"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G3189","hebrew":"μέλας","translit":"mélas","english":"black"},{"strongs":"G4526","hebrew":"σάκκος","translit":"sákkos","english":"sackcloth"},{"strongs":"G4582","hebrew":"σελήνη","translit":"selḗnē","english":"moon"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"}]},{"v":13,"text":"The stars of the sky fell to the earth, like a fig tree dropping its unripe figs when it is shaken by a great wind.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ ἀστέρες τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἔπεσαν εἰς τὴν γῆν, “The stars of the sky fell to the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. ὡς συκῆ βάλλει τοὺς ὀλύνθους αὐτῆς ὑπὸ ἀνέμου μεγάλου σειομένη, “like a fig tree dropping its unripe figs when it is shaken by a great wind” — ὄλυνθος, the late, unripe winter figs. Isaiah used the same picture of the heavens' host falling like fruit from a fig tree. The created order seems to come apart.","words":[{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4808","hebrew":"συκῆ","translit":"sykē","english":"fig tree"},{"strongs":"G3653","hebrew":"ὄλυνθος","translit":"ólynthos","english":"unripe figs"},{"strongs":"G417","hebrew":"ἄνεμος","translit":"ánemos","english":"wind"},{"strongs":"G4579","hebrew":"σείω","translit":"seíō","english":"shaken"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The sky was removed like a scroll when it is rolled up. Every mountain and island were moved out of their places.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ οὐρανὸς ἀπεχωρίσθη ὡς βιβλίον ἑλισσόμενον, “The sky was removed like a scroll when it is rolled up” — βιβλίον, from the book family: the same word as the Lamb's scroll; ἑλίσσω, to roll up. The sky that seemed eternal is rolled away like a finished document (Isaiah 34:4). καὶ πᾶν ὄρος καὶ νῆσος ἐκ τῶν τόπων αὐτῶν ἐκινήθησαν, “Every mountain and island were moved out of their places” — κινέω, the word used of Ephesus' lampstand (2:5). Nothing in creation is permanent except God.","words":[{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G673","hebrew":"ἀποχωρίζω","translit":"apochōrízō","english":"removed"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"scroll"},{"strongs":"G1667","hebrew":"ἑλίσσω","translit":"helíssō","english":"rolled up"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"},{"strongs":"G3520","hebrew":"νῆσος","translit":"nēsos","english":"island"},{"strongs":"G2795","hebrew":"κινέω","translit":"kinéō","english":"moved"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"places"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The kings of the earth, the princes, the commanding officers, the rich, the strong, and every slave and free person, hid themselves in the caves and in the rocks of the mountains.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ βασιλεῖς τῆς γῆς καὶ οἱ μεγιστᾶνες καὶ οἱ χιλίαρχοι καὶ οἱ πλούσιοι καὶ οἱ ἰσχυροί, “The kings of the earth, the princes, the commanding officers, the rich, the strong” — γῆ, from the world family; ἰσχυρός, from the power family: the mighty in physical force. καὶ πᾶς δοῦλος καὶ ἐλεύθερος, “and every slave and free person” — δοῦλος, from the servant family. Seven classes, from emperor to slave: all humanity without God, from top to bottom. ἔκρυψαν ἑαυτοὺς εἰς τὰ σπήλαια καὶ εἰς τὰς πέτρας τῶν ὀρέων, “hid themselves in the caves and in the rocks of the mountains” — as Isaiah foresaw people hiding in the rocks ‘from the terror of the LORD’.","words":[{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3175","hebrew":"μεγιστᾶνες","translit":"megistânes","english":"princes"},{"strongs":"G5506","hebrew":"χιλίαρχος","translit":"chilíarchos","english":"commanding officers"},{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"strong"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"slave"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G2928","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","english":"hid"},{"strongs":"G4693","hebrew":"σπήλαιον","translit":"spḗlaion","english":"caves"},{"strongs":"G4073","hebrew":"πέτρα","translit":"pétra","english":"rocks"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountains"}]},{"v":16,"text":"They told the mountains and the rocks, “Fall on us, and hide us from the face of him who sits on the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb,","understanding":"καὶ λέγουσιν τοῖς ὄρεσιν καὶ ταῖς πέτραις· Πέσετε ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς, “They told the mountains and the rocks, ‘Fall on us’” — Hosea's words, which Jesus quoted on the way to the cross (Luke 23:30). καὶ κρύψατε ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ προσώπου τοῦ καθημένου ἐπὶ τοῦ θρόνου, “and hide us from the face of him who sits on the throne” — the face His people long to see (22:4) is the face His enemies cannot bear. καὶ ἀπὸ τῆς ὀργῆς τοῦ ἀρνίου, “and from the wrath of the Lamb” — ὀργή, from the wrath family: settled, holy anger, as distinct from θυμός, anger as a passionate outburst; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The most startling phrase in the book: the gentlest of creatures, angry. Rejected love becomes judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountains"},{"strongs":"G4073","hebrew":"πέτρα","translit":"pétra","english":"rocks"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"Fall"},{"strongs":"G2928","hebrew":"κρύπτω","translit":"krýptō","english":"hide"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":17,"text":"for the great day of his wrath has come; and who is able to stand?”","understanding":"ὅτι ἦλθεν ἡ ἡμέρα ἡ μεγάλη τῆς ὀργῆς αὐτῶν, “for the great day of his wrath has come” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: ‘the day of the LORD’ of the prophets; ὀργή, from the wrath family. Many manuscripts read ‘their wrath’ — of God and the Lamb together. καὶ τίς δύναται σταθῆναι; “and who is able to stand?” — δύναμαι, from the power family: who has the ability? The question is left hanging — and chapter 7 answers it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stand"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Four Horsemen","verseRef":"v. 1-8","image":"Picture four horses galloping out from the throne one after another: a white horse whose rider draws a bow, a red horse whose rider swings a great sword, a black horse whose rider holds up a pair of scales, and a sickly pale horse ridden by Death, with the grave following behind.","meaning":"“I saw that the Lamb opened one of the seven seals” (v. 1). Conquest, war, famine and death are the familiar sorrows of a fallen world. Revelation does not deny them, but it shows that even they are released and limited by the Lamb — ‘authority over one fourth of the earth’, and no more (v. 8)."},{"title":"Souls Under the Altar","verseRef":"v. 9-11","image":"Picture the great altar of heaven, and at its base, where the blood of sacrifices was poured, the souls of martyrs crying out. Then see an angel moving among them, placing a long white robe on each one, and speaking quietly: ‘Rest a little longer.’","meaning":"“How long, Master, the holy and true, until you judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?” (v. 10). “A long white robe was given to each of them. They were told that they should rest yet for a while” (v. 11). God hears the cry for justice. His delay is not indifference; it is patience with a plan."},{"title":"The Sky Rolled Up","verseRef":"v. 12-14","image":"Picture the ground heaving, the sun going black, the moon turning blood-red, stars falling like figs shaken from a tree in a gale — and the sky itself rolled up like a scroll, leaving nothing above but God.","meaning":"“The sky was removed like a scroll when it is rolled up. Every mountain and island were moved out of their places” (v. 14). Everything people trust as solid and permanent can be moved. Only God and those who are His stand firm."},{"title":"Hiding from the Lamb","verseRef":"v. 15-17","image":"Picture emperors, generals, billionaires, soldiers and slaves all crowding into the same caves, begging the mountains to fall on them. Not because of an army, but because they have seen a face on a throne and a Lamb whose patience has ended.","meaning":"“Fall on us, and hide us from the face of him who sits on the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb, for the great day of his wrath has come; and who is able to stand?” (vv. 16–17). Only those sheltered by the Lamb's blood can stand before the Lamb's wrath."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Four Horsemen (v. 1-8)","body":"The Lamb opens the first four seals; at each a living creature calls out, and a rider goes forth: on a white horse with a bow and a crown, conquering; on a red horse with a great sword, taking peace from the earth; on a black horse with scales, bringing famine prices; on a pale horse, Death, with Hades following, given authority over a quarter of the earth."},{"label":"The Cry of the Martyrs (v. 9-11)","body":"At the fifth seal John sees under the altar the souls of those slain for God's word and their testimony; they cry out for God to judge and avenge their blood; each is given a white robe and told to rest a little longer until the number of their fellow servants is complete."},{"label":"The Great Day of Wrath (v. 12-17)","body":"At the sixth seal there is a great earthquake; the sun goes black, the moon turns to blood, the stars fall, the sky is rolled up and mountains and islands move; everyone from kings to slaves hides in the caves and begs the mountains to hide them from the face of God and the wrath of the Lamb, for the great day of wrath has come."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Lamb Opens the Seals","paragraphs":["The scroll is now in the Lamb's hand, and He begins to open it: “I saw that the Lamb opened one of the seven seals” (v. 1). This is the first of three series of seven judgements in Revelation — seals (chs. 6–8), trumpets (chs. 8–11) and bowls (ch. 16). Each series grows more intense: the seals affect a quarter of the earth (6:8), the trumpets a third (8:7–12), the bowls the whole (16:1–21). Many see the three series as overlapping views of the same period, each ending at the end of history; others see them as following one another in sequence.","Whatever the order, the main point is clear. These terrible things do not happen because God has lost control. The Lamb opens each seal; a living creature from the throne calls each rider; the power of each is ‘given’ (vv. 2, 4, 8). Evil is real, but it runs on a leash held by the Lamb.","Jesus spoke of the same things: “You will hear of wars and rumors of wars. See that you aren’t troubled, for all this must happen, but the end is not yet” (Matthew 24:6), and “nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom; and there will be famines, plagues, and earthquakes in various places. But all these things are the beginning of birth pains” (Matthew 24:7–8). The four horsemen picture the sorrows that run through all history between Christ's first and second comings."]},{"heading":"The Four Horsemen","paragraphs":["The first rider, on a white horse, carries a bow and wears a crown, and “he came out conquering, and to conquer” (v. 2). Because Christ later rides a white horse (19:11), some see here the victorious spread of the gospel. But this rider belongs with the three that follow, which all bring disaster; and his weapon is a bow, not the sword of Christ's mouth. The strongest reading is that he represents conquest and the lust for power — perhaps a counterfeit of Christ, looking righteous but conquering by force.","The second rider takes peace from the earth so that people kill one another (v. 4). The third brings famine: “A choenix of wheat for a denarius” (v. 6) — a whole day's wage for one person's daily bread. Yet the oil and wine are spared: the judgement is real but limited. The fourth rider is Death himself, followed by Hades, and they are given authority “over one fourth of the earth, to kill with the sword, with famine, with death, and by the wild animals of the earth” (v. 8).","Conquest, war, famine and death: this is what human sin and rebellion produce in a fallen world. God does not need to invent new punishments; He often lets sin run its course. But He sets limits — one quarter, no more — and these sorrows are meant to warn and to call people to repentance (9:20–21)."]},{"heading":"How Long?","paragraphs":["The fifth seal moves from earth to heaven. John sees “underneath the altar the souls of those who had been killed for the Word of God, and for the testimony of the Lamb which they had” (v. 9). Their blood was poured out like a sacrifice; they were ‘slain’ with the same word used of the Lamb. Their cry is the cry of the oppressed in every age: “How long, Master, the holy and true, until you judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?” (v. 10).","This is not a cry for personal revenge. It is a plea that God would show Himself to be what He is — holy and true — by putting things right in public. Jesus promised, “Won’t God avenge his chosen ones, who are crying out to him day and night, and yet he exercises patience with them? I tell you that he will avenge them quickly” (Luke 18:7–8). Paul told believers, “Don’t seek revenge yourselves, beloved, but give place to God’s wrath” (Romans 12:19).","God's answer comes in two parts: a white robe for each, and a word of patience: “they should rest yet for a while, until their fellow servants and their brothers, who would also be killed even as they were, should complete their course” (v. 11). They are already vindicated in heaven; their full public vindication waits for God's appointed time. The rest of the book is, in a sense, the answer to their prayer."]},{"heading":"The Great Day of Wrath","paragraphs":["The sixth seal brings the collapse of the universe: earthquake, a black sun, a blood-red moon, falling stars, the sky rolled up like a scroll, mountains and islands moved (vv. 12–14). These are the prophets' pictures of the day of the Lord, used by Jesus too: “There will be signs in the sun, moon, and stars; and on the earth anxiety of nations” (Luke 21:25). Whether taken literally or as pictures of the overthrow of the powers of this world, they announce that the end has come.","Every class of person, from kings to slaves, tries to hide: “Fall on us, and hide us from the face of him who sits on the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb” (v. 16). The phrase ‘the wrath of the Lamb’ holds together what we often separate. The Lamb who died for sinners is also the Judge of those who refuse Him. His wrath is ὀργή — not a loss of temper, but the settled, holy opposition of love to everything that destroys.","The chapter ends with a question: “for the great day of his wrath has come; and who is able to stand?” (v. 17). It is left hanging — and the next chapter answers it. Those who can stand are those sealed by God and washed in the blood of the Lamb (7:3, 14)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Beginning of Birth Pains","paragraphs":["The seals follow the order of Jesus' teaching on the Mount of Olives: false messiahs, wars, famines, plagues, earthquakes, persecution and martyrdom, and finally cosmic signs and the coming of the Son of Man. “But all these things are the beginning of birth pains” (Matthew 24:8). Pain, yes — but the pain of birth, leading to new creation.","On His way to the cross, Jesus told the weeping women of Jerusalem that days were coming when people would say, “Then they will begin to tell the mountains, ‘Fall on us!’ and tell the hills, ‘Cover us.’” (Luke 23:30). The sixth seal shows that cry on a universal scale.","Paul promised suffering believers that God would act: “Since it is a righteous thing with God to repay affliction to those who afflict you, and to give relief to you who are afflicted with us, when the Lord Jesus is revealed from heaven with his mighty angels in flaming fire” (2 Thessalonians 1:6–7). The souls under the altar receive the first part of that relief — rest and white robes — while they wait for the rest."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Wrath","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","strongs":"G3709 (wrath θυμός, thymós, G2372)","verses":"v. 16, 17; 11:18; 14:10; 16:19; 19:15","insight":"Strong's: “properly, desire (as a reaching forth or excitement of the mind), i.e. (by analogy), violent passion (ire, or (justifiable) abhorrence); by implication punishment.” ὀργή, from the wrath family, is settled, purposeful anger — God's just and steady opposition to evil, which ends in punishment. Its sibling θυμός is anger as a boiling passion. ‘The wrath of the Lamb’ (v. 16) is not a contradiction: the same love that died for sinners must oppose everything that destroys those it loves. Those who refuse the Lamb's mercy meet the Lamb's judgement."},{"english":"Sword (short)","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","strongs":"G3162 (sword ῥομφαία, rhomphaía, G4501)","verses":"v. 4; 13:10, 14","insight":"Strong's: “a knife, i.e. dirk; figuratively, war, judicial punishment.” μάχαιρα, from the sword family, is the soldier's short sword — and the symbol of the state's power to punish (Romans 13:4). The red horse's rider receives ‘a great μάχαιρα’ to take peace from the earth. The beast's wound is from a μάχαιρα (13:14). But Christ's weapon is always the ῥομφαία, the long sword that comes from His mouth (1:16; 19:15): He conquers not by violence but by His word."},{"english":"Altar","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","strongs":"G2379 (souls ψυχή, psychḗ, G5590)","verses":"v. 9; 8:3, 5; 9:13; 14:18; 16:7","insight":"Strong's: “a place of sacrifice, i.e. an altar (special or genitive case, literal or figurative).” θυσιαστήριον, from θυσία (sacrifice), was where offerings were made and where blood was poured out at the base. The martyrs are ‘under the altar’: their deaths are seen as sacrifices offered to God. In chapter 8 the incense of the saints' prayers rises from the golden altar, and fire from it is thrown on the earth (8:3–5). In 16:7 the altar itself speaks: ‘true and righteous are your judgments’. Their prayers are being answered."},{"english":"Master","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","strongs":"G1203 (true ἀληθινός, alēthinós, G228)","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “an absolute ruler (\"despot\").” δεσπότης is the master of a household with full rights over his slaves. The martyrs address God this way: He has absolute authority over all, including their persecutors. Jude and 2 Peter use the same word for Christ, ‘the Master who bought them’ (2 Peter 2:1). To call God δεσπότης is to say: You own everything, You can act — so how long?"},{"english":"Avenge","hebrew":"ἐκδικέω","translit":"ekdikéō","strongs":"G1556 (judge κρίνω, krínō, G2919)","verses":"v. 10; 19:2","insight":"Strong's: “to vindicate, retaliate, punish.” ἐκδικέω, from ἐκ (out of) and δίκη (justice), means to bring justice out — to vindicate the wronged and punish the wrongdoer. The martyrs ask God to do it (6:10); in 19:2 heaven rejoices that He ‘has avenged the blood of his servants’ at Babylon's fall. Believers are told not to avenge themselves (Romans 12:19) precisely because God will; the cry of the martyrs hands justice over to Him."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you that even the darkest events of history are on a leash held by the Lamb.","items":[{"title":"The Lamb Holds the Seals","body":"War, famine, disease and disaster are frightening. But every rider goes out only when “the Lamb opened one of the seven seals” (v. 1), and each is given limited power. The Spirit wants you to face the news of the world without panic: the One who holds the seals loves you."},{"title":"Bring Your ‘How Long?’ to God","body":"The martyrs cry, “How long, Master?” (v. 10), and God does not rebuke them. He gives them white robes and rest. The Spirit invites you to bring your own cry for justice and your own impatience honestly to God — and to receive His comfort while you wait."},{"title":"Who Can Stand?","body":"When the great day of wrath comes, the question will be, “Who is able to stand?” (v. 17). The Spirit wants you to know the answer now: no one stands by their power, wealth or position. You stand only by the blood of the Lamb. Make sure you are sheltered there."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Fear Check","body":"Which of the four horsemen — conquest, war, famine, death — do you most fear? How does knowing that the Lamb opens the seals change that fear (v. 1)?"},{"title":"The Justice Check","body":"Is there a wrong done to you or others that you are trying to avenge yourself? Can you hand it to the holy and true Master (v. 10)?"},{"title":"The Hiding Check","body":"Are you hiding anything from the face of God? Would you be among those running to the caves, or those running to the Lamb (vv. 16–17)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Pray for the Persecuted","body":"This week, find out about one country where Christians are being killed for their faith. Pray daily with the martyrs' words, ‘How long, Master?’, and ask God to strengthen those who suffer and to bring justice and repentance."},{"title":"Release Your Revenge","body":"Write down the name of someone who has wronged you. Pray over it: ‘Master, holy and true, I give this to You. You will judge rightly.’ Then pray a blessing over that person, and do one kind thing for them if you can."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, Lamb of God, You hold the scroll and You open the seals. When I hear of wars and rumours of wars, of famine and plague and death, keep me from fear, because nothing happens outside Your hand. Thank You that every power of evil is given limits by You. Holy and true Master, I join the cry of Your martyrs: how long? Look on Your suffering people across the world, and bring justice in Your time. Thank You for the white robes You give them, and for the rest they have with You. Teach me patience while I wait, and keep me faithful. Lord, when the great day of Your wrath comes and the sky is rolled up like a scroll, I do not want to hide from Your face; I want to run to it. Hide me in Your blood, Lamb of God, so that I may stand. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Zechariah's Horses and Chariots","ref":"Zechariah 1:7-17; 6:1-8","relatedVerses":"v. 1-8","summary":"In a night vision Zechariah saw “a man riding on a red horse, and he stood among the myrtle trees that were in a ravine; and behind him there were red, brown, and white horses” (Zechariah 1:8). Later he saw four chariots with red, black, white and dappled horses, which were “the four winds of the sky, which go out from standing before the Lord of all the earth” (Zechariah 6:5). The horses patrolled the earth on God's behalf. Revelation's four horsemen draw on Zechariah's vision: the forces at work in the world go out from the presence of God and under His command."},{"title":"Abel's Blood Cries Out","ref":"Genesis 4:1-16","relatedVerses":"v. 9-10","summary":"When Cain murdered his brother Abel in the field, God said to him, ‘The voice of your brother’s blood cries to me from the ground.’ The first martyr's blood called for justice, and God heard it. The souls under the altar continue that cry — and Hebrews tells us that the blood of Jesus ‘speaks better than that of Abel’ (Hebrews 12:24): it cries not only for justice but for mercy."},{"title":"The Day of Pentecost","ref":"Acts 2:14-21","relatedVerses":"v. 12-13","summary":"On the day the Holy Spirit came, Peter explained what was happening by quoting the prophet Joel: “The sun will be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and glorious day of the Lord comes” (Acts 2:20). Then he added the promise: “It will be that whoever will call on the name of the Lord will be saved” (Acts 2:21). The signs of the sixth seal are the signs of that great day; but before it comes, the door of salvation is open to everyone who calls on the Lord."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":7,"chapterTitle":"The Sealed Servants and the Great Multitude","themeTitle":"“Salvation Be to Our God, Who Sits on the Throne, and to the Lamb!”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"After this, I saw four angels standing at the four corners of the earth, holding the four winds of the earth, so that no wind would blow on the earth, or on the sea, or on any tree.","understanding":"Μετὰ τοῦτο εἶδον τέσσαρας ἀγγέλους ἑστῶτας ἐπὶ τὰς τέσσαρας γωνίας τῆς γῆς, “After this, I saw four angels standing at the four corners of the earth” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); γῆ, from the world family; ‘the four corners’ is an everyday phrase for the whole earth. κρατοῦντας τοὺς τέσσαρας ἀνέμους τῆς γῆς, “holding the four winds of the earth” — κρατέω, from the hold family: gripping them back. The winds of judgement (like Zechariah's four winds, Zechariah 6:5) are held in check. Chapter 7 is a pause between the sixth and seventh seals that answers the question of 6:17: ‘who is able to stand?’","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G1137","hebrew":"γωνία","translit":"gōnía","english":"corners"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"holding"},{"strongs":"G417","hebrew":"ἄνεμος","translit":"ánemos","english":"winds"},{"strongs":"G4154","hebrew":"πνέω","translit":"pnéō","english":"blow"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G1186","hebrew":"δένδρον","translit":"déndron","english":"tree"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I saw another angel ascend from the sunrise, having the seal of the living God. He cried with a loud voice to the four angels to whom it was given to harm the earth and the sea,","understanding":"καὶ εἶδον ἄλλον ἄγγελον ἀναβαίνοντα ἀπὸ ἀνατολῆς ἡλίου, “I saw another angel ascend from the sunrise” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). ἔχοντα σφραγῖδα θεοῦ ζῶντος, “having the seal of the living God” — σφραγίς, a signet ring; ζάω, from the life family: unlike dead idols, the living God marks His own. καὶ ἔκραξεν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ τοῖς τέσσαρσιν ἀγγέλοις οἷς ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς ἀδικῆσαι τὴν γῆν καὶ τὴν θάλασσαν, “He cried with a loud voice to the four angels to whom it was given to harm the earth and the sea” — ἀδικέω, to harm; γῆ, from the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"ascend"},{"strongs":"G395","hebrew":"ἀνατολή","translit":"anatolḗ","english":"sunrise"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"harm"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"}]},{"v":3,"text":"saying, “Don’t harm the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, until we have sealed the bondservants of our God on their foreheads!”","understanding":"λέγων· Μὴ ἀδικήσητε τὴν γῆν μήτε τὴν θάλασσαν μήτε τὰ δένδρα, “saying, ‘Don’t harm the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees’” — γῆ, from the world family. ἄχρι σφραγίσωμεν τοὺς δούλους τοῦ θεοῦ ἡμῶν ἐπὶ τῶν μετώπων αὐτῶν, “until we have sealed the bondservants of our God on their foreheads!” — σφραγίζω, to stamp with a seal: ownership and protection; δοῦλος, from the servant family. In Ezekiel 9 a mark was put on the foreheads of those who grieved over Jerusalem's sins, and they were spared. Later the beast will counterfeit this with his own mark on the forehead (13:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"harm"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G1186","hebrew":"δένδρον","translit":"déndron","english":"trees"},{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"sealed"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"foreheads"}]},{"v":4,"text":"I heard the number of those who were sealed, one hundred forty-four thousand, sealed out of every tribe of the children of Israel:","understanding":"Καὶ ἤκουσα τὸν ἀριθμὸν τῶν ἐσφραγισμένων, “I heard the number of those who were sealed” — ἀκούω: John hears the number. ἑκατὸν τεσσεράκοντα τέσσαρες χιλιάδες, “one hundred forty-four thousand” — 12 × 12 × 1,000: the twelve tribes multiplied by the twelve apostles (21:12–14) and by a thousand, a vast complete number. ἐσφραγισμένοι ἐκ πάσης φυλῆς υἱῶν Ἰσραήλ, “sealed out of every tribe of the children of Israel” — φυλή, tribe; υἱός, son. Some read the 144,000 literally as Jewish believers; the strongest reading, in light of verse 9, is the whole people of God pictured as the true Israel, numbered and organised like an army ready for battle.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"sealed"},{"strongs":"G1540","hebrew":"ἑκατόν","translit":"hekatón","english":"one hundred"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"},{"strongs":"G5064","hebrew":"τέσσαρες","translit":"téssares","english":"four"},{"strongs":"G5505","hebrew":"χιλιάς","translit":"chiliás","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2474","hebrew":"Ἰσραήλ","translit":"Israḗl","english":"Israel"}]},{"v":5,"text":"of the tribe of Judah were sealed twelve thousand, of the tribe of Reuben twelve thousand, of the tribe of Gad twelve thousand,","understanding":"ἐκ φυλῆς Ἰούδα δώδεκα χιλιάδες ἐσφραγισμένοι, “of the tribe of Judah were sealed twelve thousand” — Judah comes first, not Reuben the firstborn: Judah is the tribe of the Lion (5:5), the Messiah's tribe. ἐκ φυλῆς Ῥουβὴν... ἐκ φυλῆς Γάδ, “of the tribe of Reuben twelve thousand, of the tribe of Gad twelve thousand” — σφραγίζω.","words":[{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G2455","hebrew":"Ἰούδας","translit":"Ioúdas","english":"Judah"},{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"sealed"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G5505","hebrew":"χιλιάς","translit":"chiliás","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G4502","hebrew":"Ῥουβήν","translit":"Rhoubḗn","english":"Reuben"},{"strongs":"G1045","hebrew":"Γάδ","translit":"Gád","english":"Gad"}]},{"v":6,"text":"of the tribe of Asher twelve thousand, of the tribe of Naphtali twelve thousand, of the tribe of Manasseh twelve thousand,","understanding":"ἐκ φυλῆς Ἀσὴρ... ἐκ φυλῆς Νεφθαλὶμ... ἐκ φυλῆς Μανασσῆ, “of the tribe of Asher twelve thousand, of the tribe of Naphtali twelve thousand, of the tribe of Manasseh twelve thousand” — Manasseh, one of Joseph's two sons, is listed here as well as ‘Joseph’ in verse 8; Dan is missing from the list.","words":[{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G768","hebrew":"Ἀσήρ","translit":"Asḗr","english":"Asher"},{"strongs":"G3508","hebrew":"Νεφθαλείμ","translit":"Nephthaleím","english":"Naphtali"},{"strongs":"G3128","hebrew":"Μανασσῆς","translit":"Manassēs","english":"Manasseh"}]},{"v":7,"text":"of the tribe of Simeon twelve thousand, of the tribe of Levi twelve thousand, of the tribe of Issachar twelve thousand,","understanding":"ἐκ φυλῆς Συμεὼν... ἐκ φυλῆς Λευὶ... ἐκ φυλῆς Ἰσσαχάρ, “of the tribe of Simeon twelve thousand, of the tribe of Levi twelve thousand, of the tribe of Issachar twelve thousand” — Levi, the priestly tribe that received no land, is included: all God's people are now priests (1:6).","words":[{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G4826","hebrew":"Συμεών","translit":"Symeṓn","english":"Simeon"},{"strongs":"G3017","hebrew":"Λευΐ","translit":"Leuḯ","english":"Levi"},{"strongs":"G2466","hebrew":"Ἰσαχάρ","translit":"Isachár","english":"Issachar"}]},{"v":8,"text":"of the tribe of Zebulun twelve thousand, of the tribe of Joseph twelve thousand, of the tribe of Benjamin were sealed twelve thousand.","understanding":"ἐκ φυλῆς Ζαβουλὼν... ἐκ φυλῆς Ἰωσὴφ... ἐκ φυλῆς Βενιαμὶν ἐσφραγισμένοι, “of the tribe of Zebulun twelve thousand, of the tribe of Joseph twelve thousand, of the tribe of Benjamin were sealed twelve thousand” — σφραγίζω. The list is unique in the Bible: no Old Testament list of the tribes is exactly like it. Why Dan is left out is uncertain; early Christians linked Dan with idolatry (Judges 18), and some with the coming of the antichrist. The order and changes show this is a symbolic, not a census, list.","words":[{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G2194","hebrew":"Ζαβουλών","translit":"Zaboulṓn","english":"Zebulun"},{"strongs":"G2501","hebrew":"Ἰωσήφ","translit":"Iōsḗph","english":"Joseph"},{"strongs":"G958","hebrew":"Βενιαμίν","translit":"Beniamín","english":"Benjamin"},{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"sealed"}]},{"v":9,"text":"After these things I looked, and behold, a great multitude, which no man could count, out of every nation and of all tribes, peoples, and languages, standing before the throne and before the Lamb, dressed in white robes, with palm branches in their hands.","understanding":"Μετὰ ταῦτα εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ ὄχλος πολύς, “After these things I looked, and behold, a great multitude” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). As in chapter 5, John hears one thing and sees another: he heard a Lion and saw a Lamb; he heard 144,000 from Israel and sees a countless crowd from all nations. ὃν ἀριθμῆσαι αὐτὸν οὐδεὶς ἐδύνατο, “which no man could count” — God's promise to Abraham of descendants like the stars. ἐκ παντὸς ἔθνους καὶ φυλῶν καὶ λαῶν καὶ γλωσσῶν, “out of every nation and of all tribes, peoples, and languages” — the people bought by the Lamb (5:9). ἑστῶτας ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου καὶ ἐνώπιον τοῦ ἀρνίου, “standing before the throne and before the Lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: ‘who is able to stand?’ (6:17) — these are. περιβεβλημένους στολὰς λευκάς, καὶ φοίνικες ἐν ταῖς χερσὶν αὐτῶν, “dressed in white robes, with palm branches in their hands” — φοῖνιξ, palm branch: the symbol of victory and of the Feast of Tabernacles.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"looked"},{"strongs":"G3793","hebrew":"ὄχλος","translit":"óchlos","english":"multitude"},{"strongs":"G4183","hebrew":"πολύς","translit":"polýs","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G705","hebrew":"ἀριθμέω","translit":"arithméō","english":"count"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nation"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribes"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"peoples"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G4749","hebrew":"στολή","translit":"stolḗ","english":"robes"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G5404","hebrew":"φοῖνιξ","translit":"phoînix","english":"palm branches"}]},{"v":10,"text":"They cried with a loud voice, saying, “Salvation be to our God, who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb!”","understanding":"καὶ κράζουσιν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ λέγοντες, “They cried with a loud voice, saying” — κράζω, present tense: they keep crying. Ἡ σωτηρία τῷ θεῷ ἡμῶν τῷ καθημένῳ ἐπὶ τῷ θρόνῳ καὶ τῷ ἀρνίῳ, “Salvation be to our God, who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb!” — σωτηρία, salvation, rescue, deliverance; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. Salvation belongs to God and the Lamb: they did not save themselves.","words":[{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"Salvation"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":11,"text":"All the angels were standing around the throne, the elders, and the four living creatures; and they fell on their faces before his throne, and worshiped God,","understanding":"καὶ πάντες οἱ ἄγγελοι εἱστήκεισαν κύκλῳ τοῦ θρόνου, “All the angels were standing around the throne” — the circles of chapter 5 again. καὶ ἔπεσαν ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου ἐπὶ τὰ πρόσωπα αὐτῶν καὶ προσεκύνησαν τῷ θεῷ, “and they fell on their faces before his throne, and worshiped God” — προσκυνέω. The angels, who have never needed rescue, worship God for the salvation of sinners.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"faces"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":12,"text":"saying, “Amen! Blessing, glory, wisdom, thanksgiving, honor, power, and might, be to our God forever and ever! Amen.”","understanding":"λέγοντες· Ἀμήν, ἡ εὐλογία καὶ ἡ δόξα καὶ ἡ σοφία καὶ ἡ εὐχαριστία καὶ ἡ τιμὴ καὶ ἡ δύναμις καὶ ἡ ἰσχὺς τῷ θεῷ ἡμῶν, “Amen! Blessing, glory, wisdom, thanksgiving, honor, power, and might, be to our God” — seven words, each with its own article. σοφία, from the wisdom family; εὐχαριστία, from the pray family: thanksgiving; δύναμις and ἰσχύς, both from the power family — ability and forceful strength, as in 5:12. εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων· ἀμήν, “forever and ever! Amen” — αἰών, from the time family and the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"Blessing"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G2169","hebrew":"εὐχαριστία","translit":"eucharistía","english":"thanksgiving"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2479","hebrew":"ἰσχύς","translit":"ischýs","english":"might"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":13,"text":"One of the elders answered, saying to me, “These who are arrayed in the white robes, who are they, and from where did they come?”","understanding":"Καὶ ἀπεκρίθη εἷς ἐκ τῶν πρεσβυτέρων λέγων μοι, “One of the elders answered, saying to me” — a teacher's question. Οὗτοι οἱ περιβεβλημένοι τὰς στολὰς τὰς λευκὰς τίνες εἰσὶν καὶ πόθεν ἦλθον; “These who are arrayed in the white robes, who are they, and from where did they come?” — στολή, the long robe of honour.","words":[{"strongs":"G611","hebrew":"ἀποκρίνομαι","translit":"apokrínomai","english":"answered"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"arrayed"},{"strongs":"G4749","hebrew":"στολή","translit":"stolḗ","english":"robes"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"}]},{"v":14,"text":"I told him, “My lord, you know.” He said to me, “These are those who came out of the great tribulation. They washed their robes, and made them white in the Lamb’s blood.","understanding":"καὶ εἴρηκα αὐτῷ· Κύριέ μου, σὺ οἶδας, “I told him, ‘My lord, you know’” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family: a humble confession, as in Ezekiel's ‘Lord GOD, you know.’ Οὗτοί εἰσιν οἱ ἐρχόμενοι ἐκ τῆς θλίψεως τῆς μεγάλης, “These are those who came out of the great tribulation” — θλῖψις, crushing pressure: present tense, ‘those who are coming out’. Some take this as a final period of distress at the end; others as the whole of the church's suffering between Christ's first and second comings (1:9). καὶ ἔπλυναν τὰς στολὰς αὐτῶν καὶ ἐλεύκαναν αὐτὰς ἐν τῷ αἵματι τοῦ ἀρνίου, “They washed their robes, and made them white in the Lamb’s blood” — πλύνω, to launder; λευκαίνω, to whiten; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The great paradox: blood makes them white.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"lord"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G2347","hebrew":"θλῖψις","translit":"thlîpsis","english":"tribulation"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4150","hebrew":"πλύνω","translit":"plýnō","english":"washed"},{"strongs":"G4749","hebrew":"στολή","translit":"stolḗ","english":"robes"},{"strongs":"G3021","hebrew":"λευκαίνω","translit":"leukaínō","english":"made them white"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb’s"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Therefore they are before the throne of God, they serve him day and night in his temple. He who sits on the throne will spread his tabernacle over them.","understanding":"διὰ τοῦτό εἰσιν ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου τοῦ θεοῦ, “Therefore they are before the throne of God” — because they are washed, they may stand in His presence. καὶ λατρεύουσιν αὐτῷ ἡμέρας καὶ νυκτὸς ἐν τῷ ναῷ αὐτοῦ, “they serve him day and night in his temple” — λατρεύω, from the servant family: to serve as a worshipper, priestly service, as distinct from δουλεύω, a slave's service; ἡμέρα, from the time family. καὶ ὁ καθήμενος ἐπὶ τοῦ θρόνου σκηνώσει ἐπ᾽ αὐτούς, “He who sits on the throne will spread his tabernacle over them” — σκηνόω, to pitch a tent: God's presence over them like the cloud over the tabernacle, and like the Word who ‘lived among us’ (John 1:14), literally ‘tabernacled’.","words":[{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G4637","hebrew":"σκηνόω","translit":"skēnóō","english":"spread his tabernacle"}]},{"v":16,"text":"They will never be hungry, neither thirsty any more; neither will the sun beat on them, nor any heat;","understanding":"οὐ πεινάσουσιν ἔτι οὐδὲ διψήσουσιν ἔτι, “They will never be hungry, neither thirsty any more” — πεινάω, διψάω: from Isaiah 49, the promise to Israel returning from exile. οὐδὲ μὴ πέσῃ ἐπ᾽ αὐτοὺς ὁ ἥλιος οὐδὲ πᾶν καῦμα, “neither will the sun beat on them, nor any heat” — καῦμα, scorching heat. The trials of the wilderness are over: no hunger, no thirst, no burning sun.","words":[{"strongs":"G3983","hebrew":"πεινάω","translit":"peináō","english":"hungry"},{"strongs":"G1372","hebrew":"διψάω","translit":"dipsáō","english":"thirsty"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"beat"},{"strongs":"G2738","hebrew":"καῦμα","translit":"kaûma","english":"heat"}]},{"v":17,"text":"for the Lamb who is in the middle of the throne shepherds them, and leads them to springs of life-giving waters. And God will wipe away every tear from their eyes.”","understanding":"ὅτι τὸ ἀρνίον τὸ ἀνὰ μέσον τοῦ θρόνου ποιμανεῖ αὐτούς, “for the Lamb who is in the middle of the throne shepherds them” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family; ποιμαίνω, to shepherd: the Lamb becomes the Shepherd (Psalm 23; John 10:11). καὶ ὁδηγήσει αὐτοὺς ἐπὶ ζωῆς πηγὰς ὑδάτων, “and leads them to springs of life-giving waters” — ζωή, from the life family; πηγή, a spring. καὶ ἐξαλείψει ὁ θεὸς πᾶν δάκρυον ἐκ τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν αὐτῶν, “And God will wipe away every tear from their eyes” — ἐξαλείφω, to wipe away completely (the word used of blotting out names in 3:5): Isaiah's promise of the end of death (Isaiah 25:8), repeated in 21:4.","words":[{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G3319","hebrew":"μέσος","translit":"mésos","english":"middle"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G4165","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","english":"shepherds"},{"strongs":"G3594","hebrew":"ὁδηγέω","translit":"hodēgéō","english":"leads"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life-giving"},{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"springs"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1813","hebrew":"ἐξαλείφω","translit":"exaleíphō","english":"wipe away"},{"strongs":"G1144","hebrew":"δάκρυ","translit":"dákry","english":"tear"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Winds Held Back","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture four mighty angels at the four corners of the earth, straining to hold back four howling winds of destruction. Not a leaf stirs; the sea is still. Then another angel rises from the sunrise with a signet ring, crying, ‘Wait! Not yet!’","meaning":"“Don’t harm the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, until we have sealed the bondservants of our God on their foreheads!” (v. 3). Before judgement falls, God marks and protects His own. The storm waits until His people are safe."},{"title":"Heard and Seen","verseRef":"v. 4, 9","image":"Picture John hearing a roll-call — twelve tribes, twelve thousand each, ordered like an army. Then he turns to look, and sees not neat ranks but an ocean of faces from every continent, every skin colour, every language, stretching beyond counting.","meaning":"“I heard the number of those who were sealed, one hundred forty-four thousand” (v. 4). “After these things I looked, and behold, a great multitude, which no man could count, out of every nation and of all tribes, peoples, and languages” (v. 9). God's people are both precisely known and beyond counting."},{"title":"Washed White in Blood","verseRef":"v. 13-14","image":"Picture garments stained and torn from a long and terrible journey. They are dipped into a crimson stream — and lifted out dazzling white.","meaning":"“They washed their robes, and made them white in the Lamb’s blood” (v. 14). No one in that crowd is there because of their own goodness. Every white robe was washed in the same blood."},{"title":"The Lamb Who Shepherds","verseRef":"v. 15-17","image":"Picture weary travellers out of a scorching desert, sunburned, hungry, thirsty and weeping. A Lamb walks ahead of them like a shepherd, leading them to cool, clear springs; and God Himself kneels and wipes the tears from each face.","meaning":"“For the Lamb who is in the middle of the throne shepherds them, and leads them to springs of life-giving waters. And God will wipe away every tear from their eyes” (v. 17). Every tear you shed in faithfulness will be wiped away by God's own hand."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Winds Held Back (v. 1-3)","body":"Four angels hold back the four winds of the earth; another angel with the seal of the living God tells them not to harm earth, sea or trees until God's servants are sealed on their foreheads."},{"label":"The 144,000 Sealed (v. 4-8)","body":"John hears the number of the sealed: 144,000, twelve thousand from each of twelve tribes of Israel, beginning with Judah."},{"label":"The Great Multitude (v. 9-12)","body":"John looks and sees a countless multitude from every nation, tribe, people and language, standing before the throne and the Lamb in white robes with palm branches, crying ‘Salvation belongs to our God and to the Lamb’; all the angels fall and worship with a sevenfold doxology."},{"label":"Who They Are (v. 13-17)","body":"An elder explains: they have come out of the great tribulation and washed their robes white in the blood of the Lamb; they serve God day and night in His temple; He will shelter them; they will never hunger, thirst or be scorched; the Lamb will shepherd them to springs of living water, and God will wipe away every tear."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Who Can Stand? An Interlude","paragraphs":["Chapter 6 ended with the question, “who is able to stand?” (6:17). Before the seventh seal is opened, chapter 7 answers it. There is a pause in the judgements: four angels hold back the winds of the earth “so that no wind would blow on the earth, or on the sea, or on any tree” (v. 1), while another angel cries, “Don’t harm the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, until we have sealed the bondservants of our God on their foreheads!” (v. 3).","The seal marks God's people as His property and under His protection. It does not mean they will escape all suffering — the multitude of verse 9 has come ‘out of the great tribulation’ — but it does mean that God's wrath will not fall on them and that nothing can separate them from Him. Paul says believers are sealed with the Holy Spirit: “in whom, having also believed, you were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit” (Ephesians 1:13). The seal of God stands against the mark of the beast (13:16–17): everyone bears one or the other.","Revelation pauses like this before the seventh seal, before the seventh trumpet (chs. 10–11), and before the final bowls (ch. 15). Each time, before the last judgement in a series, John is shown the safety and victory of God's people."]},{"heading":"The 144,000 and the Great Multitude","paragraphs":["John hears the number of the sealed: 144,000, “sealed out of every tribe of the children of Israel” (v. 4), twelve thousand from each tribe. Then he looks and sees “a great multitude, which no man could count, out of every nation and of all tribes, peoples, and languages” (v. 9). Are these two different groups or the same group seen in two ways?","Some Christians read the 144,000 as a literal number of Jewish believers, protected through a final tribulation, distinct from the multitude of Gentile believers. Others see them as Jewish Christians, or as martyrs. But there are strong reasons to see them as the same people. In chapter 5 John heard of a Lion and turned to see a Lamb (5:5–6): what he hears is explained by what he sees. Here he hears an ordered, numbered Israel and sees a countless, international crowd. The list of tribes is irregular — Judah first, Dan missing, Levi included, Joseph and Manasseh both named — which suggests a symbolic rather than ethnic roll-call. And the New Testament teaches that all who belong to Christ are Abraham's true children: “If you are Christ’s, then you are Abraham’s offspring and heirs according to promise” (Galatians 3:29).","The strongest reading, then, is that the 144,000 is the whole people of God pictured as the true Israel, complete in number (12 × 12 × 1,000) and ready like an army for battle — and that the great multitude is the same people seen as they really are: countless, from every nation, victorious. Faithful Christians differ on this, but all agree on the main point: God knows and keeps every one of His own."]},{"heading":"Washed in the Blood of the Lamb","paragraphs":["The multitude stands — the very thing no one could do in 6:17 — “before the throne and before the Lamb, dressed in white robes, with palm branches in their hands” (v. 9). Palm branches were waved at the Feast of Tabernacles and at victory celebrations, and the crowd waved them when Jesus entered Jerusalem. Their song gives all credit to God: “Salvation be to our God, who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb!” (v. 10). The angels join with their own sevenfold praise (v. 12).","One of the elders asks who they are, and John wisely answers, “My lord, you know” (v. 14). The elder explains: “These are those who came out of the great tribulation. They washed their robes, and made them white in the Lamb’s blood” (v. 14). Some take ‘the great tribulation’ as a specific final period of unequalled distress, as Jesus described (Matthew 24:21); others take it as the whole suffering of the church in this age (1:9). Either way, these believers have come through it — and not one of them is there because of their own righteousness. Their robes are white because they have been washed in the blood of the Lamb.","It is the great paradox of the gospel: blood, which stains, makes them white. The death of the Lamb is the cleansing of His people."]},{"heading":"The Shepherd Lamb","paragraphs":["The elder then describes their future, in words drawn from the prophets: “Therefore they are before the throne of God, they serve him day and night in his temple. He who sits on the throne will spread his tabernacle over them” (v. 15). They serve as priests (λατρεύω, worship-service), and God shelters them as the cloud covered the tabernacle in the wilderness.","“They will never be hungry, neither thirsty any more; neither will the sun beat on them, nor any heat” (v. 16). The hardships of their earthly pilgrimage — hunger, thirst, scorching trials — are over for good.","And then the most tender words of the chapter: “for the Lamb who is in the middle of the throne shepherds them, and leads them to springs of life-giving waters. And God will wipe away every tear from their eyes” (v. 17). The Lamb becomes the Shepherd. The One who was slain for the sheep now leads them, as in Psalm 23, to still waters. And God Himself wipes their tears away. This promise will be repeated almost word for word in the vision of the new creation (21:4)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Good Shepherd and the Living Water","paragraphs":["Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep” (John 10:11), and “My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me. I give eternal life to them. They will never perish, and no one will snatch them out of my hand” (John 10:27–28). Revelation 7 shows the Good Shepherd, now on the throne, still leading His flock.","He promised the Samaritan woman, “but whoever drinks of the water that I will give him will never thirst again; but the water that I will give him will become in him a well of water springing up to eternal life” (John 4:14). The ‘springs of life-giving waters’ of verse 17 are that promise fulfilled.","The sealing of God's servants echoes Paul: “However God’s firm foundation stands, having this seal, ‘The Lord knows those who are his’” (2 Timothy 2:19). And God's ‘tabernacling’ over His people (v. 15) completes what began when “The Word became flesh, and lived among us. We saw his glory” (John 1:14)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Seal","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","strongs":"G4972 (foreheads μέτωπον, métōpon, G3359)","verses":"v. 3-8; 9:4","insight":"Strong's: “to stamp (with a signet or private mark) for security or preservation (literally or figuratively); by implication, to keep secret, to attest.” σφραγίζω marks something as owned, protected and genuine. God's servants are sealed on their foreheads before judgement falls; in 9:4 the locusts may harm only those ‘who don’t have God’s seal on their foreheads’. The seal does not keep believers from all suffering, but it keeps them for God. The beast's counterfeit mark (13:16) shows that every person bears someone's seal."},{"english":"Washed","hebrew":"πλύνω","translit":"plýnō","strongs":"G4150 (made them white λευκαίνω, leukaínō, G3021)","verses":"v. 14; 22:14","insight":"Strong's: “to \"plunge\", i.e. launder clothing.” πλύνω is the ordinary word for washing clothes. The multitude ‘washed their robes, and made them white in the Lamb’s blood’ — the robes are cleansed by the very thing that should stain them. In 22:14 the final blessing of the book is on ‘those who wash their robes’ (in many manuscripts). The washing is something they did — they came to the Lamb — but its power is entirely His blood."},{"english":"Serve (worship)","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","strongs":"G3000 (temple ναός, naós, G3485)","verses":"v. 15; 22:3","insight":"Strong's: “to minister (to God), i.e. render religious homage.” λατρεύω, from the servant family, is the service of worship — the work of priests in the temple. It differs from δουλεύω, the service of a slave, and διακονέω, practical service at table. The multitude ‘serve him day and night in his temple’ (v. 15); in the new Jerusalem ‘his servants will serve him’ (22:3), using this word. Eternity is not idleness but joyful, priestly worship."},{"english":"Spread His tabernacle","hebrew":"σκηνόω","translit":"skēnóō","strongs":"G4637 (throne θρόνος, thrónos, G2362)","verses":"v. 15; 12:12; 13:6; 21:3","insight":"Strong's: “to tent or encamp, i.e. (figuratively) to occupy (as a mansion) or (specially), to reside (as God did in the Tabernacle of old, a symbol of protection and communion).” σκηνόω, from σκηνή (tent), is the word John used when ‘the Word became flesh, and lived among us’ (John 1:14) — literally ‘pitched His tent’. Here God spreads His tent over the redeemed for protection; in 21:3 the climax of the book is that ‘God’s dwelling (σκηνή) is with people, and he will dwell (σκηνόω) with them.’"},{"english":"Shepherd","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","strongs":"G4165 (Lamb ἀρνίον, arníon, G721)","verses":"v. 17; 2:27; 12:5; 19:15","insight":"Strong's: “to tend as a shepherd of (figuratively, superviser).” ποιμαίνω means both to feed and protect a flock and to rule. In 2:27, 12:5 and 19:15 Christ ‘rules’ (ποιμαίνω) the nations with a rod of iron — the shepherd's staff turned against wolves. Here the same verb describes His tender care: the Lamb shepherds His people to springs of living water. The Lamb who was led to the slaughter now leads the flock."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit answers the most frightening question of chapter 6 — ‘who is able to stand?’ — with a picture of safety and joy.","items":[{"title":"You Are Sealed","body":"Before the winds of judgement blow, God seals His servants on their foreheads (v. 3). If you belong to Christ, you are marked by His Spirit as His own. The Spirit wants you to live with that security: you are known, owned and kept by the living God."},{"title":"You Belong to a Countless Family","body":"John sees “a great multitude, which no man could count, out of every nation and of all tribes, peoples, and languages” (v. 9). Your faith is not small or lonely. The Spirit invites you to see yourself as part of that vast crowd, and to love believers of every nation and culture as your family."},{"title":"Every Tear Counted, Every Tear Wiped","body":"“And God will wipe away every tear from their eyes” (v. 17). The Spirit brings this promise to whatever grief you carry today. Your tears are not forgotten. The Shepherd Lamb is leading you toward springs of living water, and God Himself will wipe your face."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Seal Check","body":"Whose mark is on your life — in what you think about (forehead) and what you do? Does your life show that you belong to God (v. 3)?"},{"title":"The Robe Check","body":"Are you trusting in your own goodness, or in the blood of the Lamb, to stand before God (v. 14)?"},{"title":"The Nations Check","body":"Do you pray for, welcome and learn from believers of other nations, tribes and languages (v. 9)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Worship with the Multitude","body":"This week, listen to or sing a worship song in a language other than your own, or attend a service of believers from another culture. As you worship, picture the great multitude of verse 9, and cry with them: ‘Salvation belongs to our God and to the Lamb!’"},{"title":"Let the Shepherd Lead","body":"Read Psalm 23 alongside Revelation 7:15–17 each morning this week. Each day, name one ‘desert’ in your life — a hunger, a thirst, a scorching trial — and ask the Shepherd Lamb to lead you to living water in it."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Living God, thank You that before the winds of judgement blow, You seal Your servants as Your own. Thank You that Your Spirit has marked me, and that I am known and kept by You. I praise You for the great multitude no one can count, from every nation, tribe, people and language, and I thank You that I am part of it. Salvation belongs to You, our God who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb! I did not save myself. My robe is white only because it has been washed in the blood of the Lamb. Blessing and glory and wisdom and thanksgiving and honour and power and might be to You forever and ever. Lord Jesus, Lamb at the centre of the throne, be my Shepherd. Lead me to springs of living water. Shelter me with Your presence. And on the day when You wipe every tear from my eyes, let me see Your face. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Mark on the Foreheads","ref":"Ezekiel 9:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 2-3","summary":"In a vision before Jerusalem's fall, Ezekiel saw six executioners come to the temple, and with them a man clothed in linen carrying a writer's inkhorn. God told the man to go through the city and put a mark on the foreheads of those who sighed and groaned over all the abominations done in it. Then the executioners were sent out, with the command not to touch anyone who had the mark. Revelation 7 echoes this scene: before judgement falls, God marks those who are His."},{"title":"The Triumphal Entry","ref":"John 12:12-19","relatedVerses":"v. 9-10","summary":"When Jesus rode into Jerusalem before the Passover, a great crowd “took the branches of the palm trees, and went out to meet him, and cried out, ‘Hosanna! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, the King of Israel!’” (John 12:13). Within a week that crowd's King was crucified. In Revelation 7 a far greater crowd, from every nation, waves palm branches before the throne — not on the way to the cross, but on the far side of it, celebrating the salvation the Lamb won there."},{"title":"The Shepherd's Psalm","ref":"Psalm 23","relatedVerses":"v. 16-17","summary":"David, once a shepherd boy, wrote of the LORD as his Shepherd: He makes him lie down in green pastures, leads him beside still waters, restores his soul, walks with him through the valley of the shadow of death, prepares a table before him, and brings him to dwell in the house of the LORD forever. In Revelation 7 the Lamb Himself becomes that Shepherd, leading His flock — now safely through the valley — to springs of living water in the house of God."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":8,"chapterTitle":"The Seventh Seal and the First Four Trumpets","themeTitle":"“The Smoke of the Incense, with the Prayers of the Saints, Went Up Before God”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"When he opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven for about half an hour.","understanding":"Καὶ ὅταν ἤνοιξεν τὴν σφραγῖδα τὴν ἑβδόμην, “When he opened the seventh seal” — σφραγίς; the Lamb opens the last seal. ἐγένετο σιγὴ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ ὡς ἡμιώριον, “there was silence in heaven for about half an hour” — σιγή, silence; ἡμιώριον, half an hour. After the endless songs of chapters 4–7, heaven falls silent: awe before judgement, as the prophets said, “Be silent at the presence of the Lord Yahweh, for the day of Yahweh is at hand” (Zephaniah 1:7) — and so that the prayers of the saints may be heard (vv. 3–4).","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G1442","hebrew":"ἕβδομος","translit":"hébdomos","english":"seventh"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G4602","hebrew":"σιγή","translit":"sigḗ","english":"silence"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2256","hebrew":"ἡμιώριον","translit":"hēmiṓrion","english":"half an hour"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I saw the seven angels who stand before God, and seven trumpets were given to them.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον τοὺς ἑπτὰ ἀγγέλους οἳ ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ ἑστήκασιν, “I saw the seven angels who stand before God” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). Jewish writings spoke of seven archangels who stand in God's presence (Gabriel says, ‘I am Gabriel, who stands in the presence of God’, Luke 1:19). καὶ ἐδόθησαν αὐτοῖς ἑπτὰ σάλπιγγες, “and seven trumpets were given to them” — σάλπιγξ: trumpets announced war, called assemblies, and heralded the coming of God at Sinai and of the King.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stand"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpets"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Another angel came and stood over the altar, having a golden censer. Much incense was given to him, that he should add it to the prayers of all the saints on the golden altar which was before the throne.","understanding":"Καὶ ἄλλος ἄγγελος ἦλθεν καὶ ἐστάθη ἐπὶ τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου, “Another angel came and stood over the altar” — θυσιαστήριον: the same altar under which the martyrs cried ‘How long?’ (6:9–10). ἔχων λιβανωτὸν χρυσοῦν, “having a golden censer” — λιβανωτός, a censer for burning frankincense. καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ θυμιάματα πολλὰ ἵνα δώσει ταῖς προσευχαῖς τῶν ἁγίων πάντων, “Much incense was given to him, that he should add it to the prayers of all the saints” — προσευχή, from the pray family. ἐπὶ τὸ θυσιαστήριον τὸ χρυσοῦν τὸ ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου, “on the golden altar which was before the throne” — the altar of incense, which stood before the veil in the tabernacle. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stood"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G3031","hebrew":"λιβανωτός","translit":"libanōtós","english":"censer"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G2368","hebrew":"θυμίαμα","translit":"thymíama","english":"incense"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayers"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"}]},{"v":4,"text":"The smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, went up before God out of the angel’s hand.","understanding":"καὶ ἀνέβη ὁ καπνὸς τῶν θυμιαμάτων ταῖς προσευχαῖς τῶν ἁγίων, “The smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, went up” — καπνός, smoke; προσευχή, from the pray family. ἐκ χειρὸς τοῦ ἀγγέλου ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ, “before God out of the angel’s hand” — the prayers of weak, persecuted believers rise into the very presence of God, made fragrant. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G2368","hebrew":"θυμίαμα","translit":"thymíama","english":"incense"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayers"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"went up"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"}]},{"v":5,"text":"The angel took the censer, and he filled it with the fire of the altar, and threw it on the earth. Thunders, sounds, lightnings, and an earthquake followed.","understanding":"καὶ εἴληφεν ὁ ἄγγελος τὸν λιβανωτόν, καὶ ἐγέμισεν αὐτὸν ἐκ τοῦ πυρὸς τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου, “The angel took the censer, and he filled it with the fire of the altar” — λιβανωτός. καὶ ἔβαλεν εἰς τὴν γῆν, “and threw it on the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. The same censer that carried the prayers up now carries fire down: the judgements that follow are God's answer to the prayers of His people (6:10). καὶ ἐγένοντο βρονταὶ καὶ φωναὶ καὶ ἀστραπαὶ καὶ σεισμός, “Thunders, sounds, lightnings, and an earthquake followed” — the storm of the throne (4:5), now with an earthquake added.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"took"},{"strongs":"G3031","hebrew":"λιβανωτός","translit":"libanōtós","english":"censer"},{"strongs":"G1072","hebrew":"γεμίζω","translit":"gemízō","english":"filled"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"threw"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"Thunders"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sounds"},{"strongs":"G796","hebrew":"ἀστραπή","translit":"astrapḗ","english":"lightnings"},{"strongs":"G4578","hebrew":"σεισμός","translit":"seismós","english":"earthquake"}]},{"v":6,"text":"The seven angels who had the seven trumpets prepared themselves to sound.","understanding":"Καὶ οἱ ἑπτὰ ἄγγελοι οἱ ἔχοντες τὰς ἑπτὰ σάλπιγγας ἡτοίμασαν αὑτοὺς ἵνα σαλπίσωσιν, “The seven angels who had the seven trumpets prepared themselves to sound” — σάλπιγξ, σαλπίζω. Like the trumpets that brought down the walls of Jericho, these announce the downfall of a world in rebellion.","words":[{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpets"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sound"}]},{"v":7,"text":"The first sounded, and there followed hail and fire, mixed with blood, and they were thrown to the earth. One third of the earth was burned up, and one third of the trees were burned up, and all green grass was burned up.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ πρῶτος ἐσάλπισεν, “The first sounded” — σαλπίζω. καὶ ἐγένετο χάλαζα καὶ πῦρ μεμιγμένα ἐν αἵματι, “and there followed hail and fire, mixed with blood” — the seventh plague of Egypt: hail and fire together. καὶ ἐβλήθη εἰς τὴν γῆν, “and they were thrown to the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. καὶ τὸ τρίτον τῆς γῆς κατεκάη, “One third of the earth was burned up” — τρίτος: the seals touched a quarter (6:8); the trumpets touch a third. The judgements grow, but they are still partial: a warning, not yet the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G5464","hebrew":"χάλαζα","translit":"chálaza","english":"hail"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G2618","hebrew":"κατακαίω","translit":"katakaíō","english":"burned up"},{"strongs":"G1186","hebrew":"δένδρον","translit":"déndron","english":"trees"},{"strongs":"G5528","hebrew":"χόρτος","translit":"chórtos","english":"grass"},{"strongs":"G5515","hebrew":"χλωρός","translit":"chlōrós","english":"green"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The second angel sounded, and something like a great burning mountain was thrown into the sea. One third of the sea became blood,","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ δεύτερος ἄγγελος ἐσάλπισεν, “The second angel sounded” — σαλπίζω. καὶ ὡς ὄρος μέγα πυρὶ καιόμενον ἐβλήθη εἰς τὴν θάλασσαν, “and something like a great burning mountain was thrown into the sea” — ὡς, ‘something like’: John describes what it resembled. Jeremiah called Babylon a ‘burnt mountain’. καὶ ἐγένετο τὸ τρίτον τῆς θαλάσσης αἷμα, “One third of the sea became blood” — the first plague of Egypt, the Nile turned to blood.","words":[{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2545","hebrew":"καίω","translit":"kaíō","english":"burning"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"}]},{"v":9,"text":"and one third of the living creatures which were in the sea died. One third of the ships were destroyed.","understanding":"καὶ ἀπέθανεν τὸ τρίτον τῶν κτισμάτων τῶν ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ, “and one third of the living creatures which were in the sea died” — κτίσμα, a created thing. καὶ τὸ τρίτον τῶν πλοίων διεφθάρησαν, “One third of the ships were destroyed” — πλοῖον; διαφθείρω, to ruin completely. Rome's wealth depended on its sea trade (18:17–19); the judgement strikes the empire's lifeline.","words":[{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G2938","hebrew":"κτίσμα","translit":"ktísma","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G4143","hebrew":"πλοῖον","translit":"ploîon","english":"ships"},{"strongs":"G1311","hebrew":"διαφθείρω","translit":"diaphtheírō","english":"destroyed"}]},{"v":10,"text":"The third angel sounded, and a great star fell from the sky, burning like a torch, and it fell on one third of the rivers, and on the springs of the waters.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ τρίτος ἄγγελος ἐσάλπισεν, “The third angel sounded” — σαλπίζω. καὶ ἔπεσεν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἀστὴρ μέγας καιόμενος ὡς λαμπάς, “and a great star fell from the sky, burning like a torch” — ἀστήρ, λαμπάς. Isaiah spoke of the king of Babylon falling from heaven like the morning star. καὶ ἔπεσεν ἐπὶ τὸ τρίτον τῶν ποταμῶν καὶ ἐπὶ τὰς πηγὰς τῶν ὑδάτων, “and it fell on one third of the rivers, and on the springs of the waters” — fresh water, the source of life, is poisoned.","words":[{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"star"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G2545","hebrew":"καίω","translit":"kaíō","english":"burning"},{"strongs":"G2985","hebrew":"λαμπάς","translit":"lampás","english":"torch"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"rivers"},{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"springs"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"}]},{"v":11,"text":"The name of the star is called “Wormwood.” One third of the waters became wormwood. Many people died from the waters, because they were made bitter.","understanding":"καὶ τὸ ὄνομα τοῦ ἀστέρος λέγεται ὁ Ἄψινθος, “The name of the star is called ‘Wormwood’” — ἄψινθος, a bitter plant; in the prophets God gives His unfaithful people wormwood to drink (Jeremiah 9:15). καὶ ἐγένετο τὸ τρίτον τῶν ὑδάτων εἰς ἄψινθον, “One third of the waters became wormwood” — the reverse of Marah, where Moses threw a tree into bitter water and it became sweet. καὶ πολλοὶ τῶν ἀνθρώπων ἀπέθανον ἐκ τῶν ὑδάτων ὅτι ἐπικράνθησαν, “Many people died from the waters, because they were made bitter” — πικραίνω.","words":[{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"star"},{"strongs":"G894","hebrew":"ἄψινθος","translit":"ápsinthos","english":"Wormwood"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"},{"strongs":"G4087","hebrew":"πικραίνω","translit":"pikraínō","english":"made bitter"}]},{"v":12,"text":"The fourth angel sounded, and one third of the sun was struck, and one third of the moon, and one third of the stars; so that one third of them would be darkened, and the day wouldn’t shine for one third of it, and the night in the same way.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ τέταρτος ἄγγελος ἐσάλπισεν, “The fourth angel sounded” — σαλπίζω. καὶ ἐπλήγη τὸ τρίτον τοῦ ἡλίου καὶ τὸ τρίτον τῆς σελήνης καὶ τὸ τρίτον τῶν ἀστέρων, “and one third of the sun was struck, and one third of the moon, and one third of the stars” — πλήσσω, to strike: the root of πληγή, ‘plague’. ἵνα σκοτισθῇ τὸ τρίτον αὐτῶν, “so that one third of them would be darkened” — σκοτίζω, from the darkness family: to make dark, the verb related to σκότος. The ninth plague of Egypt was darkness. καὶ ἡ ἡμέρα μὴ φάνῃ τὸ τρίτον αὐτῆς, “and the day wouldn’t shine for one third of it” — ἡμέρα, from the time family; φαίνω, from the appear family: to give light.","words":[{"strongs":"G5067","hebrew":"τέταρτος","translit":"tétartos","english":"fourth"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G4141","hebrew":"πλήσσω","translit":"plḗssō","english":"struck"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G4582","hebrew":"σελήνη","translit":"selḗnē","english":"moon"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G4654","hebrew":"σκοτίζω","translit":"skotízō","english":"darkened"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"shine"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I saw, and I heard an eagle, flying in mid heaven, saying with a loud voice, “Woe! Woe! Woe for those who dwell on the earth, because of the other voices of the trumpets of the three angels, who are yet to sound!”","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἤκουσα ἑνὸς ἀετοῦ πετομένου ἐν μεσουρανήματι, “I saw, and I heard an eagle, flying in mid heaven” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἀετός, an eagle (or vulture, the bird that gathers over the dead); μεσουράνημα, the highest point of the sky, where all can hear. λέγοντος φωνῇ μεγάλῃ· Οὐαί, οὐαί, οὐαί, “saying with a loud voice, ‘Woe! Woe! Woe’” — οὐαί, a cry of grief and doom. The last three trumpets are the three woes. τοὺς κατοικοῦντας ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “for those who dwell on the earth” — γῆ, from the world family: those who belong to the world rather than to God.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G105","hebrew":"ἀετός","translit":"aetós","english":"eagle"},{"strongs":"G4072","hebrew":"πέτομαι","translit":"pétomai","english":"flying"},{"strongs":"G3321","hebrew":"μεσουράνημα","translit":"mesouránēma","english":"mid heaven"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"Woe"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpets"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sound"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Half an Hour of Silence","verseRef":"v. 1","image":"Picture the throne room of chapters 4–7, ringing with thunder, harps and the songs of millions. The Lamb breaks the seventh seal — and suddenly everything stops. Not a harp string, not a wingbeat, not a voice. Silence, for half an hour.","meaning":"“When he opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven for about half an hour” (v. 1). Some moments are too holy for words. Heaven holds its breath before the judgement of God — and in the silence, the prayers of the saints are heard."},{"title":"The Censer Goes Up, the Fire Comes Down","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture an angel at the golden altar with a censer full of incense and the prayers of God's people. The fragrant smoke rises up to God. Then the angel fills the same censer with fire from the altar and hurls it down to earth — and thunder, lightning and earthquake follow.","meaning":"“The smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, went up before God out of the angel’s hand” (v. 4). “The angel took the censer, and he filled it with the fire of the altar, and threw it on the earth” (v. 5). The prayers of ordinary believers move the hand of God in history."},{"title":"The Bitter Star","verseRef":"v. 10-11","image":"Picture a great blazing star falling from the night sky into the rivers and springs. The water that once gave life turns bitter as poison, and people who drink it die.","meaning":"“The name of the star is called ‘Wormwood.’ One third of the waters became wormwood” (v. 11). When people turn from the true God to idols, the things they trust for life turn bitter and deadly. Sin poisons the springs."},{"title":"The Eagle's Cry","verseRef":"v. 13","image":"Picture a single great eagle soaring at the very top of the sky, where everyone on earth can see and hear it, crying out three times: ‘Woe! Woe! Woe!’","meaning":"“Woe! Woe! Woe for those who dwell on the earth, because of the other voices of the trumpets of the three angels, who are yet to sound!” (v. 13). God warns before He strikes. Every trumpet is a call to repent while there is still time."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Silence and the Seven Trumpets (v. 1-2)","body":"The Lamb opens the seventh seal and there is silence in heaven for about half an hour; the seven angels who stand before God are given seven trumpets."},{"label":"The Prayers of the Saints (v. 3-5)","body":"Another angel offers much incense with the prayers of all the saints on the golden altar; the smoke rises before God; then the angel fills the censer with fire from the altar and throws it to the earth, bringing thunder, voices, lightning and earthquake."},{"label":"The First Four Trumpets (v. 6-12)","body":"The angels prepare to sound; the first brings hail and fire mixed with blood, burning a third of the earth, trees and grass; the second, a burning mountain thrown into the sea, turning a third of it to blood; the third, a star called Wormwood falling on a third of the rivers and springs, making them bitter; the fourth strikes a third of the sun, moon and stars with darkness."},{"label":"The Eagle's Warning (v. 13)","body":"An eagle flying in mid-heaven cries ‘Woe, woe, woe’ to those who dwell on the earth because of the three trumpets still to sound."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Silence in Heaven","paragraphs":["The opening of the seventh seal brings not a new disaster but silence: “When he opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven for about half an hour” (v. 1). After the thunderous worship of chapters 4–7, this silence is startling. In the prophets, silence comes before the Lord's appearing in judgement: “Be silent at the presence of the Lord Yahweh, for the day of Yahweh is at hand” (Zephaniah 1:7), and “Be silent, all flesh, before Yahweh; for he has roused himself from his holy habitation!” (Zechariah 2:13).","The silence also makes room for prayer. Jewish tradition said that the angels' praise fell silent so that the prayers of Israel could be heard. In verses 3–4 that is exactly what happens: in the hush of heaven, the prayers of the saints rise before God.","The seventh seal seems to contain the seven trumpets, as the seventh trumpet will contain the seven bowls (11:15; 15:1). Revelation's judgements unfold like a telescope, each series opening into the next."]},{"heading":"The Power of the Saints' Prayers","paragraphs":["Before any trumpet sounds, an angel stands at the golden altar with a censer, and is given much incense “that he should add it to the prayers of all the saints on the golden altar which was before the throne” (v. 3). The smoke and the prayers rise together before God (v. 4). These are the prayers of chapter 5 kept in golden bowls (5:8), and the cry of the martyrs under the altar: ‘How long?’ (6:10).","Then the angel fills the same censer with fire from the altar and throws it to the earth (v. 5). The judgements of the trumpets are God's answer to His people's prayers for justice. The weakest believers, praying in hiding in Smyrna or Pergamum, are moving the hand that moves history.","This is one of the most encouraging scenes in the Bible for those who pray. Prayers that seem to vanish into silence are in fact stored before God, mingled with incense, and they will be answered in His time. James says, “The prayer of faith will heal him who is sick” and “The insistent prayer of a righteous person is powerfully effective” (James 5:15–16)."]},{"heading":"The First Four Trumpets","paragraphs":["The first four trumpets strike the natural world: the land (v. 7), the sea (vv. 8–9), the fresh waters (vv. 10–11) and the heavenly lights (v. 12). Each affects ‘one third’ — more than the quarter of the seals, but still partial. These are warning judgements, not yet final; their purpose is to call people to repentance (9:20–21).","They echo the plagues of Egypt: hail and fire (the seventh plague), water turned to blood (the first), darkness (the ninth). As God judged Pharaoh to free His people from slavery, so He judges the world-system that oppresses His church, in order to bring about a new exodus. The trumpets also recall Jericho, where seven trumpets on the seventh day brought down the walls.","Whether these judgements should be read literally or as symbols of disasters that strike the world throughout history, their message is the same: the created order that people worship instead of the Creator (Romans 1:25) is not reliable. When God shakes it, the things people trust — land, sea, rivers, sky — turn against them."]},{"heading":"Wormwood and Woe","paragraphs":["The third trumpet brings a falling star named “Wormwood” that makes a third of the waters bitter, and “Many people died from the waters, because they were made bitter” (v. 11). In the prophets, God gave wormwood to His people when they forsook Him for idols. Idolatry poisons what should give life. When the true God is rejected, even good gifts become bitter.","The fourth trumpet darkens a third of the sun, moon and stars (v. 12). Darkness was the ninth plague of Egypt, and the prophets' sign of the day of the Lord. Creation itself withdraws its light from a world that has rejected the Light.","Then an eagle flies through mid-heaven crying, “Woe! Woe! Woe for those who dwell on the earth” (v. 13). The last three trumpets are worse, and they fall directly on people. But even this cry is mercy: God announces judgement before it comes, so that people may turn. As Peter wrote, the Lord “is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (2 Peter 3:9)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Trumpet of God","paragraphs":["Trumpets in the New Testament announce the coming of the Lord: “For the Lord himself will descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with God’s trumpet. The dead in Christ will rise first” (1 Thessalonians 4:16), and “He will send out his angels with a great sound of a trumpet, and they will gather together his chosen ones from the four winds” (Matthew 24:31). Paul speaks of “the last trumpet. For the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised incorruptible, and we will be changed” (1 Corinthians 15:52). Revelation's seventh trumpet (11:15) is that last trumpet.","Gabriel told Zechariah the priest, as he stood at the altar of incense while the people prayed outside, “I am Gabriel, who stands in the presence of God” (Luke 1:19). He had come to announce that the prayers of a lifetime had been heard. Revelation 8:2–4 shows the same scene in heaven: angels who stand before God, and prayers rising with incense.","Paul describes the root of the world's bitterness: people “traded the glory of the incorruptible God for the likeness of an image of corruptible man” (Romans 1:23), and so “their senseless heart was darkened” (Romans 1:21). The trumpets show the outward result of that inward darkness."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Silence","hebrew":"σιγή","translit":"sigḗ","strongs":"G4602 (half an hour ἡμιώριον, hēmiṓrion, G2256)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “silence.” σιγή is the absence of all sound. It appears only twice in the New Testament (with Acts 21:40). In Revelation, heaven is never silent — the living creatures ‘have no rest day and night’ (4:8) — except here. The silence is awe before the coming of God in judgement, and expectation before the answer to prayer. Sometimes the right response to God is not to speak but to be still."},{"english":"Censer","hebrew":"λιβανωτός","translit":"libanōtós","strongs":"G3031 (prayers προσευχή, proseuchḗ, G4335)","verses":"v. 3, 5","insight":"Strong's: “frankincense, i.e. (by extension) a censer for burning it.” λιβανωτός, from λίβανος (frankincense, one of the gifts of the wise men), here means the vessel for burning it. The same golden censer carries the prayers of the saints up to God and carries fire from the altar down to the earth. In Revelation, worship and judgement are not separate: the God who receives His people's prayers acts on them."},{"english":"Trumpet","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","strongs":"G4536 (sounded σαλπίζω, salpízō, G4537)","verses":"v. 2, 6, 13; 1:10; 4:1; 9:14","insight":"Strong's: “a trumpet.” σάλπιγξ was used for signals in war, for calling Israel to assemble, and to announce feasts, kings and the presence of God at Sinai. The seven trumpets of Revelation recall the seven trumpets at Jericho: blown in sequence, they bring down the walls of a hostile city. They are also warnings, like the watchman's trumpet in Ezekiel 33: whoever hears the trumpet and does not take warning is responsible for himself."},{"english":"Wormwood","hebrew":"ἄψινθος","translit":"ápsinthos","strongs":"G894 (made bitter πικραίνω, pikraínō, G4087)","verses":"v. 11","insight":"Strong's: “wormwood (as a type of bitterness, i.e. (figuratively) calamity).” ἄψινθος is a bitter plant. In Deuteronomy and the prophets it pictures the bitter fruit of idolatry: God said He would feed unfaithful Israel ‘with wormwood’. Here a falling star of that name turns a third of the waters bitter and deadly. The judgement fits the sin: those who abandon the fountain of living water find their own wells poisoned."},{"english":"Woe","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","strongs":"G3759 (eagle ἀετός, aetós, G105)","verses":"v. 13; 9:12; 11:14; 12:12; 18:10, 16, 19","insight":"Strong's: “\"woe\".” οὐαί is a cry of grief and doom — part lament, part warning. Jesus used it over the cities that rejected Him and over the hypocritical leaders. The three woes of 8:13 are the last three trumpets. At the fall of Babylon the kings, merchants and sailors cry ‘Woe, woe’ over the city (18:10, 16, 19). Yet every ‘woe’ spoken before judgement is also an invitation: there is still time to turn."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you how much your prayers matter in heaven.","items":[{"title":"Be Still Before Him","body":"Heaven itself falls silent for half an hour (v. 1). The Spirit invites you into that silence. Before you speak to God or ask Him for anything, be still. Let His holiness and His nearness fill the space. Silence is not empty; it is full of God."},{"title":"Your Prayers Move Heaven","body":"“The smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, went up before God” (v. 4), and the answer came down as fire on the earth. The Spirit wants you to know that your prayers for justice, for the lost, for the persecuted church are not wasted. Keep praying. Heaven is listening."},{"title":"Hear the Trumpet","body":"The trumpets are warnings before the final judgement. The Spirit asks: what is God using in your life right now to get your attention? Do not harden your heart. When the trumpet sounds, turn to Him."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Silence Check","body":"When did you last spend time in silence before God, without speaking or asking (v. 1)?"},{"title":"The Prayer Check","body":"Do you believe your prayers make a difference in the world (vv. 3–5)? How would you pray differently if you did?"},{"title":"The Bitterness Check","body":"Is there a ‘spring’ in your life — a pleasure, a relationship, a pursuit — that once seemed to give life but has turned bitter because it took God's place (v. 11)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Half an Hour of Silence","body":"This week, set aside thirty minutes to be silent before God. Read Revelation 8:1–4, then sit quietly. If thoughts crowd in, offer each one to God as incense and return to stillness. End by praying for one person who is suffering for their faith."},{"title":"Keep a Prayer Bowl","body":"Write your prayer requests on slips of paper and put them in a bowl. Each week, take them out and pray over them again; when one is answered, write the answer on the back and thank God. Let it remind you that your prayers are kept before the throne (v. 3)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Holy God, I am silent before You. Heaven itself holds its breath when the Lamb opens the last seal; teach me to be still in Your presence. Thank You that the prayers of all Your saints — my prayers too — rise before You with incense from the golden altar, and that You hear them. Thank You that You act on them, and that the prayers of weak and hidden believers move Your hand in history. Lord, when You shake the earth and the sea and the rivers and the sky, keep my trust in You and not in Your creation. Where sin has made sweet things bitter in my life, forgive me and heal the springs. Let me hear Your warnings and turn to You quickly. And make me a faithful intercessor for those who do not yet know You, while there is still time. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Walls of Jericho","ref":"Joshua 6:1-21","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 6","summary":"When Israel came to Jericho, God told Joshua to march around the city once a day for six days, with seven priests carrying seven trumpets before the ark. On the seventh day they marched around it seven times; the priests blew the trumpets, the people shouted, and the walls fell flat. The seven trumpets of Revelation recall Jericho: God's people do not bring down the strongholds of evil by their own strength, but by obeying God, and at His signal the walls fall."},{"title":"The Plagues of Egypt","ref":"Exodus 7–10","relatedVerses":"v. 7-12","summary":"When Pharaoh refused to let Israel go, God struck Egypt with plagues: the Nile turned to blood, frogs, gnats, flies, disease on livestock, boils, hail mixed with fire, locusts, and darkness that could be felt. Each plague showed that the gods of Egypt were powerless, and each was a call to Pharaoh to repent — which he refused. The trumpets of Revelation repeat the plagues on a world scale: blood, hail and fire, darkness, and then locusts in chapter 9. Their purpose is the same: to free God's people and to expose the false gods."},{"title":"Zechariah at the Altar of Incense","ref":"Luke 1:5-25","relatedVerses":"v. 2-4","summary":"The aged priest Zechariah was chosen by lot to burn incense in the temple, while the whole crowd prayed outside. As he stood at the golden altar, an angel appeared at its right side: “I am Gabriel, who stands in the presence of God” (Luke 1:19). He announced that Zechariah's prayer had been heard: his wife Elizabeth would bear a son, John, who would prepare the way for the Lord. In Revelation 8 the same picture appears in heaven — incense, prayers and angels who stand before God — and the answer to the prayers changes the world."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":9,"chapterTitle":"The Fifth and Sixth Trumpets: Locusts from the Abyss and Horsemen from the Euphrates","themeTitle":"“They Didn’t Repent”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"The fifth angel sounded, and I saw a star from the sky which had fallen to the earth. The key to the pit of the abyss was given to him.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ πέμπτος ἄγγελος ἐσάλπισεν, “The fifth angel sounded” — σαλπίζω: the first ‘woe’. καὶ εἶδον ἀστέρα ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ πεπτωκότα εἰς τὴν γῆν, “and I saw a star from the sky which had fallen to the earth” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); γῆ, from the world family. Perfect tense: already fallen. Stars often stand for angels (1:20); a fallen star suggests a fallen angel — Jesus said, “I saw Satan having fallen like lightning from heaven” (Luke 10:18). καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ ἡ κλεὶς τοῦ φρέατος τῆς ἀβύσσου, “The key to the pit of the abyss was given to him” — ‘given’: even the forces of hell act only by permission. Christ holds the keys of death and Hades (1:18).","words":[{"strongs":"G3991","hebrew":"πέμπτος","translit":"pémptos","english":"fifth"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"star"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fallen"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G2807","hebrew":"κλείς","translit":"kleís","english":"key"},{"strongs":"G5421","hebrew":"φρέαρ","translit":"phréar","english":"pit"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"}]},{"v":2,"text":"He opened the pit of the abyss, and smoke went up out of the pit, like the smoke from a burning furnace. The sun and the air were darkened because of the smoke from the pit.","understanding":"καὶ ἤνοιξεν τὸ φρέαρ τῆς ἀβύσσου, “He opened the pit of the abyss” — ἄβυσσος, the bottomless deep, the prison of demons (Luke 8:31). καὶ ἀνέβη καπνὸς ἐκ τοῦ φρέατος ὡς καπνὸς καμίνου μεγάλης, “and smoke went up out of the pit, like the smoke from a burning furnace” — like the smoke over Sodom. καὶ ἐσκοτώθη ὁ ἥλιος καὶ ὁ ἀὴρ ἐκ τοῦ καπνοῦ τοῦ φρέατος, “The sun and the air were darkened because of the smoke from the pit” — σκοτόω, from the darkness family: to darken, to obscure. Evil from the abyss blots out the light.","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G5421","hebrew":"φρέαρ","translit":"phréar","english":"pit"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"went up"},{"strongs":"G2575","hebrew":"κάμινος","translit":"káminos","english":"furnace"},{"strongs":"G4656","hebrew":"σκοτόω","translit":"skotóō","english":"darkened"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G109","hebrew":"ἀήρ","translit":"aḗr","english":"air"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Then out of the smoke came locusts on the earth, and power was given to them, as the scorpions of the earth have power.","understanding":"καὶ ἐκ τοῦ καπνοῦ ἐξῆλθον ἀκρίδες εἰς τὴν γῆν, “Then out of the smoke came locusts on the earth” — ἀκρίς: the eighth plague of Egypt, and Joel's devastating locust army; γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐδόθη αὐταῖς ἐξουσία ὡς ἔχουσιν ἐξουσίαν οἱ σκορπίοι τῆς γῆς, “and power was given to them, as the scorpions of the earth have power” — ἐξουσία, from the power family, twice: delegated authority, as distinct from δύναμις, inherent ability; ‘given’ again: they act only by permission.","words":[{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G200","hebrew":"ἀκρίς","translit":"akrís","english":"locusts"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4651","hebrew":"σκορπίος","translit":"skorpíos","english":"scorpions"}]},{"v":4,"text":"They were told that they should not hurt the grass of the earth, neither any green thing, neither any tree, but only those people who don’t have God’s seal on their foreheads.","understanding":"καὶ ἐρρέθη αὐταῖς ἵνα μὴ ἀδικήσουσιν τὸν χόρτον τῆς γῆς, “They were told that they should not hurt the grass of the earth” — ἀδικέω; γῆ, from the world family. Ordinary locusts eat grass; these do not. οὐδὲ πᾶν χλωρὸν οὐδὲ πᾶν δένδρον, “neither any green thing, neither any tree” — εἰ μὴ τοὺς ἀνθρώπους οἵτινες οὐκ ἔχουσι τὴν σφραγῖδα τοῦ θεοῦ ἐπὶ τῶν μετώπων, “but only those people who don’t have God’s seal on their foreheads” — σφραγίς: the seal of chapter 7 protects God's servants. The demonic plague cannot touch those who belong to God.","words":[{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"hurt"},{"strongs":"G5528","hebrew":"χόρτος","translit":"chórtos","english":"grass"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5515","hebrew":"χλωρός","translit":"chlōrós","english":"green"},{"strongs":"G1186","hebrew":"δένδρον","translit":"déndron","english":"tree"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G4973","hebrew":"σφραγίς","translit":"sphragís","english":"seal"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"foreheads"}]},{"v":5,"text":"They were given power, not to kill them, but to torment them for five months. Their torment was like the torment of a scorpion, when it strikes a person.","understanding":"καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς ἵνα μὴ ἀποκτείνωσιν αὐτούς, ἀλλ᾽ ἵνα βασανισθήσονται μῆνας πέντε, “They were given power, not to kill them, but to torment them for five months” — βασανίζω, to torture; μήν, from the time family: a month. Five months is the life cycle of a locust: a limited time. καὶ ὁ βασανισμὸς αὐτῶν ὡς βασανισμὸς σκορπίου, ὅταν παίσῃ ἄνθρωπον, “Their torment was like the torment of a scorpion, when it strikes a person” — βασανισμός, torment: painful, but not fatal.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"kill"},{"strongs":"G928","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","english":"torment"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"months"},{"strongs":"G4002","hebrew":"πέντε","translit":"pénte","english":"five"},{"strongs":"G929","hebrew":"βασανισμός","translit":"basanismós","english":"torment"},{"strongs":"G4651","hebrew":"σκορπίος","translit":"skorpíos","english":"scorpion"},{"strongs":"G3817","hebrew":"παίω","translit":"paíō","english":"strikes"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"person"}]},{"v":6,"text":"In those days people will seek death, and will in no way find it. They will desire to die, and death will flee from them.","understanding":"καὶ ἐν ταῖς ἡμέραις ἐκείναις ζητήσουσιν οἱ ἄνθρωποι τὸν θάνατον καὶ οὐ μὴ εὑρήσουσιν αὐτόν, “In those days people will seek death, and will in no way find it” — ἡμέρα, from the time family. οὐ μή, the strongest negative. καὶ ἐπιθυμήσουσιν ἀποθανεῖν, καὶ φεύγει ὁ θάνατος ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν, “They will desire to die, and death will flee from them” — the despair of a life without God: the torment they suffer is the inner anguish of those who have rejected Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seek"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"find"},{"strongs":"G1937","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμέω","translit":"epithyméō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"flee"}]},{"v":7,"text":"The shapes of the locusts were like horses prepared for war. On their heads were something like golden crowns, and their faces were like people’s faces.","understanding":"Καὶ τὰ ὁμοιώματα τῶν ἀκρίδων ὅμοια ἵπποις ἡτοιμασμένοις εἰς πόλεμον, “The shapes of the locusts were like horses prepared for war” — ὁμοίωμα, from the image family: a likeness, a form that resembles, as distinct from εἰκών, an image that represents. Joel said the locusts' appearance was like horses. καὶ ἐπὶ τὰς κεφαλὰς αὐτῶν ὡς στέφανοι ὅμοιοι χρυσῷ, “On their heads were something like golden crowns” — στέφανος, from the crown family: they look like victors, but it is only a likeness. καὶ τὰ πρόσωπα αὐτῶν ὡς πρόσωπα ἀνθρώπων, “and their faces were like people’s faces” — intelligent, personal evil.","words":[{"strongs":"G3667","hebrew":"ὁμοίωμα","translit":"homoíōma","english":"shapes"},{"strongs":"G200","hebrew":"ἀκρίς","translit":"akrís","english":"locusts"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crowns"},{"strongs":"G5557","hebrew":"χρυσός","translit":"chrysós","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"faces"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people’s"}]},{"v":8,"text":"They had hair like women’s hair, and their teeth were like those of lions.","understanding":"καὶ εἶχον τρίχας ὡς τρίχας γυναικῶν, “They had hair like women’s hair” — perhaps long and flowing, like the hair of the feared Parthian warriors. καὶ οἱ ὀδόντες αὐτῶν ὡς λεόντων ἦσαν, “and their teeth were like those of lions” — Joel described the locust army as having the teeth of a lion. A picture of terrible, devouring power.","words":[{"strongs":"G2359","hebrew":"θρίξ","translit":"thríx","english":"hair"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"women’s"},{"strongs":"G3599","hebrew":"ὀδούς","translit":"odoús","english":"teeth"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lions"}]},{"v":9,"text":"They had breastplates, like breastplates of iron. The sound of their wings was like the sound of chariots, or of many horses rushing to war.","understanding":"καὶ εἶχον θώρακας ὡς θώρακας σιδηροῦς, “They had breastplates, like breastplates of iron” — θώραξ: invulnerable to human resistance. καὶ ἡ φωνὴ τῶν πτερύγων αὐτῶν ὡς φωνὴ ἁρμάτων ἵππων πολλῶν τρεχόντων εἰς πόλεμον, “The sound of their wings was like the sound of chariots, or of many horses rushing to war” — Joel's words again: the noise of an invading army.","words":[{"strongs":"G2382","hebrew":"θώραξ","translit":"thṓrax","english":"breastplates"},{"strongs":"G4603","hebrew":"σιδήρεος","translit":"sidḗreos","english":"iron"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G4420","hebrew":"πτέρυξ","translit":"ptéryx","english":"wings"},{"strongs":"G716","hebrew":"ἅρμα","translit":"hárma","english":"chariots"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G5143","hebrew":"τρέχω","translit":"tréchō","english":"rushing"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"}]},{"v":10,"text":"They have tails like those of scorpions, and stings. In their tails they have power to harm men for five months.","understanding":"καὶ ἔχουσιν οὐρὰς ὁμοίας σκορπίοις καὶ κέντρα, “They have tails like those of scorpions, and stings” — κέντρον, a sting, a goad. καὶ ἐν ταῖς οὐραῖς αὐτῶν ἡ ἐξουσία αὐτῶν ἀδικῆσαι τοὺς ἀνθρώπους μῆνας πέντε, “In their tails they have power to harm men for five months” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: their authority, given and limited; μήν, from the time family: five months, again.","words":[{"strongs":"G3769","hebrew":"οὐρά","translit":"ourá","english":"tails"},{"strongs":"G4651","hebrew":"σκορπίος","translit":"skorpíos","english":"scorpions"},{"strongs":"G2759","hebrew":"κέντρον","translit":"kéntron","english":"stings"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"harm"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"months"},{"strongs":"G4002","hebrew":"πέντε","translit":"pénte","english":"five"}]},{"v":11,"text":"They have over them as king the angel of the abyss. His name in Hebrew is “Abaddon”, but in Greek, he has the name “Apollyon”.","understanding":"ἔχουσιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν βασιλέα τὸν ἄγγελον τῆς ἀβύσσου, “They have over them as king the angel of the abyss” — Proverbs says ‘the locusts have no king’; these do. ὄνομα αὐτῷ Ἑβραϊστὶ Ἀβαδδών, καὶ ἐν τῇ Ἑλληνικῇ ὄνομα ἔχει Ἀπολλύων, “His name in Hebrew is ‘Abaddon’, but in Greek, he has the name ‘Apollyon’” — both mean ‘Destroyer’. Some hear a jab at Domitian, who liked to be associated with the god Apollo. Satan's true character is destruction; Christ's is salvation.","words":[{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1447","hebrew":"Ἑβραϊστί","translit":"Hebraïstí","english":"Hebrew"},{"strongs":"G3","hebrew":"Ἀβαδδών","translit":"Abaddṓn","english":"Abaddon"},{"strongs":"G1673","hebrew":"Ἑλληνικός","translit":"Hellēnikós","english":"Greek"},{"strongs":"G623","hebrew":"Ἀπολλύων","translit":"Apollýōn","english":"Apollyon"}]},{"v":12,"text":"The first woe is past. Behold, there are still two woes coming after this.","understanding":"Ἡ οὐαὶ ἡ μία ἀπῆλθεν, “The first woe is past” — οὐαί. ἰδοὺ ἔρχεται ἔτι δύο οὐαὶ μετὰ ταῦτα, “Behold, there are still two woes coming after this” — the eagle's warning (8:13) is being fulfilled step by step.","words":[{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"woe"},{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"past"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"}]},{"v":13,"text":"The sixth angel sounded. I heard a voice from the horns of the golden altar which is before God,","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ ἕκτος ἄγγελος ἐσάλπισεν, “The sixth angel sounded” — σαλπίζω: the second woe. καὶ ἤκουσα φωνὴν μίαν ἐκ τῶν κεράτων τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου τοῦ χρυσοῦ τοῦ ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ, “I heard a voice from the horns of the golden altar which is before God” — θυσιαστήριον: the altar where the saints' prayers were offered (8:3). The command comes from the place of prayer: this judgement too is an answer to the martyrs' cry.","words":[{"strongs":"G1623","hebrew":"ἕκτος","translit":"héktos","english":"sixth"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":14,"text":"saying to the sixth angel who had the trumpet, “Free the four angels who are bound at the great river Euphrates!”","understanding":"λέγοντα τῷ ἕκτῳ ἀγγέλῳ, ὁ ἔχων τὴν σάλπιγγα, “saying to the sixth angel who had the trumpet” — σάλπιγξ. Λῦσον τοὺς τέσσαρας ἀγγέλους τοὺς δεδεμένους ἐπὶ τῷ ποταμῷ τῷ μεγάλῳ Εὐφράτῃ, “Free the four angels who are bound at the great river Euphrates!” — λύω, to loose; δέω, to bind. The Euphrates was the eastern border of the Roman Empire and of Israel's promised land; beyond it lay Parthia, Rome's feared enemy, and in the Old Testament the invading armies of Assyria and Babylon.","words":[{"strongs":"G1623","hebrew":"ἕκτος","translit":"héktos","english":"sixth"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4536","hebrew":"σάλπιγξ","translit":"sálpinx","english":"trumpet"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"Free"},{"strongs":"G5064","hebrew":"τέσσαρες","translit":"téssares","english":"four"},{"strongs":"G1210","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","english":"bound"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"river"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2166","hebrew":"Εὐφράτης","translit":"Euphrátēs","english":"Euphrates"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The four angels were freed who had been prepared for that hour and day and month and year, so that they might kill one third of mankind.","understanding":"καὶ ἐλύθησαν οἱ τέσσαρες ἄγγελοι οἱ ἡτοιμασμένοι εἰς τὴν ὥραν καὶ ἡμέραν καὶ μῆνα καὶ ἐνιαυτόν, “The four angels were freed who had been prepared for that hour and day and month and year” — four members of the time family in one phrase, joined by a single Greek article: ὥρα, the appointed hour; ἡμέρα, day; μήν, month; ἐνιαυτός, year. Not four periods added together, but one exact moment fixed by God down to the hour. ἵνα ἀποκτείνωσιν τὸ τρίτον τῶν ἀνθρώπων, “so that they might kill one third of mankind” — τρίτος: now human life itself is struck.","words":[{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"freed"},{"strongs":"G5064","hebrew":"τέσσαρες","translit":"téssares","english":"four"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"month"},{"strongs":"G1763","hebrew":"ἐνιαυτός","translit":"eniautós","english":"year"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"kill"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"mankind"}]},{"v":16,"text":"The number of the armies of the horsemen was two hundred million. I heard the number of them.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς τῶν στρατευμάτων τοῦ ἱππικοῦ δισμυριάδες μυριάδων, “The number of the armies of the horsemen was two hundred million” — literally ‘twice ten thousand times ten thousand’: an unimaginable, uncountable host, a mirror image of the countless angels around the throne (5:11). ἤκουσα τὸν ἀριθμὸν αὐτῶν, “I heard the number of them” — ἀκούω: as with the 144,000 (7:4), John hears a number.","words":[{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G4753","hebrew":"στράτευμα","translit":"stráteuma","english":"armies"},{"strongs":"G2461","hebrew":"ἱππικόν","translit":"hippikón","english":"horsemen"},{"strongs":"G3461","hebrew":"μυριάς","translit":"myriás","english":"million"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Thus I saw the horses in the vision, and those who sat on them, having breastplates of fiery red, hyacinth blue, and sulfur yellow; and the horses’ heads resembled lions’ heads. Out of their mouths proceed fire, smoke, and sulfur.","understanding":"Καὶ οὕτως εἶδον τοὺς ἵππους ἐν τῇ ὁράσει, “Thus I saw the horses in the vision” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ὅρασις, a vision. καὶ τοὺς καθημένους ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν, ἔχοντας θώρακας πυρίνους καὶ ὑακινθίνους καὶ θειώδεις, “and those who sat on them, having breastplates of fiery red, hyacinth blue, and sulfur yellow” — the colours of the fire, smoke and sulfur that come from the horses' mouths. καὶ αἱ κεφαλαὶ τῶν ἵππων ὡς κεφαλαὶ λεόντων, “and the horses’ heads resembled lions’ heads” — καὶ ἐκ τῶν στομάτων αὐτῶν ἐκπορεύεται πῦρ καὶ καπνὸς καὶ θεῖον, “Out of their mouths proceed fire, smoke, and sulfur” — θεῖον, brimstone: the judgement of Sodom, and of the lake of fire (19:20).","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G3706","hebrew":"ὅρασις","translit":"hórasis","english":"vision"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2382","hebrew":"θώραξ","translit":"thṓrax","english":"breastplates"},{"strongs":"G4447","hebrew":"πύρινος","translit":"pýrinos","english":"fiery red"},{"strongs":"G5191","hebrew":"ὑακίνθινος","translit":"hyakínthinos","english":"hyacinth blue"},{"strongs":"G2306","hebrew":"θειώδης","translit":"theiṓdēs","english":"sulfur yellow"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lions’"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouths"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G2303","hebrew":"θεῖον","translit":"theîon","english":"sulfur"}]},{"v":18,"text":"By these three plagues were one third of mankind killed: by the fire, the smoke, and the sulfur, which proceeded out of their mouths.","understanding":"ἀπὸ τῶν τριῶν πληγῶν τούτων ἀπεκτάνθησαν τὸ τρίτον τῶν ἀνθρώπων, “By these three plagues were one third of mankind killed” — πληγή, a blow, a plague: from πλήσσω (8:12). ἐκ τοῦ πυρὸς καὶ τοῦ καπνοῦ καὶ τοῦ θείου τοῦ ἐκπορευομένου ἐκ τῶν στομάτων αὐτῶν, “by the fire, the smoke, and the sulfur, which proceeded out of their mouths” — what comes from their mouths kills; what comes from Christ's mouth is the sword of truth (1:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"mankind"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G2303","hebrew":"θεῖον","translit":"theîon","english":"sulfur"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouths"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For the power of the horses is in their mouths, and in their tails. For their tails are like serpents, and have heads, and with them they harm.","understanding":"ἡ γὰρ ἐξουσία τῶν ἵππων ἐν τῷ στόματι αὐτῶν ἐστιν καὶ ἐν ταῖς οὐραῖς αὐτῶν, “For the power of the horses is in their mouths, and in their tails” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: delegated authority, once more. αἱ γὰρ οὐραὶ αὐτῶν ὅμοιαι ὄφεσιν, ἔχουσαι κεφαλάς, “For their tails are like serpents, and have heads” — ὄφις, a serpent: the sign of the ancient serpent, the devil (12:9). καὶ ἐν αὐταῖς ἀδικοῦσιν, “and with them they harm.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouths"},{"strongs":"G3769","hebrew":"οὐρά","translit":"ourá","english":"tails"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G3789","hebrew":"ὄφις","translit":"óphis","english":"serpents"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"harm"}]},{"v":20,"text":"The rest of mankind, who were not killed with these plagues, didn’t repent of the works of their hands, that they wouldn’t worship demons, and the idols of gold, and of silver, and of brass, and of stone, and of wood; which can’t see, hear, or walk.","understanding":"Καὶ οἱ λοιποὶ τῶν ἀνθρώπων, οἳ οὐκ ἀπεκτάνθησαν ἐν ταῖς πληγαῖς ταύταις, “The rest of mankind, who were not killed with these plagues” — πληγή. οὐδὲ μετενόησαν ἐκ τῶν ἔργων τῶν χειρῶν αὐτῶν, “didn’t repent of the works of their hands” — μετανοέω; ἔργον, from the work family: the idols they made. ἵνα μὴ προσκυνήσουσιν τὰ δαιμόνια καὶ τὰ εἴδωλα, “that they wouldn’t worship demons, and the idols” — δαιμόνιον and εἴδωλον: behind the idols stand demons (1 Corinthians 10:20). ἃ οὔτε βλέπειν δύνανται οὔτε ἀκούειν οὔτε περιπατεῖν, “which can’t see, hear, or walk” — βλέπω, from the see family; δύναμαι, from the power family: idols have no ability at all — the Psalms' mockery of idols. People worship what is less than themselves.","words":[{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"mankind"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"},{"strongs":"G1497","hebrew":"εἴδωλον","translit":"eídōlon","english":"idols"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G693","hebrew":"ἀργύρεος","translit":"argýreos","english":"silver"},{"strongs":"G5470","hebrew":"χάλκεος","translit":"chálkeos","english":"brass"},{"strongs":"G3035","hebrew":"λίθινος","translit":"líthinos","english":"stone"},{"strongs":"G3585","hebrew":"ξύλινος","translit":"xýlinos","english":"wood"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"can’t"}]},{"v":21,"text":"They didn’t repent of their murders, their sorceries, their sexual immorality, or their thefts.","understanding":"καὶ οὐ μετενόησαν ἐκ τῶν φόνων αὐτῶν, “They didn’t repent of their murders” — μετανοέω, a second time: the tragic refrain. οὔτε ἐκ τῶν φαρμάκων αὐτῶν, “their sorceries” — φαρμακεία, the use of drugs and potions in magic and the occult. οὔτε ἐκ τῆς πορνείας αὐτῶν οὔτε ἐκ τῶν κλεμμάτων αὐτῶν, “their sexual immorality, or their thefts” — πορνεία, κλέμμα. Idolatry (v. 20) leads to the breaking of every commandment toward one's neighbour (v. 21). Judgement alone does not change hearts.","words":[{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G5408","hebrew":"φόνος","translit":"phónos","english":"murders"},{"strongs":"G5331","hebrew":"φαρμακεία","translit":"pharmakeía","english":"sorceries"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G2809","hebrew":"κλέμμα","translit":"klémma","english":"thefts"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Pit Opened","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a fallen star crashing to earth, handed a key by an unseen hand. He unlocks a shaft that goes down forever. Black smoke pours out like a furnace, blotting out the sun — and out of the smoke swarm creatures like locusts, millions of them.","meaning":"“The key to the pit of the abyss was given to him” (v. 1). Even the powers of hell act only when they are ‘given’ leave. The darkness is real and terrible, but it is on a leash."},{"title":"The Sealed Are Safe","verseRef":"v. 4","image":"Picture the locust swarm descending on a city. They pass over fields and trees, and they swarm over the people — but every time they come near someone with God's seal on their forehead, they turn away as if from fire.","meaning":"“They were told that they should not hurt the grass of the earth... but only those people who don’t have God’s seal on their foreheads” (v. 4). Those who belong to God are not untouched by the troubles of the world, but they are protected from the demonic torment that falls on those without Him."},{"title":"Seeking Death","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture people in agony, not dying, longing for death as an escape — but death runs from them. Their pain is not only in their bodies but in their souls: lives empty of God, filled with torment.","meaning":"“In those days people will seek death, and will in no way find it. They will desire to die, and death will flee from them” (v. 6). A life cut off from God is a kind of torment that no escape can end. Only turning to Him brings rest."},{"title":"They Did Not Repent","verseRef":"v. 20-21","image":"Picture the survivors of the plagues walking back into their temples, bowing again before golden statues that cannot see, hear or walk — and then going out to cheat, steal, sleep around and kill, as if nothing had happened.","meaning":"“The rest of mankind, who were not killed with these plagues, didn’t repent of the works of their hands” (v. 20). “They didn’t repent of their murders, their sorceries, their sexual immorality, or their thefts” (v. 21). Suffering alone does not change hearts. Only grace does."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Fifth Trumpet: Locusts from the Abyss (v. 1-12)","body":"A fallen star is given the key to the abyss and opens it; smoke darkens the sun, and locusts come out with power like scorpions to torment, not kill, those without God's seal for five months; people seek death and cannot find it; the locusts look like war horses with crowns, human faces, women's hair, lions' teeth and iron breastplates; their king is the angel of the abyss, Abaddon or Apollyon; the first woe is past."},{"label":"The Sixth Trumpet: Horsemen from the Euphrates (v. 13-19)","body":"A voice from the golden altar orders the release of four angels bound at the Euphrates, prepared for the exact hour, to kill a third of mankind; a cavalry of two hundred million appears, with breastplates of fire, hyacinth and sulfur, and horses with lions' heads breathing fire, smoke and sulfur, and tails like serpents."},{"label":"No Repentance (v. 20-21)","body":"The rest of mankind, who were not killed, still did not repent of worshipping demons and lifeless idols, nor of their murders, sorceries, sexual immorality or thefts."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Locusts from the Abyss","paragraphs":["The fifth trumpet is the first of the three woes, and it moves from the natural world to the demonic. A star that has fallen from heaven is given “The key to the pit of the abyss” (v. 1). This is probably a fallen angel, perhaps Satan himself. He opens the abyss, the prison of evil spirits, and out of the smoke come locusts with the power of scorpions (vv. 2–3).","These are no ordinary locusts. They do not eat grass or trees (v. 4); they have human faces, women's hair, lions' teeth and iron breastplates (vv. 7–9); and they have a king, “the angel of the abyss” (v. 11). The prophet Joel had described a locust plague as an invading army; John describes a demonic army in the form of locusts. Their purpose is to torment people, not to kill them, “for five months” (v. 5).","Notice the limits. The key is ‘given’ to the fallen star (v. 1); power is ‘given’ to the locusts (v. 3); they may not harm vegetation, and above all they may not harm “those people who... have God’s seal on their foreheads” (v. 4). Their time is limited. Evil is real and terrible, but it is restrained by God, and it cannot destroy His people."]},{"heading":"The Destroyer","paragraphs":["The locusts' king is named in two languages: “His name in Hebrew is ‘Abaddon’, but in Greek, he has the name ‘Apollyon’” (v. 11). Both mean ‘Destroyer’. This is the true nature of the powers of darkness: they destroy. The devil comes, as Jesus said, only to steal, kill and destroy; Christ came that we might have life.","The torment the locusts bring is so terrible that “people will seek death, and will in no way find it” (v. 6). Many readers see in this a picture of the inner anguish of life without God: despair, addiction, fear, emptiness — the spiritual torment that sin and demonic deception bring. People seek escape but cannot find it.","Yet believers are protected. The seal of God is stronger than the sting of the scorpion. Paul says the Spirit warns that “in later times some will fall away from the faith, paying attention to seducing spirits and doctrines of demons” (1 Timothy 4:1); those sealed by God are kept from that destruction."]},{"heading":"The Army from the Euphrates","paragraphs":["The sixth trumpet sounds, and a voice comes “from the horns of the golden altar which is before God” (v. 13) — the altar of the saints' prayers. Four angels bound at the Euphrates are released, “prepared for that hour and day and month and year” (v. 15). God's timing is exact, down to the hour. Their task is to kill a third of mankind — the first time in the trumpets that human life is directly struck.","An enormous cavalry appears: “two hundred million” (v. 16). The horses have lions' heads and breathe fire, smoke and sulfur, and their tails are like serpents (vv. 17–19). For John's readers, the Euphrates suggested the Parthian cavalry that threatened Rome's eastern border, and before that the armies of Assyria and Babylon that God used to judge Israel. Some read this as a literal end-time army; others as demonic forces behind all the great wars of history. Either way it is a picture of overwhelming, devouring judgement.","Again the details point to the source: fire and sulfur (the judgement of Sodom and of the lake of fire), serpents (the ancient serpent, 12:9). The powers that serve evil end up destroying the very people who serve them."]},{"heading":"Yet They Did Not Repent","paragraphs":["The chapter ends with the saddest words in the trumpets: “The rest of mankind, who were not killed with these plagues, didn’t repent of the works of their hands, that they wouldn’t worship demons, and the idols of gold, and of silver, and of brass, and of stone, and of wood; which can’t see, hear, or walk” (v. 20). Like Pharaoh, they hardened their hearts.","The problem is first worship. Behind every idol stand demons (1 Corinthians 10:20). People bow to things that cannot see, hear or walk — things they made with their own hands — rather than to the living God who made them. And wrong worship always leads to wrong living: “They didn’t repent of their murders, their sorceries, their sexual immorality, or their thefts” (v. 21). The first commandments broken lead to the breaking of the rest.","This shows the purpose of the trumpets. They are judgements, but they are also calls to repentance, like the plagues of Egypt. That people do not repent shows that judgement alone cannot change the human heart. Only the grace of God, the testimony of His witnesses and the blood of the Lamb can do that — which is why chapters 10–11 turn to the message of God and His witnesses. Jesus said, “I tell you, no, but unless you repent, you will all perish in the same way” (Luke 13:3)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Abyss, the Destroyer and the Call to Repent","paragraphs":["The demons Jesus cast out from the man of the Gerasenes “begged him that he would not command them to go into the abyss” (Luke 8:31). Other demons cried, “What do we have to do with you, Jesus, Son of God? Have you come here to torment us before the time?” (Matthew 8:29). The demons know the abyss is their prison, and that their time is limited.","Jesus said, “I saw Satan having fallen like lightning from heaven” (Luke 10:18) — the fallen star of verse 1. Paul warns that “even Satan masquerades as an angel of light” (2 Corinthians 11:14) — like locusts with golden crowns and human faces, evil often looks glorious.","The list of sins in verse 21 matches Paul's list of the deeds of the flesh: “idolatry, sorcery, hatred, strife, jealousies, outbursts of anger, rivalries, divisions, heresies, envy, murders, drunkenness, orgies, and things like these” (Galatians 5:20–21). Paul warned that those who practise them will not inherit God's kingdom — and offered the way out: the fruit of the Spirit in those who belong to Christ."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Abyss","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","strongs":"G12 (pit φρέαρ, phréar, G5421)","verses":"v. 1, 2, 11; 11:7; 17:8; 20:1, 3","insight":"Strong's: “depthless, i.e. (specially) (infernal) \"abyss\".” ἄβυσσος, from a- (not) and βυθός (depth), is the bottomless deep — in the Old Testament the watery deep of creation; in the New the prison of demons (Luke 8:31). In Revelation the locusts come out of it (9:2), the beast rises from it (11:7; 17:8), and in the end Satan is thrown into it and locked in (20:1–3). The key is always held by another: the abyss is a prison, not a throne."},{"english":"Apollyon / Destroyer","hebrew":"Ἀπολλύων","translit":"Apollýōn","strongs":"G623 (Abaddon Ἀβαδδών, Abaddṓn, G3)","verses":"v. 11","insight":"Strong's: “a destroyer (i.e. Satan).” Ἀπολλύων, from ἀπόλλυμι (to destroy), is the Greek name of the angel of the abyss; Ἀβαδδών, from the Hebrew word for destruction, is his Hebrew name. Both describe what he does. The contrast with the Lamb could not be sharper: the Destroyer brings torment and death; the Lamb, who was slain, brings life. The devil's name tells you his purpose."},{"english":"Locusts","hebrew":"ἀκρίς","translit":"akrís","strongs":"G200 (scorpions σκορπίος, skorpíos, G4651)","verses":"v. 3, 7","insight":"Strong's: “a locust (as pointed, or as lighting on the top of vegetation).” ἀκρίς is the ordinary locust — John the Baptist ate them. A locust swarm could strip a whole region bare; it was the eighth plague of Egypt and the subject of Joel's prophecy. Revelation's locusts are demonic: they have a king, human faces, crowns, and the sting of scorpions. They are a picture of evil spirits unleashed on a world that has rejected God."},{"english":"Torment","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","strongs":"G928 (torment βασανισμός, basanismós, G929)","verses":"v. 5; 11:10; 12:2; 14:10; 20:10","insight":"Strong's: “to torture.” βασανίζω comes from βάσανος, a touchstone used to test metals, and then the torture used to test a witness. The locusts torment but do not kill (9:5). In 14:10 and 20:10 the word is used of the final punishment of the beast's worshippers and of the devil. Strikingly, in 11:10 the two prophets ‘tormented’ the earth-dwellers — by their testimony. The truth itself is torment to those who refuse it."},{"english":"Sorceries","hebrew":"φαρμακεία","translit":"pharmakeía","strongs":"G5331 (demons δαιμόνιον, daimónion, G1140)","verses":"v. 21; 18:23","insight":"Strong's: “medication (\"pharmacy\"), i.e. (by extension) magic (literally or figuratively).” φαρμακεία, from φάρμακον (a drug), is the use of drugs, potions and spells in magic and the occult. Our word ‘pharmacy’ comes from it. In the ancient world drugs, magic and idol worship were closely linked. Babylon ‘deceived all the nations’ by her φαρμακεία (18:23). Paul lists it among the deeds of the flesh (Galatians 5:20). It stands for every attempt to gain spiritual power apart from God."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit pulls back the curtain on the spiritual forces at work in the world — and on the human heart.","items":[{"title":"Evil Is on a Leash","body":"The key to the abyss is ‘given’; the locusts' power is ‘given’; their time is limited to five months (vv. 1, 3, 5). The Spirit wants you to know that however dark things become, Satan acts only within limits God sets. You do not need to fear the Destroyer."},{"title":"God's Seal Protects You","body":"The locusts could harm only “those people who don’t have God’s seal on their foreheads” (v. 4). If you belong to Christ, you are sealed by His Spirit. The Spirit invites you to stand firm against fear, deception and spiritual attack, knowing you are His."},{"title":"Don't Harden Your Heart","body":"Even after terrible judgements, people “didn’t repent” (vv. 20–21). The Spirit asks gently: is there an area where God has been warning you, and you have not turned? Do not wait for the next trumpet. Repent today, while your heart is soft."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Idol Check","body":"What ‘works of your hands’ — achievements, possessions, technology, appearance — do you trust or serve in place of God (v. 20)?"},{"title":"The Occult Check","body":"Have you ever sought spiritual power, guidance or healing through horoscopes, fortune-telling, charms or other practices outside God (v. 21)? Have you renounced them?"},{"title":"The Repentance Check","body":"When hardship comes, does it soften your heart toward God or harden it (v. 20)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Clean House","body":"This week, walk through your home and your phone. Remove anything connected with the occult, idolatry or sexual immorality (v. 21). As you do, pray aloud: ‘I belong to the living God. I renounce every other power.’"},{"title":"Pray for the Hardened","body":"Think of someone who has gone through great suffering yet still refuses God. Pray for them daily this week, asking that God would soften their heart and send someone to show them the Lamb's love."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord God Almighty, You hold the key to the abyss, and no evil power moves without Your permission. Thank You that the Destroyer cannot destroy those who bear Your seal. Thank You that I am sealed by Your Spirit as Your own. Protect me and my family from every deception, every torment and every spiritual attack. Where darkness has risen like smoke in my life or my community, let Your light break through. Lord, I confess that my heart can be hard. Show me any idol I have made with my own hands — anything I trust or serve in Your place — and give me grace to turn from it. Keep me from every sin of murder in my heart, every occult practice, every immorality and every theft. Soften the hearts of those I love who do not yet repent. Let them see the Lamb who was slain for them, and turn and live. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Locust Plague of Egypt","ref":"Exodus 10:1-20","relatedVerses":"v. 3-10","summary":"When Pharaoh still refused to let Israel go, Moses stretched out his staff and the Lord sent an east wind that brought locusts over all Egypt. They covered the face of the land so that it was dark, and ate every plant and every fruit the hail had left. Pharaoh hurriedly confessed his sin and begged Moses to pray — but as soon as the locusts were gone, he hardened his heart again. The locusts of Revelation 9 recall that plague, and the refusal to repent in verses 20–21 recalls Pharaoh's hard heart."},{"title":"Joel's Locust Army","ref":"Joel 1–2","relatedVerses":"v. 7-9","summary":"The prophet Joel saw a locust plague so terrible he described it as an invading army: the land was like Eden before them and a desolate wilderness behind; they looked like horses and ran like war horses; they leaped with the noise of chariots; the sun and moon were darkened. Joel called it the day of the Lord and summoned the people to return to God with fasting, weeping and mourning — ‘Tear your heart and not your garments’ — because He is gracious and merciful. Revelation's locusts carry the same message: turn to God before it is too late."},{"title":"The Legion and the Abyss","ref":"Luke 8:26-39","relatedVerses":"v. 1-2, 11","summary":"In the country of the Gerasenes, Jesus met a man possessed by many demons, who lived naked among the tombs and could not be chained. When Jesus asked his name, he said, ‘Legion’. The demons “begged him that he would not command them to go into the abyss” (Luke 8:31), and Jesus allowed them to go into a herd of pigs, which rushed into the lake and drowned. The man was found sitting at Jesus' feet, clothed and in his right mind. The powers of the abyss fear Jesus — and He sets their captives free."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":10,"chapterTitle":"The Mighty Angel and the Little Scroll","themeTitle":"“Sweet as Honey in My Mouth… My Stomach Was Made Bitter”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw a mighty angel coming down out of the sky, clothed with a cloud. A rainbow was on his head. His face was like the sun, and his feet like pillars of fire.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἄλλον ἄγγελον ἰσχυρὸν καταβαίνοντα ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, “I saw a mighty angel coming down out of the sky” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἰσχυρός, from the power family: strong, forceful. Another pause, like chapter 7, before the seventh trumpet. περιβεβλημένον νεφέλην, καὶ ἡ ἶρις ἐπὶ τὴν κεφαλὴν αὐτοῦ, “clothed with a cloud. A rainbow was on his head” — the cloud of God's presence and the rainbow of His throne (4:3). καὶ τὸ πρόσωπον αὐτοῦ ὡς ὁ ἥλιος, καὶ οἱ πόδες αὐτοῦ ὡς στῦλοι πυρός, “His face was like the sun, and his feet like pillars of fire” — his appearance reflects Christ's glory (1:15–16), and his feet recall the pillar of fire in the wilderness. Some think this angel is Christ Himself; but Revelation never calls Christ an angel, and he swears by God (v. 6). He is a mighty messenger who carries Christ's glory.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"coming down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G2463","hebrew":"ἶρις","translit":"îris","english":"rainbow"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G4769","hebrew":"στῦλος","translit":"stŷlos","english":"pillars"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]},{"v":2,"text":"He had in his hand a little open book. He set his right foot on the sea, and his left on the land.","understanding":"καὶ ἔχων ἐν τῇ χειρὶ αὐτοῦ βιβλαρίδιον ἠνεῳγμένον, “He had in his hand a little open book” — βιβλαρίδιον, from the book family: a little scroll, as distinct from the great βιβλίον of chapter 5. It is open: its message is to be revealed and proclaimed. καὶ ἔθηκεν τὸν πόδα αὐτοῦ τὸν δεξιὸν ἐπὶ τῆς θαλάσσης, τὸν δὲ εὐώνυμον ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “He set his right foot on the sea, and his left on the land” — γῆ, from the world family: the whole world is claimed for God, and his message is for all.","words":[{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G974","hebrew":"βιβλιαρίδιον","translit":"bibliarídion","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"set"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"foot"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G2176","hebrew":"εὐώνυμος","translit":"euṓnymos","english":"left"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"land"}]},{"v":3,"text":"He cried with a loud voice, as a lion roars. When he cried, the seven thunders uttered their voices.","understanding":"καὶ ἔκραξεν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ ὥσπερ λέων μυκᾶται, “He cried with a loud voice, as a lion roars” — μυκάομαι, to roar; Amos and Hosea speak of the LORD roaring like a lion. καὶ ὅτε ἔκραξεν, ἐλάλησαν αἱ ἑπτὰ βρονταὶ τὰς ἑαυτῶν φωνάς, “When he cried, the seven thunders uttered their voices” — βροντή: Psalm 29 speaks of ‘the voice of the LORD’ seven times, as thunder.","words":[{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lion"},{"strongs":"G3455","hebrew":"μυκάομαι","translit":"mykáomai","english":"roars"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunders"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"uttered"}]},{"v":4,"text":"When the seven thunders sounded, I was about to write; but I heard a voice from the sky saying, “Seal up the things which the seven thunders said, and don’t write them.”","understanding":"καὶ ὅτε ἐλάλησαν αἱ ἑπτὰ βρονταί, ἤμελλον γράφειν, “When the seven thunders sounded, I was about to write” — γράφω. καὶ ἤκουσα φωνὴν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ λέγουσαν· Σφράγισον ἃ ἐλάλησαν αἱ ἑπτὰ βρονταί, καὶ μὴ αὐτὰ γράψῃς, “but I heard a voice from the sky saying, ‘Seal up the things which the seven thunders said, and don’t write them’” — σφραγίζω, to seal, to keep secret. Revelation does not tell us everything. Some things God keeps to Himself; our task is to obey what He has revealed.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunders"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"about"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"write"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"Seal up"}]},{"v":5,"text":"The angel whom I saw standing on the sea and on the land lifted up his right hand to the sky,","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ ἄγγελος ὃν εἶδον ἑστῶτα ἐπὶ τῆς θαλάσσης καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “The angel whom I saw standing on the sea and on the land” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); γῆ, from the world family. ἦρεν τὴν χεῖρα αὐτοῦ τὴν δεξιὰν εἰς τὸν οὐρανόν, “lifted up his right hand to the sky” — the gesture of a solemn oath, as in Daniel 12:7, where an angel lifts both hands to heaven and swears by Him who lives forever.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"land"},{"strongs":"G142","hebrew":"αἴρω","translit":"aírō","english":"lifted up"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"}]},{"v":6,"text":"and swore by him who lives forever and ever, who created heaven and the things that are in it, the earth and the things that are in it, and the sea and the things that are in it, that there will no longer be delay,","understanding":"καὶ ὤμοσεν ἐν τῷ ζῶντι εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “and swore by him who lives forever and ever” — ὀμνύω, to swear an oath; ζάω, from the life family; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. ὃς ἔκτισεν τὸν οὐρανὸν... καὶ τὴν γῆν... καὶ τὴν θάλασσαν, “who created heaven... the earth... and the sea” — κτίζω; γῆ, from the world family: the Creator of all has the right to bring all to its end. ὅτι χρόνος οὐκέτι ἔσται, “that there will no longer be delay” — χρόνος, from the time family: a span of time. The older ‘time shall be no more’ misses the point: not that time will cease, but that God's patient delay (6:11) is ending.","words":[{"strongs":"G3660","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","english":"swore"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G2936","hebrew":"κτίζω","translit":"ktízō","english":"created"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"delay"}]},{"v":7,"text":"but in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he is about to sound, then the mystery of God is finished, as he declared to his servants, the prophets.","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ ἐν ταῖς ἡμέραις τῆς φωνῆς τοῦ ἑβδόμου ἀγγέλου, ὅταν μέλλῃ σαλπίζειν, “but in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he is about to sound” — ἡμέρα, from the time family; σαλπίζω. καὶ ἐτελέσθη τὸ μυστήριον τοῦ θεοῦ, “then the mystery of God is finished” — τελέω, from the perfect family: to bring to its goal, to complete; μυστήριον, God's hidden plan now revealed. ὡς εὐηγγέλισεν τοὺς ἑαυτοῦ δούλους τοὺς προφήτας, “as he declared to his servants, the prophets” — εὐαγγελίζω, to announce good news; δοῦλος, from the servant family. The end of history is good news, long promised.","words":[{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G1442","hebrew":"ἕβδομος","translit":"hébdomos","english":"seventh"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"declared"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The voice which I heard from heaven, again speaking with me, said, “Go, take the book which is open in the hand of the angel who stands on the sea and on the land.”","understanding":"Καὶ ἡ φωνὴ ἣν ἤκουσα ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, “The voice which I heard from heaven” — the voice of verse 4. Ὕπαγε λάβε τὸ βιβλίον τὸ ἠνεῳγμένον ἐν τῇ χειρὶ τοῦ ἀγγέλου, “Go, take the book which is open in the hand of the angel” — βιβλίον, from the book family (here the ordinary word for the little scroll of v. 2). The angel ‘stands on the sea and on the land’ — γῆ, from the world family. John is no longer only a spectator; he must receive the message himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaking"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"Go"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"take"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"open"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stands"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"land"}]},{"v":9,"text":"I went to the angel, telling him to give me the little book. He said to me, “Take it, and eat it up. It will make your stomach bitter, but in your mouth it will be as sweet as honey.”","understanding":"καὶ ἀπῆλθα πρὸς τὸν ἄγγελον, λέγων αὐτῷ δοῦναί μοι τὸ βιβλαρίδιον, “I went to the angel, telling him to give me the little book” — βιβλαρίδιον, from the book family. Λάβε καὶ κατάφαγε αὐτό, “Take it, and eat it up” — κατεσθίω, to devour: Ezekiel was told to eat the scroll before he spoke. The prophet must digest God's word before he can deliver it. καὶ πικρανεῖ σου τὴν κοιλίαν, ἀλλ᾽ ἐν τῷ στόματί σου ἔσται γλυκὺ ὡς μέλι, “It will make your stomach bitter, but in your mouth it will be as sweet as honey” — πικραίνω, as in the wormwood of 8:11; γλυκύς, sweet; μέλι, honey.","words":[{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"went"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G974","hebrew":"βιβλιαρίδιον","translit":"bibliarídion","english":"little book"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"Take"},{"strongs":"G2719","hebrew":"κατεσθίω","translit":"katesthíō","english":"eat it up"},{"strongs":"G4087","hebrew":"πικραίνω","translit":"pikraínō","english":"bitter"},{"strongs":"G2836","hebrew":"κοιλία","translit":"koilía","english":"stomach"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G1099","hebrew":"γλυκύς","translit":"glykýs","english":"sweet"},{"strongs":"G3192","hebrew":"μέλι","translit":"méli","english":"honey"}]},{"v":10,"text":"I took the little book out of the angel’s hand, and ate it up. It was as sweet as honey in my mouth. When I had eaten it, my stomach was made bitter.","understanding":"καὶ ἔλαβον τὸ βιβλαρίδιον ἐκ τῆς χειρὸς τοῦ ἀγγέλου καὶ κατέφαγον αὐτό, “I took the little book out of the angel’s hand, and ate it up” — βιβλαρίδιον, from the book family. καὶ ἦν ἐν τῷ στόματί μου ὡς μέλι γλυκύ, “It was as sweet as honey in my mouth” — God's word is sweet: His promises, His victory, His coming kingdom. καὶ ὅτε ἔφαγον αὐτό, ἐπικράνθη ἡ κοιλία μου, “When I had eaten it, my stomach was made bitter” — πικραίνω. But the message includes suffering for God's witnesses and judgement for the world (ch. 11). The true prophet tastes both.","words":[{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"took"},{"strongs":"G974","hebrew":"βιβλιαρίδιον","translit":"bibliarídion","english":"little book"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel’s"},{"strongs":"G2719","hebrew":"κατεσθίω","translit":"katesthíō","english":"ate it up"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G1099","hebrew":"γλυκύς","translit":"glykýs","english":"sweet"},{"strongs":"G3192","hebrew":"μέλι","translit":"méli","english":"honey"},{"strongs":"G5315","hebrew":"φάγω","translit":"phágō","english":"eaten"},{"strongs":"G2836","hebrew":"κοιλία","translit":"koilía","english":"stomach"},{"strongs":"G4087","hebrew":"πικραίνω","translit":"pikraínō","english":"made bitter"}]},{"v":11,"text":"They told me, “You must prophesy again over many peoples, nations, languages, and kings.”","understanding":"καὶ λέγουσίν μοι· Δεῖ σε πάλιν προφητεῦσαι, “They told me, ‘You must prophesy again’” — δεῖ, it is necessary; προφητεύω, to speak God's word. John's commission is renewed. ἐπὶ λαοῖς καὶ ἔθνεσιν καὶ γλώσσαις καὶ βασιλεῦσιν πολλοῖς, “over many peoples, nations, languages, and kings” — the formula of 5:9 and 7:9, with ‘kings’ in place of ‘tribes’: the message is for the whole world and its rulers.","words":[{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"must"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"},{"strongs":"G3825","hebrew":"πάλιν","translit":"pálin","english":"again"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"peoples"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Colossus of Light","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a giant angel stepping down out of the sky, wrapped in cloud, a rainbow around his head, his face blazing like the sun, his legs like columns of fire. He plants one foot on the sea and the other on the land, holds up a small open scroll, and roars like a lion — and seven thunders answer.","meaning":"“He set his right foot on the sea, and his left on the land. He cried with a loud voice, as a lion roars” (vv. 2–3). The whole world — land and sea — belongs to God, and His message is for all of it."},{"title":"Thunders Sealed","verseRef":"v. 4","image":"Picture John with his pen raised, ready to write down what the seven thunders have said — and a voice from heaven stopping him: ‘Seal it up. Don't write it.’","meaning":"“Seal up the things which the seven thunders said, and don’t write them” (v. 4). God has not told us everything. There are things we will not know until the end. Our task is to trust Him with the unknown and obey what He has made known."},{"title":"No More Delay","verseRef":"v. 5-7","image":"Picture the mighty angel raising his right hand to heaven like a witness in court, swearing by the eternal Creator: the waiting is over; God's plan is about to be completed.","meaning":"“That there will no longer be delay, but in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he is about to sound, then the mystery of God is finished” (vv. 6–7). God's patience is long, but it has an end. His purposes will be completed exactly as He promised."},{"title":"The Sweet and Bitter Scroll","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture John taking the little scroll and eating it. In his mouth it tastes like honey — rich, sweet, delightful. But as he swallows, it turns bitter inside him, and he grips his stomach.","meaning":"“It was as sweet as honey in my mouth. When I had eaten it, my stomach was made bitter” (v. 10). God's word is sweet: it is His truth and His victory. But taking it in fully means sharing in the suffering of His witnesses and grieving over a world under judgement."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Mighty Angel (v. 1-4)","body":"A mighty angel comes down from heaven, robed in a cloud, with a rainbow, a face like the sun and legs like pillars of fire, holding a little open scroll; he stands on sea and land and shouts like a lion; seven thunders speak, but John is told to seal up what they said and not write it."},{"label":"The Oath (v. 5-7)","body":"The angel raises his hand and swears by the eternal Creator that there will be no more delay: when the seventh angel sounds, the mystery of God will be completed, as He announced to His servants the prophets."},{"label":"The Little Scroll Eaten (v. 8-11)","body":"The heavenly voice tells John to take the open scroll; the angel tells him to eat it, and it is sweet as honey in his mouth but bitter in his stomach; John is told he must prophesy again about many peoples, nations, languages and kings."}],"sections":[{"heading":"An Interlude Before the Last Trumpet","paragraphs":["As chapter 7 came between the sixth and seventh seals, so chapters 10:1–11:14 come between the sixth and seventh trumpets. After the terrible judgements of chapter 9 and the refusal of people to repent (9:20–21), the focus turns to God's word and God's witnesses. Judgement alone did not bring repentance; now we see the message the church must carry.","A mighty angel descends, clothed with a cloud, crowned with a rainbow, with a face like the sun and feet like pillars of fire (v. 1). Many of these features reflect the glory of Christ (1:15–16) and of God's throne (4:3). Some have thought the angel is Christ; but he is called ‘another angel’, and he swears by God rather than as God (v. 6). He is a messenger carrying the glory of the One who sent him.","He stands with one foot on the sea and one on the land (v. 2), claiming the whole world. Later the beast will rise from the sea and another from the land (13:1, 11); this angel shows that both realms belong to God."]},{"heading":"The Sealed Thunders and the Oath","paragraphs":["When the angel shouts, seven thunders speak, and John prepares to write. But he is told, “Seal up the things which the seven thunders said, and don’t write them” (v. 4). This is the only time in Revelation that John is told not to write. It reminds us that the book does not reveal everything about the future. Those who construct detailed timetables of the end should notice: some things God has deliberately left sealed.","Then the angel swears by the eternal Creator “that there will no longer be delay” (v. 6). This answers the martyrs' cry, ‘How long?’ (6:10), and their instruction to wait ‘yet for a while’ (6:11). The word is χρόνος: not that time itself will cease, but that the period of waiting is over.","“But in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he is about to sound, then the mystery of God is finished, as he declared to his servants, the prophets” (v. 7). The ‘mystery’ in the New Testament is God's plan, once hidden and now revealed in Christ, to bring all things together in Him and to include the nations in His people. When the last trumpet sounds, it will be completed. And it is ‘good news’: the word ‘declared’ is εὐαγγελίζω, to announce the gospel."]},{"heading":"Sweet and Bitter","paragraphs":["John is told to take the open scroll and eat it (vv. 8–9). This recalls Ezekiel, who was given a scroll written with ‘lamentations, mourning, and woe’ and told to eat it; it was sweet as honey in his mouth, and then he was sent to speak to a rebellious people. A prophet must first take God's word into himself before he can speak it to others.","The scroll is “as sweet as honey in my mouth” (v. 10). God's word is sweet: it reveals His glory, His love, His victory and His kingdom. The psalmist said God's words are sweeter than honey. But “When I had eaten it, my stomach was made bitter” (v. 10). The message includes suffering for God's witnesses (11:7–10) and judgement on the world. Anyone who truly takes in God's word will taste both its sweetness and its bitterness.","Then John is told, “You must prophesy again over many peoples, nations, languages, and kings” (v. 11). The rest of the book is the content of that renewed prophecy. And it is a model for the church: we too must take God's word into ourselves, and then speak it to the nations, whatever it costs."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Mystery Revealed","paragraphs":["Paul often speaks of God's ‘mystery’ — His long-hidden plan now revealed. It “in other generations was not made known to the children of men, as it has now been revealed to his holy apostles and prophets in the Spirit; that the Gentiles are fellow heirs, and fellow members of the body, and fellow partakers of his promise in Christ Jesus through the Good News” (Ephesians 3:5–6). It is “the mystery which has been hidden for ages and generations. But now it has been revealed to his saints” (Colossians 1:26) — “Christ in you, the hope of glory” (Colossians 1:27).","Revelation 10:7 says this mystery will be ‘finished’ at the seventh trumpet. Paul speaks of “the last trumpet. For the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised incorruptible, and we will be changed” (1 Corinthians 15:52).","Jesus Himself fed on God's word. Tempted to make bread, He answered from Deuteronomy that a person does not live by bread alone, ‘but by every word that proceeds out of the mouth of God’ (Matthew 4:4). John's eating of the scroll pictures what every disciple must do."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Little book","hebrew":"βιβλιαρίδιον","translit":"bibliarídion","strongs":"G974 (book βιβλίον, biblíon, G975)","verses":"v. 2, 9, 10","insight":"Strong's: “a booklet.” βιβλαρίδιον, from the book family, is a ‘little scroll’ — a double diminutive of βίβλος. It is distinct from the great sealed βιβλίον of chapter 5, which only the Lamb could open. This little scroll is already open, and John is to eat it and proclaim it. Many see in it the message of chapter 11: the witness and suffering of the church, and God's final triumph."},{"english":"Swore","hebrew":"ὀμνύω","translit":"omnýō","strongs":"G3660 (delay χρόνος, chrónos, G5550)","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “to swear, i.e. take (or declare on) oath.” ὀμνύω means to declare something under oath, calling on a higher power as witness. The angel lifts his hand to heaven and swears ‘by him who lives forever and ever’ — the highest oath possible. Hebrews says that God, wanting to show the certainty of His promise, confirmed it with an oath. The end of God's patient delay is as certain as His own eternal life."},{"english":"Mystery","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","strongs":"G3466 (finished τελέω, teléō, G5055)","verses":"v. 7; 1:20; 17:5, 7","insight":"Strong's: “a secret or \"mystery\" (through the idea of silence imposed by initiation into religious rites).” μυστήριον in pagan religion was a secret rite known only to initiates. In the New Testament it is the opposite: God's once-hidden plan, now openly revealed in Christ to all who believe. ‘The mystery of God’ (v. 7) is His plan for history, centred on Christ and including the nations. At the seventh trumpet it is ‘finished’ — τελέω, from the perfect family: brought to its goal."},{"english":"Sweet","hebrew":"γλυκύς","translit":"glykýs","strongs":"G1099 (honey μέλι, méli, G3192)","verses":"v. 9, 10","insight":"Strong's: “sweet (i.e. not bitter nor salt).” γλυκύς is the opposite of πικρός (bitter). James asks whether a spring can pour out both sweet and bitter water (James 3:11). Here one scroll is both sweet and bitter. God's word is sweet because it is His, true and full of hope; it is bitter because it tells of suffering and judgement. The faithful prophet never offers only the sweet."},{"english":"Prophesy","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","strongs":"G4395 (prophets προφήτης, prophḗtēs, G4396)","verses":"v. 11; 11:3","insight":"Strong's: “to foretell events, divine, speak under inspiration, exercise the prophetic office.” προφητεύω means more than predicting the future; it is speaking God's word under the Spirit's inspiration. John must ‘prophesy again’ to the nations (10:11), and the two witnesses ‘prophesy’ for 1,260 days (11:3). Revelation calls itself ‘the prophecy’ (1:3; 22:7), and says that ‘the testimony of Jesus is the Spirit of Prophecy’ (19:10): the whole church is a prophetic community, bearing witness to Jesus."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit calls you to take God's word into yourself — all of it.","items":[{"title":"Trust What Is Sealed","body":"John was told not to write what the thunders said (v. 4). The Spirit wants you to be at peace with not knowing everything. You do not need every detail of the future; you need to know the One who holds it. Rest in what He has revealed."},{"title":"God Keeps His Word on Time","body":"The angel swears “that there will no longer be delay” (v. 6). God's waiting is not forgetting. The Spirit reminds you that every promise of God will be fulfilled at exactly the right moment. Wait for Him with hope."},{"title":"Eat the Whole Scroll","body":"John's scroll was sweet in his mouth and bitter in his stomach (v. 10). The Spirit invites you not to pick only the sweet parts of the Bible, but to take in all of it — the promises and the warnings, the comfort and the cost. Then speak it to others."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Curiosity Check","body":"Are you more interested in decoding the future than in obeying what God has clearly told you (v. 4)?"},{"title":"The Digestion Check","body":"Do you take God's word deeply into your life, or only taste it (vv. 9–10)? When did a passage last change how you live?"},{"title":"The Bitterness Check","body":"Do you avoid parts of Scripture because they are hard or uncomfortable? Which ones?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Eat a Scroll","body":"Choose one short chapter of the Bible this week and ‘eat’ it: read it every day, memorise one verse, write down how it is sweet to you and how it is hard for you. At the end of the week, tell someone what God said to you through it."},{"title":"Prophesy to Someone","body":"Ask God for one person to whom you can speak His word this week — a promise, an encouragement or a gentle warning (v. 11). Pray first, then speak with love."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord God, Creator of heaven and earth and sea, You live forever and ever, and Your purposes cannot fail. Thank You that there will be no more delay when Your time comes, and that the mystery of Your plan in Christ will be completed, just as You promised Your servants the prophets. Thank You for the things You have revealed, and help me trust You with the things You have sealed. Give me a hunger for Your word. Let me take it in deeply, all of it — the sweetness of Your promises and the bitterness of its cost. Make Your word sweeter to me than honey. And then send me to speak it, with love and courage, to the people around me and to the nations. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Ezekiel Eats the Scroll","ref":"Ezekiel 2:8–3:4","relatedVerses":"v. 8-11","summary":"When God called Ezekiel to be a prophet to the exiles, a hand reached out to him holding a scroll, written on the front and back with lamentations, mourning and woe. God said, ‘Son of man, eat this scroll, and go, speak to the house of Israel.’ Ezekiel ate it, and it was as sweet as honey in his mouth. Then God sent him to a people with hard foreheads and stubborn hearts. John's experience follows Ezekiel's exactly — but with the added bitterness: the prophet who speaks God's word to a rebellious world will share in its cost."},{"title":"Daniel's Sealed Book","ref":"Daniel 12:4-9","relatedVerses":"v. 4-7","summary":"At the end of his visions, Daniel was told to ‘shut up the words, and seal the book, even to the time of the end.’ He saw a man clothed in linen above the river, who lifted both hands to heaven and swore by Him who lives forever that it would be for ‘a time, times, and a half’. Daniel asked what the outcome would be, and was told, ‘Go your way, Daniel; for the words are shut up and sealed until the time of the end.’ Revelation 10 draws on this scene — but where Daniel's book was sealed, John is told that the delay is now over."},{"title":"Jesus in the Wilderness","ref":"Matthew 4:1-4","relatedVerses":"v. 9-10","summary":"After fasting forty days in the wilderness, Jesus was hungry, and the devil tempted Him to turn stones into bread. Jesus answered, “It is written, ‘Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds out of the mouth of God’” (Matthew 4:4). He lived on God's word, even when it led Him into hunger and, at the end, to the cross. John's eating of the scroll is a picture of the same way of life: God's word is food that sustains, even when it is bitter."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":11,"chapterTitle":"The Two Witnesses and the Seventh Trumpet","themeTitle":"“The Kingdom of the World Has Become the Kingdom of Our Lord”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"A reed like a rod was given to me. Someone said, “Rise, and measure God’s temple, and the altar, and those who worship in it.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐδόθη μοι κάλαμος ὅμοιος ῥάβδῳ, “A reed like a rod was given to me” — κάλαμος, a measuring reed. λέγων· Ἔγειρε καὶ μέτρησον τὸν ναὸν τοῦ θεοῦ, “Someone said, ‘Rise, and measure God’s temple’” — ἐγείρω, from the rise family: get up; μετρέω, to measure. In Ezekiel and Zechariah, measuring a temple or a city marks it out for protection and restoration. ναός is the inner sanctuary. καὶ τὸ θυσιαστήριον καὶ τοὺς προσκυνοῦντας ἐν αὐτῷ, “and the altar, and those who worship in it” — people are measured, not just stones. The strongest reading is that the temple is God's people, protected by Him (1 Corinthians 3:16); some read it as a literal temple rebuilt in Jerusalem at the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G2563","hebrew":"κάλαμος","translit":"kálamos","english":"reed"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"rod"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"Rise"},{"strongs":"G3354","hebrew":"μετρέω","translit":"metréō","english":"measure"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Leave out the court which is outside of the temple, and don’t measure it, for it has been given to the nations. They will tread the holy city under foot for forty-two months.","understanding":"καὶ τὴν αὐλὴν τὴν ἔξωθεν τοῦ ναοῦ ἔκβαλε ἔξωθεν καὶ μὴ αὐτὴν μετρήσῃς, “Leave out the court which is outside of the temple, and don’t measure it” — αὐλή, the outer court. ὅτι ἐδόθη τοῖς ἔθνεσιν, “for it has been given to the nations” — given, again: by God's permission. καὶ τὴν πόλιν τὴν ἁγίαν πατήσουσιν μῆνας τεσσεράκοντα δύο, “They will tread the holy city under foot for forty-two months” — πατέω, to trample; μήν, from the time family. Forty-two months is three and a half years — Daniel's ‘time, times, and half a time’, the period of the persecution of God's people. Jesus said, “Jerusalem will be trampled down by the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled” (Luke 21:24). Inner sanctuary protected, outer court trampled: God's people are spiritually safe but physically exposed. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G833","hebrew":"αὐλή","translit":"aulḗ","english":"court"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G1544","hebrew":"ἐκβάλλω","translit":"ekbállō","english":"Leave out"},{"strongs":"G3354","hebrew":"μετρέω","translit":"metréō","english":"measure"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G3961","hebrew":"πατέω","translit":"patéō","english":"tread"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"months"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"}]},{"v":3,"text":"I will give power to my two witnesses, and they will prophesy one thousand two hundred sixty days, clothed in sackcloth.”","understanding":"καὶ δώσω τοῖς δυσὶν μάρτυσίν μου, “I will give power to my two witnesses” — μάρτυς, from the witness family. Two, because the Law required two witnesses for valid testimony; and Jesus sent His disciples out two by two. καὶ προφητεύσουσιν ἡμέρας χιλίας διακοσίας ἑξήκοντα, “and they will prophesy one thousand two hundred sixty days” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: 1,260 days is again three and a half years — the same period as the trampling (v. 2). The church's witness and the world's oppression run side by side. περιβεβλημένοι σάκκους, “clothed in sackcloth” — the clothing of mourning and repentance, the dress of prophets like Elijah.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witnesses"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesy"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"one thousand"},{"strongs":"G1250","hebrew":"διακόσιοι","translit":"diakósioi","english":"two hundred"},{"strongs":"G1835","hebrew":"ἑξήκοντα","translit":"hexḗkonta","english":"sixty"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G4526","hebrew":"σάκκος","translit":"sákkos","english":"sackcloth"}]},{"v":4,"text":"These are the two olive trees and the two lamp stands, standing before the Lord of the earth.","understanding":"Οὗτοί εἰσιν αἱ δύο ἐλαῖαι καὶ αἱ δύο λυχνίαι, “These are the two olive trees and the two lamp stands” — from Zechariah 4, where two olive trees supply oil to the lampstand: “These are the two anointed ones who stand by the Lord of the whole earth” (Zechariah 4:14). There they were Zerubbabel the governor and Joshua the priest — king and priest. In Revelation lampstands are churches (1:20). The strongest reading is that the two witnesses represent the witnessing church — a kingdom of priests (1:6) — empowered by the Spirit. αἱ ἐνώπιον τοῦ κυρίου τῆς γῆς ἑστῶτες, “standing before the Lord of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"},{"strongs":"G1636","hebrew":"ἐλαία","translit":"elaía","english":"olive trees"},{"strongs":"G3087","hebrew":"λυχνία","translit":"lychnía","english":"lamp stands"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":5,"text":"If anyone desires to harm them, fire proceeds out of their mouth and devours their enemies. If anyone desires to harm them, he must be killed in this way.","understanding":"καὶ εἴ τις αὐτοὺς θέλει ἀδικῆσαι, πῦρ ἐκπορεύεται ἐκ τοῦ στόματος αὐτῶν καὶ κατεσθίει τοὺς ἐχθροὺς αὐτῶν, “If anyone desires to harm them, fire proceeds out of their mouth and devours their enemies” — like Elijah, who called down fire on the soldiers sent to arrest him; and like Jeremiah, to whom God said, ‘I will make my words in your mouth fire.’ Their weapon is their word. καὶ εἴ τις θελήσῃ αὐτοὺς ἀδικῆσαι, οὕτως δεῖ αὐτὸν ἀποκτανθῆναι, “If anyone desires to harm them, he must be killed in this way” — until their testimony is finished (v. 7), they cannot be stopped.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desires"},{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"harm"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G1607","hebrew":"ἐκπορεύομαι","translit":"ekporeúomai","english":"proceeds"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G2719","hebrew":"κατεσθίω","translit":"katesthíō","english":"devours"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemies"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"must"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"}]},{"v":6,"text":"These have the power to shut up the sky, that it may not rain during the days of their prophecy. They have power over the waters, to turn them into blood, and to strike the earth with every plague, as often as they desire.","understanding":"οὗτοι ἔχουσιν τὴν ἐξουσίαν κλεῖσαι τὸν οὐρανόν, ἵνα μὴ ὑετὸς βρέχῃ τὰς ἡμέρας τῆς προφητείας αὐτῶν, “These have the power to shut up the sky, that it may not rain during the days of their prophecy” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: delegated authority; ἡμέρα, from the time family. Elijah shut the sky for three and a half years (Luke 4:25; James 5:17). καὶ ἐξουσίαν ἔχουσιν ἐπὶ τῶν ὑδάτων στρέφειν αὐτὰ εἰς αἷμα, “They have power over the waters, to turn them into blood” — ἐξουσία again; Moses turned the Nile to blood. καὶ πατάξαι τὴν γῆν ἐν πάσῃ πληγῇ, “and to strike the earth with every plague” — γῆ, from the world family. The witnesses minister in the power of Moses and Elijah, the Law and the Prophets, who appeared with Jesus on the mountain.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2808","hebrew":"κλείω","translit":"kleíō","english":"shut up"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G1026","hebrew":"βρέχω","translit":"bréchō","english":"rain"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G4762","hebrew":"στρέφω","translit":"stréphō","english":"turn"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G3960","hebrew":"πατάσσω","translit":"patássō","english":"strike"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plague"}]},{"v":7,"text":"When they have finished their testimony, the beast that comes up out of the abyss will make war with them, and overcome them, and kill them.","understanding":"καὶ ὅταν τελέσωσιν τὴν μαρτυρίαν αὐτῶν, “When they have finished their testimony” — τελέω, from the perfect family: to complete; μαρτυρία, from the witness family. They cannot be killed until their task is done. τὸ θηρίον τὸ ἀναβαῖνον ἐκ τῆς ἀβύσσου, “the beast that comes up out of the abyss” — the first mention of θηρίον, the wild beast, introduced as if already known (ch. 13; Daniel 7). ποιήσει μετ᾽ αὐτῶν πόλεμον καὶ νικήσει αὐτοὺς καὶ ἀποκτενεῖ αὐτούς, “will make war with them, and overcome them, and kill them” — νικάω: the beast ‘overcomes’ them as the world overcame Jesus at the cross — and like Him, they will rise.","words":[{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"comes up"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"kill"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Their dead bodies will be in the street of the great city, which spiritually is called Sodom and Egypt, where also their Lord was crucified.","understanding":"καὶ τὸ πτῶμα αὐτῶν ἐπὶ τῆς πλατείας τῆς πόλεως τῆς μεγάλης, “Their dead bodies will be in the street of the great city” — πτῶμα, a corpse: left unburied, the worst dishonour. In Revelation ‘the great city’ is Babylon (16:19; 17:18). ἥτις καλεῖται πνευματικῶς Σόδομα καὶ Αἴγυπτος, “which spiritually is called Sodom and Egypt” — πνευματικῶς: not literally, but by its spiritual character — immoral like Sodom, oppressive like Egypt. ὅπου καὶ ὁ κύριος αὐτῶν ἐσταυρώθη, “where also their Lord was crucified” — Jerusalem, which killed the prophets (Luke 13:34). The great city is every city of man that sets itself against God: Jerusalem, Rome, and every Babylon since.","words":[{"strongs":"G4430","hebrew":"πτῶμα","translit":"ptōma","english":"dead bodies"},{"strongs":"G4113","hebrew":"πλατεῖα","translit":"plateîa","english":"street"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4153","hebrew":"πνευματικῶς","translit":"pneumatikōs","english":"spiritually"},{"strongs":"G4670","hebrew":"Σόδομα","translit":"Sódoma","english":"Sodom"},{"strongs":"G125","hebrew":"Αἴγυπτος","translit":"Aígyptos","english":"Egypt"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4717","hebrew":"σταυρόω","translit":"stauróō","english":"crucified"}]},{"v":9,"text":"From among the peoples, tribes, languages, and nations people will look at their dead bodies for three and a half days, and will not allow their dead bodies to be laid in a tomb.","understanding":"καὶ βλέπουσιν ἐκ τῶν λαῶν καὶ φυλῶν καὶ γλωσσῶν καὶ ἐθνῶν τὸ πτῶμα αὐτῶν, “From among the peoples, tribes, languages, and nations people will look at their dead bodies” — βλέπω, from the see family: the whole world stares. ἡμέρας τρεῖς καὶ ἥμισυ, “for three and a half days” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: a short time, compared with the three and a half years of their witness. καὶ τὰ πτώματα αὐτῶν οὐκ ἀφίουσιν τεθῆναι εἰς μνῆμα, “and will not allow their dead bodies to be laid in a tomb” — contempt even in death.","words":[{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"peoples"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribes"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"look"},{"strongs":"G4430","hebrew":"πτῶμα","translit":"ptōma","english":"dead bodies"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G2255","hebrew":"ἥμισυ","translit":"hḗmisy","english":"half"},{"strongs":"G863","hebrew":"ἀφίημι","translit":"aphíēmi","english":"allow"},{"strongs":"G3418","hebrew":"μνῆμα","translit":"mnēma","english":"tomb"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Those who dwell on the earth rejoice over them, and they will be glad. They will give gifts to one another, because these two prophets tormented those who dwell on the earth.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ κατοικοῦντες ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς χαίρουσιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτοῖς καὶ εὐφραίνονται, “Those who dwell on the earth rejoice over them, and they will be glad” — γῆ, from the world family: the earth-dwellers. A grim parody of Christmas: καὶ δῶρα πέμψουσιν ἀλλήλοις, “They will give gifts to one another” — δῶρον, from the gift family and the offering family: a present, and in worship an offering. ὅτι οὗτοι οἱ δύο προφῆται ἐβασάνισαν τοὺς κατοικοῦντας ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “because these two prophets tormented those who dwell on the earth” — βασανίζω: the truth torments those who refuse it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G2165","hebrew":"εὐφραίνω","translit":"euphraínō","english":"glad"},{"strongs":"G1435","hebrew":"δῶρον","translit":"dōron","english":"gifts"},{"strongs":"G3992","hebrew":"πέμπω","translit":"pémpō","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G928","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","english":"tormented"}]},{"v":11,"text":"After the three and a half days, the breath of life from God entered into them, and they stood on their feet. Great fear fell on those who saw them.","understanding":"καὶ μετὰ τὰς τρεῖς ἡμέρας καὶ ἥμισυ, “After the three and a half days” — ἡμέρα, from the time family. πνεῦμα ζωῆς ἐκ τοῦ θεοῦ εἰσῆλθεν ἐν αὐτοῖς, “the breath of life from God entered into them” — ζωή, from the life family; πνεῦμα, breath or spirit: Ezekiel's valley of dry bones, where God's breath came into the dead and they stood up. καὶ ἔστησαν ἐπὶ τοὺς πόδας αὐτῶν, “and they stood on their feet” — καὶ φόβος μέγας ἐπέπεσεν ἐπὶ τοὺς θεωροῦντας αὐτούς, “Great fear fell on those who saw them” — θεωρέω, from the see family: to gaze, to watch intently, as a spectator.","words":[{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G2255","hebrew":"ἥμισυ","translit":"hḗmisy","english":"half"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"breath"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"entered"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stood"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"Great"},{"strongs":"G1968","hebrew":"ἐπιπίπτω","translit":"epipíptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G2334","hebrew":"θεωρέω","translit":"theōréō","english":"saw"}]},{"v":12,"text":"I heard a loud voice from heaven saying to them, “Come up here!” They went up into heaven in the cloud, and their enemies saw them.","understanding":"καὶ ἤκουσαν φωνῆς μεγάλης ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ λεγούσης αὐτοῖς· Ἀνάβατε ὧδε, “I heard a loud voice from heaven saying to them, ‘Come up here!’” — the words spoken to John (4:1). καὶ ἀνέβησαν εἰς τὸν οὐρανὸν ἐν τῇ νεφέλῃ, “They went up into heaven in the cloud” — like Elijah in the whirlwind, and like Jesus at His ascension (Acts 1:9). καὶ ἐθεώρησαν αὐτοὺς οἱ ἐχθροὶ αὐτῶν, “and their enemies saw them” — θεωρέω, from the see family: the witnesses are publicly vindicated. The path of the witnessing church is the path of its Lord: witness, death, resurrection, ascension.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"Come up"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G2190","hebrew":"ἐχθρός","translit":"echthrós","english":"enemies"},{"strongs":"G2334","hebrew":"θεωρέω","translit":"theōréō","english":"saw"}]},{"v":13,"text":"In that day there was a great earthquake, and a tenth of the city fell. Seven thousand people were killed in the earthquake, and the rest were terrified, and gave glory to the God of heaven.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐν ἐκείνῃ τῇ ὥρᾳ ἐγένετο σεισμὸς μέγας, “In that day there was a great earthquake” — ὥρα, from the time family: literally ‘in that hour’, though the WEB renders it ‘day’. καὶ τὸ δέκατον τῆς πόλεως ἔπεσεν, καὶ ἀπεκτάνθησαν ἐν τῷ σεισμῷ ὀνόματα ἀνθρώπων χιλιάδες ἑπτά, “and a tenth of the city fell. Seven thousand people were killed in the earthquake” — a tenth and seven thousand: limited judgement. καὶ οἱ λοιποὶ ἔμφοβοι ἐγένοντο καὶ ἔδωκαν δόξαν τῷ θεῷ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, “and the rest were terrified, and gave glory to the God of heaven” — the reverse of 9:20: the plagues brought no repentance, but the witness, death and resurrection of the prophets does. In Elijah's day God kept seven thousand faithful; here nine-tenths turn to give God glory.","words":[{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G4578","hebrew":"σεισμός","translit":"seismós","english":"earthquake"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G1182","hebrew":"δέκατος","translit":"dékatos","english":"tenth"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"Seven"},{"strongs":"G5505","hebrew":"χιλιάς","translit":"chiliás","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G1719","hebrew":"ἔμφοβος","translit":"émphobos","english":"terrified"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The second woe is past. Behold, the third woe comes quickly.","understanding":"Ἡ οὐαὶ ἡ δευτέρα ἀπῆλθεν, “The second woe is past” — οὐαί: the sixth trumpet (9:13–11:13). ἰδοὺ ἡ οὐαὶ ἡ τρίτη ἔρχεται ταχύ, “Behold, the third woe comes quickly” — ταχύ: the seventh trumpet, which brings the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"woe"},{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"past"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"comes"},{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"quickly"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The seventh angel sounded, and great voices in heaven followed, saying, “The kingdom of the world has become the Kingdom of our Lord, and of his Christ. He will reign forever and ever!”","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ ἕβδομος ἄγγελος ἐσάλπισεν, “The seventh angel sounded” — σαλπίζω: the last trumpet (1 Corinthians 15:52). καὶ ἐγένοντο φωναὶ μεγάλαι ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “and great voices in heaven followed” — not a new plague but a song. Ἐγένετο ἡ βασιλεία τοῦ κόσμου τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν καὶ τοῦ χριστοῦ αὐτοῦ, “The kingdom of the world has become the Kingdom of our Lord, and of his Christ” — κόσμος, from the world family: the whole ordered system of human life, now under new ownership; βασιλεία, singular: one kingdom. Psalm 2's ‘the LORD and his Anointed’. καὶ βασιλεύσει εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “He will reign forever and ever!” — βασιλεύω, to rule; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. Handel set this verse in the Hallelujah Chorus.","words":[{"strongs":"G1442","hebrew":"ἕβδομος","translit":"hébdomos","english":"seventh"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4537","hebrew":"σαλπίζω","translit":"salpízō","english":"sounded"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voices"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reign"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":16,"text":"The twenty-four elders, who sit on their thrones before God’s throne, fell on their faces and worshiped God,","understanding":"καὶ οἱ εἴκοσι τέσσαρες πρεσβύτεροι οἱ ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ καθήμενοι ἐπὶ τοὺς θρόνους αὐτῶν, “The twenty-four elders, who sit on their thrones before God’s throne” — πρεσβύτερος. ἔπεσαν ἐπὶ τὰ πρόσωπα αὐτῶν καὶ προσεκύνησαν τῷ θεῷ, “fell on their faces and worshiped God” — προσκυνέω: the response to God's reign is worship.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sit"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"thrones"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"faces"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"}]},{"v":17,"text":"saying: “We give you thanks, Lord God, the Almighty, the one who is and who was; because you have taken your great power, and reigned.","understanding":"λέγοντες· Εὐχαριστοῦμέν σοι, κύριε ὁ θεὸς ὁ παντοκράτωρ, “saying: ‘We give you thanks, Lord God, the Almighty’” — εὐχαριστέω, from the pray family: to give thanks. ὁ ὢν καὶ ὁ ἦν, “the one who is and who was” — the title of 1:4 and 4:8, but now without ‘and who is to come’: He has come. ὅτι εἴληφας τὴν δύναμίν σου τὴν μεγάλην καὶ ἐβασίλευσας, “because you have taken your great power, and reigned” — δύναμις, from the power family: inherent might; βασιλεύω, aorist: You have begun to reign.","words":[{"strongs":"G2168","hebrew":"εὐχαριστέω","translit":"eucharistéō","english":"give thanks"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"taken"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reigned"}]},{"v":18,"text":"The nations were angry, and your wrath came, as did the time for the dead to be judged, and to give your bondservants the prophets, their reward, as well as to the saints, and those who fear your name, to the small and the great; and to destroy those who destroy the earth.”","understanding":"καὶ τὰ ἔθνη ὠργίσθησαν, καὶ ἦλθεν ἡ ὀργή σου, “The nations were angry, and your wrath came” — ὀργίζω and ὀργή, two members of the wrath family set side by side: the nations' rage (Psalm 2) met by God's settled, just wrath. καὶ ὁ καιρὸς τῶν νεκρῶν κριθῆναι, “as did the time for the dead to be judged” — καιρός, from the time family: the decisive appointed season; κρίνω, from the judge family. καὶ δοῦναι τὸν μισθὸν τοῖς δούλοις σου τοῖς προφήταις καὶ τοῖς ἁγίοις καὶ τοῖς φοβουμένοις τὸ ὄνομά σου, “and to give your bondservants the prophets, their reward, as well as to the saints, and those who fear your name” — δοῦλος, from the servant family; μισθός, wages, reward. καὶ διαφθεῖραι τοὺς διαφθείροντας τὴν γῆν, “and to destroy those who destroy the earth” — διαφθείρω twice: God's judgement fits the crime; γῆ, from the world family. God cares for His creation. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G3710","hebrew":"ὀργίζω","translit":"orgízō","english":"angry"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"reward"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"small"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G1311","hebrew":"διαφθείρω","translit":"diaphtheírō","english":"destroy"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":19,"text":"God’s temple that is in heaven was opened, and the ark of the Lord’s covenant was seen in his temple. Lightnings, sounds, thunders, an earthquake, and great hail followed.","understanding":"καὶ ἠνοίγη ὁ ναὸς τοῦ θεοῦ ὁ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “God’s temple that is in heaven was opened” — ναός: the inner sanctuary, once closed to all but the high priest once a year, is opened. καὶ ὤφθη ἡ κιβωτὸς τῆς διαθήκης αὐτοῦ ἐν τῷ ναῷ αὐτοῦ, “and the ark of the Lord’s covenant was seen in his temple” — ὁράω, from the see family; κιβωτός, the ark, lost when Babylon destroyed Jerusalem; διαθήκη, covenant. God's covenant faithfulness is on display. καὶ ἐγένοντο ἀστραπαὶ καὶ φωναὶ καὶ βρονταὶ καὶ σεισμὸς καὶ χάλαζα μεγάλη, “Lightnings, sounds, thunders, an earthquake, and great hail followed” — the throne-storm of 4:5, growing again (8:5; 16:18–21).","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G2787","hebrew":"κιβωτός","translit":"kibōtós","english":"ark"},{"strongs":"G1242","hebrew":"διαθήκη","translit":"diathḗkē","english":"covenant"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G796","hebrew":"ἀστραπή","translit":"astrapḗ","english":"Lightnings"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sounds"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunders"},{"strongs":"G4578","hebrew":"σεισμός","translit":"seismós","english":"earthquake"},{"strongs":"G5464","hebrew":"χάλαζα","translit":"chálaza","english":"hail"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Measured and Protected","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture John walking through a temple with a long measuring reed, carefully measuring the sanctuary, the altar and each worshipper. Outside, in the open court, crowds of soldiers trample through. But the measured ones inside are marked and kept.","meaning":"“Rise, and measure God’s temple, and the altar, and those who worship in it. Leave out the court which is outside of the temple... They will tread the holy city under foot for forty-two months” (vv. 1–2). God's people may be trampled outwardly, but He knows and protects every true worshipper."},{"title":"Two Lampstands in Sackcloth","verseRef":"v. 3-6","image":"Picture two prophets in rough black sackcloth standing in the streets of a hostile city, speaking God's word day after day. Fire comes from their mouths when enemies attack; the sky locks shut at their word; but they keep speaking, like two lamps burning in the night.","meaning":"“I will give power to my two witnesses, and they will prophesy one thousand two hundred sixty days, clothed in sackcloth” (v. 3). “These are the two olive trees and the two lamp stands” (v. 4). The church's calling is to bear witness, humbly and powerfully, until its task is done."},{"title":"Three and a Half Days","verseRef":"v. 7-12","image":"Picture the two witnesses lying dead in the street, the world gathered round on screens and in crowds, celebrating, sending each other presents. Then on the fourth morning the bodies stir — breath enters them — they stand up — and a voice from heaven calls, ‘Come up here!’ and they rise into the cloud as their enemies watch.","meaning":"“After the three and a half days, the breath of life from God entered into them, and they stood on their feet” (v. 11). The world's apparent victory over God's witnesses is brief. Like their Lord, they die, rise and ascend."},{"title":"The Last Trumpet","verseRef":"v. 15-19","image":"Picture the seventh angel lifting the trumpet to his lips. The whole universe waits for the final blast of judgement — and instead, heaven bursts into song: the kingdom of the world has become the kingdom of our Lord. The temple in heaven swings open, and there is the ark of His covenant.","meaning":"“The kingdom of the world has become the Kingdom of our Lord, and of his Christ. He will reign forever and ever!” (v. 15). The end of history is not chaos but coronation. God keeps His covenant."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Temple Measured (v. 1-2)","body":"John is given a measuring rod and told to measure the temple of God, the altar and the worshippers, but to leave out the outer court, which is given to the nations, who will trample the holy city for forty-two months."},{"label":"The Two Witnesses (v. 3-6)","body":"God's two witnesses, clothed in sackcloth, will prophesy for 1,260 days; they are the two olive trees and two lampstands before the Lord; fire from their mouths consumes their enemies; they can shut the sky, turn water to blood and strike the earth with plagues."},{"label":"Death, Resurrection and Ascension (v. 7-14)","body":"When their testimony is finished, the beast from the abyss kills them, and their bodies lie in the street of the great city, spiritually called Sodom and Egypt, where their Lord was crucified; the world gloats for three and a half days; then God's breath enters them, they stand up and ascend to heaven in a cloud; a great earthquake strikes the city, and the survivors give glory to God; the second woe is past."},{"label":"The Seventh Trumpet (v. 15-19)","body":"The seventh trumpet sounds, and heaven proclaims that the kingdom of the world has become the kingdom of our Lord and His Christ; the elders give thanks that God has taken His power and begun to reign, that the time has come to judge the dead and reward His servants; God's temple in heaven is opened, the ark of the covenant is seen, and the storm of the throne breaks out."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Measuring the Temple","paragraphs":["Chapter 11 continues the interlude between the sixth and seventh trumpets. John is given a reed and told, “Rise, and measure God’s temple, and the altar, and those who worship in it” (v. 1). In Ezekiel 40–48 and Zechariah 2, measuring a temple or city marks it as belonging to God and under His protection. Here the worshippers themselves are measured.","But the outer court is not to be measured, “for it has been given to the nations. They will tread the holy city under foot for forty-two months” (v. 2). Some read this literally, of a temple rebuilt in Jerusalem in the last days. The strongest reading, in a book where the temple is God's people (3:12) and in a New Testament that calls the church God's temple (“Don’t you know that you are a temple of God, and that God’s Spirit lives in you?” 1 Corinthians 3:16), is that God's people are spiritually protected (the inner sanctuary) while outwardly exposed to persecution (the outer court).","The period — forty-two months, or 1,260 days (v. 3), or ‘a time, and times, and half a time’ (12:14) — comes from Daniel, where it is the time of the oppressor's power over God's people. All three expressions equal three and a half years: a broken seven, a limited time of trouble. Many readers see it as a literal final period; many others as a picture of the whole time between Christ's ascension and return, when the church bears witness under pressure."]},{"heading":"The Two Witnesses","paragraphs":["God says, “I will give power to my two witnesses, and they will prophesy one thousand two hundred sixty days, clothed in sackcloth” (v. 3). Who are they? Some think they are two individual prophets who will appear before Christ's return — perhaps Moses and Elijah (whose miracles they repeat, v. 6), or Enoch and Elijah (who never died). Others think they represent the witnessing church.","Several things point to the second reading. They are called “the two olive trees and the two lamp stands” (v. 4), and Revelation has already told us that lampstands are churches (1:20). There are two because the Law required two witnesses for valid testimony. They minister for exactly the same period as the trampling of the holy city — the time of the church's existence in a hostile world. The whole world sees their bodies (v. 9). And they follow the exact path of Jesus: witness, rejection, death in the great city, resurrection after three days, ascension in a cloud. The church is the body of Christ, and its story mirrors His.","Their power is the power of Moses and Elijah: fire from their mouths (God's word), the sky shut, water turned to blood (vv. 5–6). They are invincible until “they have finished their testimony” (v. 7). God's witnesses cannot be silenced until God's purpose for them is complete."]},{"heading":"Killed, Raised, Vindicated","paragraphs":["When their testimony is finished, “the beast that comes up out of the abyss will make war with them, and overcome them, and kill them” (v. 7). Their bodies lie unburied “in the street of the great city, which spiritually is called Sodom and Egypt, where also their Lord was crucified” (v. 8). The great city is Jerusalem, which killed the prophets and crucified its Lord; but it is also Sodom (immorality) and Egypt (oppression), and later Babylon (17:18). It is the city of man in rebellion against God, wherever it is found.","The world celebrates: “Those who dwell on the earth rejoice over them, and they will be glad. They will give gifts to one another, because these two prophets tormented those who dwell on the earth” (v. 10). The truth they spoke was torment to those who would not repent. Their death seems to prove the world right.","But “After the three and a half days, the breath of life from God entered into them, and they stood on their feet” (v. 11). They hear a voice, “Come up here!”, and ascend in a cloud while their enemies watch (v. 12). Then an earthquake strikes, and “the rest were terrified, and gave glory to the God of heaven” (v. 13). This is the turning point: where judgements alone produced no repentance (9:20–21), the costly witness and vindication of God's people does. The church's faithful suffering is God's way of winning the nations."]},{"heading":"The Kingdom of Our Lord","paragraphs":["Then the seventh trumpet sounds — the last trumpet. We expect a final catastrophe; instead we hear a song: “The kingdom of the world has become the Kingdom of our Lord, and of his Christ. He will reign forever and ever!” (v. 15). The whole sweep of history is summed up in one sentence. The kingdom does not become God's by gradual improvement, but by His decisive act. Paul wrote, “For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet” (1 Corinthians 15:25).","The elders give thanks to God “the one who is and who was” (v. 17) — the phrase ‘who is to come’ is gone, because He has come. They celebrate the end: “The nations were angry, and your wrath came, as did the time for the dead to be judged, and to give your bondservants the prophets, their reward, as well as to the saints, and those who fear your name, to the small and the great; and to destroy those who destroy the earth” (v. 18). Judgement and reward together; and God's care for the earth He made.","Finally God's temple in heaven is opened, and “the ark of the Lord’s covenant was seen in his temple” (v. 19). The ark, lost since the Babylonian conquest, symbolised God's presence and His covenant promises. Now it is seen: God has kept every promise. The seventh trumpet brings us to the end; the following chapters go back to show, from new angles, the conflict that leads there."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Witnesses to the Ends of the Earth","paragraphs":["Before His ascension, Jesus told His disciples, “But you will receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you. You will be witnesses to me in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the uttermost parts of the earth” (Acts 1:8). The two witnesses are that commission carried out in the face of opposition, empowered by the Spirit (the oil of the olive trees).","He warned, “Yes, and you will be brought before governors and kings for my sake, for a testimony to them and to the nations” (Matthew 10:18), and lamented, “Jerusalem, Jerusalem, that kills the prophets, and stones those who are sent to her!” (Luke 13:34) — the great city where their Lord was crucified.","He spoke of the trampling of Jerusalem “until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled” (Luke 21:24), and of Elijah's ministry “when the sky was shut up three years and six months” (Luke 4:25). And Paul describes the end the seventh trumpet announces: “Then the end comes, when he will deliver up the Kingdom to God, even the Father; when he will have abolished all rule and all authority and power” (1 Corinthians 15:24)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Measure","hebrew":"μετρέω","translit":"metréō","strongs":"G3354 (reed κάλαμος, kálamos, G2563)","verses":"v. 1, 2; 21:15-17","insight":"Strong's: “to measure (i.e. ascertain in size by a fixed standard); by implication, to admeasure (i.e. allot by rule).” μετρέω means both to take measurements and to allot a portion. To measure something in the prophets is to claim it, mark its boundaries and set it apart. John measures the temple and its worshippers (11:1); at the end an angel measures the new Jerusalem with a golden reed (21:15–17). What God measures, He keeps."},{"english":"Spiritually","hebrew":"πνευματικῶς","translit":"pneumatikōs","strongs":"G4153 (Sodom Σόδομα, Sódoma, G4670)","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “non-physically, i.e. divinely, figuratively.” πνευματικῶς tells us how to read ‘the great city’: not by its map but by its character. It is ‘Sodom’ in its immorality and ‘Egypt’ in its oppression of God's people, and it is the place ‘where also their Lord was crucified’. This one word is a key to reading Revelation: its cities, beasts and numbers are to be understood spiritually — by what they reveal — not necessarily by literal geography."},{"english":"Dead bodies","hebrew":"πτῶμα","translit":"ptōma","strongs":"G4430 (tomb μνῆμα, mnēma, G3418)","verses":"v. 8, 9","insight":"Strong's: “a ruin, i.e. (specially), lifeless body (corpse, carrion).” πτῶμα, from πίπτω (to fall), is a fallen body, a carcass. To leave a body unburied was the ultimate disgrace in the ancient world. The witnesses' enemies refuse them burial and gloat over them. Yet God's breath enters those corpses, and they stand. What the world treats as carrion, God raises to glory."},{"english":"Reign","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","strongs":"G936 (kingdom βασιλεία, basileía, G932)","verses":"v. 15, 17; 5:10; 20:4, 6; 22:5","insight":"Strong's: “to rule (literally or figuratively).” βασιλεύω, from βασιλεύς (king), is the verb of kingship. At the seventh trumpet, God ‘has taken his great power, and reigned’ (v. 17) — the aorist marks the moment His reign is openly established. And the promise of the book is that His people will reign with Him: ‘we will reign on the earth’ (5:10); ‘they will reign forever and ever’ (22:5). Those who suffer with the Lamb will reign with Him."},{"english":"Ark","hebrew":"κιβωτός","translit":"kibōtós","strongs":"G2787 (covenant διαθήκη, diathḗkē, G1242)","verses":"v. 19","insight":"Strong's: “a box, i.e. the sacred ark and that of Noah.” κιβωτός is used of Noah's ark and of the ark of the covenant — both places of God's saving presence. The ark of the covenant held the tablets of the Law and was covered by the mercy seat, where blood was sprinkled on the Day of Atonement. It disappeared when Babylon destroyed the temple. Its appearance in heaven at the seventh trumpet declares that God's covenant faithfulness has never been lost."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you the calling and destiny of the witnessing church.","items":[{"title":"You Are Measured","body":"John measures the temple and “those who worship in it” (v. 1). The Spirit wants you to know that God knows you by name and keeps you, even when the world tramples the outer court. Your outward circumstances may be hard, but your life is hidden with Christ in God."},{"title":"Witness Until It Is Finished","body":"The two witnesses cannot be stopped until their work is done; only “When they have finished their testimony” (v. 7) can the beast touch them. The Spirit calls you to faithful witness, clothed in humility, empowered by His oil. You are immortal until your work is done. Do not be afraid."},{"title":"The Kingdom Is Coming","body":"“The kingdom of the world has become the Kingdom of our Lord, and of his Christ” (v. 15). The Spirit lifts your eyes to the end of the story. Whatever kingdom seems to rule now — politics, money, culture, evil — it will become the kingdom of Christ. Live now as a citizen of that kingdom."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Witness Check","body":"Who in your life has heard your testimony about Jesus this year (v. 3)? Who has not?"},{"title":"The Sackcloth Check","body":"Is your witness marked by humility and grief over sin, or by pride and anger (v. 3)?"},{"title":"The Kingdom Check","body":"Which ‘kingdom of the world’ do you most look to for security — government, money, success, a person (v. 15)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Two by Two","body":"Find one other believer and agree to be ‘two witnesses’ this month: pray together weekly for people you know who do not follow Jesus, and look for one opportunity each to share your faith."},{"title":"Sing the Last Trumpet","body":"Listen to or sing a setting of Revelation 11:15 (such as the Hallelujah Chorus) this week. As you do, name three things in the world that make you anxious, and declare over each: ‘This will become the kingdom of our Lord and of His Christ.’"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord God Almighty, You are, and You were, and You have come. Thank You that You measure and keep Your people, even when the world tramples the outer court. Make me a faithful witness, clothed in humility, filled with Your Spirit like an olive tree full of oil, shining like a lampstand. Let Your word be fire in my mouth. Give me courage to keep testifying until my work is done, and to trust You even if the world rejoices over my defeat. Thank You that the breath of life from You raised the witnesses, as it raised Jesus, and that it will raise me. I give You thanks that the kingdom of the world will become the kingdom of our Lord and of His Christ, and He will reign forever and ever. Reign in me now. Thank You that You keep Your covenant, and that You will judge the dead, reward Your servants, and destroy those who destroy Your earth. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Elijah Shuts the Sky","ref":"1 Kings 17:1; 18:1-46","relatedVerses":"v. 5-6","summary":"Elijah stood before King Ahab and declared that there would be no dew or rain except at his word. For three and a half years the sky was shut. Then on Mount Carmel Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal; fire fell from heaven and consumed his sacrifice, and the people cried, ‘The LORD, he is God!’ Then he prayed, and rain came. The two witnesses minister in Elijah's power — shutting the sky, calling down fire — and like Elijah, they are finally taken up into heaven."},{"title":"The Valley of Dry Bones","ref":"Ezekiel 37:1-14","relatedVerses":"v. 11","summary":"The Spirit set Ezekiel down in a valley full of dry bones and asked, ‘Can these bones live?’ At God's command Ezekiel prophesied, and the bones came together, covered with sinews and flesh. Then he prophesied to the breath, and breath came into them, and they stood on their feet, a vast army. God said this was the whole house of Israel, which He would raise from its graves. In Revelation 11 the same breath of life from God enters the dead witnesses, and they stand on their feet."},{"title":"The Ascension of Jesus","ref":"Acts 1:6-11","relatedVerses":"v. 12","summary":"Forty days after His resurrection, Jesus told His disciples they would be His witnesses to the ends of the earth. Then He was taken up before their eyes, and a cloud received Him out of their sight. Two men in white said, “This Jesus, who was received up from you into the sky, will come back in the same way as you saw him going into the sky” (Acts 1:11). The two witnesses follow the same path: after their witness, death and resurrection, they hear ‘Come up here!’ and go up in a cloud."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":12,"chapterTitle":"The Woman, the Child and the Dragon","themeTitle":"“They Overcame Him Because of the Lamb’s Blood”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"A great sign was seen in heaven: a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars.","understanding":"Καὶ σημεῖον μέγα ὤφθη ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “A great sign was seen in heaven” — σημεῖον, from the signs family: a sign that points beyond itself, as distinct from τέρας, a wonder; ὁράω, from the see family. Chapters 12–14 step back to show the spiritual war behind all of history. γυνὴ περιβεβλημένη τὸν ἥλιον, καὶ ἡ σελήνη ὑποκάτω τῶν ποδῶν αὐτῆς, “a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet” — καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς κεφαλῆς αὐτῆς στέφανος ἀστέρων δώδεκα, “and on her head a crown of twelve stars” — στέφανος, from the crown family: a victor's wreath. Joseph dreamed of the sun, moon and eleven stars bowing to him — his father, mother and brothers, Israel. The woman is the people of God, who gave birth to the Messiah; Christians have also seen in her Mary, His mother.","words":[{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"sign"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G4582","hebrew":"σελήνη","translit":"selḗnē","english":"moon"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"}]},{"v":2,"text":"She was with child. She cried out in pain, laboring to give birth.","understanding":"καὶ ἐν γαστρὶ ἔχουσα, “She was with child” — καὶ κράζει ὠδίνουσα καὶ βασανιζομένη τεκεῖν, “She cried out in pain, laboring to give birth” — ὠδίνω, the pains of childbirth; βασανίζω, torment; τίκτω, from the birth family: to bring forth, as distinct from γεννάω, to beget. Isaiah and Micah pictured Zion in labour, bringing forth the Messiah and a new people. The long suffering of Israel waiting for the Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G1064","hebrew":"γαστήρ","translit":"gastḗr","english":"with child"},{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried out"},{"strongs":"G5605","hebrew":"ὠδίνω","translit":"ōdínō","english":"pain"},{"strongs":"G928","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","english":"laboring"},{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"give birth"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Another sign was seen in heaven. Behold, a great red dragon, having seven heads and ten horns, and on his heads seven crowns.","understanding":"καὶ ὤφθη ἄλλο σημεῖον ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “Another sign was seen in heaven” — ἄλλος, from the another family: another of the same kind, a second sign alongside the first, as distinct from ἕτερος, another of a different kind; σημεῖον, from the signs family; ὁράω, from the see family. καὶ ἰδοὺ δράκων μέγας πυρρός, “Behold, a great red dragon” — δράκων, a monstrous serpent, identified in verse 9 as Satan; red, the colour of blood and murder (6:4). ἔχων κεφαλὰς ἑπτὰ καὶ κέρατα δέκα, “having seven heads and ten horns” — like Daniel's fourth beast: total, worldly power. καὶ ἐπὶ τὰς κεφαλὰς αὐτοῦ ἑπτὰ διαδήματα, “and on his heads seven crowns” — διάδημα, from the crown family: the royal diadem of ruling authority, not the στέφανος of victory. He claims to rule; the woman has won.","words":[{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"sign"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"seen"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4450","hebrew":"πυῤῥός","translit":"pyrrhós","english":"red"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G1176","hebrew":"δέκα","translit":"déka","english":"ten"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G1238","hebrew":"διάδημα","translit":"diádēma","english":"crowns"}]},{"v":4,"text":"His tail drew one third of the stars of the sky, and threw them to the earth. The dragon stood before the woman who was about to give birth, so that when she gave birth he might devour her child.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ οὐρὰ αὐτοῦ σύρει τὸ τρίτον τῶν ἀστέρων τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, καὶ ἔβαλεν αὐτοὺς εἰς τὴν γῆν, “His tail drew one third of the stars of the sky, and threw them to the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. Daniel's little horn threw down some of the host of heaven; many see here the angels who fell with Satan. καὶ ὁ δράκων ἕστηκεν ἐνώπιον τῆς γυναικὸς τῆς μελλούσης τεκεῖν, “The dragon stood before the woman who was about to give birth” — τίκτω, from the birth family. ἵνα ὅταν τέκῃ τὸ τέκνον αὐτῆς καταφάγῃ, “so that when she gave birth he might devour her child” — τέκνον, from the children family: Herod's slaughter of the infants of Bethlehem was one face of this ancient hatred.","words":[{"strongs":"G3769","hebrew":"οὐρά","translit":"ourá","english":"tail"},{"strongs":"G4951","hebrew":"σύρω","translit":"sýrō","english":"drew"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"stars"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"threw"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stood"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"give birth"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G2719","hebrew":"κατεσθίω","translit":"katesthíō","english":"devour"}]},{"v":5,"text":"She gave birth to a son, a male child, who is to rule all the nations with a rod of iron. Her child was caught up to God, and to his throne.","understanding":"καὶ ἔτεκεν υἱὸν ἄρσεν, “She gave birth to a son, a male child” — τίκτω, from the birth family. ὃς μέλλει ποιμαίνειν πάντα τὰ ἔθνη ἐν ῥάβδῳ σιδηρᾷ, “who is to rule all the nations with a rod of iron” — Psalm 2: this child is the Messiah. ποιμαίνω, to shepherd and rule. καὶ ἡρπάσθη τὸ τέκνον αὐτῆς πρὸς τὸν θεὸν καὶ πρὸς τὸν θρόνον αὐτοῦ, “Her child was caught up to God, and to his throne” — ἁρπάζω, to snatch away; τέκνον, from the children family. The whole life of Jesus, from birth to ascension, in one sentence: the dragon failed to devour Him; the cross became the path to the throne.","words":[{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"gave birth"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G730","hebrew":"ἄῤῥην","translit":"árrhēn","english":"male"},{"strongs":"G4165","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"rod"},{"strongs":"G4603","hebrew":"σιδήρεος","translit":"sidḗreos","english":"iron"},{"strongs":"G726","hebrew":"ἁρπάζω","translit":"harpázō","english":"caught up"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"child"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"}]},{"v":6,"text":"The woman fled into the wilderness, where she has a place prepared by God, that there they may nourish her one thousand two hundred sixty days.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ γυνὴ ἔφυγεν εἰς τὴν ἔρημον, “The woman fled into the wilderness” — ἔρημος: as Israel fled from Egypt into the wilderness, and Elijah fled from Jezebel, and Mary and Joseph fled to Egypt. Now the woman is the church after Christ's ascension. ὅπου ἔχει ἐκεῖ τόπον ἡτοιμασμένον ἀπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ, “where she has a place prepared by God” — ἵνα ἐκεῖ τρέφωσιν αὐτὴν ἡμέρας χιλίας διακοσίας ἑξήκοντα, “that there they may nourish her one thousand two hundred sixty days” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: the same period as the two witnesses (11:3). The wilderness is a place of danger, but also of God's provision, like manna.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"fled"},{"strongs":"G2048","hebrew":"ἔρημος","translit":"érēmos","english":"wilderness"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5142","hebrew":"τρέφω","translit":"tréphō","english":"nourish"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"one thousand"},{"strongs":"G1250","hebrew":"διακόσιοι","translit":"diakósioi","english":"two hundred"},{"strongs":"G1835","hebrew":"ἑξήκοντα","translit":"hexḗkonta","english":"sixty"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"}]},{"v":7,"text":"There was war in the sky. Michael and his angels made war on the dragon. The dragon and his angels made war.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐγένετο πόλεμος ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “There was war in the sky” — πόλεμος. ὁ Μιχαὴλ καὶ οἱ ἄγγελοι αὐτοῦ τοῦ πολεμῆσαι μετὰ τοῦ δράκοντος, “Michael and his angels made war on the dragon” — Μιχαήλ, ‘Who is like God?’: in Daniel the great prince who protects God's people. καὶ ὁ δράκων ἐπολέμησεν καὶ οἱ ἄγγελοι αὐτοῦ, “The dragon and his angels made war” — the heavenly side of the battle won on earth at the cross (v. 11).","words":[{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G3413","hebrew":"Μιχαήλ","translit":"Michaḗl","english":"Michael"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4170","hebrew":"πολεμέω","translit":"poleméō","english":"made war"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"}]},{"v":8,"text":"They didn’t prevail, neither was a place found for them any more in heaven.","understanding":"καὶ οὐκ ἴσχυσεν, “They didn’t prevail” — ἰσχύω, from the power family: to have strength, to be able; the dragon's force is not enough. οὐδὲ τόπος εὑρέθη αὐτῶν ἔτι ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “neither was a place found for them any more in heaven” — εὑρίσκω. The woman has a place prepared by God (v. 6); the dragon loses his.","words":[{"strongs":"G2480","hebrew":"ἰσχύω","translit":"ischýō","english":"prevail"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"}]},{"v":9,"text":"The great dragon was thrown down, the old serpent, he who is called the devil and Satan, the deceiver of the whole world. He was thrown down to the earth, and his angels were thrown down with him.","understanding":"καὶ ἐβλήθη ὁ δράκων ὁ μέγας, “The great dragon was thrown down” — βάλλω, passive: thrown out by God. ὁ ὄφις ὁ ἀρχαῖος, “the old serpent” — the serpent of Eden. ὁ καλούμενος Διάβολος καὶ ὁ Σατανᾶς, “he who is called the devil and Satan” — διάβολος, the slanderer (Greek); Σατανᾶς, the adversary (Hebrew). ὁ πλανῶν τὴν οἰκουμένην ὅλην, “the deceiver of the whole world” — πλανάω, present tense: he keeps on deceiving; οἰκουμένη, from the world family: the inhabited earth. ἐβλήθη εἰς τὴν γῆν, “He was thrown down to the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. Five names, one enemy: dragon, serpent, devil, Satan, deceiver.","words":[{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown down"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G744","hebrew":"ἀρχαῖος","translit":"archaîos","english":"old"},{"strongs":"G3789","hebrew":"ὄφις","translit":"óphis","english":"serpent"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceiver"},{"strongs":"G3625","hebrew":"οἰκουμένη","translit":"oikouménē","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"}]},{"v":10,"text":"I heard a loud voice in heaven, saying, “Now the salvation, the power, and the Kingdom of our God, and the authority of his Christ has come; for the accuser of our brothers has been thrown down, who accuses them before our God day and night.","understanding":"καὶ ἤκουσα φωνὴν μεγάλην ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ λέγουσαν, “I heard a loud voice in heaven, saying” — Ἄρτι ἐγένετο ἡ σωτηρία καὶ ἡ δύναμις καὶ ἡ βασιλεία τοῦ θεοῦ ἡμῶν καὶ ἡ ἐξουσία τοῦ χριστοῦ αὐτοῦ, “Now the salvation, the power, and the Kingdom of our God, and the authority of his Christ has come” — δύναμις and ἐξουσία, two members of the power family: God's inherent might and Christ's rightful authority. ἄρτι, now: through the cross and resurrection. ὅτι ἐβλήθη ὁ κατήγωρ τῶν ἀδελφῶν ἡμῶν, “for the accuser of our brothers has been thrown down” — κατήγωρ, the prosecutor in court. ὁ κατηγορῶν αὐτοὺς ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ ἡμῶν ἡμέρας καὶ νυκτός, “who accuses them before our God day and night” — ἡμέρα, from the time family. As in Job and Zechariah, Satan's role is to accuse. His case is now thrown out of court.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G2725","hebrew":"κατήγορος","translit":"katḗgoros","english":"accuser"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown down"},{"strongs":"G2723","hebrew":"κατηγορέω","translit":"katēgoréō","english":"accuses"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"}]},{"v":11,"text":"They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death.","understanding":"καὶ αὐτοὶ ἐνίκησαν αὐτὸν διὰ τὸ αἷμα τοῦ ἀρνίου, “They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood” — νικάω; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The accuser's charges are answered by the blood: their sins are paid for. καὶ διὰ τὸν λόγον τῆς μαρτυρίας αὐτῶν, “and because of the word of their testimony” — λόγος, from the word family; μαρτυρία, from the witness family. καὶ οὐκ ἠγάπησαν τὴν ψυχὴν αὐτῶν ἄχρι θανάτου, “They didn’t love their life, even to death” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; ψυχή, from the life family: their natural life, as distinct from ζωή, the life of God. Three weapons: the blood of Christ, the word of witness, and a love for Him greater than love of life.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcame"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb’s"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Therefore rejoice, heavens, and you who dwell in them. Woe to the earth and to the sea, because the devil has gone down to you, having great wrath, knowing that he has but a short time.”","understanding":"διὰ τοῦτο εὐφραίνεσθε, οὐρανοὶ καὶ οἱ ἐν αὐτοῖς σκηνοῦντες, “Therefore rejoice, heavens, and you who dwell in them” — σκηνόω, to dwell as in a tent: those who belong to heaven, in contrast to ‘those who dwell on the earth’. οὐαὶ τὴν γῆν καὶ τὴν θάλασσαν, “Woe to the earth and to the sea” — οὐαί; γῆ, from the world family. ὅτι κατέβη ὁ διάβολος πρὸς ὑμᾶς ἔχων θυμὸν μέγαν, “because the devil has gone down to you, having great wrath” — θυμός, from the wrath family: hot, passionate rage, as distinct from God's settled ὀργή (6:16). εἰδὼς ὅτι ὀλίγον καιρὸν ἔχει, “knowing that he has but a short time” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family; καιρός, from the time family: his season is short. The devil is furious because he is defeated.","words":[{"strongs":"G2165","hebrew":"εὐφραίνω","translit":"euphraínō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G4637","hebrew":"σκηνόω","translit":"skēnóō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"Woe"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"gone down"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"short"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"}]},{"v":13,"text":"When the dragon saw that he was thrown down to the earth, he persecuted the woman who gave birth to the male child.","understanding":"Καὶ ὅτε εἶδεν ὁ δράκων ὅτι ἐβλήθη εἰς τὴν γῆν, “When the dragon saw that he was thrown down to the earth” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); γῆ, from the world family. ἐδίωξεν τὴν γυναῖκα ἥτις ἔτεκεν τὸν ἄρσενα, “he persecuted the woman who gave birth to the male child” — διώκω, to pursue, to persecute; τίκτω, from the birth family. Unable to reach Christ, Satan attacks His people. Jesus asked Saul, ‘why are you persecuting me?’ — to persecute the church is to persecute Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown down"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"persecuted"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G5088","hebrew":"τίκτω","translit":"tíktō","english":"gave birth"},{"strongs":"G730","hebrew":"ἄῤῥην","translit":"árrhēn","english":"male"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Two wings of the great eagle were given to the woman, that she might fly into the wilderness to her place, so that she might be nourished for a time, and times, and half a time, from the face of the serpent.","understanding":"καὶ ἐδόθησαν τῇ γυναικὶ αἱ δύο πτέρυγες τοῦ ἀετοῦ τοῦ μεγάλου, “Two wings of the great eagle were given to the woman” — God told Israel at Sinai, ‘I bore you on eagles’ wings, and brought you to myself.’ ἵνα πέτηται εἰς τὴν ἔρημον εἰς τὸν τόπον αὐτῆς, “that she might fly into the wilderness to her place” — ὅπου τρέφεται ἐκεῖ καιρὸν καὶ καιροὺς καὶ ἥμισυ καιροῦ, “so that she might be nourished for a time, and times, and half a time” — καιρός, from the time family, three times: Daniel's phrase, three and a half ‘times’, the same as the 1,260 days of verse 6. ἀπὸ προσώπου τοῦ ὄφεως, “from the face of the serpent.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"Two"},{"strongs":"G4420","hebrew":"πτέρυξ","translit":"ptéryx","english":"wings"},{"strongs":"G105","hebrew":"ἀετός","translit":"aetós","english":"eagle"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4072","hebrew":"πέτομαι","translit":"pétomai","english":"fly"},{"strongs":"G2048","hebrew":"ἔρημος","translit":"érēmos","english":"wilderness"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"},{"strongs":"G5142","hebrew":"τρέφω","translit":"tréphō","english":"nourished"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G2255","hebrew":"ἥμισυ","translit":"hḗmisy","english":"half"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G3789","hebrew":"ὄφις","translit":"óphis","english":"serpent"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The serpent spewed water out of his mouth after the woman like a river, that he might cause her to be carried away by the stream.","understanding":"καὶ ἔβαλεν ὁ ὄφις ἐκ τοῦ στόματος αὐτοῦ ὀπίσω τῆς γυναικὸς ὕδωρ ὡς ποταμόν, “The serpent spewed water out of his mouth after the woman like a river” — a flood of lies and persecution from the mouth of the deceiver. ἵνα αὐτὴν ποταμοφόρητον ποιήσῃ, “that he might cause her to be carried away by the stream” — ποταμοφόρητος, swept away by a river: the psalmist prayed to be rescued from overwhelming waters.","words":[{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"spewed"},{"strongs":"G3789","hebrew":"ὄφις","translit":"óphis","english":"serpent"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"river"},{"strongs":"G4216","hebrew":"ποταμοφόρητος","translit":"potamophórētos","english":"carried away by the stream"}]},{"v":16,"text":"The earth helped the woman, and the earth opened its mouth and swallowed up the river which the dragon spewed out of his mouth.","understanding":"καὶ ἐβοήθησεν ἡ γῆ τῇ γυναικί, “The earth helped the woman” — βοηθέω, to run to help; γῆ, from the world family. Creation itself takes God's side. καὶ ἤνοιξεν ἡ γῆ τὸ στόμα αὐτῆς καὶ κατέπιεν τὸν ποταμὸν ὃν ἔβαλεν ὁ δράκων ἐκ τοῦ στόματος αὐτοῦ, “and the earth opened its mouth and swallowed up the river which the dragon spewed out of his mouth” — as the earth opened and swallowed Korah's rebels, and as the Red Sea opened for Israel. God's protection is resourceful.","words":[{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G997","hebrew":"βοηθέω","translit":"boēthéō","english":"helped"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G2666","hebrew":"καταπίνω","translit":"katapínō","english":"swallowed up"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"river"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"spewed"}]},{"v":17,"text":"The dragon grew angry with the woman, and went away to make war with the rest of her offspring, who keep God’s commandments and hold Jesus’ testimony.","understanding":"καὶ ὠργίσθη ὁ δράκων ἐπὶ τῇ γυναικί, “The dragon grew angry with the woman” — ὀργίζω, from the wrath family: to become enraged. καὶ ἀπῆλθεν ποιῆσαι πόλεμον μετὰ τῶν λοιπῶν τοῦ σπέρματος αὐτῆς, “and went away to make war with the rest of her offspring” — σπέρμα, seed: God promised in Eden enmity between the serpent's seed and the woman's seed. The woman is the church as a whole, kept by God; ‘the rest of her offspring’ are individual believers, who face the battle. τῶν τηρούντων τὰς ἐντολὰς τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ ἐχόντων τὴν μαρτυρίαν Ἰησοῦ, “who keep God’s commandments and hold Jesus’ testimony” — τηρέω, from the hold family; μαρτυρία, from the witness family. The marks of the church under attack: obedience and witness.","words":[{"strongs":"G3710","hebrew":"ὀργίζω","translit":"orgízō","english":"grew angry"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"went away"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G4690","hebrew":"σπέρμα","translit":"spérma","english":"offspring"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"hold"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus’"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Woman and the Dragon","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture a woman in the sky, robed in sunlight, standing on the moon, crowned with twelve stars, crying out in labour. Facing her, a gigantic blood-red dragon with seven crowned heads and ten horns, its tail sweeping stars out of the sky, crouching to devour her baby the moment it is born.","meaning":"“The dragon stood before the woman who was about to give birth, so that when she gave birth he might devour her child” (v. 4). Behind the story of Israel and the birth of Jesus lies a cosmic war. The enemy has always tried to destroy God's saving purpose — and always failed."},{"title":"Caught Up to the Throne","verseRef":"v. 5-6","image":"Picture the dragon lunging — and the child snatched up out of his reach, carried into the very throne of God. The woman runs into the desert, where a place has been prepared, and she is fed there day by day.","meaning":"“Her child was caught up to God, and to his throne. The woman fled into the wilderness, where she has a place prepared by God” (vv. 5–6). Christ is safe on the throne; His church is kept and fed in the wilderness of this world."},{"title":"The Accuser Thrown Down","verseRef":"v. 7-11","image":"Picture a courtroom in heaven. Day and night the prosecutor stands before the Judge, reading out the sins of God's people. Then the case is thrown out: the accuser is hurled out of court and out of heaven, and a voice cries, ‘Now salvation has come!’ On earth, believers stand with a cross behind them and testimony on their lips.","meaning":"“The accuser of our brothers has been thrown down, who accuses them before our God day and night. They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death” (vv. 10–11)."},{"title":"Eagle's Wings and a Swallowed Flood","verseRef":"v. 13-16","image":"Picture the woman running from the furious dragon. Suddenly great eagle's wings are given to her, and she flies into the desert. The serpent spews a torrent after her — and the ground cracks open and drinks the river down.","meaning":"“Two wings of the great eagle were given to the woman, that she might fly into the wilderness to her place” (v. 14). “The earth helped the woman” (v. 16). God has more ways of protecting His people than the enemy has of attacking them."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Woman and the Dragon (v. 1-6)","body":"A great sign appears in heaven: a woman clothed with the sun, the moon under her feet, crowned with twelve stars, in labour; another sign: a great red dragon with seven heads, ten horns and seven diadems, who sweeps down a third of the stars and waits to devour her child; she bears a son who will rule the nations, and he is caught up to God's throne; the woman flees into the wilderness to a place prepared by God for 1,260 days."},{"label":"War in Heaven (v. 7-12)","body":"Michael and his angels fight the dragon and his angels, who are defeated and thrown down to the earth; the dragon is identified as the ancient serpent, the devil and Satan, who deceives the whole world; a voice in heaven proclaims that salvation and God's kingdom have come, because the accuser is thrown down; believers have conquered him by the blood of the Lamb and the word of their testimony; heaven rejoices, but woe to earth and sea, for the devil has come down in great fury, knowing his time is short."},{"label":"The Dragon Pursues the Woman (v. 13-17)","body":"The dragon persecutes the woman, but she is given eagle's wings to fly to her place in the wilderness for a time, times and half a time; the serpent pours a river after her, but the earth swallows it; enraged, the dragon goes off to make war on the rest of her offspring, who keep God's commandments and hold to the testimony of Jesus."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Great Conflict Behind History","paragraphs":["Chapter 11 brought us to the seventh trumpet and the coming of the kingdom. Chapter 12 goes back to show the deep cause of all the conflict in the book: a war between God and the dragon, fought out in the story of Christ and His people. Chapters 12–14 introduce the enemy and his allies — the dragon (ch. 12), the beast from the sea and the beast from the land (ch. 13) — and God's answer (ch. 14).","John sees two ‘signs’. The first is “a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars” (v. 1). In Joseph's dream the sun, moon and stars were his family — Israel. The woman is the people of God, from whom the Messiah came. Christians have often seen Mary in her too, as the individual through whom that people brought forth the Christ; and from verse 6 onward she is clearly the church, God's people after Christ's ascension. The strongest reading holds these together: she is the one people of God, Old and New.","The second sign is “a great red dragon, having seven heads and ten horns, and on his heads seven crowns” (v. 3). He is named in verse 9: “the old serpent, he who is called the devil and Satan, the deceiver of the whole world.” From Eden onward, he has tried to destroy the woman's offspring, as God foretold to the serpent: there would be enmity between his seed and hers."]},{"heading":"The Birth and Ascension of the Child","paragraphs":["The dragon stands before the woman, ready to devour her child the moment He is born (v. 4). Behind Pharaoh's slaughter of Hebrew boys, behind Herod's massacre at Bethlehem, behind every attempt to destroy the line of the Messiah, stands the dragon. An angel warned Joseph: “Arise and take the young child and his mother, and flee into Egypt... for Herod will seek the young child to destroy him” (Matthew 2:13).","“She gave birth to a son, a male child, who is to rule all the nations with a rod of iron. Her child was caught up to God, and to his throne” (v. 5). In one sentence John moves from Christ's birth to His ascension. The dragon failed. Even when he seemed to win at the cross, Christ rose and was caught up to the throne. Paul says that Christ, “having stripped the principalities and the powers, he made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (Colossians 2:15).","Then “The woman fled into the wilderness, where she has a place prepared by God, that there they may nourish her one thousand two hundred sixty days” (v. 6). The 1,260 days are the same period as the witness of the two prophets (11:3) — the time of the church between Christ's ascension and return. The wilderness is a place of danger and testing, as it was for Israel after the Exodus. But it is also a place where God feeds His people, as He fed Israel with manna."]},{"heading":"The Accuser Thrown Down","paragraphs":["“There was war in the sky” (v. 7). Michael and his angels fight the dragon and his angels, and the dragon loses: “The great dragon was thrown down... He was thrown down to the earth, and his angels were thrown down with him” (v. 9). This is not simply a description of an ancient fall of Satan before creation. The song that follows makes clear when it happens: “Now the salvation, the power, and the Kingdom of our God, and the authority of his Christ has come” (v. 10). Satan's defeat in heaven is the result of Christ's victory on earth. Jesus said, as the cross approached, “Now is the judgment of this world. Now the prince of this world will be cast out” (John 12:31).","Satan is called “the accuser of our brothers... who accuses them before our God day and night” (v. 10). In Zechariah's vision, Satan stood at Joshua the high priest's right hand to accuse him, and God answered, “Yahweh rebuke you, Satan!” (Zechariah 3:2). Satan's accusations are often true — we have sinned. But Christ's death has answered them. Paul asks, “Who could bring a charge against God’s chosen ones? It is God who justifies” (Romans 8:33).","And so the saints conquer: “They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death” (v. 11). The blood of the Lamb answers every accusation. The word of testimony declares Christ's victory. And a love for Christ greater than love for life makes them unafraid of the worst the dragon can do."]},{"heading":"The Dragon's Last Rage","paragraphs":["Heaven rejoices, but earth is warned: “Woe to the earth and to the sea, because the devil has gone down to you, having great wrath, knowing that he has but a short time” (v. 12). The devil's fury on earth is not a sign of his strength but of his defeat. He knows his time is short. This explains much of the suffering of the church: a beaten enemy lashing out.","He persecutes the woman (v. 13), but God gives her “Two wings of the great eagle” (v. 14), recalling God's words at Sinai that He carried Israel on eagles' wings. He spews a flood after her, but “The earth helped the woman” (v. 16). God's people as a whole cannot be destroyed.","So the dragon turns on individual believers: he “went away to make war with the rest of her offspring, who keep God’s commandments and hold Jesus’ testimony” (v. 17). The church as a body is safe; its members will be attacked. Their marks are obedience to God and faithful witness to Jesus. Peter wrote, “Your adversary, the devil, walks around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour. Withstand him steadfast in your faith” (1 Peter 5:8–9). The next chapter shows the instruments the dragon will use."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Defeat of the Devil","paragraphs":["The New Testament everywhere teaches that Christ's death and resurrection defeated Satan. Jesus “partook of the same, that through death he might bring to nothing him who had the power of death, that is, the devil” (Hebrews 2:14). He said, “I saw Satan having fallen like lightning from heaven” (Luke 10:18) and “Now the prince of this world will be cast out” (John 12:31).","Paul answers the accuser: “Who is he who condemns? It is Christ who died, yes rather, who was raised from the dead, who is at the right hand of God, who also makes intercession for us” (Romans 8:34). And he promises, “And the God of peace will quickly crush Satan under your feet” (Romans 16:20) — the final fulfilment of God's word to the serpent in Eden.","The birth of the child echoes Paul's summary of the incarnation: “But when the fullness of the time came, God sent out his Son, born to a woman, born under the law” (Galatians 4:4)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Dragon","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","strongs":"G1404 (serpent ὄφις, óphis, G3789)","verses":"v. 3-17; 13:2, 4; 16:13; 20:2","insight":"Strong's: “a fabulous kind of serpent (perhaps as supposed to fascinate).” δράκων, a great serpent or sea-monster, appears thirteen times in Revelation and nowhere else in the New Testament. The Old Testament spoke of Leviathan and Rahab, sea monsters God crushed — pictures of chaos and of Egypt. Revelation identifies the dragon plainly: ‘the old serpent, he who is called the devil and Satan’ (v. 9). He is red with blood, crowned with stolen authority, and doomed."},{"english":"Accuser","hebrew":"κατήγορος","translit":"katḗgoros","strongs":"G2725 (accuses κατηγορέω, katēgoréō, G2723)","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “against one in the assembly, i.e. a complainant at law; specially, Satan.” κατήγωρ is the prosecutor who brings charges in court — the meaning of the Hebrew ‘Satan’. In Job and Zechariah he stands before God accusing the righteous. His accusations against believers are thrown out because the Lamb's blood has paid every debt. When guilt and condemnation press on you, remember: the accuser has been thrown down."},{"english":"Caught up","hebrew":"ἁρπάζω","translit":"harpázō","strongs":"G726 (throne θρόνος, thrónos, G2362)","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “to seize (in various applications).” ἁρπάζω means to snatch, to seize suddenly and with force. It is used of the Spirit catching Philip away, of Paul being caught up to the third heaven, and of believers being ‘caught up’ to meet the Lord in the air (1 Thessalonians 4:17). Here the Child is snatched up to God's throne out of the dragon's reach: Christ's ascension is His decisive escape and enthronement."},{"english":"Labor pains","hebrew":"ὠδίνω","translit":"ōdínō","strongs":"G5605 (give birth τίκτω, tíktō, G5088)","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “to experience the pains of parturition (literally or figuratively).” ὠδίνω is the agony of childbirth. Paul uses it of his longing for converts — ‘my little children, of whom I am again in travail until Christ is formed in you’ (Galatians 4:19) — and of creation groaning in labour (Romans 8:22). Jesus called the troubles before the end ‘birth pains’ (Matthew 24:8). The woman's pain is the long suffering of God's people before the Messiah came; but labour pains end in new life."},{"english":"Sign","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","strongs":"G4592 (seen ὁράω, horáō, G3708)","verses":"v. 1, 3; 13:13-14; 15:1","insight":"Strong's: “an indication, especially ceremonially or supernaturally.” σημεῖον, from the signs family, is something that points beyond itself — as distinct from τέρας, a wonder that amazes, and δύναμις, a mighty work. John's Gospel calls Jesus' miracles ‘signs’. Here the woman and the dragon are ‘signs’: they are not literal creatures in the sky but pictures that reveal what is happening in history. The false prophet will also do ‘great signs’ (13:13) — counterfeits that point the wrong way."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you the war behind your struggles — and the victory already won.","items":[{"title":"The Accuser Has Lost His Case","body":"Satan accuses believers “before our God day and night” (v. 10), but he has been thrown down. The Spirit wants you to stop listening to the voice of condemnation. When the accuser reminds you of your sins, answer with the blood of the Lamb: it has paid for them all."},{"title":"Overcome the Way the Saints Do","body":"“They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death” (v. 11). The Spirit gives you the same weapons: trust in Christ's blood, speak your testimony, and love Jesus more than your own comfort or life."},{"title":"God Has Prepared a Place for You","body":"The woman has “a place prepared by God” in the wilderness, where she is fed (v. 6). If you are in a wilderness season, the Spirit wants you to know that God has prepared it, and He will nourish you there. You will not be swept away."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Accusation Check","body":"What accusations does the enemy most often whisper to you about your past or your worth? How does the blood of the Lamb answer them (vv. 10–11)?"},{"title":"The Testimony Check","body":"When did you last tell someone what Jesus has done for you (v. 11)?"},{"title":"The Love-of-Life Check","body":"What would you be unwilling to lose for Jesus — comfort, reputation, safety, relationships (v. 11)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Answer the Accuser","body":"Write down three accusations you often hear in your mind. Beside each, write a Bible verse about Christ's forgiveness (for example, Romans 8:1, 1 John 1:9, Revelation 12:11). Each time the accusation comes this week, read the verse aloud."},{"title":"Write Your Testimony","body":"Write a one-page account of how Jesus has saved and changed you. Practise telling it in three minutes. Pray for an opportunity to share it this week (v. 11)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are the Child the dragon could not devour, caught up to the throne of God, who will rule the nations. Thank You that You have defeated the ancient serpent, the devil and Satan, the deceiver of the whole world. Thank You that the accuser of my brothers and sisters has been thrown down. When he accuses me, I plead Your blood. Now salvation and power and the kingdom of our God and the authority of His Christ have come! Give me grace to overcome as Your saints do: by Your blood, by the word of my testimony, and by loving You more than my own life. Thank You for the place You have prepared for Your people in the wilderness, and for feeding us there. Carry us on eagle's wings. When the enemy pours out a flood of lies or persecution, open the earth to swallow it. Help me keep God's commandments and hold fast the testimony of Jesus. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Serpent in the Garden","ref":"Genesis 3:1-15","relatedVerses":"v. 9, 17","summary":"In Eden the serpent deceived Eve, and through her Adam, and sin and death entered the world. But God said to the serpent, ‘I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your offspring and her offspring. He will bruise your head, and you will bruise his heel.’ Revelation 12 calls the dragon ‘the old serpent’ and shows the long war between his offspring and the woman's, until the Child is caught up to the throne and the serpent is thrown down. The promise of Eden is kept."},{"title":"Herod and the Infants of Bethlehem","ref":"Matthew 2:1-18","relatedVerses":"v. 4-6","summary":"When wise men came asking for the newborn King of the Jews, Herod was troubled. He tried to trick them into revealing the child's location, and when they did not return, he killed all the boys in Bethlehem two years old and under. But an angel had warned Joseph: “Arise and take the young child and his mother, and flee into Egypt” (Matthew 2:13). Herod's rage was one face of the dragon standing before the woman to devour her child — and God's protection was stronger."},{"title":"Joshua the High Priest Accused","ref":"Zechariah 3:1-5","relatedVerses":"v. 10","summary":"Zechariah saw “Joshua the high priest standing before Yahweh’s angel, and Satan standing at his right hand to be his adversary” (Zechariah 3:1). Joshua was dressed in filthy clothes. But the LORD rebuked Satan, and the angel ordered the filthy garments removed and clean robes put on him, saying, ‘I have caused your iniquity to pass from you.’ This is what the Lamb's blood does for every believer: the accuser is silenced, and the filthy garments are replaced with white."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":13,"chapterTitle":"The Beast from the Sea and the Beast from the Land","themeTitle":"“Here Is the Endurance and the Faith of the Saints”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Then I stood on the sand of the sea. I saw a beast coming up out of the sea, having ten horns and seven heads. On his horns were ten crowns, and on his heads, blasphemous names.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐστάθη ἐπὶ τὴν ἄμμον τῆς θαλάσσης, “Then I stood on the sand of the sea” — many manuscripts read ‘he stood’: the dragon takes his stand on the shore to summon his helper. καὶ εἶδον ἐκ τῆς θαλάσσης θηρίον ἀναβαῖνον, “I saw a beast coming up out of the sea” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); θηρίον, a wild, dangerous animal, as distinct from ζῷον, the living creatures of the throne (4:6). In Daniel 7 four beasts rise from the sea: four empires. To John's readers, Rome came from across the sea. ἔχων κέρατα δέκα καὶ κεφαλὰς ἑπτά, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κεράτων αὐτοῦ δέκα διαδήματα, “having ten horns and seven heads. On his horns were ten crowns” — διάδημα, from the crown family: royal crowns, like the dragon's (12:3). καὶ ἐπὶ τὰς κεφαλὰς αὐτοῦ ὀνόματα βλασφημίας, “and on his heads, blasphemous names” — emperors took titles such as ‘divine’, ‘son of god’ and ‘lord’.","words":[{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stood"},{"strongs":"G285","hebrew":"ἄμμος","translit":"ámmos","english":"sand"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"coming up"},{"strongs":"G1176","hebrew":"δέκα","translit":"déka","english":"ten"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G1238","hebrew":"διάδημα","translit":"diádēma","english":"crowns"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"blasphemous"}]},{"v":2,"text":"The beast which I saw was like a leopard, and his feet were like those of a bear, and his mouth like the mouth of a lion. The dragon gave him his power, his throne, and great authority.","understanding":"καὶ τὸ θηρίον ὃ εἶδον ἦν ὅμοιον παρδάλει, “The beast which I saw was like a leopard” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). καὶ οἱ πόδες αὐτοῦ ὡς ἄρκου, καὶ τὸ στόμα αὐτοῦ ὡς στόμα λέοντος, “and his feet were like those of a bear, and his mouth like the mouth of a lion” — Daniel's first three beasts (lion, bear, leopard) combined in one: this beast sums up all the oppressive empires of history. καὶ ἔδωκεν αὐτῷ ὁ δράκων τὴν δύναμιν αὐτοῦ καὶ τὸν θρόνον αὐτοῦ καὶ ἐξουσίαν μεγάλην, “The dragon gave him his power, his throne, and great authority” — δύναμις and ἐξουσία, two members of the power family: ability and authority, both on loan from Satan. It is the offer Jesus refused in the wilderness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G3917","hebrew":"πάρδαλις","translit":"párdalis","english":"leopard"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G715","hebrew":"ἄρκτος","translit":"árktos","english":"bear"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lion"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":3,"text":"One of his heads looked like it had been wounded fatally. His fatal wound was healed, and the whole earth marveled at the beast.","understanding":"καὶ μίαν ἐκ τῶν κεφαλῶν αὐτοῦ ὡς ἐσφαγμένην εἰς θάνατον, “One of his heads looked like it had been wounded fatally” — σφάζω, the word used of the Lamb ‘as though it had been slain’ (5:6): the beast is a counterfeit Christ, with a counterfeit death. καὶ ἡ πληγὴ τοῦ θανάτου αὐτοῦ ἐθεραπεύθη, “His fatal wound was healed” — θεραπεύω, from the heal family: to restore, to cure — a counterfeit resurrection. Many see the Roman Empire's recovery after Nero's suicide and the chaos of AD 69; there were also rumours that Nero would return. καὶ ἐθαυμάσθη ὅλη ἡ γῆ ὀπίσω τοῦ θηρίου, “and the whole earth marveled at the beast” — γῆ, from the world family: the world follows what impresses it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"wounded"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"fatally"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"wound"},{"strongs":"G2323","hebrew":"θεραπεύω","translit":"therapeúō","english":"healed"},{"strongs":"G3650","hebrew":"ὅλος","translit":"hólos","english":"whole"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2296","hebrew":"θαυμάζω","translit":"thaumázō","english":"marveled"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"}]},{"v":4,"text":"They worshiped the dragon, because he gave his authority to the beast, and they worshiped the beast, saying, “Who is like the beast? Who is able to make war with him?”","understanding":"καὶ προσεκύνησαν τῷ δράκοντι ὅτι ἔδωκεν τὴν ἐξουσίαν τῷ θηρίῳ, “They worshiped the dragon, because he gave his authority to the beast” — προσκυνέω; ἐξουσία, from the power family. Worship of the state is worship of Satan behind it. καὶ προσεκύνησαν τῷ θηρίῳ λέγοντες· Τίς ὅμοιος τῷ θηρίῳ; “and they worshiped the beast, saying, ‘Who is like the beast?’” — a parody of Israel's song, ‘Who is like you, LORD?’ and of Michael's name, ‘Who is like God?’ καὶ τίς δύναται πολεμῆσαι μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ; “Who is able to make war with him?” — δύναμαι, from the power family: the world worships power.","words":[{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G4170","hebrew":"πολεμέω","translit":"poleméō","english":"make war"}]},{"v":5,"text":"A mouth speaking great things and blasphemy was given to him. Authority to make war for forty-two months was given to him.","understanding":"Καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ στόμα λαλοῦν μεγάλα καὶ βλασφημίας, “A mouth speaking great things and blasphemy was given to him” — Daniel's little horn ‘had a mouth speaking great things’. ‘Was given’: the passive appears again and again in this chapter — the beast acts only because God permits it. καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ ἐξουσία ποιῆσαι μῆνας τεσσεράκοντα δύο, “Authority to make war for forty-two months was given to him” — ἐξουσία, from the power family; μήν, from the time family: the same period as the trampling of the holy city and the witness of the two prophets (11:2–3). His time is fixed and short.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaking"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great things"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"blasphemy"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"Authority"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"months"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"}]},{"v":6,"text":"He opened his mouth for blasphemy against God, to blaspheme his name, and his dwelling, those who dwell in heaven.","understanding":"καὶ ἤνοιξεν τὸ στόμα αὐτοῦ εἰς βλασφημίας πρὸς τὸν θεόν, “He opened his mouth for blasphemy against God” — βλασφημία, slander against God. βλασφημῆσαι τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ καὶ τὴν σκηνὴν αὐτοῦ, τοὺς ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ σκηνοῦντας, “to blaspheme his name, and his dwelling, those who dwell in heaven” — σκηνή and σκηνόω, tent and tabernacling: God's dwelling is His people, who belong to heaven even while on earth (12:12).","words":[{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"blasphemy"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blaspheme"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"dwelling"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G4637","hebrew":"σκηνόω","translit":"skēnóō","english":"dwell"}]},{"v":7,"text":"It was given to him to make war with the saints, and to overcome them. Authority over every tribe, people, language, and nation was given to him.","understanding":"καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ ποιῆσαι πόλεμον μετὰ τῶν ἁγίων καὶ νικῆσαι αὐτούς, “It was given to him to make war with the saints, and to overcome them” — νικάω: as with the two witnesses (11:7), the beast ‘overcomes’ the saints by killing them; but in God's eyes they overcome him by dying faithfully (12:11; 15:2). καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ ἐξουσία ἐπὶ πᾶσαν φυλὴν καὶ λαὸν καὶ γλῶσσαν καὶ ἔθνος, “Authority over every tribe, people, language, and nation was given to him” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: a counterfeit of the Lamb's people from every tribe (5:9). ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"Authority"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nation"}]},{"v":8,"text":"All who dwell on the earth will worship him, everyone whose name has not been written from the foundation of the world in the book of life of the Lamb who has been killed.","understanding":"καὶ προσκυνήσουσιν αὐτὸν πάντες οἱ κατοικοῦντες ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “All who dwell on the earth will worship him” — γῆ, from the world family: the earth-dwellers, as distinct from those who dwell in heaven (v. 6). οὗ οὐ γέγραπται τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ ἐν τῷ βιβλίῳ τῆς ζωῆς τοῦ ἀρνίου τοῦ ἐσφαγμένου ἀπὸ καταβολῆς κόσμου, “everyone whose name has not been written from the foundation of the world in the book of life of the Lamb who has been killed” — βιβλίον, from the book family; ζωή, from the life family; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family; κόσμος, from the world family. ‘From the foundation of the world’ may go with ‘written’ (as in 17:8) or with ‘killed’ — the cross was God's plan before creation (1 Peter 1:20). Either way, God's people are known and kept from eternity.","words":[{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G2602","hebrew":"καταβολή","translit":"katabolḗ","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"killed"}]},{"v":9,"text":"If anyone has an ear, let him hear.","understanding":"Εἴ τις ἔχει οὖς ἀκουσάτω, “If anyone has an ear, let him hear” — the refrain of the seven letters (2:7), now without ‘what the Spirit says to the assemblies’: this chapter is the test the churches were being prepared for.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ear"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hear"}]},{"v":10,"text":"If anyone is to go into captivity, he will go into captivity. If anyone is to be killed with the sword, he must be killed. Here is the endurance and the faith of the saints.","understanding":"εἴ τις εἰς αἰχμαλωσίαν, εἰς αἰχμαλωσίαν ὑπάγει, “If anyone is to go into captivity, he will go into captivity” — echoing Jeremiah: what God has appointed will happen. εἴ τις ἐν μαχαίρῃ ἀποκτανθῆναι, αὐτὸν ἐν μαχαίρῃ ἀποκτανθῆναι, “If anyone is to be killed with the sword, he must be killed” — μάχαιρα, from the sword family: the state's sword. Believers are not to fight back with the beast's weapons (Matthew 26:52). Ὧδέ ἐστιν ἡ ὑπομονὴ καὶ ἡ πίστις τῶν ἁγίων, “Here is the endurance and the faith of the saints” — ὑπομονή, from the endurance family: remaining under pressure; πίστις, faithfulness. The saints' response to the beast is not violence or panic but faithful endurance. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G161","hebrew":"αἰχμαλωσία","translit":"aichmalōsía","english":"captivity"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"go"},{"strongs":"G3162","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"endurance"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":11,"text":"I saw another beast coming up out of the earth. He had two horns like a lamb, and he spoke like a dragon.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἄλλο θηρίον ἀναβαῖνον ἐκ τῆς γῆς, “I saw another beast coming up out of the earth” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἄλλος, from the another family: another of the same kind, a partner of the first; γῆ, from the world family. Later he is called ‘the false prophet’ (16:13; 19:20). καὶ εἶχεν κέρατα δύο ὅμοια ἀρνίῳ, “He had two horns like a lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: he looks gentle, religious, Christ-like. Jesus warned of false prophets ‘in sheep’s clothing’. καὶ ἐλάλει ὡς δράκων, “and he spoke like a dragon” — his words reveal his master.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"coming up"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"had"},{"strongs":"G1417","hebrew":"δύο","translit":"dýo","english":"two"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"lamb"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"}]},{"v":12,"text":"He exercises all the authority of the first beast in his presence. He makes the earth and those who dwell in it to worship the first beast, whose fatal wound was healed.","understanding":"καὶ τὴν ἐξουσίαν τοῦ πρώτου θηρίου πᾶσαν ποιεῖ ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ, “He exercises all the authority of the first beast in his presence” — ἐξουσία, from the power family. In John's world, the local priests and councils of the imperial cult enforced emperor-worship in every city of Asia. καὶ ποιεῖ τὴν γῆν καὶ τοὺς ἐν αὐτῇ κατοικοῦντας ἵνα προσκυνήσουσιν τὸ θηρίον τὸ πρῶτον, “He makes the earth and those who dwell in it to worship the first beast” — γῆ, from the world family. Religion serving political power. οὗ ἐθεραπεύθη ἡ πληγὴ τοῦ θανάτου αὐτοῦ, “whose fatal wound was healed” — θεραπεύω, from the heal family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"exercises"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"wound"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"fatal"},{"strongs":"G2323","hebrew":"θεραπεύω","translit":"therapeúō","english":"healed"}]},{"v":13,"text":"He performs great signs, even making fire come down out of the sky to the earth in the sight of people.","understanding":"καὶ ποιεῖ σημεῖα μεγάλα, “He performs great signs” — σημεῖον, from the signs family: counterfeit miracles. Jesus warned that false prophets would ‘show great signs and wonders’. ἵνα καὶ πῦρ ποιῇ ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ καταβαίνειν εἰς τὴν γῆν ἐνώπιον τῶν ἀνθρώπων, “even making fire come down out of the sky to the earth in the sight of people” — imitating Elijah and the two witnesses (11:5); γῆ, from the world family. Miracles alone prove nothing; the test is whether they lead to the worship of God.","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"performs"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"signs"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"come down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"}]},{"v":14,"text":"He deceives my own people who dwell on the earth because of the signs he was granted to do in front of the beast; saying to those who dwell on the earth, that they should make an image to the beast who had the sword wound and lived.","understanding":"καὶ πλανᾷ τοὺς κατοικοῦντας ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς διὰ τὰ σημεῖα, “He deceives my own people who dwell on the earth because of the signs” — πλανάω, to lead astray: the very work of the dragon, ‘the deceiver of the whole world’; γῆ, from the world family; σημεῖον, from the signs family. (Most manuscripts read simply ‘those who dwell on the earth’.) λέγων τοῖς κατοικοῦσιν ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς ποιῆσαι εἰκόνα τῷ θηρίῳ, “saying to those who dwell on the earth, that they should make an image to the beast” — εἰκών, from the image family: a statue, a representation, as distinct from ὁμοίωμα, a mere likeness. Temples to the emperors stood in Ephesus, Pergamum and Smyrna, with images before which citizens burned incense. ὃς ἔχει τὴν πληγὴν τῆς μαχαίρης καὶ ἔζησεν, “who had the sword wound and lived” — μάχαιρα, from the sword family; ζάω, from the life family: the beast's counterfeit resurrection again.","words":[{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceives"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"signs"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"granted"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"wound"},{"strongs":"G3162","hebrew":"μάχαιρα","translit":"máchaira","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lived"}]},{"v":15,"text":"It was given to him to give breath to it, to the image of the beast, that the image of the beast should both speak, and cause as many as wouldn’t worship the image of the beast to be killed.","understanding":"καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ δοῦναι πνεῦμα τῇ εἰκόνι τοῦ θηρίου, “It was given to him to give breath to it, to the image of the beast” — πνεῦμα, breath; εἰκών, from the image family. Trickery with speaking statues was known in pagan temples; and every idol is given ‘life’ by the devotion of its worshippers. A grim parody of the breath of life entering the witnesses (11:11). ἵνα καὶ λαλήσῃ ἡ εἰκὼν τοῦ θηρίου, καὶ ποιήσῃ ἵνα ὅσοι ἐὰν μὴ προσκυνήσωσιν τῇ εἰκόνι τοῦ θηρίου ἀποκτανθῶσιν, “that the image of the beast should both speak, and cause as many as wouldn’t worship the image of the beast to be killed” — εἰκών three times. Like Nebuchadnezzar's golden image (Daniel 3): bow or die.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"breath"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speak"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"}]},{"v":16,"text":"He causes all, the small and the great, the rich and the poor, and the free and the slave, to be given marks on their right hands, or on their foreheads;","understanding":"καὶ ποιεῖ πάντας, τοὺς μικροὺς καὶ τοὺς μεγάλους, καὶ τοὺς πλουσίους καὶ τοὺς πτωχούς, καὶ τοὺς ἐλευθέρους καὶ τοὺς δούλους, “He causes all, the small and the great, the rich and the poor, and the free and the slave” — δοῦλος, from the servant family: no class escapes. ἵνα δῶσιν αὐτοῖς χάραγμα ἐπὶ τῆς χειρὸς αὐτῶν τῆς δεξιᾶς ἢ ἐπὶ τὸ μέτωπον αὐτῶν, “to be given marks on their right hands, or on their foreheads” — χάραγμα, an engraved stamp: the word for the emperor's image stamped on coins and documents, and for the brand on a slave. It is the beast's counterfeit of God's seal on His servants' foreheads (7:3). The strongest reading is that it marks allegiance — in thought (forehead) and action (hand); many expect also a literal mark at the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"causes"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"small"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4145","hebrew":"πλούσιος","translit":"ploúsios","english":"rich"},{"strongs":"G4434","hebrew":"πτωχός","translit":"ptōchós","english":"poor"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"slave"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"marks"},{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hands"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"foreheads"}]},{"v":17,"text":"and that no one would be able to buy or to sell, unless he has that mark, the name of the beast or the number of his name.","understanding":"καὶ ἵνα μή τις δύνηται ἀγοράσαι ἢ πωλῆσαι, “and that no one would be able to buy or to sell” — δύναμαι, from the power family; ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family: the same verb used of the Lamb ‘buying’ people for God (5:9). The beast controls the marketplace; the Lamb buys people out of it. In Asia, trade guilds required honouring the gods and the emperor; refusing could cost your livelihood. εἰ μὴ ὁ ἔχων τὸ χάραγμα, τὸ ὄνομα τοῦ θηρίου ἢ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦ ὀνόματος αὐτοῦ, “unless he has that mark, the name of the beast or the number of his name.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"buy"},{"strongs":"G4453","hebrew":"πωλέω","translit":"pōléō","english":"sell"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"mark"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Here is wisdom. He who has understanding, let him calculate the number of the beast, for it is the number of a man. His number is six hundred sixty-six.","understanding":"Ὧδε ἡ σοφία ἐστίν, “Here is wisdom” — σοφία, from the wisdom family: not cleverness for puzzles, but the discernment to see through the beast. ὁ ἔχων νοῦν ψηφισάτω τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦ θηρίου, “He who has understanding, let him calculate the number of the beast” — νοῦς, the mind; ψηφίζω, to count with pebbles. In Greek and Hebrew, letters were also numbers, so names could be added up. ἀριθμὸς γὰρ ἀνθρώπου ἐστίν, “for it is the number of a man” — a human number, or the number of a particular man. καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς αὐτοῦ ἑξακόσιοι ἑξήκοντα ἕξ, “His number is six hundred sixty-six” — ‘Nero Caesar’ written in Hebrew letters adds up to 666, and some manuscripts read 616, which matches the Latin spelling — a strong sign that early readers thought of Nero. Many also see 6, one short of the perfect 7, tripled: humanity's best attempt at deity, always falling short. Both may be meant: the beast is a Nero-like ruler and every power that sets itself up as God.","words":[{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"understanding"},{"strongs":"G5585","hebrew":"ψηφίζω","translit":"psēphízō","english":"calculate"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G1812","hebrew":"ἑξακόσιοι","translit":"hexakósioi","english":"six hundred"},{"strongs":"G1835","hebrew":"ἑξήκοντα","translit":"hexḗkonta","english":"sixty"},{"strongs":"G1803","hebrew":"ἕξ","translit":"héx","english":"six"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Beast from the Sea","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture the dragon standing on the beach as the sea heaves. Out of the waves rises a monster — leopard's body, bear's feet, lion's jaws, seven heads, ten crowned horns, blasphemous names on every forehead. One head carries a terrible wound, now healed. All over the world, crowds gasp in admiration and bow.","meaning":"“The dragon gave him his power, his throne, and great authority” (v. 2). “They worshiped the beast, saying, ‘Who is like the beast? Who is able to make war with him?’” (v. 4). Every power that demands ultimate loyalty borrows its strength from the dragon."},{"title":"Given, Given, Given","verseRef":"v. 5-7","image":"Picture the beast boasting and blaspheming, waging war on God's people — but notice, each time, a hand behind the scenes giving permission, setting a time limit: forty-two months, and not a day longer.","meaning":"“A mouth speaking great things and blasphemy was given to him. Authority to make war for forty-two months was given to him” (v. 5). The beast seems all-powerful, but everything he has is ‘given’, and his time is short."},{"title":"The Lamb-Like Dragon","verseRef":"v. 11-15","image":"Picture a second beast, gentle-looking, with two small horns like a lamb. He works miracles, calls down fire, sets up a statue that seems to breathe and speak — and when he opens his mouth, you hear the voice of the dragon.","meaning":"“He had two horns like a lamb, and he spoke like a dragon” (v. 11). Not everything that looks like Jesus is from Jesus. Test what you hear by the voice, not the appearance."},{"title":"The Two Marks","verseRef":"v. 16-18","image":"Picture two crowds. On one, the beast's stamp on hand and forehead — they can buy and sell freely. On the other, God's seal on their foreheads — shut out of the marketplace, but written in the Lamb's book of life.","meaning":"“That no one would be able to buy or to sell, unless he has that mark” (v. 17). Everyone bears someone's mark. Faithfulness to Christ may cost you in this world's marketplace; it will never cost you your place in the book of life (v. 8)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Beast from the Sea (v. 1-4)","body":"A beast rises from the sea with ten horns, seven heads, ten diadems and blasphemous names; it is like a leopard, bear and lion; the dragon gives it his power, throne and authority; one head seemed fatally wounded but was healed; the whole earth marvels and worships the dragon and the beast."},{"label":"Its War on the Saints (v. 5-10)","body":"The beast is given a mouth to blaspheme God, His name and His dwelling, and authority for forty-two months; it is allowed to make war on the saints and conquer them, and given authority over every nation; all whose names are not in the Lamb's book of life worship it; John calls for hearing, acceptance of captivity or death, and the endurance and faith of the saints."},{"label":"The Beast from the Land (v. 11-17)","body":"Another beast rises from the earth with two horns like a lamb but speaks like a dragon; it exercises the first beast's authority and makes the earth worship it; it performs great signs, even calling down fire; it deceives people into making an image of the first beast, gives the image breath to speak, and has those who refuse to worship killed; it forces everyone to receive a mark on the right hand or forehead, without which no one can buy or sell."},{"label":"The Number of the Beast (v. 18)","body":"This calls for wisdom: the one with understanding should calculate the number of the beast, for it is a human number — 666."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Dragon's Allies","paragraphs":["Chapter 12 ended with the dragon going off to make war on the woman's offspring. Chapter 13 shows his instruments: a beast from the sea and a beast from the land. Together with the dragon they form an unholy trinity — a counterfeit of the Father, the Son and the Spirit. The dragon gives his authority to the beast as the Father gives authority to the Son; the second beast leads people to worship the first as the Spirit leads people to worship Christ.","The first beast rises from the sea, like the four beasts of Daniel 7, which were four empires. It combines features of all four — leopard, bear and lion, with ten horns (v. 2). It is the sum of all the oppressive empires of history. For John's first readers it was clearly Rome: the empire across the sea, ruled by seven-headed succession, its emperors claiming blasphemous titles. But Rome is not the whole story; the beast reappears whenever a political power claims ultimate allegiance and persecutes God's people. Many also expect a final, personal embodiment of it before Christ's return, the ‘man of sin’ of 2 Thessalonians 2.","The beast is a parody of the Lamb. It has a head “wounded fatally” (v. 3) using the same word as the Lamb ‘slain’ (5:6), and it has a counterfeit resurrection: “His fatal wound was healed” (v. 3). The world is amazed and asks, “Who is like the beast? Who is able to make war with him?” (v. 4) — mocking the song of Moses, ‘Who is like you, LORD?’ The world worships power."]},{"heading":"Here Is the Endurance of the Saints","paragraphs":["The beast blasphemes and wars against God's people: “It was given to him to make war with the saints, and to overcome them” (v. 7). Notice how often the passive ‘was given’ appears (vv. 5, 7, 14, 15). The beast's authority is real but borrowed and limited — forty-two months (v. 5), the same short, fixed period as in chapters 11–12. God remains sovereign even over His enemies' power.","Who will worship the beast? “All who dwell on the earth... everyone whose name has not been written from the foundation of the world in the book of life of the Lamb who has been killed” (v. 8). There are only two groups: those who belong to the earth and worship the beast, and those whose names are written in the Lamb's book. God's people are secure because they were known and chosen in Christ before the world began, as Paul says: “even as he chose us in him before the foundation of the world” (Ephesians 1:4).","So what should believers do? “If anyone is to go into captivity, he will go into captivity. If anyone is to be killed with the sword, he must be killed. Here is the endurance and the faith of the saints” (v. 10). Not armed revolt, not compromise, but patient faithfulness. The saints overcome the beast not by taking up its sword but by refusing its worship, even at the cost of their lives (12:11)."]},{"heading":"The False Prophet","paragraphs":["The second beast comes from the land. “He had two horns like a lamb, and he spoke like a dragon” (v. 11). He looks gentle and religious, but speaks Satan's words. Later he is called ‘the false prophet’ (16:13; 19:20). In John's world, he stood for the priests and councils of the imperial cult, who organised emperor-worship in every city of Asia and pressured Christians to burn incense to Caesar's image. In every age, he is religion and propaganda in the service of a godless state.","He does what religion should never do: he points people to the beast instead of to God. “He makes the earth and those who dwell in it to worship the first beast” (v. 12). He works miracles: “He performs great signs, even making fire come down out of the sky to the earth in the sight of people” (v. 13). Jesus warned, “For there will arise false christs, and false prophets, and they will show great signs and wonders, so as to lead astray, if possible, even the chosen ones” (Matthew 24:24). Paul spoke of one “whose coming is according to the working of Satan with all power and signs and lying wonders” (2 Thessalonians 2:9).","He sets up an image of the beast, gives it ‘breath’ so that it speaks, and orders the death of all who will not worship it (vv. 14–15). This is Nebuchadnezzar's golden statue all over again: bow, or burn. The faithful Christians of Asia faced exactly this choice."]},{"heading":"The Mark and the Number","paragraphs":["The false prophet forces everyone — small and great, rich and poor, free and slave — to receive “marks on their right hands, or on their foreheads; and that no one would be able to buy or to sell, unless he has that mark” (vv. 16–17). The mark is the beast's counterfeit of God's seal (7:3). The word χάραγμα was used for the emperor's stamp on coins and documents. The strongest reading is that the mark represents allegiance — what controls a person's thinking (forehead) and doing (hand). In Asia, refusing to honour Caesar and the guild gods could mean losing your trade. Some Christians expect a literal mark at the end; the principle holds either way: everyone bears someone's mark, and faithfulness to Christ may cost you economically.","Then the famous puzzle: “Here is wisdom. He who has understanding, let him calculate the number of the beast, for it is the number of a man. His number is six hundred sixty-six” (v. 18). In Hebrew and Greek, letters doubled as numbers. The name ‘Nero Caesar’, spelled in Hebrew letters, adds up to 666; and some early manuscripts read 616, which matches the Latin spelling of the same name. This strongly suggests that John's first readers would have thought of Nero, the emperor who burned Christians in Rome, and the rumours that he would return.","But 666 is also symbolic. Seven is the number of completeness in Revelation; six falls short. 666 is six repeated three times: humanity's highest attempt to be God, always falling short. The beast is ‘the number of a man’ — however divine it claims to be, it is only human. Both meanings may be intended. The wisdom called for is not to decode the newest ruler, but to recognise every power that demands the worship belonging only to God — and to refuse it."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Man of Sin and the False Prophets","paragraphs":["Paul described the same figure: “the man of sin is revealed, the son of destruction, he who opposes and exalts himself against all that is called God or that is worshiped; so that he sits as God in the temple of God, setting himself up as God” (2 Thessalonians 2:3–4). John wrote, “as you heard that the Antichrist is coming, even now many antichrists have arisen” (1 John 2:18). The beast has many forerunners.","Jesus warned, “Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly are ravening wolves” (Matthew 7:15) — the beast with lamb's horns and a dragon's voice.","The New Testament teaches respect for governing authorities: “Let every soul be in subjection to the higher authorities, for there is no authority except from God” (Romans 13:1). But when the state demands worship, Peter and John's answer stands: “Whether it is right in the sight of God to listen to you rather than to God, judge for yourselves” (Acts 4:19). Romans 13 and Revelation 13 belong together: the state is God's servant when it does good, and the dragon's beast when it claims to be God."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Beast","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","strongs":"G2342 (living creatures ζῶον, zōon, G2226)","verses":"v. 1-18; 11:7; 17:3-17; 19:19-20; 20:10","insight":"Strong's: “a dangerous animal.” θηρίον is a wild, savage beast, a predator. It is a deliberate contrast with ζῷον, the ‘living creatures’ around God's throne (4:6). The heavenly creatures lead worship of God; the beast demands worship for itself. In Daniel 7, empires are beasts and God's kingdom is given to ‘one like a son of man’ — human, as God intended. When rulers grasp at divinity, they become less than human: beasts."},{"english":"Mark","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","strongs":"G5480 (foreheads μέτωπον, métōpon, G3359)","verses":"v. 16, 17; 14:9, 11; 16:2; 19:20; 20:4","insight":"Strong's: “a scratch or etching, i.e. stamp (as a badge of servitude), or scupltured figure (statue).” χάραγμα was used for the imperial seal on documents and the emperor's image on coins, and for brands on slaves and soldiers. It is the counterfeit of the σφραγίς, God's seal on His servants (7:3). Revelation presents only two options: God's seal or the beast's mark. The mark is ‘a badge of servitude’: it shows who owns you."},{"english":"Image","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","strongs":"G1504 (beast θηρίον, thēríon, G2342)","verses":"v. 14, 15; 14:9, 11; 16:2; 19:20; 20:4","insight":"Strong's: “a likeness, i.e. (literally) statue, profile, or (figuratively) representation, resemblance.” εἰκών, from the image family, is a representation — a statue, a portrait, an image on a coin. Jesus held up a denarius and asked, ‘Whose image is this?’ Humans were made in God's image; Christ is ‘the image of the invisible God’ (Colossians 1:15). The beast's image is the great counterfeit: a man-made idol demanding the worship due to the true Image of God."},{"english":"Calculate","hebrew":"ψηφίζω","translit":"psēphízō","strongs":"G5585 (number ἀριθμός, arithmós, G706)","verses":"v. 18","insight":"Strong's: “to use pebbles in enumeration, i.e. (generally) to compute.” ψηφίζω comes from ψῆφος, a pebble (the white stone of 2:17): counting by moving pebbles on a board. John invites the wise to ‘calculate’ — to work out what the number reveals. In the ancient practice of gematria, letters were numbers. But the call is for σοφία, wisdom, the moral discernment to see through the beast's claims, not for endless speculation about names."},{"english":"Blasphemy","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","strongs":"G988 (blaspheme βλασφημέω, blasphēméō, G987)","verses":"v. 1, 5, 6; 2:9; 17:3","insight":"Strong's: “vilification (especially against God).” βλασφημία is speech that slanders or insults — above all, speech that robs God of His honour. The beast's heads bear ‘blasphemous names’: titles like ‘lord’, ‘saviour’ and ‘son of god’ that belong to God and His Christ. Its mouth speaks great things and blasphemy. To claim divine honour for any human power is blasphemy, however patriotic it sounds."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit gives you eyes to recognise the beast — and the courage to refuse it.","items":[{"title":"Don't Be Impressed by Power","body":"“The whole earth marveled at the beast” and asked, ‘Who is like the beast?’ (vv. 3–4). The Spirit warns you not to be dazzled by any power — political, economic, technological or cultural — that seems unstoppable. Only One is worthy of worship. Ask: ‘Who is like God?’"},{"title":"Test the Voice","body":"The second beast looked like a lamb but spoke like a dragon (v. 11), and worked impressive signs (v. 13). The Spirit wants you to test every teacher, movement and miracle by one question: does it lead me to worship God and follow Jesus, or to serve something else?"},{"title":"Whose Mark Do You Bear?","body":"Everyone carries someone's mark — in their thinking and in their doing (v. 16). The Spirit asks: whose name is written on your mind and your hands? If faithfulness costs you in the marketplace, remember your name is written in the Lamb's book of life."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Allegiance Check","body":"Is there any power — a nation, party, leader, employer or ideology — to which you give the loyalty that belongs only to God (v. 4)?"},{"title":"The Deception Check","body":"Are you easily impressed by signs, success or charisma, without asking what they lead people to worship (vv. 13–14)?"},{"title":"The Cost Check","body":"Would you be willing to lose income or opportunity rather than compromise your faith (v. 17)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Name the Beasts","body":"Write down the ‘beasts’ in your world — powers that demand ultimate allegiance and punish dissent. Then write beside each: ‘Its authority was given, and its time is short.’ Pray for courage to stay faithful, and for believers who live under oppressive regimes."},{"title":"Mark Your Mind and Hands","body":"Each morning this week, place your hand on your forehead and pray, ‘My mind belongs to Jesus,’ and then hold out your hands and pray, ‘My work belongs to Jesus.’ Let it remind you whose seal you bear (v. 16; 7:3)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord God, there is none like You. The world marvels at the beast and asks who can fight against it, but I know that all its power is borrowed and its time is short. Keep me from being impressed by any power that sets itself up in Your place. Give me wisdom to recognise the beast in every age, and discernment to hear the dragon's voice even when it comes from a lamb-like face. Keep me from the deception of false signs and false prophets. Thank You that my name is written in the Lamb's book of life, from the foundation of the world. Give me the endurance and faith of the saints, to refuse the beast's mark and worship, whatever it costs. Let Your seal be on my mind and on my hands. And strengthen my brothers and sisters who today face the choice to bow or die. In the name of the Lamb who was slain, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Daniel's Four Beasts","ref":"Daniel 7:1-28","relatedVerses":"v. 1-7","summary":"Daniel saw four great beasts rise from the stormy sea: a lion with eagle's wings, a bear, a leopard with four heads, and a terrifying fourth beast with iron teeth and ten horns, among which a little horn rose with a mouth speaking great things and made war on the saints ‘for a time, times, and half a time’. Then the Ancient of Days took His throne, the beast was destroyed, and the kingdom was given to one like a son of man and to the saints. Revelation's beast combines all four — and meets the same end."},{"title":"Nebuchadnezzar's Golden Image","ref":"Daniel 3:1-30","relatedVerses":"v. 14-15","summary":"King Nebuchadnezzar set up a huge golden image and commanded all peoples, nations and languages to fall down and worship it when the music sounded; whoever refused would be thrown into the fiery furnace. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused: ‘our God whom we serve is able to deliver us... But if not... we will not serve your gods, nor worship the golden image.’ They were thrown in — and a fourth figure walked with them in the fire. The image of the beast repeats Nebuchadnezzar's demand; the saints repeat their answer."},{"title":"Polycarp Before the Proconsul","ref":"Church history (about AD 155)","relatedVerses":"v. 7, 10","summary":"Some sixty years after Revelation, Polycarp, the elderly bishop of Smyrna, was arrested and brought into the stadium. The Roman governor urged him to swear by the fortune of Caesar and say ‘Caesar is Lord’, and he would be set free. Polycarp replied that he had served Christ eighty-six years and Christ had never wronged him — how could he blaspheme his King who saved him? He was burned alive. He refused the beast's demand, and overcame by the word of his testimony."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":14,"chapterTitle":"The Lamb on Mount Zion, Three Angels and Two Harvests","themeTitle":"“Blessed Are the Dead Who Die in the Lord”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw, and behold, the Lamb standing on Mount Zion, and with him a number, one hundred forty-four thousand, having his name, and the name of his Father, written on their foreheads.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ τὸ ἀρνίον ἑστὸς ἐπὶ τὸ ὄρος Σιών, “I saw, and behold, the Lamb standing on Mount Zion” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. After the dragon on the seashore (13:1) comes the Lamb on the mountain of God: Psalm 2's King set on Zion. καὶ μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ ἑκατὸν τεσσεράκοντα τέσσαρες χιλιάδες, “and with him a number, one hundred forty-four thousand” — the sealed of 7:4: God's whole people, complete. ἔχουσαι τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ καὶ τὸ ὄνομα τοῦ πατρὸς αὐτοῦ γεγραμμένον ἐπὶ τῶν μετώπων αὐτῶν, “having his name, and the name of his Father, written on their foreheads” — the answer to the beast's mark: they belong to the Lamb and the Father.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"Mount"},{"strongs":"G4622","hebrew":"Σιών","translit":"Siṓn","english":"Zion"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G1540","hebrew":"ἑκατόν","translit":"hekatón","english":"one hundred"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"},{"strongs":"G5064","hebrew":"τέσσαρες","translit":"téssares","english":"four"},{"strongs":"G5505","hebrew":"χιλιάς","translit":"chiliás","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"foreheads"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I heard a sound from heaven, like the sound of many waters, and like the sound of a great thunder. The sound which I heard was like that of harpists playing on their harps.","understanding":"καὶ ἤκουσα φωνὴν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ὡς φωνὴν ὑδάτων πολλῶν καὶ ὡς φωνὴν βροντῆς μεγάλης, “I heard a sound from heaven, like the sound of many waters, and like the sound of a great thunder” — φωνή, voice or sound: the voice of Christ in 1:15. καὶ ἡ φωνὴ ἣν ἤκουσα ὡς κιθαρῳδῶν κιθαριζόντων ἐν ταῖς κιθάραις αὐτῶν, “The sound which I heard was like that of harpists playing on their harps” — overwhelming power and exquisite beauty together.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sound"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunder"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2790","hebrew":"κιθαρῳδός","translit":"kitharōidós","english":"harpists"},{"strongs":"G2789","hebrew":"κιθαρίζω","translit":"kitharízō","english":"playing"},{"strongs":"G2788","hebrew":"κιθάρα","translit":"kithára","english":"harps"}]},{"v":3,"text":"They sing a new song before the throne, and before the four living creatures and the elders. No one could learn the song except the one hundred forty-four thousand, those who had been redeemed out of the earth.","understanding":"καὶ ᾄδουσιν ὡς ᾠδὴν καινὴν ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου, “They sing a new song before the throne” — καινός, from the new family: new in kind, the song of redemption (5:9). καὶ οὐδεὶς ἐδύνατο μαθεῖν τὴν ᾠδὴν εἰ μὴ αἱ ἑκατὸν τεσσεράκοντα τέσσαρες χιλιάδες, “No one could learn the song except the one hundred forty-four thousand” — δύναμαι, from the power family; μανθάνω, to learn: only those who have experienced redemption can sing of it. οἱ ἠγορασμένοι ἀπὸ τῆς γῆς, “those who had been redeemed out of the earth” — ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family: bought out of the marketplace the beast controls (13:17); γῆ, from the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G103","hebrew":"ᾄδω","translit":"áidō","english":"sing"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G5603","hebrew":"ᾠδή","translit":"ōidḗ","english":"song"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"could"},{"strongs":"G3129","hebrew":"μανθάνω","translit":"manthánō","english":"learn"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"redeemed"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":4,"text":"These are those who were not defiled with women, for they are virgins. These are those who follow the Lamb wherever he goes. These were redeemed by Jesus from among men, the first fruits to God and to the Lamb.","understanding":"οὗτοί εἰσιν οἳ μετὰ γυναικῶν οὐκ ἐμολύνθησαν, παρθένοι γάρ εἰσιν, “These are those who were not defiled with women, for they are virgins” — παρθένος. In the prophets, idolatry is adultery; Paul presents the church to Christ ‘as a pure virgin’. The strongest reading is spiritual faithfulness — men and women who have not prostituted themselves to the beast and Babylon (17:1–5) — rather than literal celibacy. οὗτοι οἱ ἀκολουθοῦντες τῷ ἀρνίῳ ὅπου ἂν ὑπάγῃ, “These are those who follow the Lamb wherever he goes” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: the essence of discipleship, even to the cross. οὗτοι ἠγοράσθησαν ἀπὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων ἀπαρχὴ τῷ θεῷ καὶ τῷ ἀρνίῳ, “These were redeemed by Jesus from among men, the first fruits to God and to the Lamb” — ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family; ἀπαρχή, the first sheaf of the harvest, offered to God as a pledge of the rest.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"women"},{"strongs":"G3435","hebrew":"μολύνω","translit":"molýnō","english":"defiled"},{"strongs":"G3933","hebrew":"παρθένος","translit":"parthénos","english":"virgins"},{"strongs":"G190","hebrew":"ἀκολουθέω","translit":"akolouthéō","english":"follow"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"goes"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"redeemed"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G536","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","english":"first fruits"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":5,"text":"In their mouth was found no lie, for they are blameless.","understanding":"καὶ ἐν τῷ στόματι αὐτῶν οὐχ εὑρέθη ψεῦδος, “In their mouth was found no lie” — ψεῦδος: they did not join the great lie of the beast and the false prophet, nor say ‘Caesar is Lord’. Zephaniah promised that the remnant of Israel would ‘not speak lies’. ἄμωμοί εἰσιν, “for they are blameless” — ἄμωμος, without blemish: the word for a perfect sacrificial animal, like the Lamb Himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G5579","hebrew":"ψεῦδος","translit":"pseûdos","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G299","hebrew":"ἄμωμος","translit":"ámōmos","english":"blameless"}]},{"v":6,"text":"I saw an angel flying in mid heaven, having an eternal Good News to proclaim to those who dwell on the earth, and to every nation, tribe, language, and people.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἄλλον ἄγγελον πετόμενον ἐν μεσουρανήματι, “I saw an angel flying in mid heaven” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). Most manuscripts read ‘another angel’. Where the eagle cried ‘Woe’ (8:13), this angel brings good news. ἔχοντα εὐαγγέλιον αἰώνιον εὐαγγελίσαι ἐπὶ τοὺς καθημένους ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “having an eternal Good News to proclaim to those who dwell on the earth” — εὐαγγέλιον, gospel; αἰώνιος, from the time family: eternal, unchanging; γῆ, from the world family. Even now, the earth-dwellers are offered the gospel. καὶ ἐπὶ πᾶν ἔθνος καὶ φυλὴν καὶ γλῶσσαν καὶ λαόν, “and to every nation, tribe, language, and people.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G4072","hebrew":"πέτομαι","translit":"pétomai","english":"flying"},{"strongs":"G3321","hebrew":"μεσουράνημα","translit":"mesouránēma","english":"mid heaven"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"},{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"proclaim"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nation"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribe"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"language"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":7,"text":"He said with a loud voice, “Fear the Lord, and give him glory; for the hour of his judgment has come. Worship him who made the heaven, the earth, the sea, and the springs of waters!”","understanding":"λέγων ἐν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ· Φοβήθητε τὸν θεὸν καὶ δότε αὐτῷ δόξαν, “He said with a loud voice, ‘Fear the Lord, and give him glory’” — the call of 11:13 again: turn from the beast to the Creator. ὅτι ἦλθεν ἡ ὥρα τῆς κρίσεως αὐτοῦ, “for the hour of his judgment has come” — ὥρα, from the time family; κρίσις, from the judge family: judgement as an act, a trial in progress, as distinct from κρίμα, the verdict (17:1). καὶ προσκυνήσατε τῷ ποιήσαντι τὸν οὐρανὸν καὶ τὴν γῆν καὶ θάλασσαν καὶ πηγὰς ὑδάτων, “Worship him who made the heaven, the earth, the sea, and the springs of waters!” — γῆ, from the world family. The four realms struck by the first four trumpets: the Creator of all deserves the worship the beast demands.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"Fear"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"Worship"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"made"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"springs"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Another, a second angel, followed, saying, “Babylon the great has fallen, which has made all the nations to drink of the wine of the wrath of her sexual immorality.”","understanding":"Καὶ ἄλλος ἄγγελος δεύτερος ἠκολούθησεν λέγων, “Another, a second angel, followed, saying” — ἄλλος, from the another family. Ἔπεσεν ἔπεσεν Βαβυλὼν ἡ μεγάλη, “Babylon the great has fallen” — Isaiah's cry, ‘Fallen, fallen is Babylon’. Prophetic past tense: so certain it is as good as done. Babylon, the great city of chapters 17–18, is the world-system of power, wealth and pleasure opposed to God — Rome for John's readers. ἣ ἐκ τοῦ οἴνου τοῦ θυμοῦ τῆς πορνείας αὐτῆς πεπότικεν πάντα τὰ ἔθνη, “which has made all the nations to drink of the wine of the wrath of her sexual immorality” — θυμός, from the wrath family: here the passion of her immorality, which becomes the wine of God's wrath (v. 10).","words":[{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G190","hebrew":"ἀκολουθέω","translit":"akolouthéō","english":"followed"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fallen"},{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"Babylon"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G4222","hebrew":"ποτίζω","translit":"potízō","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Another angel, a third, followed them, saying with a great voice, “If anyone worships the beast and his image, and receives a mark on his forehead, or on his hand,","understanding":"Καὶ ἄλλος ἄγγελος τρίτος ἠκολούθησεν αὐτοῖς λέγων ἐν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ, “Another angel, a third, followed them, saying with a great voice” — ἄλλος, from the another family. Εἴ τις προσκυνεῖ τὸ θηρίον καὶ τὴν εἰκόνα αὐτοῦ, “If anyone worships the beast and his image” — εἰκών, from the image family. καὶ λαμβάνει χάραγμα ἐπὶ τοῦ μετώπου αὐτοῦ ἢ ἐπὶ τὴν χεῖρα αὐτοῦ, “and receives a mark on his forehead, or on his hand” — χάραγμα: the beast threatened death for refusing his mark (13:15); God warns of something worse for accepting it.","words":[{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G190","hebrew":"ἀκολουθέω","translit":"akolouthéō","english":"followed"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worships"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receives"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"mark"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"forehead"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"}]},{"v":10,"text":"he also will drink of the wine of the wrath of God, which is prepared unmixed in the cup of his anger. He will be tormented with fire and sulfur in the presence of the holy angels, and in the presence of the Lamb.","understanding":"καὶ αὐτὸς πίεται ἐκ τοῦ οἴνου τοῦ θυμοῦ τοῦ θεοῦ, “he also will drink of the wine of the wrath of God” — θυμός, from the wrath family: God's wrath poured out in its full intensity. Those who drank Babylon's wine (v. 8) must drink God's. τοῦ κεκερασμένου ἀκράτου ἐν τῷ ποτηρίῳ τῆς ὀργῆς αὐτοῦ, “which is prepared unmixed in the cup of his anger” — ὀργή, from the wrath family: settled, judicial anger. θυμός and ὀργή in one verse: passionate intensity and settled justice together. ἄκρατος, undiluted: ancient wine was mixed with water; this is not. καὶ βασανισθήσεται ἐν πυρὶ καὶ θείῳ ἐνώπιον ἀγγέλων ἁγίων καὶ ἐνώπιον τοῦ ἀρνίου, “He will be tormented with fire and sulfur in the presence of the holy angels, and in the presence of the Lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family: the One they rejected is the witness of their judgement. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2767","hebrew":"κεράννυμι","translit":"keránnymi","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G194","hebrew":"ἄκρατος","translit":"ákratos","english":"unmixed"},{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"anger"},{"strongs":"G928","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","english":"tormented"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2303","hebrew":"θεῖον","translit":"theîon","english":"sulfur"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":11,"text":"The smoke of their torment goes up forever and ever. They have no rest day and night, those who worship the beast and his image, and whoever receives the mark of his name.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ καπνὸς τοῦ βασανισμοῦ αὐτῶν εἰς αἰῶνας αἰώνων ἀναβαίνει, “The smoke of their torment goes up forever and ever” — αἰών, from the time family and the world family: like the smoke of Sodom; the language of final, lasting judgement. Christians differ on whether it means unending conscious punishment or a final, irreversible destruction, but not on its seriousness. καὶ οὐκ ἔχουσιν ἀνάπαυσιν ἡμέρας καὶ νυκτός, “They have no rest day and night” — ἀνάπαυσις, from the rest family; ἡμέρα, from the time family: the same words as the unceasing worship of the living creatures (4:8). οἱ προσκυνοῦντες τὸ θηρίον καὶ τὴν εἰκόνα αὐτοῦ, “those who worship the beast and his image” — εἰκών, from the image family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G929","hebrew":"βασανισμός","translit":"basanismós","english":"torment"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"goes up"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"},{"strongs":"G372","hebrew":"ἀνάπαυσις","translit":"anápausis","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receives"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"mark"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Here is the perseverance of the saints, those who keep the commandments of God, and the faith of Jesus.”","understanding":"Ὧδε ἡ ὑπομονὴ τῶν ἁγίων ἐστίν, “Here is the perseverance of the saints” — ὑπομονή, from the endurance family, as in 13:10. The warnings of verses 9–11 are written to strengthen believers: it is better to suffer the beast's sword than God's wrath. οἱ τηροῦντες τὰς ἐντολὰς τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τὴν πίστιν Ἰησοῦ, “those who keep the commandments of God, and the faith of Jesus” — τηρέω, from the hold family: the marks of the woman's offspring (12:17). ‘The faith of Jesus’ is faith in Jesus, and faithfulness like His. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I heard a voice from heaven saying, “Write, ‘Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on.’” “Yes,” says the Spirit, “that they may rest from their labors; for their works follow with them.”","understanding":"Καὶ ἤκουσα φωνῆς ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ λεγούσης· Γράψον· Μακάριοι οἱ νεκροὶ οἱ ἐν κυρίῳ ἀποθνῄσκοντες ἀπ᾽ ἄρτι, “Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on” — the second of the seven blessings. In a chapter about martyrdom, death in the Lord is called blessed. ναί, λέγει τὸ πνεῦμα, ἵνα ἀναπαήσονται ἐκ τῶν κόπων αὐτῶν, “that they may rest from their labors” — ἀναπαύω, from the rest family: the opposite of the beast-worshippers' ‘no rest’ (v. 11); κόπος, from the work family: wearying toil. τὰ γὰρ ἔργα αὐτῶν ἀκολουθεῖ μετ᾽ αὐτῶν, “for their works follow with them” — ἔργον, also from the work family: κόπος is the exhausting labour, ἔργον the deed that remains. Their toil ends; its fruit goes with them.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"Write"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"die"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G534","hebrew":"ἀπάρτι","translit":"apárti","english":"from now on"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G373","hebrew":"ἀναπαύω","translit":"anapaúō","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G2873","hebrew":"κόπος","translit":"kópos","english":"labors"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G190","hebrew":"ἀκολουθέω","translit":"akolouthéō","english":"follow"}]},{"v":14,"text":"I looked, and behold, a white cloud; and on the cloud one sitting like a son of man, having on his head a golden crown, and in his hand a sharp sickle.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον, καὶ ἰδοὺ νεφέλη λευκή, “I looked, and behold, a white cloud” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). καὶ ἐπὶ τὴν νεφέλην καθήμενον ὅμοιον υἱὸν ἀνθρώπου, “and on the cloud one sitting like a son of man” — Daniel's ‘one like a son of man’ coming with the clouds, as in 1:13: this is Christ. ἔχων ἐπὶ τῆς κεφαλῆς αὐτοῦ στέφανον χρυσοῦν, “having on his head a golden crown” — στέφανος, from the crown family: the victor's wreath. καὶ ἐν τῇ χειρὶ αὐτοῦ δρέπανον ὀξύ, “and in his hand a sharp sickle” — δρέπανον, a harvesting hook: the Lord of the harvest.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"looked"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sitting"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G1407","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","english":"sickle"},{"strongs":"G3691","hebrew":"ὀξύς","translit":"oxýs","english":"sharp"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Another angel came out of the temple, crying with a loud voice to him who sat on the cloud, “Send your sickle, and reap; for the hour to reap has come; for the harvest of the earth is ripe!”","understanding":"καὶ ἄλλος ἄγγελος ἐξῆλθεν ἐκ τοῦ ναοῦ κράζων, “Another angel came out of the temple, crying” — ἄλλος, from the another family: the message comes from God's presence. Πέμψον τὸ δρέπανόν σου καὶ θέρισον, ὅτι ἦλθεν ἡ ὥρα θερίσαι, “Send your sickle, and reap; for the hour to reap has come” — θερίζω, to reap grain; ὥρα, from the time family: the Father's appointed hour (Mark 13:32). ὅτι ἐξηράνθη ὁ θερισμὸς τῆς γῆς, “for the harvest of the earth is ripe!” — θερισμός; γῆ, from the world family. The grain harvest is most naturally the gathering of God's people, as Jesus described: ‘gather the wheat into my barn’.","words":[{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"crying"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G3992","hebrew":"πέμπω","translit":"pémpō","english":"Send"},{"strongs":"G1407","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","english":"sickle"},{"strongs":"G2325","hebrew":"θερίζω","translit":"therízō","english":"reap"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G2326","hebrew":"θερισμός","translit":"therismós","english":"harvest"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3583","hebrew":"ξηραίνω","translit":"xēraínō","english":"ripe"}]},{"v":16,"text":"He who sat on the cloud thrust his sickle on the earth, and the earth was reaped.","understanding":"καὶ ἔβαλεν ὁ καθήμενος ἐπὶ τῆς νεφέλης τὸ δρέπανον αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὴν γῆν, “He who sat on the cloud thrust his sickle on the earth” — the Son of Man reaps in person; γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐθερίσθη ἡ γῆ, “and the earth was reaped” — one swift stroke: the gathering is complete. No judgement is described here; the second harvest is different.","words":[{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"cloud"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrust"},{"strongs":"G1407","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","english":"sickle"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2325","hebrew":"θερίζω","translit":"therízō","english":"reaped"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Another angel came out of the temple which is in heaven. He also had a sharp sickle.","understanding":"Καὶ ἄλλος ἄγγελος ἐξῆλθεν ἐκ τοῦ ναοῦ τοῦ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “Another angel came out of the temple which is in heaven” — ἄλλος, from the another family. ἔχων καὶ αὐτὸς δρέπανον ὀξύ, “He also had a sharp sickle” — a second harvest, carried out by an angel, as Jesus said: ‘the reapers are angels’ (Matthew 13:39).","words":[{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1407","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","english":"sickle"},{"strongs":"G3691","hebrew":"ὀξύς","translit":"oxýs","english":"sharp"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Another angel came out from the altar, he who has power over fire, and he called with a great voice to him who had the sharp sickle, saying, “Send your sharp sickle, and gather the clusters of the vine of the earth, for the earth’s grapes are fully ripe!”","understanding":"Καὶ ἄλλος ἄγγελος ἐκ τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου, ὁ ἔχων ἐξουσίαν ἐπὶ τοῦ πυρός, “Another angel came out from the altar, he who has power over fire” — ἄλλος, from the another family; ἐξουσία, from the power family; θυσιαστήριον: the altar of the martyrs' cry (6:9) and the saints' prayers (8:3–5). This harvest answers their prayers. Πέμψον σου τὸ δρέπανον τὸ ὀξὺ καὶ τρύγησον τοὺς βότρυας τῆς ἀμπέλου τῆς γῆς, “Send your sharp sickle, and gather the clusters of the vine of the earth” — τρυγάω, to gather grapes; γῆ, from the world family. Not the true Vine (John 15) but the vine of the earth. ὅτι ἤκμασαν αἱ σταφυλαὶ αὐτῆς, “for the earth’s grapes are fully ripe!” — evil has come to full ripeness.","words":[{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G5455","hebrew":"φωνέω","translit":"phōnéō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G1407","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","english":"sickle"},{"strongs":"G3691","hebrew":"ὀξύς","translit":"oxýs","english":"sharp"},{"strongs":"G5166","hebrew":"τρυγάω","translit":"trygáō","english":"gather"},{"strongs":"G1009","hebrew":"βότρυς","translit":"bótrys","english":"clusters"},{"strongs":"G288","hebrew":"ἄμπελος","translit":"ámpelos","english":"vine"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G187","hebrew":"ἀκμάζω","translit":"akmázō","english":"fully ripe"},{"strongs":"G4718","hebrew":"σταφυλή","translit":"staphylḗ","english":"grapes"}]},{"v":19,"text":"The angel thrust his sickle into the earth, and gathered the vintage of the earth, and threw it into the great wine press of the wrath of God.","understanding":"καὶ ἔβαλεν ὁ ἄγγελος τὸ δρέπανον αὐτοῦ εἰς τὴν γῆν, “The angel thrust his sickle into the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐτρύγησεν τὴν ἄμπελον τῆς γῆς, καὶ ἔβαλεν εἰς τὴν ληνὸν τοῦ θυμοῦ τοῦ θεοῦ τὸν μέγαν, “and gathered the vintage of the earth, and threw it into the great wine press of the wrath of God” — ληνός, a winepress; θυμός, from the wrath family. Isaiah saw God treading the winepress of judgement alone; Joel called the nations to the valley of decision, ‘for the winepress is full’.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrust"},{"strongs":"G1407","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","english":"sickle"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5166","hebrew":"τρυγάω","translit":"trygáō","english":"gathered"},{"strongs":"G288","hebrew":"ἄμπελος","translit":"ámpelos","english":"vintage"},{"strongs":"G3025","hebrew":"ληνός","translit":"lēnós","english":"wine press"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":20,"text":"The wine press was trodden outside of the city, and blood came out of the wine press, even to the bridles of the horses, as far as one thousand six hundred stadia.","understanding":"καὶ ἐπατήθη ἡ ληνὸς ἔξωθεν τῆς πόλεως, “The wine press was trodden outside of the city” — outside the city, where Jesus suffered (Hebrews 13:12): those who refuse His suffering for them face their own. καὶ ἐξῆλθεν αἷμα ἐκ τῆς ληνοῦ ἄχρι τῶν χαλινῶν τῶν ἵππων, “and blood came out of the wine press, even to the bridles of the horses” — a deliberately horrifying picture. ἀπὸ σταδίων χιλίων ἑξακοσίων, “as far as one thousand six hundred stadia” — about 300 kilometres, roughly the length of Palestine; 1,600 is 4 × 4 × 100: the four corners of the earth. Universal, total judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G3961","hebrew":"πατέω","translit":"patéō","english":"trodden"},{"strongs":"G3025","hebrew":"ληνός","translit":"lēnós","english":"wine press"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5469","hebrew":"χαλινός","translit":"chalinós","english":"bridles"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G4712","hebrew":"στάδιον","translit":"stádion","english":"stadia"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"one thousand"},{"strongs":"G1812","hebrew":"ἑξακόσιοι","translit":"hexakósioi","english":"six hundred"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Lamb on Mount Zion","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture, after the beasts rising from sea and land, a different scene: a Lamb standing on a mountain, and with Him a vast, ordered company, the names of the Lamb and His Father written on every forehead. From heaven comes a sound like a waterfall, like thunder, like a great choir of harps, singing a song only they can learn.","meaning":"“I saw, and behold, the Lamb standing on Mount Zion, and with him a number, one hundred forty-four thousand, having his name, and the name of his Father, written on their foreheads” (v. 1). “These are those who follow the Lamb wherever he goes” (v. 4). The beast's followers bear his mark; the Lamb's followers bear His name."},{"title":"The Eternal Gospel in Mid-Heaven","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture an angel flying high across the sky, visible to every nation, carrying a message for every tribe and language: ‘Fear God! Give Him glory! Worship the Creator!’","meaning":"“I saw an angel flying in mid heaven, having an eternal Good News to proclaim to those who dwell on the earth, and to every nation, tribe, language, and people” (v. 6). Even on the edge of judgement, God's last word to the world is good news."},{"title":"Rest and No Rest","verseRef":"v. 11-13","image":"Picture two scenes side by side. In one, smoke rises day and night from those who chose the beast; they have no rest. In the other, believers who died for Jesus lie down at last, their tired faces at peace, while all their works walk behind them like a faithful company.","meaning":"“They have no rest day and night, those who worship the beast and his image” (v. 11). “Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on.” “Yes,” says the Spirit, “that they may rest from their labors; for their works follow with them” (v. 13)."},{"title":"Two Harvests","verseRef":"v. 14-20","image":"Picture a golden wheat field and the Son of Man on a white cloud, sweeping His sickle once across it, gathering the grain home. Then picture a vineyard, its grapes heavy and dark, cut by an angel and thrown into a vast winepress outside the city — and red streaming out for miles.","meaning":"“He who sat on the cloud thrust his sickle on the earth, and the earth was reaped” (v. 16). “The angel... gathered the vintage of the earth, and threw it into the great wine press of the wrath of God” (v. 19). Every life will be harvested. The question is whether you are wheat gathered home, or grapes thrown into the winepress."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Lamb and the 144,000 (v. 1-5)","body":"John sees the Lamb on Mount Zion with the 144,000 who bear His and His Father's names; a heavenly sound like waters, thunder and harps sings a new song that only the redeemed can learn; they are spiritually pure, follow the Lamb wherever He goes, are firstfruits to God, and no lie is in their mouths."},{"label":"Three Angels (v. 6-13)","body":"One angel proclaims an eternal gospel to every nation: fear God, give Him glory, worship the Creator, for the hour of judgement has come; a second announces the fall of Babylon the great; a third warns that whoever worships the beast and receives its mark will drink the wine of God's wrath and have no rest; here is the endurance of the saints; a voice from heaven blesses those who die in the Lord, who rest from their labours."},{"label":"Two Harvests (v. 14-20)","body":"One like a son of man on a white cloud with a golden crown and a sharp sickle reaps the ripe harvest of the earth; another angel gathers the ripe grapes of the earth's vine and throws them into the great winepress of God's wrath, which is trodden outside the city, and blood flows for 1,600 stadia."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Lamb and His Army","paragraphs":["After the terrifying beasts of chapter 13, John sees the answer: “I saw, and behold, the Lamb standing on Mount Zion, and with him a number, one hundred forty-four thousand, having his name, and the name of his Father, written on their foreheads” (v. 1). The dragon stood on the shifting sand of the sea (13:1); the Lamb stands on the mountain of God. The beast's followers bear his mark (13:16); the Lamb's people bear His name. Hebrews says believers have already “come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem” (Hebrews 12:22).","They sing a new song that only they can learn, because only the redeemed know what redemption is (v. 3). They are described in four ways. They “were not defiled with women, for they are virgins” (v. 4): in the language of the prophets and Revelation, idolatry is sexual unfaithfulness, and Babylon is a prostitute (ch. 17). They have kept themselves pure for Christ, as Paul says, “I married you to one husband, that I might present you as a pure virgin to Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:2). They “follow the Lamb wherever he goes” — including the way of the cross. They are “the first fruits to God and to the Lamb” (v. 4), offered to God as the pledge of the full harvest. And “In their mouth was found no lie” (v. 5): they refused the beast's great lie."]},{"heading":"The Gospel, the Fall and the Warning","paragraphs":["Three angels fly through heaven with three messages. The first carries “an eternal Good News to proclaim to those who dwell on the earth, and to every nation, tribe, language, and people” (v. 6): “Fear the Lord, and give him glory; for the hour of his judgment has come. Worship him who made the heaven, the earth, the sea, and the springs of waters!” (v. 7). Even at this late hour, God offers the gospel to the whole world. Jesus said, “This Good News of the Kingdom will be preached in the whole world for a testimony to all the nations, and then the end will come” (Matthew 24:14).","The second angel announces, “Babylon the great has fallen” (v. 8). This is the first mention of Babylon, the great city of chapters 17–18 — the world-system of power, wealth and seduction, embodied in John's day by Rome. Her fall is announced before it is described: God's verdict is certain.","The third angel gives the most solemn warning in the Bible: whoever worships the beast “also will drink of the wine of the wrath of God, which is prepared unmixed in the cup of his anger” (v. 10). Both words for wrath appear: θυμός, the full intensity of God's anger, and ὀργή, His settled judicial wrath. The cup is ‘unmixed’ — not diluted with mercy. “The smoke of their torment goes up forever and ever” (v. 11). Faithful Christians have read this in different ways — as unending conscious punishment, or as total and final destruction — but none can read it lightly. Jesus said, “One who believes in the Son has eternal life, but one who disobeys the Son won’t see life, but the wrath of God remains on him” (John 3:36)."]},{"heading":"Blessed Are the Dead","paragraphs":["Why such a terrible warning? It is for the church: “Here is the perseverance of the saints, those who keep the commandments of God, and the faith of Jesus” (v. 12). Believers under pressure to worship the beast must weigh the cost: the beast can take their lives, but God holds their eternity. It is better to face the beast's sword than God's wrath.","Then comes one of the most comforting verses in Scripture: “Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on.” “Yes,” says the Spirit, “that they may rest from their labors; for their works follow with them” (v. 13). Those who worship the beast have no rest; those who die in the Lord rest. Their κόπος, their weary toil, is over; their ἔργα, their works of faith and love, go with them. Paul says, “your labor is not in vain in the Lord” (1 Corinthians 15:58).","This blessing is especially for martyrs, but it belongs to all who die ‘in the Lord’ — united to Christ by faith. Death for a believer is not defeat but rest. “There remains therefore a Sabbath rest for the people of God” (Hebrews 4:9)."]},{"heading":"The Grain Harvest and the Grape Harvest","paragraphs":["The chapter ends with two harvests. First, “one sitting like a son of man” on a white cloud, wearing a golden crown, reaps the earth with a sharp sickle (vv. 14–16). This is Christ Himself. The grain harvest is most naturally the gathering of His people at the end of the age: Jesus told of the harvest in which the master says, “gather the wheat into my barn” (Matthew 13:30). Some see both harvests as judgement, but the difference between them is striking: the first is reaped by Christ, and nothing is said of wrath.","The second harvest is a vintage: an angel from the altar of the martyrs' prayers gathers “the clusters of the vine of the earth” (v. 18) and throws them “into the great wine press of the wrath of God” (v. 19). Isaiah saw God treading the winepress of judgement; Joel called the nations to the valley of decision, where the winepress is full. The ‘vine of the earth’ is the opposite of Christ, the true Vine.","“The wine press was trodden outside of the city, and blood came out of the wine press, even to the bridles of the horses, as far as one thousand six hundred stadia” (v. 20). The picture is meant to horrify. Outside the city is where Jesus suffered God's judgement for sinners (Hebrews 13:12). Those who will not take shelter in His death outside the city will face judgement there themselves. Every life will be harvested; the gospel of verse 6 is still the way to be gathered as wheat."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Harvest at the End of the Age","paragraphs":["Jesus explained His parable of the wheat and the weeds: “The enemy who sowed them is the devil. The harvest is the end of the age, and the reapers are angels” (Matthew 13:39). He also said, “Behold, I tell you, lift up your eyes, and look at the fields, that they are white for harvest already” (John 4:35) — the harvest begins now, through the gospel.","James calls believers firstfruits: “Of his own will he gave birth to us by the word of truth, that we should be a kind of first fruits of his creatures” (James 1:18) — like the 144,000 of verse 4.","And the rest of verse 13 echoes Hebrews: “For he who has entered into his rest has himself also rested from his works, as God did from his” (Hebrews 4:10)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Virgins","hebrew":"παρθένος","translit":"parthénos","strongs":"G3933 (defiled μολύνω, molýnō, G3435)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “a maiden; by implication, an unmarried daughter.” παρθένος usually means an unmarried young woman; here it is applied to the whole company of the redeemed. Throughout the Bible, idolatry is spiritual adultery, and God's people are His bride. To be ‘virgins’ is to have kept oneself for Christ alone — not seduced by Babylon the prostitute or the beast. Paul's hope was ‘to present you as a pure virgin to Christ’ (2 Corinthians 11:2)."},{"english":"First fruits","hebrew":"ἀπαρχή","translit":"aparchḗ","strongs":"G536 (redeemed ἀγοράζω, agorázō, G59)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “a beginning of sacrifice, i.e. the (Jewish) first-fruit (figuratively).” ἀπαρχή was the first portion of the harvest, offered to God as a thanksgiving and a pledge that the rest would follow. Christ is ‘the first fruits of those who are asleep’ (1 Corinthians 15:20), and believers are ‘a kind of first fruits of his creatures’ (James 1:18). The 144,000 are firstfruits ‘to God and to the Lamb’: the beginning and guarantee of a far greater harvest."},{"english":"Eternal","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","strongs":"G166 (Good News εὐαγγέλιον, euangélion, G2098)","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “perpetual (also used of past time, or past and future as well).” αἰώνιος, from the time family, comes from αἰών, an age: belonging to the age to come, without end. It describes eternal life, eternal judgement, the eternal Spirit — and here the ‘eternal Good News’, the only place the gospel is called eternal. Empires rise and fall; the beast has forty-two months; but the gospel does not change."},{"english":"Sickle","hebrew":"δρέπανον","translit":"drépanon","strongs":"G1407 (harvest θερισμός, therismós, G2326)","verses":"v. 14-19","insight":"Strong's: “a gathering hook (especially for harvesting).” δρέπανον, a curved blade for cutting grain or grapes, appears seven times in verses 14–19 and nowhere else in the New Testament except Mark 4:29, where Jesus says that when the grain is ripe, the farmer ‘puts forth the sickle, because the harvest has come’. In Revelation the Son of Man holds the sickle: the harvest of history is in His hands."},{"english":"Wine press","hebrew":"ληνός","translit":"lēnós","strongs":"G3025 (wrath θυμός, thymós, G2372)","verses":"v. 19, 20; 19:15","insight":"Strong's: “a trough, i.e. wine-vat.” ληνός was a stone trough where grapes were trodden underfoot and the juice ran out through a channel. Isaiah 63 pictures God coming from Edom with garments stained red, having trodden the winepress of His wrath alone. Revelation 19:15 says Christ ‘treads the wine press of the fierceness of the wrath of God’. The judgement that Christ bore for His people on the cross, He will execute on those who reject Him."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit sets two destinies before you — and urges you to choose life.","items":[{"title":"Follow the Lamb Wherever He Goes","body":"The redeemed “follow the Lamb wherever he goes” (v. 4). The Spirit asks you to follow Jesus not only where it is easy, but where it is costly — into humility, sacrifice and even suffering. He leads to Mount Zion."},{"title":"Rest Is Coming","body":"“Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord... that they may rest from their labors; for their works follow with them” (v. 13). If you are weary from serving God, the Spirit speaks this ‘yes’ over you. Your labour is not wasted. Rest is coming, and your faithful works will go with you."},{"title":"Proclaim the Eternal Gospel","body":"God's last call to the world is good news: ‘Fear God, give Him glory, worship the Creator’ (vv. 6–7). The Spirit wants you to carry that message urgently and lovingly to every nation, tribe, language and people — and to your neighbour."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Purity Check","body":"Have you kept yourself spiritually faithful to Christ, or have you been seduced by the world's idols of money, sex and power (v. 4)?"},{"title":"The Truth Check","body":"Is there any lie in your mouth — in what you say about yourself, others or God (v. 5)?"},{"title":"The Harvest Check","body":"If the harvest came today, would you be gathered as wheat into the Lord's barn (vv. 15–16)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Share the Eternal Gospel","body":"Write a short, clear summary of the gospel in your own words, based on verses 6–7 and John 3:16. This week, share it with one person who does not know Jesus."},{"title":"Prepare to Rest","body":"Write down the ‘works’ you hope will follow you (v. 13) — people you have served, faith you have shared, love you have shown. Then choose one work of love to do this week that will last into eternity."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lamb of God, standing on Mount Zion, thank You that You have redeemed me and written Your name and Your Father's name on me. Teach me the new song of the redeemed. Keep me pure for You, free from every idol, and let no lie be found in my mouth. Help me follow You wherever You go. Thank You for the eternal gospel. Make me a messenger of it to every nation, tribe, language and people, calling them to fear God, give Him glory and worship the Creator. Lord, I tremble at the cup of Your wrath, and I thank You that You drank it for me. Give me the endurance of the saints, to keep God's commandments and the faith of Jesus. Thank You that those who die in You are blessed, and that they rest from their labours, and their works follow them. Son of Man on the white cloud, when You reap the earth, gather me with Your wheat into Your barn. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Ruth Gleaning at the Harvest","ref":"Ruth 2","relatedVerses":"v. 14-16","summary":"Ruth, a Moabite widow who had chosen Israel's God, went to glean in the barley fields of Bethlehem and happened on the field of Boaz. Boaz, her kinsman-redeemer, treated her kindly, told his reapers to leave extra grain for her, and later redeemed her and married her. From their line came David — and Jesus. The harvest in the Bible is a time of gathering and redemption: the Lord of the harvest gathers the outsider into His family."},{"title":"Fallen Is Babylon","ref":"Isaiah 21:1-10","relatedVerses":"v. 8","summary":"Isaiah saw a terrible vision of invaders coming against Babylon. A watchman on the tower kept looking, day and night, until he saw a chariot of horsemen and cried out, ‘Fallen, fallen is Babylon; and all the engraved images of her gods are broken to the ground.’ Babylon, the proud empire that had crushed Jerusalem and exiled God's people, would itself fall. Revelation takes up the same cry for the Babylon of its day — and of every day."},{"title":"The One Who Treads the Winepress Alone","ref":"Isaiah 63:1-6","relatedVerses":"v. 19-20","summary":"Isaiah saw a warrior coming from Edom, His garments stained crimson. Asked why His clothes were red like one who treads the winepress, He answered, ‘I have trodden the winepress alone; and of the peoples no one was with me... their lifeblood is sprinkled on my garments.’ He had come to bring judgement on His enemies and redemption for His people. Revelation applies this picture to the final judgement — and in 19:13 the Rider is clothed in a robe dipped in blood."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":15,"chapterTitle":"The Song of Moses and the Lamb","themeTitle":"“Great and Marvelous Are Your Works, Lord God, the Almighty!”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw another great and marvelous sign in the sky: seven angels having the seven last plagues, for in them God’s wrath is finished.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἄλλο σημεῖον ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ μέγα καὶ θαυμαστόν, “I saw another great and marvelous sign in the sky” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἄλλος, from the another family: another of the same kind, after the woman and the dragon (12:1, 3); σημεῖον, from the signs family; θαυμαστός, wonderful. ἀγγέλους ἑπτὰ ἔχοντας πληγὰς ἑπτὰ τὰς ἐσχάτας, “seven angels having the seven last plagues” — πληγή; ἔσχατος, last: the final series. ὅτι ἐν αὐταῖς ἐτελέσθη ὁ θυμὸς τοῦ θεοῦ, “for in them God’s wrath is finished” — τελέω, from the perfect family: brought to its end, completed; θυμός, from the wrath family. The bowls are not endless; they complete God's judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"sign"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2298","hebrew":"θαυμαστός","translit":"thaumastós","english":"marvelous"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I saw something like a sea of glass mixed with fire, and those who overcame the beast, his image, and the number of his name, standing on the sea of glass, having harps of God.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ὡς θάλασσαν ὑαλίνην μεμιγμένην πυρί, “I saw something like a sea of glass mixed with fire” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); the crystal sea before the throne (4:6), now glowing with the fire of judgement — or like the Red Sea at the Exodus. καὶ τοὺς νικῶντας ἐκ τοῦ θηρίου καὶ ἐκ τῆς εἰκόνος αὐτοῦ καὶ ἐκ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ τοῦ ὀνόματος αὐτοῦ, “and those who overcame the beast, his image, and the number of his name” — νικάω: the beast ‘overcame’ them by killing them (13:7); in truth they overcame him by refusing him; εἰκών, from the image family. ἑστῶτας ἐπὶ τὴν θάλασσαν τὴν ὑαλίνην, ἔχοντας κιθάρας τοῦ θεοῦ, “standing on the sea of glass, having harps of God” — like Israel on the far shore of the Red Sea.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G5193","hebrew":"ὑάλινος","translit":"hyálinos","english":"glass"},{"strongs":"G3396","hebrew":"μίγνυμι","translit":"mígnymi","english":"mixed"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcame"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"having"},{"strongs":"G2788","hebrew":"κιθάρα","translit":"kithára","english":"harps"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":3,"text":"They sang the song of Moses, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb, saying, “Great and marvelous are your works, Lord God, the Almighty! Righteous and true are your ways, you King of the nations.","understanding":"καὶ ᾄδουσιν τὴν ᾠδὴν Μωϋσέως τοῦ δούλου τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τὴν ᾠδὴν τοῦ ἀρνίου, “They sang the song of Moses, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb” — δοῦλος, from the servant family; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. One song, two names: the first exodus from Egypt and the greater exodus won by the Lamb. Μεγάλα καὶ θαυμαστὰ τὰ ἔργα σου, κύριε ὁ θεὸς ὁ παντοκράτωρ, “Great and marvelous are your works, Lord God, the Almighty!” — ἔργον, from the work family; θαυμαστός, the same word as in verse 1. δίκαιαι καὶ ἀληθιναὶ αἱ ὁδοί σου, ὁ βασιλεὺς τῶν ἐθνῶν, “Righteous and true are your ways, you King of the nations” — δίκαιος, ἀληθινός. Many manuscripts read ‘King of the ages’. The song does not praise the singers; it is all about God.","words":[{"strongs":"G103","hebrew":"ᾄδω","translit":"áidō","english":"sang"},{"strongs":"G5603","hebrew":"ᾠδή","translit":"ōidḗ","english":"song"},{"strongs":"G3475","hebrew":"Μωσεύς","translit":"Mōseús","english":"Moses"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"Great"},{"strongs":"G2298","hebrew":"θαυμαστός","translit":"thaumastós","english":"marvelous"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"Righteous"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"ways"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"King"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"}]},{"v":4,"text":"Who wouldn’t fear you, Lord, and glorify your name? For you only are holy. For all the nations will come and worship before you. For your righteous acts have been revealed.”","understanding":"τίς οὐ μὴ φοβηθῇ, κύριε, καὶ δοξάσει τὸ ὄνομά σου; “Who wouldn’t fear you, Lord, and glorify your name?” — φοβέομαι; δοξάζω: the answer to the beast's ‘Who is like the beast?’ (13:4). ὅτι μόνος ὅσιος, “For you only are holy” — ὅσιος, from the holy family: holy as faithful and right, pure and true to Himself, as distinct from ἅγιος, set apart. ὅτι πάντα τὰ ἔθνη ἥξουσιν καὶ προσκυνήσουσιν ἐνώπιόν σου, “For all the nations will come and worship before you” — from Psalm 86: a promise of the nations' conversion. ὅτι τὰ δικαιώματά σου ἐφανερώθησαν, “For your righteous acts have been revealed” — δικαίωμα, a righteous decree or act; φανερόω, from the appear family: made visible, brought into the open.","words":[{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorify"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G3441","hebrew":"μόνος","translit":"mónos","english":"only"},{"strongs":"G3741","hebrew":"ὅσιος","translit":"hósios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G1345","hebrew":"δικαίωμα","translit":"dikaíōma","english":"righteous acts"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"}]},{"v":5,"text":"After these things I looked, and the temple of the tabernacle of the testimony in heaven was opened.","understanding":"Καὶ μετὰ ταῦτα εἶδον, καὶ ἠνοίγη ὁ ναὸς τῆς σκηνῆς τοῦ μαρτυρίου ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, “After these things I looked, and the temple of the tabernacle of the testimony in heaven was opened” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); σκηνή, tent; μαρτύριον, from the witness family: testimony as evidence — the tablets of the Law kept in the ark (Exodus 25:16). The judgements come from the place where God's covenant Law is kept: they are just.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"looked"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"tabernacle"},{"strongs":"G3142","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"}]},{"v":6,"text":"The seven angels who had the seven plagues came out, clothed with pure, bright linen, and wearing golden sashes around their breasts.","understanding":"καὶ ἐξῆλθον οἱ ἑπτὰ ἄγγελοι οἱ ἔχοντες τὰς ἑπτὰ πληγὰς ἐκ τοῦ ναοῦ, “The seven angels who had the seven plagues came out” — from God's presence. ἐνδεδυμένοι λίνον καθαρὸν λαμπρόν, “clothed with pure, bright linen” — the dress of priests. καὶ περιεζωσμένοι περὶ τὰ στήθη ζώνας χρυσᾶς, “and wearing golden sashes around their breasts” — like the Son of Man (1:13): they act as His priestly servants. Judgement is holy work.","words":[{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"came out"},{"strongs":"G1746","hebrew":"ἐνδύω","translit":"endýō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G2986","hebrew":"λαμπρός","translit":"lamprós","english":"bright"},{"strongs":"G3043","hebrew":"λίνον","translit":"línon","english":"linen"},{"strongs":"G4024","hebrew":"περιζώννυμι","translit":"perizṓnnymi","english":"wearing"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G2223","hebrew":"ζώνη","translit":"zṓnē","english":"sashes"},{"strongs":"G4738","hebrew":"στῆθος","translit":"stēthos","english":"breasts"}]},{"v":7,"text":"One of the four living creatures gave to the seven angels seven golden bowls full of the wrath of God, who lives forever and ever.","understanding":"καὶ ἓν ἐκ τῶν τεσσάρων ζῴων ἔδωκεν τοῖς ἑπτὰ ἀγγέλοις ἑπτὰ φιάλας χρυσᾶς, “One of the four living creatures gave to the seven angels seven golden bowls” — φιάλη, a broad, shallow bowl: the same golden bowls that held the prayers of the saints (5:8). γεμούσας τοῦ θυμοῦ τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ ζῶντος εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “full of the wrath of God, who lives forever and ever” — θυμός, from the wrath family; ζάω, from the life family; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. The prayers come back as justice.","words":[{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowls"},{"strongs":"G1073","hebrew":"γέμω","translit":"gémō","english":"full"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The temple was filled with smoke from the glory of God, and from his power. No one was able to enter into the temple, until the seven plagues of the seven angels would be finished.","understanding":"καὶ ἐγεμίσθη ὁ ναὸς καπνοῦ ἐκ τῆς δόξης τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ ἐκ τῆς δυνάμεως αὐτοῦ, “The temple was filled with smoke from the glory of God, and from his power” — δύναμις, from the power family. As at Sinai, and when the glory filled the tabernacle and Solomon's temple. καὶ οὐδεὶς ἐδύνατο εἰσελθεῖν εἰς τὸν ναόν, “No one was able to enter into the temple” — δύναμαι, from the power family: even Moses could not enter the tent when the glory filled it. ἄχρι τελεσθῶσιν αἱ ἑπτὰ πληγαὶ τῶν ἑπτὰ ἀγγέλων, “until the seven plagues of the seven angels would be finished” — τελέω, from the perfect family. The time for intercession is over; judgement must run its course.","words":[{"strongs":"G1072","hebrew":"γεμίζω","translit":"gemízō","english":"filled"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G1410","hebrew":"δύναμαι","translit":"dýnamai","english":"able"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Standing on the Sea of Fire and Glass","verseRef":"v. 2-3","image":"Picture a vast sea like glass, glowing with fire, and standing on it a great company holding harps. They have come through the beast's persecution, as Israel came through the Red Sea, and now on the far shore they sing.","meaning":"“Those who overcame the beast, his image, and the number of his name, standing on the sea of glass, having harps of God. They sang the song of Moses, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb” (vv. 2–3). God's people are not swallowed by the sea of trouble; they stand on it."},{"title":"A Song Without ‘Me’","verseRef":"v. 3-4","image":"Picture the singers on the shore — martyrs, survivors, the faithful of every age. You might expect them to sing of their courage and suffering. Instead every line is about God: His works, His ways, His name, His holiness.","meaning":"“Great and marvelous are your works, Lord God, the Almighty! Righteous and true are your ways, you King of the nations” (v. 3). “For you only are holy” (v. 4). True worship forgets itself in the wonder of God."},{"title":"The Temple Filled with Smoke","verseRef":"v. 5-8","image":"Picture the doors of heaven's sanctuary swinging open. Seven angels in shining linen come out, and a living creature hands each one a golden bowl brimming with God's wrath. Behind them the temple fills with billowing smoke of glory, so thick that no one can go in.","meaning":"“The temple was filled with smoke from the glory of God, and from his power. No one was able to enter into the temple, until the seven plagues of the seven angels would be finished” (v. 8). There comes a time when the door of intercession closes. Now is the time to seek God."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Seven Last Plagues Announced (v. 1)","body":"John sees another great sign in heaven: seven angels with the seven last plagues, which complete the wrath of God."},{"label":"The Song of the Victors (v. 2-4)","body":"Those who have conquered the beast stand on a sea of glass mixed with fire, holding harps, and sing the song of Moses and of the Lamb: God's works are great and marvellous, His ways righteous and true; all will fear and glorify Him, for He alone is holy, and all nations will worship Him."},{"label":"The Bowls Given (v. 5-8)","body":"The sanctuary of the tent of testimony in heaven is opened; the seven angels come out clothed in pure linen with golden sashes; a living creature gives them seven golden bowls full of God's wrath; the temple fills with smoke from God's glory and power, and no one can enter until the plagues are finished."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Last Series","paragraphs":["Chapter 15 is the shortest chapter in Revelation, but it introduces the last and most severe series of judgements: “seven angels having the seven last plagues, for in them God’s wrath is finished” (v. 1). The seals affected a quarter of the earth, the trumpets a third; the bowls affect all. They are ‘last’ because they bring God's judgement to completion. After them comes the fall of Babylon, the return of Christ and the new creation.","Yet before any bowl is poured, we see the people of God safe and singing. This is Revelation's constant pattern: before judgement falls, God shows His people secure (7:1–17; 11:1; 14:1–5). The fire of judgement does not consume those who belong to the Lamb."]},{"heading":"The Song of Moses and of the Lamb","paragraphs":["John sees “something like a sea of glass mixed with fire, and those who overcame the beast, his image, and the number of his name, standing on the sea of glass, having harps of God” (v. 2). The scene recalls the Exodus. Israel passed through the Red Sea while Pharaoh's army drowned; on the far shore Moses and the people sang, ‘I will sing to the LORD, for he has triumphed gloriously.’ Now the people of God have come through the beast's persecution, and they sing “the song of Moses, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb” (v. 3).","It is one song, not two. The Exodus from Egypt was a picture; the Lamb's victory over sin, death and Satan is the reality. The Passover lamb's blood saved Israel; the Lamb's blood has saved a people from every nation.","And the song is entirely about God: “Great and marvelous are your works, Lord God, the Almighty! Righteous and true are your ways, you King of the nations. Who wouldn’t fear you, Lord, and glorify your name? For you only are holy. For all the nations will come and worship before you. For your righteous acts have been revealed” (vv. 3–4). The victors do not mention their own suffering or courage. They praise God's works, His justice and His holiness — and they look forward to the nations coming to worship Him. Even at the threshold of the final judgement, heaven's song expects the nations' conversion."]},{"heading":"The Temple Filled with Glory","paragraphs":["Then “the temple of the tabernacle of the testimony in heaven was opened” (v. 5). The ‘tabernacle of the testimony’ was the tent in the wilderness where the tablets of the Law were kept in the ark. The judgements that follow come from the place of God's covenant Law: they are not arbitrary but just.","Seven angels come out dressed like priests (v. 6), and one of the living creatures gives them “seven golden bowls full of the wrath of God, who lives forever and ever” (v. 7). These are the same kind of golden bowls that held the prayers of the saints (5:8). The prayers of God's people for justice return as the bowls of God's judgement.","Finally, “The temple was filled with smoke from the glory of God, and from his power. No one was able to enter into the temple, until the seven plagues of the seven angels would be finished” (v. 8). When the tabernacle and Solomon's temple were completed, the glory of God filled them so that no one could enter. Here the glory fills heaven's temple, and the time for intercession has ended. The sobering lesson is that there is a time to seek the Lord while He may be found."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Every Knee Shall Bow","paragraphs":["The song's hope that “all the nations will come and worship before you” (v. 4) matches Paul's: “that at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of those in heaven, those on earth, and those under the earth, and that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father” (Philippians 2:10–11).","Paul calls Christ our Passover: “For indeed Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed in our place” (1 Corinthians 5:7). The song of Moses and the Lamb celebrates the exodus He accomplished — the word Luke uses when Moses and Elijah spoke with Jesus on the mountain ‘about his departure (ἔξοδος), which he was about to accomplish at Jerusalem’ (Luke 9:31).","Paul also bursts into praise at the end of his account of God's ways with Jews and Gentiles: “Oh the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and the knowledge of God! How unsearchable are his judgments, and his ways past tracing out!” (Romans 11:33)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Holy","hebrew":"ὅσιος","translit":"hósios","strongs":"G3741 (holy ἅγιος, hágios, G40)","verses":"v. 4; 16:5","insight":"Strong's: “hallowed (pious, sacred, sure).” ὅσιος, from the holy family, is holiness as faithful rightness — being fully true, devout and right before God. It differs from ἅγιος, which stresses being set apart from the common and the unclean. ὅσιος is rare in the New Testament; Revelation uses it only of God (15:4; 16:5). ‘You only are ὅσιος’: God alone is perfectly true to Himself and His covenant. Peter applies it to the Messiah raised from the dead: God would not let His ‘Holy One’ see decay (Acts 2:27)."},{"english":"Bowl","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","strongs":"G5357 (wrath θυμός, thymós, G2372)","verses":"v. 7; 5:8; 16:1-17; 17:1; 21:9","insight":"Strong's: “a broad shallow cup (\"phial\").” φιάλη was a wide, shallow bowl used in temple worship for offerings, incense and libations. In 5:8 the golden bowls are full of the prayers of the saints; in 15:7 they are full of the wrath of God. The connection is deliberate: God's judgement is His answer to His people's cry for justice. A shallow bowl empties all at once — the bowls are poured out swiftly and completely."},{"english":"Marvelous","hebrew":"θαυμαστός","translit":"thaumastós","strongs":"G2298 (works ἔργον, érgon, G2041)","verses":"v. 1, 3","insight":"Strong's: “wondered at, i.e. (by implication) wonderful.” θαυμαστός describes what fills people with wonder. The world ‘marveled’ (θαυμάζω) at the beast (13:3) — impressed by power and a counterfeit resurrection. The redeemed marvel at God's works: ‘Great and marvelous are your works’ (15:3). What a person marvels at shows what they will worship."},{"english":"Testimony","hebrew":"μαρτύριον","translit":"martýrion","strongs":"G3142 (tabernacle σκηνή, skēnḗ, G4633)","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “something evidential, i.e. (genitive case) evidence given or (specially), the Decalogue (in the sacred Tabernacle).” μαρτύριον, from the witness family, is testimony as evidence — and especially the two tablets of the Ten Commandments kept in the ark, the ‘testimony’ of God's covenant. The ‘tabernacle of the testimony’ is the dwelling that housed them. The bowls of wrath come from there: God's judgements are the faithful enforcement of His own revealed Law."},{"english":"Righteous acts","hebrew":"δικαίωμα","translit":"dikaíōma","strongs":"G1345 (Righteous δίκαιος, díkaios, G1342)","verses":"v. 4; 19:8","insight":"Strong's: “an equitable deed; by implication, a statute or decision.” δικαίωμα has two uses in Revelation: God's ‘righteous acts have been revealed’ (15:4) in His judgements, and the bride's fine linen is ‘the righteous acts of the saints’ (19:8). God's justice is made visible in what He does; His people's righteousness is made visible in what they do."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit invites you to join the song of the victors.","items":[{"title":"You Will Stand on the Sea","body":"Those who overcame the beast stand on the sea of glass and fire (v. 2). The Spirit wants you to know that whatever sea of trouble you are passing through, God will bring you out on the other side, and you will sing."},{"title":"Make Your Worship About Him","body":"The victors' song is all about God's works, ways, name and holiness (vv. 3–4). The Spirit invites you to worship today without talking about yourself — just marvelling at who God is and what He has done."},{"title":"Seek Him While the Door Is Open","body":"In the end, “No one was able to enter into the temple” (v. 8). The Spirit reminds you that now is the day of salvation. Pray for those you love while the door of mercy is open."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Marvel Check","body":"What are you most impressed by — power, wealth, celebrity — or God's works (vv. 1, 3)?"},{"title":"The Song Check","body":"Do you have a ‘song of Moses’ — a testimony of how God has brought you through deep waters? When did you last sing it?"},{"title":"The Nations Check","body":"Do you share heaven's confidence that “all the nations will come and worship” (v. 4)? How does it shape your prayers?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write Your Exodus Song","body":"Read Exodus 15:1–18 and Revelation 15:3–4. Then write a short song or psalm of your own about how God has delivered you. Read it aloud to God this week, and share it with someone."},{"title":"A God-Only Prayer","body":"Spend ten minutes praying only about who God is — His works, ways, holiness and glory — without asking for anything or mentioning yourself (vv. 3–4)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Great and marvellous are Your works, Lord God Almighty! Righteous and true are Your ways, King of the nations. Who will not fear You, Lord, and glorify Your name? For You alone are holy. All the nations will come and worship before You, for Your righteous acts have been revealed. Thank You for bringing Your people through the sea, as You brought Israel out of Egypt, and for the greater exodus won by the Lamb. Let me stand with those who overcome the beast, with a harp in my hands and Your song in my mouth. Thank You that Your judgements are just, coming from the place where Your covenant is kept. Fill me with awe at Your glory and power. And while the door of mercy is open, help me pray for those who do not yet know You. In the name of the Lamb, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Song at the Red Sea","ref":"Exodus 14:21–15:21","relatedVerses":"v. 2-4","summary":"Trapped between Pharaoh's army and the sea, Israel watched as God drove the waters back with a strong east wind. They walked through on dry ground; the Egyptians followed and the waters returned and covered them. On the far shore Moses and the people sang: ‘I will sing to the LORD, for he has triumphed gloriously; the horse and his rider he has thrown into the sea... Who is like you, LORD, among the gods? Who is like you, glorious in holiness?’ Miriam took a tambourine and the women danced. The victors in Revelation 15 sing the same song — now the song of the Lamb."},{"title":"The Glory Fills the Tabernacle","ref":"Exodus 40:34-38","relatedVerses":"v. 5-8","summary":"When Moses finished building the tabernacle exactly as God commanded, the cloud covered the tent of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle. Moses was not able to enter the tent, because the cloud rested on it and the glory filled it. Revelation 15 shows the heavenly reality: the temple of the tent of testimony filled with smoke from God's glory, so that no one can enter until His purposes are complete."},{"title":"Solomon Dedicates the Temple","ref":"1 Kings 8:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 8","summary":"When the ark of the covenant was brought into Solomon's new temple and the priests came out of the holy place, a cloud filled the house of the LORD, ‘so that the priests could not stand to minister by reason of the cloud; for the glory of the LORD filled the house’. At the dedication of God's dwelling, His presence was so overwhelming that the priests could not do their work. In Revelation 15 that same glory fills heaven's temple as God's final purposes unfold."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":16,"chapterTitle":"The Seven Bowls of God's Wrath","themeTitle":"“True and Righteous Are Your Judgments”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I heard a loud voice out of the temple, saying to the seven angels, “Go and pour out the seven bowls of the wrath of God on the earth!”","understanding":"Καὶ ἤκουσα μεγάλης φωνῆς ἐκ τοῦ ναοῦ, “I heard a loud voice out of the temple” — from the temple no one could enter (15:8): the voice of God Himself. Ὑπάγετε καὶ ἐκχέετε τὰς ἑπτὰ φιάλας τοῦ θυμοῦ τοῦ θεοῦ εἰς τὴν γῆν, “Go and pour out the seven bowls of the wrath of God on the earth!” — ἐκχέω, to pour out: the word for pouring out drink offerings and blood; θυμός, from the wrath family; γῆ, from the world family. Where the trumpets struck a third, the bowls strike everything.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"Go"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"pour out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowls"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":2,"text":"The first went, and poured out his bowl into the earth, and it became a harmful and evil sore on the people who had the mark of the beast, and who worshiped his image.","understanding":"Καὶ ἀπῆλθεν ὁ πρῶτος καὶ ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ εἰς τὴν γῆν, “The first went, and poured out his bowl into the earth” — ἐκχέω; γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐγένετο ἕλκος κακὸν καὶ πονηρόν, “and it became a harmful and evil sore” — ἕλκος, an ulcer, like the boils of the sixth plague of Egypt. Two members of the evil family: κακός, bad, harmful in itself, and πονηρός, actively malignant, causing pain. ἐπὶ τοὺς ἀνθρώπους τοὺς ἔχοντας τὸ χάραγμα τοῦ θηρίου καὶ τοὺς προσκυνοῦντας τῇ εἰκόνι αὐτοῦ, “on the people who had the mark of the beast, and who worshiped his image” — χάραγμα; εἰκών, from the image family: the mark they accepted becomes a sore.","words":[{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"went"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1668","hebrew":"ἕλκος","translit":"hélkos","english":"sore"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"harmful"},{"strongs":"G4190","hebrew":"πονηρός","translit":"ponērós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"mark"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"}]},{"v":3,"text":"The second angel poured out his bowl into the sea, and it became blood as of a dead man. Every living thing in the sea died.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ δεύτερος ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ εἰς τὴν θάλασσαν, “The second angel poured out his bowl into the sea” — ἐκχέω. καὶ ἐγένετο αἷμα ὡς νεκροῦ, “and it became blood as of a dead man” — not a third (8:8) but all: thick, clotted, stinking. καὶ πᾶσα ψυχὴ ζωῆς ἀπέθανεν, τὰ ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ, “Every living thing in the sea died” — ψυχή and ζωή, two members of the life family together: literally ‘every soul of life’; ψυχή is a living being, ζωή the principle of life itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"living thing"},{"strongs":"G599","hebrew":"ἀποθνήσκω","translit":"apothnḗskō","english":"died"}]},{"v":4,"text":"The third poured out his bowl into the rivers and springs of water, and they became blood.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ τρίτος ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ εἰς τοὺς ποταμοὺς καὶ τὰς πηγὰς τῶν ὑδάτων, “The third poured out his bowl into the rivers and springs of water” — ἐκχέω. καὶ ἐγένετο αἷμα, “and they became blood” — the first plague of Egypt in full. Fresh water, essential to life, is gone.","words":[{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"rivers"},{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"springs"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"}]},{"v":5,"text":"I heard the angel of the waters saying, “You are righteous, who are and who were, O Holy One, because you have judged these things.","understanding":"Καὶ ἤκουσα τοῦ ἀγγέλου τῶν ὑδάτων λέγοντος, “I heard the angel of the waters saying” — Jewish tradition spoke of angels set over the elements. Δίκαιος εἶ, ὁ ὢν καὶ ὁ ἦν, ὁ ὅσιος, “You are righteous, who are and who were, O Holy One” — ὅσιος, from the holy family: holy as perfectly true and right, as distinct from ἅγιος, set apart (15:4). As in 11:17, ‘who is to come’ is missing: He has come. ὅτι ταῦτα ἔκρινας, “because you have judged these things” — κρίνω, from the judge family. Even the angel over the waters affirms the justice of the plague on them.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G3741","hebrew":"ὅσιος","translit":"hósios","english":"Holy One"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For they poured out the blood of the saints and prophets, and you have given them blood to drink. They deserve this.”","understanding":"ὅτι αἷμα ἁγίων καὶ προφητῶν ἐξέχεαν, “For they poured out the blood of the saints and prophets” — ἐκχέω: the same verb as the angels' pouring. They poured out blood; now God pours out His bowls; ἅγιος, from the holy family: the saints, God's holy ones. καὶ αἷμα αὐτοῖς δέδωκας πιεῖν, “and you have given them blood to drink” — the punishment fits the crime exactly. ἄξιοί εἰσιν, “They deserve this” — ἄξιος, worthy: the same word used of the Lamb (5:9) and of the faithful in Sardis (3:4). Each receives what they are ‘worthy’ of.","words":[{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drink"},{"strongs":"G514","hebrew":"ἄξιος","translit":"áxios","english":"deserve"}]},{"v":7,"text":"I heard the altar saying, “Yes, Lord God, the Almighty, true and righteous are your judgments.”","understanding":"καὶ ἤκουσα τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου λέγοντος, “I heard the altar saying” — the altar under which the martyrs cried ‘How long?’ (6:9–10), and from which the saints' prayers rose (8:3). Now it answers. Ναί, κύριε ὁ θεὸς ὁ παντοκράτωρ, ἀληθιναὶ καὶ δίκαιαι αἱ κρίσεις σου, “Yes, Lord God, the Almighty, true and righteous are your judgments” — κρίσις, from the judge family: judgement as an act of justice, as distinct from κρίμα, the sentence. The martyrs' prayer has been answered.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G2379","hebrew":"θυσιαστήριον","translit":"thysiastḗrion","english":"altar"},{"strongs":"G3483","hebrew":"ναί","translit":"naí","english":"Yes"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgments"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The fourth poured out his bowl on the sun, and it was given to him to scorch men with fire.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ τέταρτος ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὸν ἥλιον, “The fourth poured out his bowl on the sun” — ἐκχέω. Where the fourth trumpet darkened the sun (8:12), the fourth bowl intensifies it. καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῷ καυματίσαι τοὺς ἀνθρώπους ἐν πυρί, “and it was given to him to scorch men with fire” — καυματίζω. The sun that God promised would not strike His people (7:16) strikes those who refused Him.","words":[{"strongs":"G5067","hebrew":"τέταρτος","translit":"tétartos","english":"fourth"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G2739","hebrew":"καυματίζω","translit":"kaumatízō","english":"scorch"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]},{"v":9,"text":"People were scorched with great heat, and people blasphemed the name of God who has the power over these plagues. They didn’t repent and give him glory.","understanding":"καὶ ἐκαυματίσθησαν οἱ ἄνθρωποι καῦμα μέγα, “People were scorched with great heat” — καὶ ἐβλασφήμησαν τὸ ὄνομα τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ ἔχοντος τὴν ἐξουσίαν ἐπὶ τὰς πληγὰς ταύτας, “and people blasphemed the name of God who has the power over these plagues” — βλασφημέω: the beast's sin (13:6) has become theirs; ἐξουσία, from the power family: they know God controls the plagues, and they curse Him for it. καὶ οὐ μετενόησαν δοῦναι αὐτῷ δόξαν, “They didn’t repent and give him glory” — μετανοέω: contrast 11:13, where witness led people to give God glory.","words":[{"strongs":"G2739","hebrew":"καυματίζω","translit":"kaumatízō","english":"scorched"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"People"},{"strongs":"G2738","hebrew":"καῦμα","translit":"kaûma","english":"heat"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blasphemed"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":10,"text":"The fifth poured out his bowl on the throne of the beast, and his kingdom was darkened. They gnawed their tongues because of the pain,","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ πέμπτος ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὸν θρόνον τοῦ θηρίου, “The fifth poured out his bowl on the throne of the beast” — the throne the dragon gave him (13:2). Judgement strikes the centre of the beast's power. καὶ ἐγένετο ἡ βασιλεία αὐτοῦ ἐσκοτωμένη, “and his kingdom was darkened” — σκοτόω, from the darkness family: like the ninth plague of Egypt, darkness that could be felt. καὶ ἐμασῶντο τὰς γλώσσας αὐτῶν ἐκ τοῦ πόνου, “They gnawed their tongues because of the pain” — πόνος, anguish.","words":[{"strongs":"G3991","hebrew":"πέμπτος","translit":"pémptos","english":"fifth"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"kingdom"},{"strongs":"G4656","hebrew":"σκοτόω","translit":"skotóō","english":"darkened"},{"strongs":"G3145","hebrew":"μασσάομαι","translit":"massáomai","english":"gnawed"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongues"},{"strongs":"G4192","hebrew":"πόνος","translit":"pónos","english":"pain"}]},{"v":11,"text":"and they blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and their sores. They didn’t repent of their works.","understanding":"καὶ ἐβλασφήμησαν τὸν θεὸν τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἐκ τῶν πόνων αὐτῶν καὶ ἐκ τῶν ἑλκῶν αὐτῶν, “and they blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and their sores” — βλασφημέω; the sores of the first bowl still torment them: the bowls accumulate. καὶ οὐ μετενόησαν ἐκ τῶν ἔργων αὐτῶν, “They didn’t repent of their works” — μετανοέω; ἔργον, from the work family. The third time Revelation records this refusal (9:20–21; 16:9). Suffering does not soften a heart set against God; it hardens it.","words":[{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blasphemed"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G4192","hebrew":"πόνος","translit":"pónos","english":"pains"},{"strongs":"G1668","hebrew":"ἕλκος","translit":"hélkos","english":"sores"},{"strongs":"G3340","hebrew":"μετανοέω","translit":"metanoéō","english":"repent"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":12,"text":"The sixth poured out his bowl on the great river, the Euphrates. Its water was dried up, that the way might be prepared for the kings that come from the sunrise.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ ἕκτος ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὸν ποταμὸν τὸν μέγαν τὸν Εὐφράτην, “The sixth poured out his bowl on the great river, the Euphrates” — the eastern frontier (9:14). καὶ ἐξηράνθη τὸ ὕδωρ αὐτοῦ, “Its water was dried up” — as the Red Sea and Jordan dried up for Israel, and as Cyrus diverted the Euphrates to take ancient Babylon. ἵνα ἑτοιμασθῇ ἡ ὁδὸς τῶν βασιλέων τῶν ἀπὸ ἀνατολῆς ἡλίου, “that the way might be prepared for the kings that come from the sunrise” — to John's readers, the feared Parthian kings; in the vision, the gathering of the world's rulers for the final battle.","words":[{"strongs":"G1623","hebrew":"ἕκτος","translit":"héktos","english":"sixth"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"river"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2166","hebrew":"Εὐφράτης","translit":"Euphrátēs","english":"Euphrates"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G3583","hebrew":"ξηραίνω","translit":"xēraínō","english":"dried up"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G395","hebrew":"ἀνατολή","translit":"anatolḗ","english":"sunrise"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I saw coming out of the mouth of the dragon, and out of the mouth of the beast, and out of the mouth of the false prophet, three unclean spirits, something like frogs;","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἐκ τοῦ στόματος τοῦ δράκοντος καὶ ἐκ τοῦ στόματος τοῦ θηρίου καὶ ἐκ τοῦ στόματος τοῦ ψευδοπροφήτου, “I saw coming out of the mouth of the dragon, and out of the mouth of the beast, and out of the mouth of the false prophet” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). The unholy trinity together for the first time; the second beast (13:11) is now named ‘the false prophet’. πνεύματα τρία ἀκάθαρτα ὡς βάτραχοι, “three unclean spirits, something like frogs” — the second plague of Egypt; frogs were unclean under the Law. What comes from their mouths is propaganda — the opposite of the sword from Christ's mouth.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G5578","hebrew":"ψευδοπροφήτης","translit":"pseudoprophḗtēs","english":"false prophet"},{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G169","hebrew":"ἀκάθαρτος","translit":"akáthartos","english":"unclean"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G944","hebrew":"βάτραχος","translit":"bátrachos","english":"frogs"}]},{"v":14,"text":"for they are spirits of demons, performing signs; which go out to the kings of the whole inhabited earth, to gather them together for the war of that great day of God, the Almighty.","understanding":"εἰσὶν γὰρ πνεύματα δαιμονίων ποιοῦντα σημεῖα, “for they are spirits of demons, performing signs” — δαιμόνιον; σημεῖον, from the signs family: deceptive miracles, as in 13:13. ἃ ἐκπορεύεται ἐπὶ τοὺς βασιλεῖς τῆς οἰκουμένης ὅλης, “which go out to the kings of the whole inhabited earth” — οἰκουμένη, from the world family. συναγαγεῖν αὐτοὺς εἰς τὸν πόλεμον τῆς ἡμέρας τῆς μεγάλης τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ παντοκράτορος, “to gather them together for the war of that great day of God, the Almighty” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: the day of the LORD. They think they gather for their battle; it is God's day.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"performing"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"signs"},{"strongs":"G1607","hebrew":"ἐκπορεύομαι","translit":"ekporeúomai","english":"go out"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G3625","hebrew":"οἰκουμένη","translit":"oikouménē","english":"inhabited earth"},{"strongs":"G3650","hebrew":"ὅλος","translit":"hólos","english":"whole"},{"strongs":"G4863","hebrew":"συνάγω","translit":"synágō","english":"gather"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"}]},{"v":15,"text":"“Behold, I come like a thief. Blessed is he who watches, and keeps his clothes, so that he doesn’t walk naked, and they see his shame.”","understanding":"Ἰδοὺ ἔρχομαι ὡς κλέπτης, “Behold, I come like a thief” — Jesus breaks into the vision with a personal warning, the words He spoke to Sardis (3:3). μακάριος ὁ γρηγορῶν καὶ τηρῶν τὰ ἱμάτια αὐτοῦ, “Blessed is he who watches, and keeps his clothes” — the third blessing of the book. γρηγορεύω, from the sleep family: to stay awake; τηρέω, from the hold family. ἵνα μὴ γυμνὸς περιπατῇ καὶ βλέπωσιν τὴν ἀσχημοσύνην αὐτοῦ, “so that he doesn’t walk naked, and they see his shame” — βλέπω, from the see family. Temple guards caught asleep at their posts had their clothes set on fire; Laodicea was told to buy white garments to cover its shame (3:18). In the middle of the battle-gathering, the call is to faithful readiness.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2812","hebrew":"κλέπτης","translit":"kléptēs","english":"thief"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G1127","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","english":"watches"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"clothes"},{"strongs":"G1131","hebrew":"γυμνός","translit":"gymnós","english":"naked"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G808","hebrew":"ἀσχημοσύνη","translit":"aschēmosýnē","english":"shame"}]},{"v":16,"text":"He gathered them together into the place which is called in Hebrew, Megiddo.","understanding":"Καὶ συνήγαγεν αὐτοὺς εἰς τὸν τόπον τὸν καλούμενον Ἑβραϊστὶ Ἁρμαγεδών, “He gathered them together into the place which is called in Hebrew, Megiddo” — the WEB translates Ἁρμαγεδών as ‘Megiddo’; the Greek is literally ‘Har-Magedon’, ‘the mountain of Megiddo’ (hence ‘Armageddon’). Megiddo, in the plain of Jezreel, saw many famous battles: Deborah's victory, Josiah's death, and the mourning Zechariah foresaw ‘in the valley of Megiddon’ (Zechariah 12:11). There is a city and a plain at Megiddo, but no mountain; so many read it symbolically, as the place where the world's armies gather against God. Others expect a literal battle there. Either way, the battle itself is never described: when it comes (19:19–21), it is over in a moment.","words":[{"strongs":"G4863","hebrew":"συνάγω","translit":"synágō","english":"gathered"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G1447","hebrew":"Ἑβραϊστί","translit":"Hebraïstí","english":"Hebrew"},{"strongs":"G717","hebrew":"Ἀρμαγεδδών","translit":"Armageddṓn","english":"Megiddo"}]},{"v":17,"text":"The seventh poured out his bowl into the air. A loud voice came out of the temple of heaven, from the throne, saying, “It is done!”","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ ἕβδομος ἐξέχεεν τὴν φιάλην αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὸν ἀέρα, “The seventh poured out his bowl into the air” — ἐκχέω; ἀήρ, the realm of ‘the prince of the power of the air’ (Ephesians 2:2). καὶ ἐξῆλθεν φωνὴ μεγάλη ἐκ τοῦ ναοῦ ἀπὸ τοῦ θρόνου λέγουσα· Γέγονεν, “A loud voice came out of the temple of heaven, from the throne, saying, ‘It is done!’” — γίνομαι, perfect tense: it has come to pass. On the cross Jesus said ‘It is finished’ (τετέλεσται); from the throne God says ‘It is done’. The cross accomplished salvation; the seventh bowl completes judgement. The same word is spoken at the new creation (21:6).","words":[{"strongs":"G1442","hebrew":"ἕβδομος","translit":"hébdomos","english":"seventh"},{"strongs":"G1632","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","english":"poured out"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowl"},{"strongs":"G109","hebrew":"ἀήρ","translit":"aḗr","english":"air"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G1096","hebrew":"γίνομαι","translit":"gínomai","english":"done"}]},{"v":18,"text":"There were lightnings, sounds, and thunders; and there was a great earthquake, such as was not since there were men on the earth, so great an earthquake, so mighty.","understanding":"καὶ ἐγένοντο ἀστραπαὶ καὶ φωναὶ καὶ βρονταί, “There were lightnings, sounds, and thunders” — the throne-storm (4:5; 8:5; 11:19), now at its height. καὶ σεισμὸς ἐγένετο μέγας, οἷος οὐκ ἐγένετο ἀφ᾽ οὗ ἄνθρωπος ἐγένετο ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “and there was a great earthquake, such as was not since there were men on the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. τηλικοῦτος σεισμὸς οὕτω μέγας, “so great an earthquake, so mighty” — Haggai foretold that God would ‘shake the heavens and the earth’; Hebrews says what can be shaken will be removed, so that what cannot be shaken may remain.","words":[{"strongs":"G796","hebrew":"ἀστραπή","translit":"astrapḗ","english":"lightnings"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"sounds"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunders"},{"strongs":"G4578","hebrew":"σεισμός","translit":"seismós","english":"earthquake"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5082","hebrew":"τηλικοῦτος","translit":"tēlikoûtos","english":"so great"}]},{"v":19,"text":"The great city was divided into three parts, and the cities of the nations fell. Babylon the great was remembered in the sight of God, to give to her the cup of the wine of the fierceness of his wrath.","understanding":"καὶ ἐγένετο ἡ πόλις ἡ μεγάλη εἰς τρία μέρη, “The great city was divided into three parts” — the great city of 11:8, Babylon. καὶ αἱ πόλεις τῶν ἐθνῶν ἔπεσαν, “and the cities of the nations fell” — every human city built without God. καὶ Βαβυλὼν ἡ μεγάλη ἐμνήσθη ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ, “Babylon the great was remembered in the sight of God” — μιμνῄσκω: God has not forgotten her crimes (18:5). δοῦναι αὐτῇ τὸ ποτήριον τοῦ οἴνου τοῦ θυμοῦ τῆς ὀργῆς αὐτοῦ, “to give to her the cup of the wine of the fierceness of his wrath” — θυμός and ὀργή, both from the wrath family, joined: ‘the passion of his settled anger’ — God's just wrath in its full force. Chapters 17–18 now show Babylon's fall in detail.","words":[{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"parts"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell"},{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"Babylon"},{"strongs":"G3415","hebrew":"μνάομαι","translit":"mnáomai","english":"remembered"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"fierceness"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"}]},{"v":20,"text":"Every island fled away, and the mountains were not found.","understanding":"καὶ πᾶσα νῆσος ἔφυγεν, “Every island fled away” — as at the sixth seal (6:14), creation itself flees before God. καὶ ὄρη οὐχ εὑρέθησαν, “and the mountains were not found” — the most solid things in the world vanish. Only God and His kingdom remain.","words":[{"strongs":"G3520","hebrew":"νῆσος","translit":"nēsos","english":"island"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"fled away"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountains"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"}]},{"v":21,"text":"Great hailstones, about the weight of a talent, came down out of the sky on people. People blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail, for this plague is exceedingly severe.","understanding":"καὶ χάλαζα μεγάλη ὡς ταλαντιαία καταβαίνει ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἐπὶ τοὺς ἀνθρώπους, “Great hailstones, about the weight of a talent, came down out of the sky on people” — a talent weighed about thirty-five kilos: the hail of the seventh plague of Egypt and of Joshua's battle, beyond imagining. καὶ ἐβλασφήμησαν οἱ ἄνθρωποι τὸν θεὸν ἐκ τῆς πληγῆς τῆς χαλάζης, “People blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail” — βλασφημέω, again. Not repentance, but cursing, to the very end. ὅτι μεγάλη ἐστὶν ἡ πληγὴ αὐτῆς σφόδρα, “for this plague is exceedingly severe.”","words":[{"strongs":"G5464","hebrew":"χάλαζα","translit":"chálaza","english":"hailstones"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"Great"},{"strongs":"G5006","hebrew":"ταλαντιαῖος","translit":"talantiaîos","english":"weight of a talent"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"came down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blasphemed"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plague"},{"strongs":"G4970","hebrew":"σφόδρα","translit":"sphódra","english":"exceedingly"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Bowls Poured Out","verseRef":"v. 1-11","image":"Picture seven angels leaving the smoke-filled temple and tipping out seven golden bowls, one after another — on the land, the sea, the rivers, the sun, the beast's throne. Sores break out, waters turn to blood, the sun burns, darkness swallows the beast's kingdom. And through it all, the people under judgement curse God.","meaning":"“They didn’t repent and give him glory” (v. 9). “They didn’t repent of their works” (v. 11). God's judgements are just, but they do not change hearts that have chosen against Him. Only grace does that, and it is offered now."},{"title":"The Altar Speaks","verseRef":"v. 5-7","image":"Picture the golden altar of heaven, under which the martyrs once cried, ‘How long?’ Now, as the bowls are poured out, the altar itself speaks: ‘Yes, Lord God, the Almighty, true and righteous are your judgments.’","meaning":"“They poured out the blood of the saints and prophets, and you have given them blood to drink” (v. 6). “True and righteous are your judgments” (v. 7). The prayers of the persecuted are answered. God's justice is exact."},{"title":"Frogs from Three Mouths","verseRef":"v. 13-16","image":"Picture the dragon, the beast and the false prophet side by side, and out of each mouth hops an unclean, frog-like spirit, croaking lies, working signs, hopping off to every palace in the world to gather kings for war against God — at a place called Har-Magedon.","meaning":"“For they are spirits of demons, performing signs; which go out to the kings of the whole inhabited earth, to gather them together for the war of that great day of God, the Almighty” (v. 14). The voices that lead the world into rebellion against God come from the pit."},{"title":"Stay Awake, Stay Clothed","verseRef":"v. 15","image":"Picture a night watchman on the walls, dozing at his post. Suddenly his commander arrives without warning. The watchman wakes too late; his clothes are snatched and burned, and he stands exposed and ashamed. Another guard, awake and ready, salutes with joy.","meaning":"“Behold, I come like a thief. Blessed is he who watches, and keeps his clothes, so that he doesn’t walk naked, and they see his shame” (v. 15). In the middle of all the world's turmoil, Jesus' word to you is simple: be ready."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The First Four Bowls (v. 1-9)","body":"A voice from the temple sends the angels out; the first bowl brings painful sores on those with the beast's mark; the second turns the sea to blood and kills everything in it; the third turns rivers and springs to blood, and the angel of the waters and the altar declare God's judgements just; the fourth makes the sun scorch people, who curse God and do not repent."},{"label":"The Fifth and Sixth Bowls (v. 10-16)","body":"The fifth plunges the beast's kingdom into darkness and pain, and people curse God and do not repent; the sixth dries up the Euphrates for the kings of the east; three frog-like unclean spirits from the mouths of the dragon, beast and false prophet gather the kings of the world for the battle of the great day of God; Jesus warns, ‘I come like a thief’; the kings are gathered at Har-Magedon."},{"label":"The Seventh Bowl (v. 17-21)","body":"The seventh bowl is poured into the air, and a voice from the throne says, ‘It is done!’; lightning, thunder and the greatest earthquake ever split the great city in three and bring down the cities of the nations; Babylon is given the cup of God's wrath; islands and mountains vanish; huge hailstones fall, and people curse God."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Bowls and the Plagues of Egypt","paragraphs":["The seven bowls are the last and fullest judgements. They follow the same order as the first four trumpets — land, sea, rivers, sun — but now nothing is spared: not a third, but all. And like the trumpets, they echo the plagues of Egypt: sores (the sixth plague), water turned to blood (the first), darkness (the ninth), frogs (the second), hail (the seventh). As God judged Egypt to free Israel, He judges the beast's world to free His people.","The first bowl falls specifically “on the people who had the mark of the beast, and who worshiped his image” (v. 2). Those who sealed themselves to the beast share his judgement. Those sealed by God are not the target (7:3; 9:4). The sore is ‘harmful and evil’ — κακός and πονηρός, two words for evil side by side.","The sea and rivers become blood (vv. 3–4). Then two voices speak. The angel of the waters says, “You are righteous, who are and who were, O Holy One, because you have judged these things. For they poured out the blood of the saints and prophets, and you have given them blood to drink. They deserve this” (vv. 5–6). And the altar answers, “Yes, Lord God, the Almighty, true and righteous are your judgments” (v. 7). These are not the cries of vindictiveness but the confession that God's justice is exact. Those who poured out blood drink blood."]},{"heading":"Hardened Hearts","paragraphs":["The fourth bowl turns the sun into a scorching fire (vv. 8–9), and the fifth plunges the beast's kingdom into darkness and pain (vv. 10–11). The terrible refrain repeats: “people blasphemed the name of God who has the power over these plagues. They didn’t repent and give him glory” (v. 9); “they blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and their sores. They didn’t repent of their works” (v. 11). Even at the end, they know God is in control, and they curse Him for it.","This is one of the most sobering truths in Revelation. Suffering does not automatically produce repentance. Pharaoh hardened his heart through ten plagues. Jesus told of a rich man who asked that someone be sent from the dead to warn his brothers, and was told, “If they don’t listen to Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded if one rises from the dead” (Luke 16:31). Paul warned, “according to your hardness and unrepentant heart you are treasuring up for yourself wrath in the day of wrath” (Romans 2:5).","The fifth bowl strikes “the throne of the beast” (v. 10): the very centre of the power the dragon gave him (13:2). The beast's kingdom, which seemed unstoppable, is plunged into darkness. No earthly empire can withstand the judgement of God."]},{"heading":"Armageddon","paragraphs":["The sixth bowl dries up the Euphrates “that the way might be prepared for the kings that come from the sunrise” (v. 12). Then three unclean spirits like frogs come from the mouths of the dragon, the beast and the false prophet — the unholy trinity (v. 13). They are demonic spirits working signs, and they go out to the kings of the whole world “to gather them together for the war of that great day of God, the Almighty” (v. 14). The kings think they are gathering for their own war; they are actually being gathered for God's day of judgement.","“He gathered them together into the place which is called in Hebrew, Megiddo” (v. 16). The Greek name is Ἁρμαγεδών — ‘Har-Magedon’, the mountain of Megiddo — familiar to us as ‘Armageddon’. Megiddo guarded a pass into the plain of Jezreel, where many decisive battles were fought, and where King Josiah died. Zechariah spoke of mourning ‘in the valley of Megiddon’ (Zechariah 12:11). Some expect a literal final battle in that region. Many others note that there is no mountain at Megiddo, and read Har-Magedon as a symbolic name for the final gathering of the world's powers against God, wherever it occurs. Either way, the battle is never fought as a contest: when it comes (19:19–21; 20:8–9), the enemies are destroyed in a moment.","In the middle of this scene, Jesus speaks directly: “Behold, I come like a thief. Blessed is he who watches, and keeps his clothes, so that he doesn’t walk naked, and they see his shame” (v. 15). The point of all this prophecy is not to satisfy curiosity but to make believers ready."]},{"heading":"It Is Done","paragraphs":["The seventh bowl is poured into the air, and a voice from the throne says, “It is done!” (v. 17). On the cross Jesus cried, “It is finished” (John 19:30): the work of salvation was complete. From the throne God now declares the work of judgement complete. And when He makes all things new, He will say it again: ‘It is done’ (21:6).","An earthquake greater than any in history splits “the great city” into three parts, and “the cities of the nations fell” (v. 19). All humanity's cities built in rebellion against God fall. “Babylon the great was remembered in the sight of God, to give to her the cup of the wine of the fierceness of his wrath” (v. 19). God has not forgotten a single injustice.","Islands flee, mountains vanish, and enormous hailstones fall (vv. 20–21). And still, “People blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail” (v. 21). The chapter ends with judgement complete and hearts still hard. The next two chapters slow down to show, in detail, the fall of Babylon."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Like a Thief in the Night","paragraphs":["Jesus warned, “Therefore also be ready, for in an hour that you don’t expect, the Son of Man will come” (Matthew 24:44). Paul wrote, “For you yourselves know well that the day of the Lord comes like a thief in the night. For when they are saying, ‘Peace and safety,’ then sudden destruction will come on them” (1 Thessalonians 5:2–3) — and added, “But you, brothers, aren’t in darkness, that the day should overtake you like a thief” (1 Thessalonians 5:4).","The refusal to repent echoes Paul's warning to the self-righteous: “But according to your hardness and unrepentant heart you are treasuring up for yourself wrath in the day of wrath, revelation, and of the righteous judgment of God” (Romans 2:5).","And ‘It is done’ answers Jesus' cry from the cross: “When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, he said, ‘It is finished.’ He bowed his head, and gave up his spirit” (John 19:30). What He finished there, the Father completes from the throne."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Megiddo (Armageddon)","hebrew":"Ἀρμαγεδδών","translit":"Armageddṓn","strongs":"G717 (gathered συνάγω, synágō, G4863)","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “Armageddon (or Har-Meggiddon), a symbolic name.” Ἁρμαγεδών appears only here in the Bible. It is from the Hebrew har (mountain) and Megiddo, the fortress city overlooking the plain of Jezreel, scene of many battles. The WEB simply translates ‘Megiddo’. Even Strong's dictionary calls it ‘a symbolic name’: the place where the world's rulers, deceived by demonic spirits, gather against God — and where God ends their rebellion."},{"english":"Pour out","hebrew":"ἐκχέω","translit":"ekchéō","strongs":"G1632 (bowls φιάλη, phiálē, G5357)","verses":"v. 1-4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 17","insight":"Strong's: “to pour forth; figuratively, to bestow.” ἐκχέω is used of pouring drink offerings, of shedding blood, and of God pouring out His Spirit at Pentecost (Acts 2:17). In chapter 16 the angels pour out God's wrath — and in verse 6 the same verb describes the persecutors who ‘poured out the blood of the saints’. God's judgement mirrors their crime: they poured out blood, and God pours out His bowls."},{"english":"Frogs","hebrew":"βάτραχος","translit":"bátrachos","strongs":"G944 (unclean ἀκάθαρτος, akáthartos, G169)","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's: “a frog.” βάτραχος appears only here in the New Testament. Frogs were the second plague of Egypt, filling Pharaoh's palace and bedrooms; they were unclean under the Law of Moses. The demonic spirits from the mouths of the dragon, beast and false prophet are ‘like frogs’ — noisy, unclean, everywhere. Lying propaganda is loud and croaking; God's word is a sharp sword."},{"english":"Sore","hebrew":"ἕλκος","translit":"hélkos","strongs":"G1668 (evil πονηρός, ponērós, G4190)","verses":"v. 2, 11","insight":"Strong's: “an ulcer (as if drawn together).” ἕλκος is a festering wound or ulcer — the word used of the sores of Lazarus at the rich man's gate (Luke 16:21). The first bowl brings ‘a harmful and evil sore’ on those who took the beast's mark: the mark they accepted on their skin becomes an ulcer. It echoes the sixth plague of Egypt, when boils broke out on the Egyptians and even their magicians could not stand before Moses."},{"english":"It is done","hebrew":"γίνομαι","translit":"gínomai","strongs":"G1096 (throne θρόνος, thrónos, G2362)","verses":"v. 17; 21:6","insight":"Strong's: “to cause to be (\"gen\"-erate), i.e. (reflexively) to become (come into being), used with great latitude (literal, figurative, intensive, etc.).” γίνομαι is one of the commonest verbs in Greek. Here, in the perfect tense γέγονεν, it is a single-word declaration from the throne: ‘It has come to pass.’ God's purposes of judgement are complete. In 21:6 the same word introduces the new creation: ‘It is done! I am the Alpha and the Omega.’ The God who finishes judgement also finishes making all things new."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you the end of rebellion — and calls you to stay awake.","items":[{"title":"God's Judgements Are True and Righteous","body":"Heaven declares, “Yes, Lord God, the Almighty, true and righteous are your judgments” (v. 7). The Spirit wants you to trust God's justice, even when you cannot understand it. He sees every wrong, and He will put every wrong right."},{"title":"Don't Harden Your Heart","body":"Three times the people “didn’t repent” (vv. 9, 11, 21). The Spirit asks: is there anything you are refusing to turn from? Every hardship is an invitation to soften your heart toward God. Do not wait for the next bowl."},{"title":"Stay Awake and Clothed","body":"“Blessed is he who watches, and keeps his clothes” (v. 15). The Spirit calls you to be ready for Jesus every day — alert in prayer, clothed in His righteousness, living as though He may come today."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Readiness Check","body":"If Jesus came today, would He find you awake and clothed, or asleep and exposed (v. 15)?"},{"title":"The Propaganda Check","body":"Which ‘frog voices’ — media, politics, ideology, advertising — have the loudest influence on your thinking (v. 13)?"},{"title":"The Response Check","body":"When suffering comes, do you turn toward God or blame Him (vv. 9, 11, 21)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Watchman's Prayer","body":"Each night this week, before you sleep, pray Revelation 16:15: ‘Lord, keep me awake to You. Keep me clothed in Your righteousness.’ Review the day honestly and confess anything that left you ‘exposed’."},{"title":"Fast from the Frogs","body":"Choose one source of loud, constant voices — news, social media, a podcast — and fast from it for three days. Use the time to read Revelation 15–16 and pray for the nations and their leaders."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord God Almighty, You are righteous, You who are and who were, the Holy One. True and righteous are Your judgements. Thank You that You hear the cry of the blood of Your saints and prophets, and that You will judge every wrong. Keep my heart soft. When hardship comes, let me turn to You and give You glory, not curse You. Protect me from the unclean voices that come from the dragon, the beast and the false prophet, and from every lying sign. Lord Jesus, You are coming like a thief. Keep me awake. Keep me clothed in Your righteousness, so that I will not be ashamed at Your coming. Thank You that from the throne You will one day say, ‘It is done.’ And thank You that on the cross You said, ‘It is finished,’ so that I need not drink the cup of Your wrath. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Plagues of Egypt","ref":"Exodus 7:14–10:29","relatedVerses":"v. 2-4, 10, 13, 21","summary":"God sent plague after plague on Egypt: water turned to blood, frogs everywhere, boils on people and animals, hail mixed with fire, darkness that could be felt. Each time Pharaoh hardened his heart and refused to let God's people go. The bowls of Revelation 16 repeat these plagues on the whole world: sores, blood, darkness, frogs and hail. And like Pharaoh, those who are struck harden their hearts and curse God. The pattern of the Exodus is the pattern of the end: judgement on the oppressor, freedom for God's people."},{"title":"Josiah at Megiddo","ref":"2 Kings 23:28-30; 2 Chronicles 35:20-25","relatedVerses":"v. 16","summary":"Josiah, the godly king who restored the temple and renewed the covenant, went out to fight Pharaoh Necho at Megiddo, though Necho warned him that God had not sent him. Josiah was shot by archers and died. All Judah mourned for him, and Jeremiah wrote laments; the mourning at Megiddo became proverbial (Zechariah 12:11). Megiddo became a byword for decisive, tragic battle. Revelation's ‘Har-Magedon’ draws on that memory: the final gathering of the world's armies — but this time God's enemies, not His king, fall."},{"title":"The Rich Man and Lazarus","ref":"Luke 16:19-31","relatedVerses":"v. 9-11","summary":"A rich man feasted every day while Lazarus, covered with sores, lay at his gate. Both died; Lazarus was carried to Abraham's side, and the rich man found himself in torment. He begged Abraham to send Lazarus to warn his brothers. Abraham answered, “If they don’t listen to Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded if one rises from the dead” (Luke 16:31). Revelation 16 shows the same truth: those who refuse God's word are not persuaded even by the most terrible signs."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":17,"chapterTitle":"Babylon the Great Prostitute and the Scarlet Beast","themeTitle":"“The Lamb Will Overcome Them, for He Is Lord of Lords, and King of Kings”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls came and spoke with me, saying, “Come here. I will show you the judgment of the great prostitute who sits on many waters,","understanding":"Καὶ ἦλθεν εἷς ἐκ τῶν ἑπτὰ ἀγγέλων τῶν ἐχόντων τὰς ἑπτὰ φιάλας, “One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls came” — φιάλη: chapters 17–18 expand the seventh bowl's judgement on Babylon (16:19). Δεῦρο, δείξω σοι τὸ κρίμα τῆς πόρνης τῆς μεγάλης, “Come here. I will show you the judgment of the great prostitute” — κρίμα, from the judge family: the verdict, the sentence passed, as distinct from κρίσις, the act of judging (14:7); πόρνη, a prostitute: in the prophets, a city or nation that sells itself for wealth and power. τῆς καθημένης ἐπὶ ὑδάτων πολλῶν, “who sits on many waters” — ancient Babylon sat on the Euphrates and its canals; the waters are explained in verse 15.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowls"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"}]},{"v":2,"text":"with whom the kings of the earth committed sexual immorality; and those who dwell in the earth were made drunken with the wine of her sexual immorality.”","understanding":"μεθ᾽ ἧς ἐπόρνευσαν οἱ βασιλεῖς τῆς γῆς, “with whom the kings of the earth committed sexual immorality” — πορνεύω: political and commercial alliances that compromised with her idolatry and greed; γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐμεθύσθησαν οἱ κατοικοῦντες τὴν γῆν ἐκ τοῦ οἴνου τῆς πορνείας αὐτῆς, “and those who dwell in the earth were made drunken with the wine of her sexual immorality” — μεθύω, to be drunk: intoxicated with her luxury, pleasure and power, unable to see clearly.","words":[{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4203","hebrew":"πορνεύω","translit":"porneúō","english":"committed sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G3184","hebrew":"μεθύω","translit":"methýō","english":"made drunken"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"}]},{"v":3,"text":"He carried me away in the Spirit into a wilderness. I saw a woman sitting on a scarlet-colored beast, full of blasphemous names, having seven heads and ten horns.","understanding":"καὶ ἀπήνεγκέν με εἰς ἔρημον ἐν πνεύματι, “He carried me away in the Spirit into a wilderness” — ἔρημος: from the wilderness, where God keeps His people (12:6), John sees the world's true nature. καὶ εἶδον γυναῖκα καθημένην ἐπὶ θηρίον κόκκινον, “I saw a woman sitting on a scarlet-colored beast” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); θηρίον: the beast from the sea (13:1). The city rides on the empire's power. γέμοντα ὀνόματα βλασφημίας, ἔχων κεφαλὰς ἑπτὰ καὶ κέρατα δέκα, “full of blasphemous names, having seven heads and ten horns.”","words":[{"strongs":"G667","hebrew":"ἀποφέρω","translit":"apophérō","english":"carried"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2048","hebrew":"ἔρημος","translit":"érēmos","english":"wilderness"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sitting"},{"strongs":"G2847","hebrew":"κόκκινος","translit":"kókkinos","english":"scarlet-colored"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1073","hebrew":"γέμω","translit":"gémō","english":"full"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G988","hebrew":"βλασφημία","translit":"blasphēmía","english":"blasphemous"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G1176","hebrew":"δέκα","translit":"déka","english":"ten"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"}]},{"v":4,"text":"The woman was dressed in purple and scarlet, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls, having in her hand a golden cup full of abominations and the impurities of the sexual immorality of the earth.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ γυνὴ ἦν περιβεβλημένη πορφυροῦν καὶ κόκκινον, “The woman was dressed in purple and scarlet” — the colours of royalty and luxury. καὶ κεχρυσωμένη χρυσίῳ καὶ λίθῳ τιμίῳ καὶ μαργαρίταις, “and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls” — the very materials of the new Jerusalem (21:18–21): Babylon is the counterfeit city. ἔχουσα ποτήριον χρυσοῦν ἐν τῇ χειρὶ αὐτῆς γέμον βδελυγμάτων, “having in her hand a golden cup full of abominations” — βδέλυγμα, something detestable, especially idolatry. καὶ τὰ ἀκάθαρτα τῆς πορνείας τῆς γῆς, “and the impurities of the sexual immorality of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. Golden on the outside, filthy on the inside.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"dressed"},{"strongs":"G4210","hebrew":"πορφυροῦς","translit":"porphyroûs","english":"purple"},{"strongs":"G2847","hebrew":"κόκκινος","translit":"kókkinos","english":"scarlet"},{"strongs":"G5558","hebrew":"χρυσόω","translit":"chrysóō","english":"decked"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stones"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G3135","hebrew":"μαργαρίτης","translit":"margarítēs","english":"pearls"},{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G1073","hebrew":"γέμω","translit":"gémō","english":"full"},{"strongs":"G946","hebrew":"βδέλυγμα","translit":"bdélygma","english":"abominations"},{"strongs":"G168","hebrew":"ἀκαθάρτης","translit":"akathártēs","english":"impurities"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]},{"v":5,"text":"And on her forehead a name was written, “MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF THE PROSTITUTES AND OF THE ABOMINATIONS OF THE EARTH.”","understanding":"καὶ ἐπὶ τὸ μέτωπον αὐτῆς ὄνομα γεγραμμένον, μυστήριον, “And on her forehead a name was written, ‘MYSTERY’” — μυστήριον: a name with hidden meaning, to be understood spiritually. Roman prostitutes are said to have worn their names on headbands. Βαβυλὼν ἡ μεγάλη, ἡ μήτηρ τῶν πορνῶν καὶ τῶν βδελυγμάτων τῆς γῆς, “BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF THE PROSTITUTES AND OF THE ABOMINATIONS OF THE EARTH” — βδέλυγμα; γῆ, from the world family. Babylon destroyed Jerusalem; Rome did the same in AD 70. Peter called Rome ‘Babylon’ (1 Peter 5:13). She is the source of every idolatrous seduction.","words":[{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"forehead"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"MYSTERY"},{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"BABYLON"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"GREAT"},{"strongs":"G3384","hebrew":"μήτηρ","translit":"mḗtēr","english":"MOTHER"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"PROSTITUTES"},{"strongs":"G946","hebrew":"βδέλυγμα","translit":"bdélygma","english":"ABOMINATIONS"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"EARTH"}]},{"v":6,"text":"I saw the woman drunken with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus. When I saw her, I wondered with great amazement.","understanding":"καὶ εἶδον τὴν γυναῖκα μεθύουσαν ἐκ τοῦ αἵματος τῶν ἁγίων, “I saw the woman drunken with the blood of the saints” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἅγιος, from the holy family: God's holy ones. She makes the nations drunk with her wine; she gets drunk on the blood of believers. καὶ ἐκ τοῦ αἵματος τῶν μαρτύρων Ἰησοῦ, “and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus” — μάρτυς, from the witness family: here plainly ‘martyrs’, those who died for their testimony. Nero's persecution in Rome was within living memory. καὶ ἐθαύμασα ἰδὼν αὐτὴν θαῦμα μέγα, “When I saw her, I wondered with great amazement” — θαυμάζω: even John is dazzled by her.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G3184","hebrew":"μεθύω","translit":"methýō","english":"drunken"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"martyrs"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G2296","hebrew":"θαυμάζω","translit":"thaumázō","english":"wondered"},{"strongs":"G2295","hebrew":"θαῦμα","translit":"thaûma","english":"amazement"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":7,"text":"The angel said to me, “Why do you wonder? I will tell you the mystery of the woman, and of the beast that carries her, which has the seven heads and the ten horns.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶπέν μοι ὁ ἄγγελος· Διὰ τί ἐθαύμασας; “The angel said to me, ‘Why do you wonder?’” — θαυμάζω: a gentle rebuke. The world marvels at the beast (13:3); God's people must not. ἐγὼ ἐρῶ σοι τὸ μυστήριον τῆς γυναικὸς καὶ τοῦ θηρίου τοῦ βαστάζοντος αὐτήν, “I will tell you the mystery of the woman, and of the beast that carries her” — μυστήριον. The cure for being dazzled by evil is to understand it.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2296","hebrew":"θαυμάζω","translit":"thaumázō","english":"wonder"},{"strongs":"G3466","hebrew":"μυστήριον","translit":"mystḗrion","english":"mystery"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G941","hebrew":"βαστάζω","translit":"bastázō","english":"carries"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G1176","hebrew":"δέκα","translit":"déka","english":"ten"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"}]},{"v":8,"text":"The beast that you saw was, and is not; and is about to come up out of the abyss and to go into destruction. Those who dwell on the earth and whose names have not been written in the book of life from the foundation of the world will marvel when they see that the beast was, and is not, and shall be present.","understanding":"τὸ θηρίον ὃ εἶδες ἦν καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν, “The beast that you saw was, and is not” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). A parody of God, ‘who was and who is and who is to come’ (4:8), and of the Lamb who was dead and is alive. καὶ μέλλει ἀναβαίνειν ἐκ τῆς ἀβύσσου καὶ εἰς ἀπώλειαν ὑπάγει, “and is about to come up out of the abyss and to go into destruction” — ἀπώλεια, ruin: the beast's ‘coming’ leads only to destruction. καὶ θαυμασθήσονται οἱ κατοικοῦντες ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, ὧν οὐ γέγραπται τὸ ὄνομα ἐπὶ τὸ βιβλίον τῆς ζωῆς ἀπὸ καταβολῆς κόσμου, “Those who dwell on the earth and whose names have not been written in the book of life from the foundation of the world will marvel” — γῆ and κόσμος, both from the world family; βιβλίον, from the book family; ζωή, from the life family. βλεπόντων τὸ θηρίον ὅτι ἦν καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν καὶ παρέσται, “when they see that the beast was, and is not, and shall be present” — βλέπω, from the see family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G3195","hebrew":"μέλλω","translit":"méllō","english":"about"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"come up"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"go"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2602","hebrew":"καταβολή","translit":"katabolḗ","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2296","hebrew":"θαυμάζω","translit":"thaumázō","english":"marvel"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G3918","hebrew":"πάρειμι","translit":"páreimi","english":"present"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Here is the mind that has wisdom. The seven heads are seven mountains on which the woman sits.","understanding":"ὧδε ὁ νοῦς ὁ ἔχων σοφίαν, “Here is the mind that has wisdom” — σοφία, from the wisdom family: as in 13:18, a call for discernment. αἱ ἑπτὰ κεφαλαὶ ἑπτὰ ὄρη εἰσίν, ὅπου ἡ γυνὴ κάθηται ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν, “The seven heads are seven mountains on which the woman sits” — Rome was famous as the city on seven hills; Roman coins showed the goddess Roma seated on seven hills. For John's readers, the identification was unmistakable.","words":[{"strongs":"G3563","hebrew":"νοῦς","translit":"noûs","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountains"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"}]},{"v":10,"text":"They are seven kings. Five have fallen, the one is, the other has not yet come. When he comes, he must continue a little while.","understanding":"καὶ βασιλεῖς ἑπτά εἰσιν· οἱ πέντε ἔπεσαν, ὁ εἷς ἔστιν, ὁ ἄλλος οὔπω ἦλθεν, “They are seven kings. Five have fallen, the one is, the other has not yet come” — ἄλλος, from the another family. Many have tried to match these with particular Roman emperors, but it depends on where one starts counting and whom one includes, and no scheme is agreed. Seven, the number of completeness, suggests the whole line of beastly rulers, with the end near. καὶ ὅταν ἔλθῃ ὀλίγον αὐτὸν δεῖ μεῖναι, “When he comes, he must continue a little while” — μένω; δεῖ: even his brief time is appointed by God.","words":[{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G4002","hebrew":"πέντε","translit":"pénte","english":"Five"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fallen"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"other"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"little"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"must"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"continue"}]},{"v":11,"text":"The beast that was, and is not, is himself also an eighth, and is of the seven; and he goes to destruction.","understanding":"καὶ τὸ θηρίον ὃ ἦν καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν, καὶ αὐτὸς ὄγδοός ἐστιν, “The beast that was, and is not, is himself also an eighth” — ὄγδοος: eight was the number of resurrection (the eighth day, the day after the Sabbath); the beast's ‘resurrection’ parodies Christ's. Many in John's day expected Nero to return. καὶ ἐκ τῶν ἑπτά ἐστιν, καὶ εἰς ἀπώλειαν ὑπάγει, “and is of the seven; and he goes to destruction” — ἀπώλεια: whatever he claims, his end is ruin.","words":[{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G3590","hebrew":"ὄγδοος","translit":"ógdoos","english":"eighth"},{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G5217","hebrew":"ὑπάγω","translit":"hypágō","english":"goes"}]},{"v":12,"text":"The ten horns that you saw are ten kings who have received no kingdom as yet, but they receive authority as kings, with the beast, for one hour.","understanding":"καὶ τὰ δέκα κέρατα ἃ εἶδες δέκα βασιλεῖς εἰσιν, “The ten horns that you saw are ten kings” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). οἵτινες βασιλείαν οὔπω ἔλαβον, ἀλλὰ ἐξουσίαν ὡς βασιλεῖς μίαν ὥραν λαμβάνουσιν μετὰ τοῦ θηρίου, “who have received no kingdom as yet, but they receive authority as kings, with the beast, for one hour” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: delegated authority; ὥρα, from the time family: ‘one hour’, a very short time. Client rulers who share the beast's power — briefly.","words":[{"strongs":"G1176","hebrew":"δέκα","translit":"déka","english":"ten"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"}]},{"v":13,"text":"These have one mind, and they give their power and authority to the beast.","understanding":"οὗτοι μίαν γνώμην ἔχουσιν, “These have one mind” — γνώμη, purpose, decision: united against God. καὶ τὴν δύναμιν καὶ ἐξουσίαν αὐτῶν τῷ θηρίῳ διδόασιν, “and they give their power and authority to the beast” — δύναμις and ἐξουσία, two members of the power family: their ability and their authority, both handed over. People and nations surrender themselves to the beast.","words":[{"strongs":"G1106","hebrew":"γνώμη","translit":"gnṓmē","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"have"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"}]},{"v":14,"text":"These will war against the Lamb, and the Lamb will overcome them, for he is Lord of lords, and King of kings, and those who are with him are called chosen and faithful.”","understanding":"οὗτοι μετὰ τοῦ ἀρνίου πολεμήσουσιν, “These will war against the Lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. καὶ τὸ ἀρνίον νικήσει αὐτούς, “and the Lamb will overcome them” — νικάω: the battle of Har-Magedon (16:16) is summed up in four words. ὅτι κύριος κυρίων ἐστὶν καὶ βασιλεὺς βασιλέων, “for he is Lord of lords, and King of kings” — Daniel and Deuteronomy's titles for God, given to the Lamb. καὶ οἱ μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ κλητοὶ καὶ ἐκλεκτοὶ καὶ πιστοί, “and those who are with him are called chosen and faithful” — κλητός, called; ἐκλεκτός, chosen; πιστός, faithful: God's call and choice, and their faithful response.","words":[{"strongs":"G4170","hebrew":"πολεμέω","translit":"poleméō","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"King"},{"strongs":"G2822","hebrew":"κλητός","translit":"klētós","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"}]},{"v":15,"text":"He said to me, “The waters which you saw, where the prostitute sits, are peoples, multitudes, nations, and languages.","understanding":"Καὶ λέγει μοι· Τὰ ὕδατα ἃ εἶδες, οὗ ἡ πόρνη κάθηται, “He said to me, ‘The waters which you saw, where the prostitute sits’” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). λαοὶ καὶ ὄχλοι εἰσὶν καὶ ἔθνη καὶ γλῶσσαι, “are peoples, multitudes, nations, and languages” — Babylon sits enthroned over the whole world. The same fourfold formula describes the Lamb's people (5:9; 7:9): every nation is either seduced by Babylon or redeemed by the Lamb.","words":[{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"peoples"},{"strongs":"G3793","hebrew":"ὄχλος","translit":"óchlos","english":"multitudes"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"languages"}]},{"v":16,"text":"The ten horns which you saw, and the beast, these will hate the prostitute, will make her desolate, will strip her naked, will eat her flesh, and will burn her utterly with fire.","understanding":"καὶ τὰ δέκα κέρατα ἃ εἶδες καὶ τὸ θηρίον, οὗτοι μισήσουσιν τὴν πόρνην, “The ten horns which you saw, and the beast, these will hate the prostitute” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); μισέω. Evil turns on itself: the powers that carried Babylon destroy her. καὶ ἠρημωμένην ποιήσουσιν αὐτὴν καὶ γυμνήν, καὶ τὰς σάρκας αὐτῆς φάγονται, καὶ αὐτὴν κατακαύσουσιν ἐν πυρί, “will make her desolate, will strip her naked, will eat her flesh, and will burn her utterly with fire” — the fate of Jezebel, whose flesh the dogs ate, and of the unfaithful city in Ezekiel 16 and 23.","words":[{"strongs":"G1176","hebrew":"δέκα","translit":"déka","english":"ten"},{"strongs":"G2768","hebrew":"κέρας","translit":"kéras","english":"horns"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hate"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G2049","hebrew":"ἐρημόω","translit":"erēmóō","english":"desolate"},{"strongs":"G1131","hebrew":"γυμνός","translit":"gymnós","english":"naked"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G5315","hebrew":"φάγω","translit":"phágō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G2618","hebrew":"κατακαίω","translit":"katakaíō","english":"burn"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For God has put in their hearts to do what he has in mind, to be of one mind, and to give their kingdom to the beast, until the words of God should be accomplished.","understanding":"ὁ γὰρ θεὸς ἔδωκεν εἰς τὰς καρδίας αὐτῶν ποιῆσαι τὴν γνώμην αὐτοῦ, “For God has put in their hearts to do what he has in mind” — God's sovereignty works even through evil: the beast's purpose (γνώμη, v. 13) unknowingly carries out God's purpose (γνώμη). καὶ δοῦναι τὴν βασιλείαν αὐτῶν τῷ θηρίῳ, ἄχρι τελεσθήσονται οἱ λόγοι τοῦ θεοῦ, “and to give their kingdom to the beast, until the words of God should be accomplished” — τελέω, from the perfect family: brought to completion. The best manuscripts read λόγοι, from the word family; others ῥήματα, also from the word family. God's words will be fulfilled to the letter.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"put"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G1106","hebrew":"γνώμη","translit":"gnṓmē","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"accomplished"}]},{"v":18,"text":"The woman whom you saw is the great city, which reigns over the kings of the earth.”","understanding":"καὶ ἡ γυνὴ ἣν εἶδες ἔστιν ἡ πόλις ἡ μεγάλη, “The woman whom you saw is the great city” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). The plainest identification in the chapter. ἡ ἔχουσα βασιλείαν ἐπὶ τῶν βασιλέων τῆς γῆς, “which reigns over the kings of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. In AD 95 only one city did that: Rome. But Babylon is also every city and civilisation that builds its glory on idolatry, greed, immorality and the blood of God's people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"reigns"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Woman on the Scarlet Beast","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture a wilderness, and in it a scarlet monster with seven heads, covered in blasphemous names. On its back sits a woman in purple and scarlet, dripping with gold, jewels and pearls, holding up a golden cup — and on her forehead the name: ‘MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT.’ Look closer at the cup: it is full of filth.","meaning":"“The woman was dressed in purple and scarlet, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls, having in her hand a golden cup full of abominations” (v. 4). The world's glamour hides corruption. What looks golden from a distance is poison up close."},{"title":"Drunk on the Blood of the Saints","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture the beautiful woman lifting her cup again — and it is red. She is drunk on the blood of believers. John stares, dazzled. Then the angel touches his shoulder: ‘Why do you wonder?’","meaning":"“I saw the woman drunken with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus. When I saw her, I wondered with great amazement” (v. 6). The angel said, “Why do you wonder?” (v. 7). Do not be dazzled by a world that kills God's people. See it for what it is."},{"title":"The Lamb Wins","verseRef":"v. 14","image":"Picture ten kings with their armies massed behind the beast, all marching against one small Lamb. And then the battle is over before it begins: the Lamb stands, and they fall. Around Him stand His people — called, chosen, faithful.","meaning":"“These will war against the Lamb, and the Lamb will overcome them, for he is Lord of lords, and King of kings, and those who are with him are called chosen and faithful” (v. 14). The outcome is never in doubt."},{"title":"Evil Devours Itself","verseRef":"v. 16-17","image":"Picture the beast that once carried the woman turning on her, tearing off her jewels and robes, devouring her and setting her on fire — while above it all, God's hand guides even this toward the fulfilment of His word.","meaning":"“The ten horns which you saw, and the beast, these will hate the prostitute, will make her desolate” (v. 16). “For God has put in their hearts to do what he has in mind” (v. 17). Evil has no loyalty; it destroys its own. And God's purposes are accomplished even through His enemies."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The Prostitute and the Beast (v. 1-6)","body":"One of the bowl angels shows John the judgement of the great prostitute who sits on many waters, with whom the kings of the earth committed immorality; in the wilderness John sees a woman on a scarlet beast, dressed in purple and scarlet and covered in gold and jewels, holding a golden cup of abominations, with the name ‘Babylon the great, mother of prostitutes’ on her forehead; she is drunk with the blood of the saints and martyrs, and John is amazed."},{"label":"The Mystery Explained (v. 7-14)","body":"The angel explains: the beast was, is not, and will come up from the abyss to destruction, and the earth-dwellers will marvel; the seven heads are seven mountains and seven kings; the beast is an eighth king belonging to the seven; the ten horns are ten kings who will reign with the beast for one hour and give it their power; they will make war on the Lamb, and the Lamb will conquer them, for He is Lord of lords and King of kings."},{"label":"The Prostitute Destroyed (v. 15-18)","body":"The waters are peoples, crowds, nations and languages; the beast and the ten horns will turn on the prostitute and destroy her, for God has put it in their hearts to carry out His purpose until His words are fulfilled; the woman is the great city that rules over the kings of the earth."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Tale of Two Women","paragraphs":["Revelation contrasts two women. In chapter 12 a woman clothed with the sun gives birth to the Messiah and is protected in the wilderness. In chapter 17 a woman clothed in purple and scarlet rides a beast and is drunk on the blood of the saints. At the end of the book a third woman appears: the bride of the Lamb, clothed in fine linen (19:7–8), who is also a city, the new Jerusalem (21:2, 9–10). Babylon is the prostitute; Jerusalem is the bride. Every human heart and every culture is drawn to one or the other.","The angel promises to show John “the judgment of the great prostitute who sits on many waters, with whom the kings of the earth committed sexual immorality” (vv. 1–2). In the Old Testament, cities like Tyre, Nineveh and even Jerusalem are called prostitutes when they sell themselves for wealth and power and chase after other gods. Babylon's ‘immorality’ is her seductive offer of luxury, trade, pleasure and security in exchange for compromising with idolatry and injustice. Kings and merchants are drunk on it (v. 2).","Her appearance is dazzling: “The woman was dressed in purple and scarlet, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls” (v. 4). These are the very materials of the new Jerusalem (21:18–21). Babylon is a counterfeit of God's city: all its beauty, none of its holiness. And her golden cup is “full of abominations and the impurities of the sexual immorality of the earth” (v. 4)."]},{"heading":"Who Is Babylon?","paragraphs":["On her forehead is a name: “MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF THE PROSTITUTES AND OF THE ABOMINATIONS OF THE EARTH” (v. 5). Ancient Babylon was the empire that destroyed Jerusalem and the temple and carried God's people into exile. For Jews and Christians of John's day, Rome had become a new Babylon: it too destroyed Jerusalem and the temple, in AD 70. Peter, writing from Rome, sent greetings from “She who is in Babylon” (1 Peter 5:13).","The angel's explanation confirms it. “The seven heads are seven mountains on which the woman sits” (v. 9) — Rome was famous as the city on seven hills. And “The woman whom you saw is the great city, which reigns over the kings of the earth” (v. 18). In John's day only Rome fitted. She was “drunken with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus” (v. 6), as Nero's persecution had shown.","But Babylon is more than Rome. She is ‘MYSTERY’: a spiritual reality that takes shape in every civilisation that builds its glory on idolatry, greed, luxury and the oppression of God's people. Some Christians expect a final, literal city at the end; all can see her spirit at work in every age. James wrote, “You adulterers and adulteresses, don’t you know that friendship with the world is hostility toward God?” (James 4:4)."]},{"heading":"The Beast Who Was, and Is Not","paragraphs":["The beast that carries the woman is the beast from the sea (13:1): the political power behind the city. The angel says, “The beast that you saw was, and is not; and is about to come up out of the abyss and to go into destruction” (v. 8). This is a parody of God, ‘who was and who is and who is to come’, and of the Lamb who died and lives. Many of John's contemporaries believed Nero would return from the dead or from the east; the beast's career mimics a resurrection. The world marvels at it (v. 8).","The seven heads are also “seven kings. Five have fallen, the one is, the other has not yet come” (v. 10), and the beast himself is “an eighth, and is of the seven” (v. 11). Many attempts have been made to identify these kings with particular Roman emperors, but it depends on whom one counts, and there is no agreement. The number seven suggests the complete series of beastly powers, with the end near. The eighth, who is ‘of the seven’, is the final embodiment of the beast — and ‘he goes to destruction’.","The ten horns are ten kings who rule with the beast “for one hour” (v. 12) and give him their power and authority (v. 13). Their unity is against God. “These will war against the Lamb, and the Lamb will overcome them, for he is Lord of lords, and King of kings, and those who are with him are called chosen and faithful” (v. 14). The final battle is summed up in one sentence. The Lamb wins, and His people share His victory."]},{"heading":"Evil Turns on Itself","paragraphs":["The most surprising part of the vision is how Babylon falls: “The ten horns which you saw, and the beast, these will hate the prostitute, will make her desolate, will strip her naked, will eat her flesh, and will burn her utterly with fire” (v. 16). The powers that carried her destroy her. Evil has no loyalty. Systems built on greed and violence eventually devour one another.","And over it all stands God: “For God has put in their hearts to do what he has in mind, to be of one mind, and to give their kingdom to the beast, until the words of God should be accomplished” (v. 17). God does not cause evil, but He rules over it, using even the plans of His enemies to fulfil His word, as He used Assyria and Babylon in the Old Testament, and as He used Pilate and Herod at the cross.","For John's first readers, this was deeply encouraging. Rome looked eternal. But God showed them its end: stripped, devoured, burned. The angel's question to John is for us too: “Why do you wonder?” (v. 7). Do not be dazzled by the world's glamour or power. Babylon is already judged, and the Lamb is Lord of lords."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Do Not Love the World","paragraphs":["John wrote in his first letter, “Don’t love the world or the things that are in the world. If anyone loves the world, the Father’s love isn’t in him. For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, isn’t the Father’s, but is the world’s. The world is passing away with its lusts, but he who does God’s will remains forever” (1 John 2:15–17). Babylon is that world, dressed up as a queen.","Paul describes God as “the blessed and only Ruler, the King of kings, and Lord of lords” (1 Timothy 6:15); Revelation gives the title to the Lamb (17:14), and in 19:16 it is written on His robe: “KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS” (Revelation 19:16).","Those with the Lamb are “called chosen and faithful” (v. 14). Jesus said, “For many are called, but few chosen” (Matthew 22:14) — the called who are truly His show it by faithfulness."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Prostitute","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","strongs":"G4204 (committed sexual immorality πορνεύω, porneúō, G4203)","verses":"v. 1, 5, 15, 16; 19:2","insight":"Strong's: “a strumpet; figuratively, an idolater.” πόρνη is a prostitute; Strong's notes its figurative use for an idolater. The prophets called Nineveh, Tyre and even unfaithful Jerusalem prostitutes: they sold themselves for wealth, security and pleasure, and abandoned the true God. Babylon the prostitute stands opposite the bride of the Lamb (19:7). Every culture, and every heart, is courted by one or the other."},{"english":"Abominations","hebrew":"βδέλυγμα","translit":"bdélygma","strongs":"G946 (cup ποτήριον, potḗrion, G4221)","verses":"v. 4, 5; 21:27","insight":"Strong's: “a detestation, i.e. (specially) idolatry.” βδέλυγμα is something that God finds detestable — especially idolatry. Daniel and Jesus spoke of ‘the abomination of desolation’ standing in the holy place. Babylon's golden cup is full of abominations, and she is ‘the mother of the abominations of the earth’. Nothing abominable will enter the new Jerusalem (21:27)."},{"english":"Destruction","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","strongs":"G684 (abyss ἄβυσσος, ábyssos, G12)","verses":"v. 8, 11","insight":"Strong's: “ruin or loss (physical, spiritual or eternal).” ἀπώλεια is utter ruin, the opposite of salvation. Jesus spoke of the broad road ‘that leads to destruction’; Judas is ‘the son of destruction’. Twice the angel says the beast ‘goes to destruction’ (vv. 8, 11): whatever its apparent resurrection and power, its destiny is fixed. Its name, Apollyon (9:11), comes from the same root."},{"english":"Judgment","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","strongs":"G2917 (prostitute πόρνη, pórnē, G4204)","verses":"v. 1; 18:20; 20:4","insight":"Strong's: “a decision (the function or the effect, for or against (\"crime\")).” κρίμα, from the judge family, is the verdict, the sentence handed down — as distinct from κρίσις, the act or process of judging (14:7; 16:7). The angel shows John ‘the judgment (κρίμα) of the great prostitute’: her sentence has been passed. In 18:20 God has ‘judged your judgment on her’; in 20:4 judgement (κρίμα) is given to the saints."},{"english":"Chosen","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","strongs":"G1588 (called κλητός, klētós, G2822)","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “select; by implication, favorite.” ἐκλεκτός, from ἐκλέγομαι (to pick out), describes those God has chosen. The Lamb's companions are ‘called (κλητοί) and chosen (ἐκλεκτοί) and faithful (πιστοί)’ — a whole theology in three words: God calls, God chooses, and those He has chosen respond with faithfulness. The beast's followers are those whose names are not in the book of life (v. 8); the Lamb's are chosen from the foundation of the world."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit strips the glamour from the world, so you can see it clearly.","items":[{"title":"Don't Be Dazzled","body":"Even John “wondered with great amazement” at Babylon (v. 6), and the angel asked, “Why do you wonder?” (v. 7). The Spirit wants to break the spell that the world's wealth, beauty and power cast over you. Look at the cup in her hand: it is full of filth."},{"title":"Which Woman Will You Love?","body":"There is a prostitute and a bride; Babylon and the new Jerusalem. The Spirit asks: whose embrace are you in? Where have you been seduced by the world's offers of comfort, success and pleasure? Return to your first love, the Lamb."},{"title":"The Lamb Wins","body":"“The Lamb will overcome them, for he is Lord of lords, and King of kings” (v. 14). The Spirit wants you to face the powers of the world with confidence. You are called, chosen and faithful, and you are on the winning side."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Glamour Check","body":"What in the world most dazzles you — wealth, celebrity, luxury, influence (v. 4)? Has it drawn your heart away from God?"},{"title":"The Intoxication Check","body":"Are you ‘drunk’ on anything the world offers — so absorbed that you cannot see clearly (v. 2)?"},{"title":"The Loyalty Check","body":"Could you be described as ‘called, chosen and faithful’ (v. 14)? Which of the three is weakest in your life?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Unmask Babylon","body":"Choose one advertisement, show or online feed you see this week. Ask: what is it offering, and what does it cost the soul? Write down what you notice. Pray 1 John 2:15–17 over your heart."},{"title":"Give Away Some Gold","body":"Babylon is decked with gold and pearls. This week, give away something valuable — money, a possession, time — to someone in need, as a declaration that your treasure is not in Babylon."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, Lamb of God, You are Lord of lords and King of kings. Open my eyes to see the world as You see it. Strip the glamour from Babylon, so that I am not dazzled by her purple and gold, nor drunk on the wine she offers. Forgive me where I have loved the world and the things in the world. Thank You for Your saints and martyrs, whose blood Babylon has shed; strengthen those who suffer today. Thank You that Babylon is already judged, that the beast goes to destruction, and that even Your enemies' plans serve Your purposes until Your words are fulfilled. Keep me with You — called, chosen and faithful. Let me belong to the bride, not the prostitute; to the new Jerusalem, not to Babylon. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Tower of Babel","ref":"Genesis 11:1-9","relatedVerses":"v. 5, 18","summary":"After the flood, people gathered on a plain in Shinar and said, ‘Come, let’s build ourselves a city, and a tower whose top reaches to the sky, and let’s make a name for ourselves.’ God came down, confused their language and scattered them. The city was called Babel — Babylon. From the beginning, Babylon is humanity's attempt to make a name for itself without God, to reach heaven by its own achievement. Revelation's Babylon is the last and greatest form of that same proud city."},{"title":"Jezebel's End","ref":"2 Kings 9:30-37","relatedVerses":"v. 16","summary":"When Jehu came to Jezreel to bring God's judgement on Ahab's house, Queen Jezebel painted her eyes, arranged her hair and looked down from a window to taunt him. At Jehu's word, her own servants threw her down. When they went to bury her, they found only her skull, her feet and her hands; the dogs had eaten her flesh, as Elijah had prophesied. The queen who had seduced Israel into idolatry was destroyed by her own household. Babylon's end in verse 16 echoes Jezebel's."},{"title":"Daniel and the Fall of Babylon","ref":"Daniel 5:1-31","relatedVerses":"v. 1-6, 16","summary":"King Belshazzar of Babylon threw a great feast and drank wine from the gold and silver cups taken from the temple in Jerusalem, praising gods of gold and silver. Suddenly a hand appeared and wrote on the wall. Daniel read it: God had numbered his kingdom and brought it to an end; he had been weighed and found wanting. That very night Belshazzar was killed and Babylon fell to the Medes and Persians. Revelation's Babylon, drinking from her golden cup, will fall just as suddenly."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":18,"chapterTitle":"The Fall of Babylon","themeTitle":"“Come Out of Her, My People”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"After these things, I saw another angel coming down out of the sky, having great authority. The earth was illuminated with his glory.","understanding":"Μετὰ ταῦτα εἶδον ἄλλον ἄγγελον καταβαίνοντα ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, “After these things, I saw another angel coming down out of the sky” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἄλλος, from the another family. ἔχοντα ἐξουσίαν μεγάλην, “having great authority” — ἐξουσία, from the power family. καὶ ἡ γῆ ἐφωτίσθη ἐκ τῆς δόξης αὐτοῦ, “The earth was illuminated with his glory” — γῆ, from the world family; φωτίζω, to give light: as Ezekiel saw the earth shine with God's glory. Babylon's lamps are about to go out (v. 23); heaven's light fills the earth.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"coming down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"illuminated"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"}]},{"v":2,"text":"He cried with a mighty voice, saying, “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great, and she has become a habitation of demons, a prison of every unclean spirit, and a prison of every unclean and hateful bird!","understanding":"καὶ ἔκραξεν ἐν ἰσχυρᾷ φωνῇ λέγων, “He cried with a mighty voice, saying” — ἰσχυρός, from the power family: forceful strength. Ἔπεσεν ἔπεσεν Βαβυλὼν ἡ μεγάλη, “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great” — Isaiah's cry over ancient Babylon (14:8). καὶ ἐγένετο κατοικητήριον δαιμονίων, “and she has become a habitation of demons” — the prophets pictured ruined Babylon, Edom and Nineveh as haunts of wild animals and evil spirits. καὶ φυλακὴ παντὸς πνεύματος ἀκαθάρτου καὶ φυλακὴ παντὸς ὀρνέου ἀκαθάρτου καὶ μεμισημένου, “a prison of every unclean spirit, and a prison of every unclean and hateful bird!” — φυλακή, a prison or a haunt. The glittering city becomes a ghost town.","words":[{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"Fallen"},{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"Babylon"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G2732","hebrew":"κατοικητήριον","translit":"katoikētḗrion","english":"habitation"},{"strongs":"G1140","hebrew":"δαιμόνιον","translit":"daimónion","english":"demons"},{"strongs":"G5438","hebrew":"φυλακή","translit":"phylakḗ","english":"prison"},{"strongs":"G169","hebrew":"ἀκάθαρτος","translit":"akáthartos","english":"unclean"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G3732","hebrew":"ὄρνεον","translit":"órneon","english":"bird"},{"strongs":"G3404","hebrew":"μισέω","translit":"miséō","english":"hateful"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For all the nations have drunk of the wine of the wrath of her sexual immorality, the kings of the earth committed sexual immorality with her, and the merchants of the earth grew rich from the abundance of her luxury.”","understanding":"ὅτι ἐκ τοῦ οἴνου τοῦ θυμοῦ τῆς πορνείας αὐτῆς πέπωκαν πάντα τὰ ἔθνη, “For all the nations have drunk of the wine of the wrath of her sexual immorality” — θυμός, from the wrath family: the passion of her immorality, which brings God's wrath (14:8). καὶ οἱ βασιλεῖς τῆς γῆς μετ᾽ αὐτῆς ἐπόρνευσαν, “the kings of the earth committed sexual immorality with her” — γῆ, from the world family. καὶ οἱ ἔμποροι τῆς γῆς ἐκ τῆς δυνάμεως τοῦ στρήνους αὐτῆς ἐπλούτησαν, “and the merchants of the earth grew rich from the abundance of her luxury” — ἔμπορος, a wholesale merchant; δύναμις, from the power family: here rendered ‘abundance’ — literally ‘the power of her luxury’, the economic might of her extravagance; στρῆνος, arrogant luxury.","words":[{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4095","hebrew":"πίνω","translit":"pínō","english":"drunk"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4203","hebrew":"πορνεύω","translit":"porneúō","english":"committed sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G1713","hebrew":"ἔμπορος","translit":"émporos","english":"merchants"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"grew rich"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"abundance"},{"strongs":"G4764","hebrew":"στρῆνος","translit":"strēnos","english":"luxury"}]},{"v":4,"text":"I heard another voice from heaven, saying, “Come out of her, my people, that you have no participation in her sins, and that you don’t receive of her plagues,","understanding":"Καὶ ἤκουσα ἄλλην φωνὴν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ λέγουσαν, “I heard another voice from heaven, saying” — ἄλλος, from the another family. Ἐξέλθατε ὁ λαός μου ἐξ αὐτῆς, “Come out of her, my people” — the call of Isaiah and Jeremiah to the exiles in Babylon: ‘Go out from her, my people.’ Not necessarily to leave a place, but to refuse its values, idols and compromises. ἵνα μὴ συγκοινωνήσητε ταῖς ἁμαρτίαις αὐτῆς, “that you have no participation in her sins” — συγκοινωνέω, to share as partners; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. καὶ ἐκ τῶν πληγῶν αὐτῆς ἵνα μὴ λάβητε, “and that you don’t receive of her plagues” — those who share her sins will share her judgement.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"another"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"Come out"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G4790","hebrew":"συγκοινωνέω","translit":"synkoinōnéō","english":"participation"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"}]},{"v":5,"text":"for her sins have reached to the sky, and God has remembered her iniquities.","understanding":"ὅτι ἐκολλήθησαν αὐτῆς αἱ ἁμαρτίαι ἄχρι τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, “for her sins have reached to the sky” — κολλάω, to glue together: her sins are piled up and stuck together into a tower reaching heaven — like Babel's tower (Genesis 11:4); ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. καὶ ἐμνημόνευσεν ὁ θεὸς τὰ ἀδικήματα αὐτῆς, “and God has remembered her iniquities” — ἀδίκημα, a wrong done, a crime: God forgets the sins of the forgiven, but not those of the unrepentant.","words":[{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G2853","hebrew":"κολλάω","translit":"kolláō","english":"reached"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3421","hebrew":"μνημονεύω","translit":"mnēmoneúō","english":"remembered"},{"strongs":"G92","hebrew":"ἀδίκημα","translit":"adíkēma","english":"iniquities"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Return to her just as she returned, and repay her double as she did, and according to her works. In the cup which she mixed, mix to her double.","understanding":"ἀπόδοτε αὐτῇ ὡς καὶ αὐτὴ ἀπέδωκεν, “Return to her just as she returned” — ἀποδίδωμι, to pay back: addressed to the agents of God's judgement, not to believers taking revenge (Romans 12:19). καὶ διπλώσατε τὰ διπλᾶ κατὰ τὰ ἔργα αὐτῆς, “and repay her double as she did, and according to her works” — διπλόω; ἔργον, from the work family. ‘Double’ is a Hebrew way of saying ‘full measure’, the exact equivalent (Jeremiah 16:18). ἐν τῷ ποτηρίῳ ᾧ ἐκέρασεν κεράσατε αὐτῇ διπλοῦν, “In the cup which she mixed, mix to her double” — her golden cup (17:4) is filled for her to drink.","words":[{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"Return"},{"strongs":"G1363","hebrew":"διπλόω","translit":"diplóō","english":"repay double"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G4221","hebrew":"ποτήριον","translit":"potḗrion","english":"cup"},{"strongs":"G2767","hebrew":"κεράννυμι","translit":"keránnymi","english":"mixed"},{"strongs":"G1362","hebrew":"διπλοῦς","translit":"diploûs","english":"double"}]},{"v":7,"text":"However much she glorified herself, and grew wanton, so much give her of torment and mourning. For she says in her heart, ‘I sit a queen, and am no widow, and will in no way see mourning.’","understanding":"ὅσα ἐδόξασεν αὐτὴν καὶ ἐστρηνίασεν, τοσοῦτον δότε αὐτῇ βασανισμὸν καὶ πένθος, “However much she glorified herself, and grew wanton, so much give her of torment and mourning” — δοξάζω: she took the glory that belongs to God; στρηνιάω, to live in arrogant luxury. ὅτι ἐν τῇ καρδίᾳ αὐτῆς λέγει ὅτι Κάθημαι βασίλισσα καὶ χήρα οὐκ εἰμὶ καὶ πένθος οὐ μὴ ἴδω, “For she says in her heart, ‘I sit a queen, and am no widow, and will in no way see mourning’” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). The boast of ancient Babylon in Isaiah 47: pride and false security.","words":[{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorified"},{"strongs":"G4763","hebrew":"στρηνιάω","translit":"strēniáō","english":"grew wanton"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G929","hebrew":"βασανισμός","translit":"basanismós","english":"torment"},{"strongs":"G3997","hebrew":"πένθος","translit":"pénthos","english":"mourning"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sit"},{"strongs":"G938","hebrew":"βασίλισσα","translit":"basílissa","english":"queen"},{"strongs":"G5503","hebrew":"χήρα","translit":"chḗra","english":"widow"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"see"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Therefore in one day her plagues will come: death, mourning, and famine; and she will be utterly burned with fire; for the Lord God who has judged her is strong.","understanding":"διὰ τοῦτο ἐν μιᾷ ἡμέρᾳ ἥξουσιν αἱ πληγαὶ αὐτῆς, “Therefore in one day her plagues will come” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: suddenly, as Isaiah said of Babylon: ‘in a moment, in one day’. θάνατος καὶ πένθος καὶ λιμός, “death, mourning, and famine” — the opposite of her boast. καὶ ἐν πυρὶ κατακαυθήσεται, “and she will be utterly burned with fire” — ὅτι ἰσχυρὸς κύριος ὁ θεὸς ὁ κρίνας αὐτήν, “for the Lord God who has judged her is strong” — ἰσχυρός, from the power family: Babylon called herself ‘the strong city’ (v. 10), but God is stronger; κρίνω, from the judge family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G3997","hebrew":"πένθος","translit":"pénthos","english":"mourning"},{"strongs":"G3042","hebrew":"λιμός","translit":"limós","english":"famine"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2618","hebrew":"κατακαίω","translit":"katakaíō","english":"burned"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"strong"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"}]},{"v":9,"text":"The kings of the earth, who committed sexual immorality and lived wantonly with her, will weep and wail over her, when they look at the smoke of her burning,","understanding":"Καὶ κλαύσουσιν καὶ κόψονται ἐπ᾽ αὐτὴν οἱ βασιλεῖς τῆς γῆς, “The kings of the earth... will weep and wail over her” — κλαίω; κόπτω, to beat the breast; γῆ, from the world family. The first of three laments: kings, merchants, sailors. οἱ μετ᾽ αὐτῆς πορνεύσαντες καὶ στρηνιάσαντες, “who committed sexual immorality and lived wantonly with her” — ὅταν βλέπωσιν τὸν καπνὸν τῆς πυρώσεως αὐτῆς, “when they look at the smoke of her burning” — βλέπω, from the see family. They mourn their lost profits, not her sins.","words":[{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weep"},{"strongs":"G2875","hebrew":"κόπτω","translit":"kóptō","english":"wail"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4203","hebrew":"πορνεύω","translit":"porneúō","english":"committed sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G4763","hebrew":"στρηνιάω","translit":"strēniáō","english":"lived wantonly"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"look"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G4451","hebrew":"πύρωσις","translit":"pýrōsis","english":"burning"}]},{"v":10,"text":"standing far away for the fear of her torment, saying, ‘Woe, woe, the great city, Babylon, the strong city! For your judgment has come in one hour.’","understanding":"ἀπὸ μακρόθεν ἑστηκότες διὰ τὸν φόβον τοῦ βασανισμοῦ αὐτῆς, “standing far away for the fear of her torment” — they keep their distance: no loyalty, only fear for themselves. λέγοντες· Οὐαὶ οὐαί, ἡ πόλις ἡ μεγάλη, Βαβυλὼν ἡ πόλις ἡ ἰσχυρά, “saying, ‘Woe, woe, the great city, Babylon, the strong city!’” — οὐαί; ἰσχυρός, from the power family: her strength could not save her. ὅτι μιᾷ ὥρᾳ ἦλθεν ἡ κρίσις σου, “For your judgment has come in one hour” — ὥρα, from the time family; κρίσις, from the judge family: the act of judgement, sudden and complete.","words":[{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G3113","hebrew":"μακρόθεν","translit":"makróthen","english":"far away"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G929","hebrew":"βασανισμός","translit":"basanismós","english":"torment"},{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"Woe"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"Babylon"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"strong"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"}]},{"v":11,"text":"The merchants of the earth weep and mourn over her, for no one buys their merchandise any more;","understanding":"Καὶ οἱ ἔμποροι τῆς γῆς κλαίουσιν καὶ πενθοῦσιν ἐπ᾽ αὐτήν, “The merchants of the earth weep and mourn over her” — ἔμπορος; γῆ, from the world family. ὅτι τὸν γόμον αὐτῶν οὐδεὶς ἀγοράζει οὐκέτι, “for no one buys their merchandise any more” — γόμος, cargo; ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family: the beast controlled who could buy (13:17); now there is no one left to buy. The Lamb ‘bought’ people for God (5:9); Babylon only buys and sells.","words":[{"strongs":"G1713","hebrew":"ἔμπορος","translit":"émporos","english":"merchants"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weep"},{"strongs":"G3996","hebrew":"πενθέω","translit":"penthéō","english":"mourn"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"buys"},{"strongs":"G1117","hebrew":"γόμος","translit":"gómos","english":"merchandise"}]},{"v":12,"text":"merchandise of gold, silver, precious stones, pearls, fine linen, purple, silk, scarlet, all expensive wood, every vessel of ivory, every vessel made of most precious wood, and of brass, and iron, and marble;","understanding":"γόμον χρυσοῦ καὶ ἀργύρου καὶ λίθου τιμίου καὶ μαργαριτῶν, “merchandise of gold, silver, precious stones, pearls” — the cargo list (vv. 12–13) is a catalogue of Rome's luxury imports from across the empire and beyond, like Ezekiel's list of Tyre's trade. καὶ βυσσίνου καὶ πορφύρας καὶ σηρικοῦ καὶ κοκκίνου, “fine linen, purple, silk, scarlet” — silk came by caravan from China. καὶ πᾶν ξύλον θύϊνον καὶ πᾶν σκεῦος ἐλεφάντινον, “all expensive wood, every vessel of ivory” — citron wood tables cost a fortune. καὶ χαλκοῦ καὶ σιδήρου καὶ μαρμάρου, “and of brass, and iron, and marble.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1117","hebrew":"γόμος","translit":"gómos","english":"merchandise"},{"strongs":"G5557","hebrew":"χρυσός","translit":"chrysós","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G696","hebrew":"ἄργυρος","translit":"árgyros","english":"silver"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stones"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G3135","hebrew":"μαργαρίτης","translit":"margarítēs","english":"pearls"},{"strongs":"G1039","hebrew":"βύσσινος","translit":"býssinos","english":"fine linen"},{"strongs":"G4209","hebrew":"πορφύρα","translit":"porphýra","english":"purple"},{"strongs":"G4596","hebrew":"σηρικός","translit":"sērikós","english":"silk"},{"strongs":"G2847","hebrew":"κόκκινος","translit":"kókkinos","english":"scarlet"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"wood"},{"strongs":"G2367","hebrew":"θύϊνος","translit":"thýïnos","english":"expensive"},{"strongs":"G4632","hebrew":"σκεῦος","translit":"skeûos","english":"vessel"},{"strongs":"G1661","hebrew":"ἐλεφάντινος","translit":"elephántinos","english":"ivory"},{"strongs":"G5475","hebrew":"χαλκός","translit":"chalkós","english":"brass"},{"strongs":"G4604","hebrew":"σίδηρος","translit":"sídēros","english":"iron"},{"strongs":"G3139","hebrew":"μάρμαρος","translit":"mármaros","english":"marble"}]},{"v":13,"text":"and cinnamon, incense, perfume, frankincense, wine, olive oil, fine flour, wheat, sheep, horses, chariots, and people’s bodies and souls.","understanding":"καὶ κιννάμωμον... καὶ θυμιάματα καὶ μύρον καὶ λίβανον, “and cinnamon, incense, perfume, frankincense” — καὶ οἶνον καὶ ἔλαιον καὶ σεμίδαλιν καὶ σῖτον, “wine, olive oil, fine flour, wheat” — καὶ κτήνη καὶ πρόβατα καὶ ἵππων καὶ ῥεδῶν, “sheep, horses, chariots” — καὶ σωμάτων, καὶ ψυχὰς ἀνθρώπων, “and people’s bodies and souls” — the list ends with slaves. σῶμα, ‘bodies’, was the trade term for slaves; John adds ψυχή, from the life family: these are human lives, persons, not merchandise. The climax of the list exposes the evil at the heart of Babylon's economy.","words":[{"strongs":"G2792","hebrew":"κινάμωμον","translit":"kinámōmon","english":"cinnamon"},{"strongs":"G2368","hebrew":"θυμίαμα","translit":"thymíama","english":"incense"},{"strongs":"G3464","hebrew":"μύρον","translit":"mýron","english":"perfume"},{"strongs":"G3030","hebrew":"λίβανος","translit":"líbanos","english":"frankincense"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G1637","hebrew":"ἔλαιον","translit":"élaion","english":"olive oil"},{"strongs":"G4585","hebrew":"σεμίδαλις","translit":"semídalis","english":"fine flour"},{"strongs":"G4621","hebrew":"σῖτος","translit":"sîtos","english":"wheat"},{"strongs":"G4263","hebrew":"πρόβατον","translit":"próbaton","english":"sheep"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G4480","hebrew":"ῥέδα","translit":"rhéda","english":"chariots"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"bodies"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people’s"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The fruits which your soul lusted after have been lost to you, and all things that were dainty and sumptuous have perished from you, and you will find them no more at all.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ ὀπώρα σου τῆς ἐπιθυμίας τῆς ψυχῆς ἀπῆλθεν ἀπὸ σοῦ, “The fruits which your soul lusted after have been lost to you” — ὀπώρα, ripe late-summer fruit; ἐπιθυμία, craving; ψυχή, from the life family: her very life was bound up in what she craved. καὶ πάντα τὰ λιπαρὰ καὶ τὰ λαμπρὰ ἀπώλετο ἀπὸ σοῦ, “and all things that were dainty and sumptuous have perished from you” — ἀπόλλυμι, to perish. καὶ οὐκέτι οὐ μὴ αὐτὰ εὑρήσουσιν, “and you will find them no more at all” — a double negative: never again.","words":[{"strongs":"G3703","hebrew":"ὀπώρα","translit":"opṓra","english":"fruits"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusted after"},{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"lost"},{"strongs":"G3045","hebrew":"λιπαρός","translit":"liparós","english":"dainty"},{"strongs":"G2986","hebrew":"λαμπρός","translit":"lamprós","english":"sumptuous"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perished"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"find"}]},{"v":15,"text":"The merchants of these things, who were made rich by her, will stand far away for the fear of her torment, weeping and mourning;","understanding":"οἱ ἔμποροι τούτων οἱ πλουτήσαντες ἀπ᾽ αὐτῆς, “The merchants of these things, who were made rich by her” — ἔμπορος; πλουτέω. ἀπὸ μακρόθεν στήσονται διὰ τὸν φόβον τοῦ βασανισμοῦ αὐτῆς, “will stand far away for the fear of her torment” — κλαίοντες καὶ πενθοῦντες, “weeping and mourning” — the same distance and the same self-pity as the kings.","words":[{"strongs":"G1713","hebrew":"ἔμπορος","translit":"émporos","english":"merchants"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"made rich"},{"strongs":"G3113","hebrew":"μακρόθεν","translit":"makróthen","english":"far away"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stand"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G929","hebrew":"βασανισμός","translit":"basanismós","english":"torment"},{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weeping"},{"strongs":"G3996","hebrew":"πενθέω","translit":"penthéō","english":"mourning"}]},{"v":16,"text":"saying, ‘Woe, woe, the great city, she who was dressed in fine linen, purple, and scarlet, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls!","understanding":"λέγοντες· Οὐαὶ οὐαί, ἡ πόλις ἡ μεγάλη, “saying, ‘Woe, woe, the great city’” — οὐαί. ἡ περιβεβλημένη βύσσινον καὶ πορφυροῦν καὶ κόκκινον, καὶ κεχρυσωμένη ἐν χρυσίῳ καὶ λίθῳ τιμίῳ καὶ μαργαρίτῃ, “she who was dressed in fine linen, purple, and scarlet, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls!” — the description of the prostitute (17:4), now in the merchants' mouths: they grieve for her appearance, her trade, her glamour.","words":[{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"Woe"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"dressed"},{"strongs":"G1039","hebrew":"βύσσινος","translit":"býssinos","english":"fine linen"},{"strongs":"G4210","hebrew":"πορφυροῦς","translit":"porphyroûs","english":"purple"},{"strongs":"G2847","hebrew":"κόκκινος","translit":"kókkinos","english":"scarlet"},{"strongs":"G5558","hebrew":"χρυσόω","translit":"chrysóō","english":"decked"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stones"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G3135","hebrew":"μαργαρίτης","translit":"margarítēs","english":"pearls"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For in an hour such great riches are made desolate.’ Every ship master, and everyone who sails anywhere, and mariners, and as many as gain their living by sea, stood far away,","understanding":"ὅτι μιᾷ ὥρᾳ ἠρημώθη ὁ τοσοῦτος πλοῦτος, “For in an hour such great riches are made desolate” — ὥρα, from the time family; ἐρημόω: the city that put God's people into the wilderness becomes a wilderness. Καὶ πᾶς κυβερνήτης καὶ πᾶς ὁ ἐπὶ τόπον πλέων καὶ ναῦται καὶ ὅσοι τὴν θάλασσαν ἐργάζονται, “Every ship master, and everyone who sails anywhere, and mariners, and as many as gain their living by sea” — ἐργάζομαι, from the work family: to work, to make a living. ἀπὸ μακρόθεν ἔστησαν, “stood far away.”","words":[{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G4149","hebrew":"πλοῦτος","translit":"ploûtos","english":"riches"},{"strongs":"G2049","hebrew":"ἐρημόω","translit":"erēmóō","english":"made desolate"},{"strongs":"G2942","hebrew":"κυβερνήτης","translit":"kybernḗtēs","english":"ship master"},{"strongs":"G4126","hebrew":"πλέω","translit":"pléō","english":"sails"},{"strongs":"G3492","hebrew":"ναύτης","translit":"naútēs","english":"mariners"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G2038","hebrew":"ἐργάζομαι","translit":"ergázomai","english":"gain their living"},{"strongs":"G3113","hebrew":"μακρόθεν","translit":"makróthen","english":"far away"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stood"}]},{"v":18,"text":"and cried out as they looked at the smoke of her burning, saying, ‘What is like the great city?’","understanding":"καὶ ἔκραζον βλέποντες τὸν καπνὸν τῆς πυρώσεως αὐτῆς, “and cried out as they looked at the smoke of her burning” — βλέπω, from the see family. λέγοντες· Τίς ὁμοία τῇ πόλει τῇ μεγάλῃ; “saying, ‘What is like the great city?’” — the same question the world asked of the beast (13:4). Their worship of the city outlives her.","words":[{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried out"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"looked"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G4451","hebrew":"πύρωσις","translit":"pýrōsis","english":"burning"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":19,"text":"They cast dust on their heads, and cried, weeping and mourning, saying, ‘Woe, woe, the great city, in which all who had their ships in the sea were made rich by reason of her great wealth!’ For she is made desolate in one hour.","understanding":"καὶ ἔβαλον χοῦν ἐπὶ τὰς κεφαλὰς αὐτῶν, “They cast dust on their heads” — the sign of mourning, as Ezekiel's sailors did over Tyre. καὶ ἔκραζον κλαίοντες καὶ πενθοῦντες, λέγοντες· Οὐαὶ οὐαί, ἡ πόλις ἡ μεγάλη, “and cried, weeping and mourning, saying, ‘Woe, woe, the great city’” — οὐαί. ἐν ᾗ ἐπλούτησαν πάντες οἱ ἔχοντες τὰ πλοῖα ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ ἐκ τῆς τιμιότητος αὐτῆς, “in which all who had their ships in the sea were made rich by reason of her great wealth!” — τιμιότης, costliness. ὅτι μιᾷ ὥρᾳ ἠρημώθη, “For she is made desolate in one hour” — ὥρα, from the time family: the third time, ‘one hour’.","words":[{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"cast"},{"strongs":"G5522","hebrew":"χόος","translit":"chóos","english":"dust"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"heads"},{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G2799","hebrew":"κλαίω","translit":"klaíō","english":"weeping"},{"strongs":"G3996","hebrew":"πενθέω","translit":"penthéō","english":"mourning"},{"strongs":"G3759","hebrew":"οὐαί","translit":"ouaí","english":"Woe"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G4147","hebrew":"πλουτέω","translit":"ploutéō","english":"made rich"},{"strongs":"G4143","hebrew":"πλοῖον","translit":"ploîon","english":"ships"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G5094","hebrew":"τιμιότης","translit":"timiótēs","english":"wealth"},{"strongs":"G5610","hebrew":"ὥρα","translit":"hṓra","english":"hour"},{"strongs":"G2049","hebrew":"ἐρημόω","translit":"erēmóō","english":"made desolate"}]},{"v":20,"text":"“Rejoice over her, O heaven, you saints, apostles, and prophets; for God has judged your judgment on her.”","understanding":"Εὐφραίνου ἐπ᾽ αὐτῇ, οὐρανέ, καὶ οἱ ἅγιοι καὶ οἱ ἀπόστολοι καὶ οἱ προφῆται, “Rejoice over her, O heaven, you saints, apostles, and prophets” — εὐφραίνω; ἅγιος, from the holy family. Earth mourns; heaven rejoices — not at suffering, but at justice for the oppressed. ὅτι ἔκρινεν ὁ θεὸς τὸ κρίμα ὑμῶν ἐξ αὐτῆς, “for God has judged your judgment on her” — κρίνω and κρίμα, two members of the judge family: God has passed the verdict in your case against her. The martyrs' prayer of 6:10 is fully answered.","words":[{"strongs":"G2165","hebrew":"εὐφραίνω","translit":"euphraínō","english":"Rejoice"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostles"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"}]},{"v":21,"text":"A mighty angel took up a stone like a great millstone and cast it into the sea, saying, “Thus with violence will Babylon, the great city, be thrown down, and will be found no more at all.","understanding":"Καὶ ἦρεν εἷς ἄγγελος ἰσχυρὸς λίθον ὡς μύλινον μέγαν, “A mighty angel took up a stone like a great millstone” — ἰσχυρός, from the power family; μύλος, a millstone so large it needed a donkey to turn it. καὶ ἔβαλεν εἰς τὴν θάλασσαν, “and cast it into the sea” — as Jeremiah's scroll against Babylon was tied to a stone and sunk in the Euphrates: ‘Thus Babylon will sink, and will not rise again.’ Οὕτως ὁρμήματι βληθήσεται Βαβυλὼν ἡ μεγάλη πόλις, καὶ οὐ μὴ εὑρεθῇ ἔτι, “Thus with violence will Babylon, the great city, be thrown down, and will be found no more at all” — ὅρμημα, a violent rush. Jesus said it would be better for one who harms His little ones to be drowned with a millstone.","words":[{"strongs":"G142","hebrew":"αἴρω","translit":"aírō","english":"took up"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stone"},{"strongs":"G3458","hebrew":"μύλος","translit":"mýlos","english":"millstone"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"cast"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G3731","hebrew":"ὅρμημα","translit":"hórmēma","english":"violence"},{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"Babylon"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"}]},{"v":22,"text":"The voice of harpists, minstrels, flute players, and trumpeters will be heard no more at all in you. No craftsman, of whatever craft, will be found any more at all in you. The sound of a mill will be heard no more at all in you.","understanding":"καὶ φωνὴ κιθαρῳδῶν καὶ μουσικῶν καὶ αὐλητῶν καὶ σαλπιστῶν οὐ μὴ ἀκουσθῇ ἐν σοὶ ἔτι, “The voice of harpists, minstrels, flute players, and trumpeters will be heard no more at all in you” — the music stops. καὶ πᾶς τεχνίτης πάσης τέχνης οὐ μὴ εὑρεθῇ ἐν σοὶ ἔτι, “No craftsman, of whatever craft, will be found any more at all in you” — the workshops fall silent. καὶ φωνὴ μύλου οὐ μὴ ἀκουσθῇ ἐν σοὶ ἔτι, “The sound of a mill will be heard no more at all in you” — the most ordinary sound of daily life — grinding grain — ceases. ‘No more’ (οὐ μή) six times in verses 21–23: total, final silence.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G2790","hebrew":"κιθαρῳδός","translit":"kitharōidós","english":"harpists"},{"strongs":"G3451","hebrew":"μουσικός","translit":"mousikós","english":"minstrels"},{"strongs":"G834","hebrew":"αὐλητής","translit":"aulētḗs","english":"flute players"},{"strongs":"G4538","hebrew":"σαλπιστής","translit":"salpistḗs","english":"trumpeters"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5079","hebrew":"τεχνίτης","translit":"technítēs","english":"craftsman"},{"strongs":"G5078","hebrew":"τέχνη","translit":"téchnē","english":"craft"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G3458","hebrew":"μύλος","translit":"mýlos","english":"mill"}]},{"v":23,"text":"The light of a lamp will shine no more at all in you. The voice of the bridegroom and of the bride will be heard no more at all in you; for your merchants were the princes of the earth; for with your sorcery all the nations were deceived.","understanding":"καὶ φῶς λύχνου οὐ μὴ φάνῃ ἐν σοὶ ἔτι, “The light of a lamp will shine no more at all in you” — φαίνω, from the appear family: to give light. Jeremiah warned Judah that God would take away ‘the sound of the millstones and the light of the lamp’. καὶ φωνὴ νυμφίου καὶ νύμφης οὐ μὴ ἀκουσθῇ ἐν σοὶ ἔτι, “The voice of the bridegroom and of the bride will be heard no more at all in you” — νυμφίος, νύμφη: Babylon has no wedding; the true bride and Bridegroom are coming (19:7). ὅτι οἱ ἔμποροί σου ἦσαν οἱ μεγιστᾶνες τῆς γῆς, “for your merchants were the princes of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family: wealth was her god. ὅτι ἐν τῇ φαρμακείᾳ σου ἐπλανήθησαν πάντα τὰ ἔθνη, “for with your sorcery all the nations were deceived” — φαρμακεία, sorcery: the spell of her seduction; πλανάω.","words":[{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G3088","hebrew":"λύχνος","translit":"lýchnos","english":"lamp"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"shine"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3566","hebrew":"νυμφίος","translit":"nymphíos","english":"bridegroom"},{"strongs":"G3565","hebrew":"νύμφη","translit":"nýmphē","english":"bride"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G1713","hebrew":"ἔμπορος","translit":"émporos","english":"merchants"},{"strongs":"G3175","hebrew":"μεγιστᾶνες","translit":"megistânes","english":"princes"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5331","hebrew":"φαρμακεία","translit":"pharmakeía","english":"sorcery"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"}]},{"v":24,"text":"In her was found the blood of prophets and of saints, and of all who have been slain on the earth.”","understanding":"καὶ ἐν αὐτῇ αἷμα προφητῶν καὶ ἁγίων εὑρέθη, “In her was found the blood of prophets and of saints” — ἅγιος, from the holy family. The final reason for her judgement. καὶ πάντων τῶν ἐσφαγμένων ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “and of all who have been slain on the earth” — σφάζω, the word used of the Lamb (5:6); γῆ, from the world family. Babylon is responsible for all the innocent blood shed by the world-system through history — as Jesus said of Jerusalem's leaders.","words":[{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G4969","hebrew":"σφάζω","translit":"spházō","english":"slain"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Come Out of Her","verseRef":"v. 4-5","image":"Picture a dazzling city at night, lit up with wealth and pleasure, music spilling from every window. Then hear a voice from heaven, gentle but urgent, speaking to the believers who live there: ‘Come out. Don't share her sins. Don't share her plagues.’","meaning":"“Come out of her, my people, that you have no participation in her sins, and that you don’t receive of her plagues, for her sins have reached to the sky” (vv. 4–5). Leaving Babylon is not about geography but about the heart: refusing her idols, her greed and her compromises."},{"title":"Three Laments from a Distance","verseRef":"v. 9-19","image":"Picture smoke rising from a burning city on the horizon. Kings stand on a distant hill, merchants on another, sailors on the decks of their ships far out at sea — all weeping, all keeping their distance, all mourning not for the people but for their lost profits.","meaning":"“Woe, woe, the great city... For in an hour such great riches are made desolate” (vv. 16–17). Those who love Babylon love what she gives them. When she falls, no one is loyal to her. The world's friendships are only transactions."},{"title":"Bodies and Souls","verseRef":"v. 11-13","image":"Picture a merchant's cargo manifest: gold, silver, pearls, silk, ivory, spices, wine, wheat, horses, chariots... and at the very end, ‘bodies — and souls of men’.","meaning":"“And people’s bodies and souls” (v. 13). The final item reveals what Babylon's luxury rests on. Every economy that treats human beings as merchandise stands under God's judgement."},{"title":"The Millstone and the Silence","verseRef":"v. 21-23","image":"Picture a mighty angel heaving a huge millstone and hurling it into the sea. It plunges and is gone. Then silence falls over the city: no music, no craftsmen, no mill, no lamp, no wedding song.","meaning":"“Thus with violence will Babylon, the great city, be thrown down, and will be found no more at all” (v. 21). Everything Babylon offered — music, work, light, love — was a gift of God she twisted. In the new creation it will be restored; in Babylon it ends forever."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Babylon's Fall Announced (v. 1-3)","body":"An angel with great authority, lighting the earth with his glory, cries that Babylon the great has fallen and become a haunt of demons and unclean spirits, because the nations drank her wine and the kings and merchants grew rich from her."},{"label":"Come Out of Her (v. 4-8)","body":"Another voice calls God's people to come out of her so as not to share her sins and plagues; her sins are piled to heaven; she is to be repaid in full; because she boasted she would never know mourning, her plagues will come in one day, and she will be burned, for the Lord God who judges her is mighty."},{"label":"The Laments (v. 9-19)","body":"The kings of the earth weep and mourn from a distance; the merchants mourn because no one buys their cargo — gold, silks, spices, livestock and human beings; the sea captains and sailors throw dust on their heads; all cry ‘Woe, woe, the great city’, for in one hour she has been laid waste."},{"label":"Heaven Rejoices; the Millstone (v. 20-24)","body":"Heaven, saints, apostles and prophets are called to rejoice, for God has judged her for them; a mighty angel throws a millstone into the sea as a sign that Babylon will be thrown down and never found again; her music, craft, mill, lamp and weddings will cease, for her merchants were the great ones of the earth, her sorcery deceived the nations, and in her was found the blood of prophets, saints and all the slain."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Fallen, Fallen","paragraphs":["Chapter 18 is a funeral song for Babylon, full of the language of the Old Testament prophecies against Babylon and Tyre. A glorious angel announces: “Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great, and she has become a habitation of demons, a prison of every unclean spirit, and a prison of every unclean and hateful bird!” (v. 2). The city that seemed to sparkle with life is exposed as a ruin haunted by evil.","The reasons are given: “For all the nations have drunk of the wine of the wrath of her sexual immorality, the kings of the earth committed sexual immorality with her, and the merchants of the earth grew rich from the abundance of her luxury” (v. 3). Babylon's sin is not only religious idolatry but economic and political: luxury bought at the price of others' suffering, power sold in exchange for compromise.","Rome in John's day was the hub of a vast trading empire. Its wealth flowed from every corner of the known world, and its elites lived in staggering luxury while much of the population lived in slavery or poverty. But Babylon is wherever wealth and power become gods."]},{"heading":"Come Out of Her, My People","paragraphs":["In the middle of the judgement comes a call to believers: “Come out of her, my people, that you have no participation in her sins, and that you don’t receive of her plagues” (v. 4). The words come from Isaiah and Jeremiah, who called the exiles to leave Babylon when God judged it. For Christians in Asia, it meant refusing to take part in idolatrous trade guilds, emperor worship and the immoral pleasures of the city — even at great cost.","‘Coming out’ does not usually mean moving to a desert. Jesus prayed not that His disciples would be taken out of the world, but that they would be kept from the evil one. It means living in Babylon without belonging to Babylon. Paul wrote, “‘Come out from among them, and be separate,’ says the Lord. ‘Touch no unclean thing. I will receive you’” (2 Corinthians 6:17).","The sin at Babylon's heart is pride: “For she says in her heart, ‘I sit a queen, and am no widow, and will in no way see mourning’” (v. 7). She believes she is untouchable. But “in one day her plagues will come: death, mourning, and famine; and she will be utterly burned with fire; for the Lord God who has judged her is strong” (v. 8). Jesus told of a rich man who said, ‘Soul, you have many goods laid up for many years’ — and God said, “You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you” (Luke 12:20)."]},{"heading":"The World Mourns Its Idol","paragraphs":["Three groups lament Babylon's fall: kings (vv. 9–10), merchants (vv. 11–17) and seafarers (vv. 17–19). All of them stand “far away” (vv. 10, 15, 17). They do not try to help her; they mourn what they have lost. The kings lose their pleasures, the merchants their market, the sailors their trade. Three times they cry that her judgement came “in one hour” (vv. 10, 17, 19).","The merchants' cargo list (vv. 12–13) reads like an inventory of Rome's luxury trade: gold, silver, jewels, pearls, fine linen, purple, silk, scarlet, rare wood, ivory, bronze, iron, marble, spices, perfumes, wine, oil, flour, wheat, cattle, sheep, horses, chariots — and finally, “people’s bodies and souls” (v. 13). ‘Bodies’ was the ordinary commercial term for slaves. John adds ‘souls’: these are human beings. The list builds to its shocking end: Babylon's luxury is built on trafficking people.","James warned the rich of his day: “Come now, you rich, weep and howl for your miseries that are coming on you. Your riches are corrupted and your garments are moth-eaten” (James 5:1–2). Jesus asked, “For what will it profit a man, if he gains the whole world, and forfeits his life?” (Matthew 16:26)."]},{"heading":"Heaven Rejoices; Babylon Falls Silent","paragraphs":["While earth mourns, heaven is called to rejoice: “Rejoice over her, O heaven, you saints, apostles, and prophets; for God has judged your judgment on her” (v. 20). This is not delight in suffering. It is joy that justice has finally been done for those she persecuted. The question of the martyrs, ‘How long?’ (6:10), has been answered.","A mighty angel throws a huge millstone into the sea: “Thus with violence will Babylon, the great city, be thrown down, and will be found no more at all” (v. 21). Then comes a haunting litany of things that will never be heard or seen in her again: music, craftsmen, the sound of the mill, the light of a lamp, the voice of bridegroom and bride (vv. 22–23). All the good gifts of human culture — art, work, food, light, marriage — had been twisted in Babylon to serve pride and greed. In the new Jerusalem they will be restored to their true purpose.","The final verdict: “for your merchants were the princes of the earth; for with your sorcery all the nations were deceived. In her was found the blood of prophets and of saints, and of all who have been slain on the earth” (vv. 23–24). Babylon's three great crimes are idolising wealth, deceiving the nations, and shedding innocent blood. Jesus said the blood of all the righteous, “from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zachariah” (Matthew 23:35), would be required. God forgets nothing."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Treasure on Earth and in Heaven","paragraphs":["Jesus told the parable of a rich fool who planned bigger barns and said, “Soul, you have many goods laid up for many years. Take your ease, eat, drink, be merry” (Luke 12:19). But God said, “You foolish one, tonight your soul is required of you. The things which you have prepared—whose will they be?” (Luke 12:20). Babylon is that rich fool on a world scale.","James describes the same judgement on wealth built on injustice: “Your gold and your silver are corroded, and their corrosion will be for a testimony against you, and will eat your flesh like fire. You have laid up your treasure in the last days” (James 5:3).","And Jesus warned that harming His people brings judgement like a millstone: “but whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to stumble, it would be better for him that a huge millstone should be hung around his neck, and that he should be sunk in the depths of the sea” (Matthew 18:6)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Merchants","hebrew":"ἔμπορος","translit":"émporos","strongs":"G1713 (merchandise γόμος, gómos, G1117)","verses":"v. 3, 11, 15, 23","insight":"Strong's: “a (wholesale) tradesman.” ἔμπορος is a wholesale merchant, a trader who travels to buy and sell — our ‘emporium’ comes from it. Revelation does not condemn trade itself, but Babylon's merchants ‘were the princes of the earth’ (v. 23): wealth had become the ruling power. They grew rich from her luxury and mourn her fall only because ‘no one buys their merchandise any more’. Their cargo ends with human beings."},{"english":"Luxury","hebrew":"στρῆνος","translit":"strēnos","strongs":"G4764 (grew wanton στρηνιάω, strēniáō, G4763)","verses":"v. 3, 7, 9","insight":"Strong's: “a \"straining\", \"strenuousness\" or \"strength\", i.e. (figuratively) luxury (voluptuousness).” στρῆνος is arrogant, excessive luxury — self-indulgence that overflows into pride and insolence. The verb στρηνιάω (vv. 7, 9) means to live in such luxury. Babylon's wealth was not merely enjoyed but flaunted; it became her strength and her boast: ‘I sit a queen.’ The merchants grew rich from ‘the power (δύναμις) of her luxury’ (v. 3)."},{"english":"Participation","hebrew":"συγκοινωνέω","translit":"synkoinōnéō","strongs":"G4790 (sins ἁμαρτία, hamartía, G266)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to share in company with, i.e. co-participate in.” συγκοινωνέω, from σύν (together) and κοινωνέω (to share), means to be partners in something. The fellowship (κοινωνία) of believers is with God and one another (1 John 1:3); the danger is to become partners in Babylon's sins instead. Paul uses the same word: ‘Have no fellowship with the unfruitful deeds of darkness’ (Ephesians 5:11). Coming out of Babylon means ending the partnership."},{"english":"Millstone / mill","hebrew":"μύλος","translit":"mýlos","strongs":"G3458 (stone λίθος, líthos, G3037)","verses":"v. 21, 22","insight":"Strong's: “a \"mill\", i.e. (by implication), a grinder (millstone).” μύλος is both the mill and its heavy grinding stone. In verse 21 an angel hurls a great millstone into the sea: Babylon will sink like a stone. In verse 22 ‘the sound of a mill’ — the everyday noise of grinding grain for bread — falls silent. Jesus used the millstone as a picture of judgement on anyone who causes His little ones to stumble (Matthew 18:6)."},{"english":"Bride","hebrew":"νύμφη","translit":"nýmphē","strongs":"G3565 (bridegroom νυμφίος, nymphíos, G3566)","verses":"v. 23; 21:2, 9; 22:17","insight":"Strong's: “compare Latin \"nupto,\" to marry); a young married woman (as veiled), including a betrothed girl; by implication, a son's wife.” νύμφη is a bride. In Babylon, ‘the voice of the bridegroom and of the bride will be heard no more’ (v. 23) — Jeremiah's words of desolation. But the very next chapter announces ‘the marriage of the Lamb’ (19:7), and the new Jerusalem comes down ‘prepared like a bride adorned for her husband’ (21:2). The prostitute's city falls silent; the bride's city rings with joy."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit calls you out of Babylon — and shows you why.","items":[{"title":"Come Out of Her","body":"“Come out of her, my people” (v. 4). The Spirit asks you to examine where Babylon's values have taken hold in your life: love of luxury, trust in money, compromise for success, indifference to the exploited. Come out — not by running away, but by refusing to share her sins."},{"title":"Don't Say, ‘I Sit a Queen’","body":"Babylon's pride said she would never mourn (v. 7). The Spirit warns against the false security of wealth, health and success. Everything Babylon trusted vanished in one hour. Put your security in God alone."},{"title":"See the People Behind the Products","body":"Babylon's cargo ended with “people’s bodies and souls” (v. 13). The Spirit wants to open your eyes to the human cost of what we buy and consume. Love your neighbour also in how you spend."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Babylon Check","body":"Where are you sharing in Babylon's sins — through greed, compromise, exploitation, or simple indifference (v. 4)?"},{"title":"The Security Check","body":"What would you lose if your wealth, job or comforts disappeared ‘in one hour’ (v. 17)? Would your faith still stand?"},{"title":"The Justice Check","body":"Do you know anything about how the things you buy are made, and whether people are exploited in making them (v. 13)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Come Out in One Area","body":"Choose one area where Babylon's values have a grip on you — spending, entertainment, ambition, comfort. Take one concrete step this week to ‘come out’: cut back, give away, refuse, or simplify."},{"title":"Pray for the Exploited","body":"Learn about one form of modern slavery or exploitation (forced labour, trafficking, child labour). Pray daily this week for its victims and for those working to free them, and look for one practical way to help."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Mighty Lord God, You have judged Babylon, and Your judgements are true. Thank You that no empire of greed, pride and cruelty will stand forever. Thank You that You remember the blood of Your prophets and saints and of all who have been slain on the earth. Lord, I hear Your call: come out of her, my people. Show me where I share in Babylon's sins — in my love of luxury, my trust in wealth, my indifference to those who suffer for my comfort. Give me grace to come out, and to live in this world without belonging to it. Keep me from the pride that says, ‘I sit a queen, I will never mourn.’ Let my treasure be in heaven. And as Babylon falls silent, let me look forward to the city where the voice of the Bridegroom and the bride will be heard forever. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Jeremiah's Scroll Sunk in the Euphrates","ref":"Jeremiah 51:59-64","relatedVerses":"v. 21","summary":"Jeremiah wrote on a scroll all the disasters that would come on Babylon and gave it to Seraiah, who was travelling there. He told him to read it aloud in Babylon, then tie a stone to it and throw it into the Euphrates, saying, ‘Thus will Babylon sink, and will not rise again because of the evil that I will bring on her.’ In Revelation 18 a mighty angel does the same with a great millstone: Babylon will be thrown down and never found again."},{"title":"The Lament over Tyre","ref":"Ezekiel 27–28","relatedVerses":"v. 9-19","summary":"Ezekiel sang a lament over Tyre, the great trading city of the ancient world, pictured as a magnificent ship. He listed her trading partners and their goods — silver, iron, tin, horses, ivory, purple, linen, spices, wine, wool and slaves. Then the ship sank, and the sailors and merchants threw dust on their heads and wailed. Her king had said, ‘I am a god.’ Revelation 18's laments for Babylon draw heavily on Ezekiel's: the pattern of proud commercial power is the same."},{"title":"Lot Leaves Sodom","ref":"Genesis 19:12-29","relatedVerses":"v. 4","summary":"Angels warned Lot to take his family out of Sodom before God destroyed it. Lot hesitated; the angels took him, his wife and daughters by the hand and led them out, saying, ‘Escape for your life! Don’t look behind you.’ But Lot's wife looked back and became a pillar of salt. ‘Come out of her, my people’ (v. 4) is the same urgent call: leave the doomed city in heart and life, and do not look back with longing."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":19,"chapterTitle":"Hallelujah, the Marriage of the Lamb, and the Rider on the White Horse","themeTitle":"“Hallelujah! For the Lord Our God, the Almighty, Reigns!”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"After these things I heard something like a loud voice of a great multitude in heaven, saying, “Hallelujah! Salvation, power, and glory belong to our God:","understanding":"Μετὰ ταῦτα ἤκουσα ὡς φωνὴν μεγάλην ὄχλου πολλοῦ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ λεγόντων, “After these things I heard something like a loud voice of a great multitude in heaven, saying” — heaven's response to Babylon's fall (18:20). Ἁλληλουϊά, “Hallelujah!” — Hebrew, ‘Praise Yah!’: the only place in the New Testament this word appears, four times in verses 1–6. ἡ σωτηρία καὶ ἡ δόξα καὶ ἡ δύναμις τοῦ θεοῦ ἡμῶν, “Salvation, power, and glory belong to our God” — δύναμις, from the power family: inherent might.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3793","hebrew":"ὄχλος","translit":"óchlos","english":"multitude"},{"strongs":"G4183","hebrew":"πολύς","translit":"polýs","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G239","hebrew":"ἀλληλουϊα","translit":"allēlouïa","english":"Hallelujah"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"Salvation"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":2,"text":"for true and righteous are his judgments. For he has judged the great prostitute, who corrupted the earth with her sexual immorality, and he has avenged the blood of his servants at her hand.”","understanding":"ὅτι ἀληθιναὶ καὶ δίκαιαι αἱ κρίσεις αὐτοῦ, “for true and righteous are his judgments” — κρίσις, from the judge family, as the altar said (16:7). ὅτι ἔκρινεν τὴν πόρνην τὴν μεγάλην ἥτις ἔφθειρεν τὴν γῆν ἐν τῇ πορνείᾳ αὐτῆς, “For he has judged the great prostitute, who corrupted the earth with her sexual immorality” — κρίνω, also from the judge family; φθείρω, to ruin, corrupt; γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐξεδίκησεν τὸ αἷμα τῶν δούλων αὐτοῦ ἐκ χειρὸς αὐτῆς, “and he has avenged the blood of his servants at her hand” — ἐκδικέω, the martyrs' word (6:10), now fulfilled; δοῦλος, from the servant family.","words":[{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgments"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G4204","hebrew":"πόρνη","translit":"pórnē","english":"prostitute"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G5351","hebrew":"φθείρω","translit":"phtheírō","english":"corrupted"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4202","hebrew":"πορνεία","translit":"porneía","english":"sexual immorality"},{"strongs":"G1556","hebrew":"ἐκδικέω","translit":"ekdikéō","english":"avenged"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"}]},{"v":3,"text":"A second said, “Hallelujah! Her smoke goes up forever and ever.”","understanding":"καὶ δεύτερον εἴρηκαν· Ἁλληλουϊά, “A second said, ‘Hallelujah!’” — καὶ ὁ καπνὸς αὐτῆς ἀναβαίνει εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “Her smoke goes up forever and ever” — αἰών, from the time family and the world family: like Isaiah's picture of Edom's smoke rising forever. Her destruction is permanent; she will never rise again.","words":[{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G239","hebrew":"ἀλληλουϊα","translit":"allēlouïa","english":"Hallelujah"},{"strongs":"G2586","hebrew":"καπνός","translit":"kapnós","english":"smoke"},{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"goes up"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":4,"text":"The twenty-four elders and the four living creatures fell down and worshiped God who sits on the throne, saying, “Amen! Hallelujah!”","understanding":"καὶ ἔπεσαν οἱ πρεσβύτεροι οἱ εἴκοσι τέσσαρες καὶ τὰ τέσσαρα ζῷα, “The twenty-four elders and the four living creatures fell down” — the throne room of chapters 4–5 once more. καὶ προσεκύνησαν τῷ θεῷ τῷ καθημένῳ ἐπὶ τῷ θρόνῳ, λέγοντες· Ἀμήν, Ἁλληλουϊά, “and worshiped God who sits on the throne, saying, ‘Amen! Hallelujah!’” — the words of Psalm 106:48: the elders seal heaven's praise.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"living creatures"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell down"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"},{"strongs":"G239","hebrew":"ἀλληλουϊα","translit":"allēlouïa","english":"Hallelujah"}]},{"v":5,"text":"A voice came from the throne, saying, “Give praise to our God, all you his servants, you who fear him, the small and the great!”","understanding":"Καὶ φωνὴ ἀπὸ τοῦ θρόνου ἐξῆλθεν λέγουσα, “A voice came from the throne, saying” — Αἰνεῖτε τῷ θεῷ ἡμῶν, πάντες οἱ δοῦλοι αὐτοῦ, “Give praise to our God, all you his servants” — αἰνέω, to praise: the Greek of ‘Hallelujah’; δοῦλος, from the servant family. οἱ φοβούμενοι αὐτόν, οἱ μικροὶ καὶ οἱ μεγάλοι, “you who fear him, the small and the great!” — everyone, from the least to the greatest, is called to praise.","words":[{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G134","hebrew":"αἰνέω","translit":"ainéō","english":"Give praise"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"small"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":6,"text":"I heard something like the voice of a great multitude, and like the voice of many waters, and like the voice of mighty thunders, saying, “Hallelujah! For the Lord our God, the Almighty, reigns!","understanding":"Καὶ ἤκουσα ὡς φωνὴν ὄχλου πολλοῦ καὶ ὡς φωνὴν ὑδάτων πολλῶν καὶ ὡς φωνὴν βροντῶν ἰσχυρῶν, “I heard something like the voice of a great multitude, and like the voice of many waters, and like the voice of mighty thunders” — ἰσχυρός, from the power family. λεγόντων· Ἁλληλουϊά, ὅτι ἐβασίλευσεν κύριος ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶν ὁ παντοκράτωρ, “saying, ‘Hallelujah! For the Lord our God, the Almighty, reigns!’” — βασιλεύω, aorist: He has begun to reign, openly and finally. παντοκράτωρ, the All-Ruler. The great climax of heaven's praise.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3793","hebrew":"ὄχλος","translit":"óchlos","english":"multitude"},{"strongs":"G4183","hebrew":"πολύς","translit":"polýs","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"waters"},{"strongs":"G1027","hebrew":"βροντή","translit":"brontḗ","english":"thunders"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G239","hebrew":"ἀλληλουϊα","translit":"allēlouïa","english":"Hallelujah"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reigns"}]},{"v":7,"text":"Let us rejoice and be exceedingly glad, and let us give the glory to him. For the marriage of the Lamb has come, and his wife has made herself ready.”","understanding":"χαίρωμεν καὶ ἀγαλλιῶμεν, καὶ δώσομεν τὴν δόξαν αὐτῷ, “Let us rejoice and be exceedingly glad, and let us give the glory to him” — ἀγαλλιάω, to leap for joy. ὅτι ἦλθεν ὁ γάμος τοῦ ἀρνίου, “For the marriage of the Lamb has come” — γάμος, the wedding feast; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The prostitute is judged; now the true bride appears. καὶ ἡ γυνὴ αὐτοῦ ἡτοίμασεν ἑαυτήν, “and his wife has made herself ready” — ἑτοιμάζω: the church has prepared through faithfulness, as a bride prepares for her wedding.","words":[{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G21","hebrew":"ἀγαλλιάω","translit":"agalliáō","english":"exceedingly glad"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G1062","hebrew":"γάμος","translit":"gámos","english":"marriage"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"made herself ready"}]},{"v":8,"text":"It was given to her that she would array herself in bright, pure, fine linen: for the fine linen is the righteous acts of the saints.","understanding":"καὶ ἐδόθη αὐτῇ ἵνα περιβάληται βύσσινον λαμπρὸν καθαρόν, “It was given to her that she would array herself in bright, pure, fine linen” — ‘given’: grace. Contrast the prostitute's purple and scarlet (17:4). τὸ γὰρ βύσσινον τὰ δικαιώματα τῶν ἁγίων ἐστίν, “for the fine linen is the righteous acts of the saints” — δικαίωμα, righteous deeds; ἅγιος, from the holy family. Both sides of salvation: the dress is God's gift, and it is woven from the lives of His people (Ephesians 2:10).","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"array"},{"strongs":"G1039","hebrew":"βύσσινος","translit":"býssinos","english":"fine linen"},{"strongs":"G2986","hebrew":"λαμπρός","translit":"lamprós","english":"bright"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G1345","hebrew":"δικαίωμα","translit":"dikaíōma","english":"righteous acts"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"}]},{"v":9,"text":"He said to me, “Write, ‘Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb.’” He said to me, “These are true words of God.”","understanding":"Καὶ λέγει μοι· Γράψον· Μακάριοι οἱ εἰς τὸ δεῖπνον τοῦ γάμου τοῦ ἀρνίου κεκλημένοι, “He said to me, ‘Write, “Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb”’” — the fourth blessing of the book. δεῖπνον, the evening banquet; γάμος; καλέω, to invite; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The church is both the bride and the guests: two pictures of the same joy. καὶ λέγει μοι· Οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι ἀληθινοὶ τοῦ θεοῦ εἰσιν, “He said to me, ‘These are true words of God’” — λόγος, from the word family.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"Write"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"invited"},{"strongs":"G1062","hebrew":"γάμος","translit":"gámos","english":"marriage"},{"strongs":"G1173","hebrew":"δεῖπνον","translit":"deîpnon","english":"supper"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":10,"text":"I fell down before his feet to worship him. He said to me, “Look! Don’t do it! I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers who hold the testimony of Jesus. Worship God, for the testimony of Jesus is the Spirit of Prophecy.”","understanding":"καὶ ἔπεσα ἔμπροσθεν τῶν ποδῶν αὐτοῦ προσκυνῆσαι αὐτῷ, “I fell down before his feet to worship him” — even an apostle can be overwhelmed into wrong worship. καὶ λέγει μοι· Ὅρα μή, “He said to me, ‘Look! Don’t do it!’” — ὁράω, from the see family: ‘see that you don't!’ σύνδουλός σού εἰμι καὶ τῶν ἀδελφῶν σου τῶν ἐχόντων τὴν μαρτυρίαν Ἰησοῦ, “I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers who hold the testimony of Jesus” — μαρτυρία, from the witness family. τῷ θεῷ προσκύνησον, “Worship God” — the message of the whole book in two words. ἡ γὰρ μαρτυρία Ἰησοῦ ἐστιν τὸ πνεῦμα τῆς προφητείας, “for the testimony of Jesus is the Spirit of Prophecy” — μαρτυρία again: all true prophecy bears witness to Jesus, and those who witness to Him share the Spirit of prophecy.","words":[{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell down"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"Look"},{"strongs":"G4889","hebrew":"σύνδουλος","translit":"sýndoulos","english":"fellow bondservant"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"hold"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"Prophecy"}]},{"v":11,"text":"I saw the heaven opened, and behold, a white horse, and he who sat on it is called Faithful and True. In righteousness he judges and makes war.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον τὸν οὐρανὸν ἠνεῳγμένον, “I saw the heaven opened” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). In 4:1 a door was opened; now heaven itself opens for the King's return. καὶ ἰδοὺ ἵππος λευκός, “and behold, a white horse” — a conqueror's steed; the true Rider whom the first horseman imitated (6:2). καὶ ὁ καθήμενος ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν καλούμενος πιστὸς καὶ ἀληθινός, “and he who sat on it is called Faithful and True” — Christ's titles to Laodicea (3:14). καὶ ἐν δικαιοσύνῃ κρίνει καὶ πολεμεῖ, “In righteousness he judges and makes war” — κρίνω, from the judge family: His war is just judgement, not conquest for its own sake.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"Faithful"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"True"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judges"},{"strongs":"G4170","hebrew":"πολεμέω","translit":"poleméō","english":"makes war"}]},{"v":12,"text":"His eyes are a flame of fire, and on his head are many crowns. He has names written and a name written which no one knows but he himself.","understanding":"οἱ δὲ ὀφθαλμοὶ αὐτοῦ φλὸξ πυρός, “His eyes are a flame of fire” — as in 1:14 and 2:18: nothing is hidden from Him. καὶ ἐπὶ τὴν κεφαλὴν αὐτοῦ διαδήματα πολλά, “and on his head are many crowns” — διάδημα, from the crown family: the royal diadem. The dragon had seven, the beast ten (12:3; 13:1); Christ has ‘many’: all authority belongs to Him. ἔχων ὄνομα γεγραμμένον ὃ οὐδεὶς οἶδεν εἰ μὴ αὐτός, “He has names written and a name written which no one knows but he himself” — οἶδα, from the know family and the see family: however much is revealed, Christ remains beyond our full grasp.","words":[{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G5395","hebrew":"φλόξ","translit":"phlóx","english":"flame"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"head"},{"strongs":"G1238","hebrew":"διάδημα","translit":"diádēma","english":"crowns"},{"strongs":"G4183","hebrew":"πολύς","translit":"polýs","english":"many"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knows"}]},{"v":13,"text":"He is clothed in a garment sprinkled with blood. His name is called “The Word of God.”","understanding":"καὶ περιβεβλημένος ἱμάτιον βεβαμμένον αἵματι, “He is clothed in a garment sprinkled with blood” — the WEB follows manuscripts reading ‘sprinkled’ (ῥαντίζω); many read ‘dipped’ (βάπτω). Whose blood? Isaiah 63 pictures God's garments stained from treading His enemies in the winepress (v. 15). But His robe is bloodied before the battle begins; many see His own blood, the Lamb's (5:6, 9). Both may be meant: the One who shed His blood for His people now judges those who refuse it. καὶ κέκληται τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ ὁ λόγος τοῦ θεοῦ, “His name is called ‘The Word of God’” — λόγος, from the word family: John's title for Christ in his Gospel (‘In the beginning was the Word’). He conquers by His word.","words":[{"strongs":"G4016","hebrew":"περιβάλλω","translit":"peribállō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garment"},{"strongs":"G4472","hebrew":"ῥαντίζω","translit":"rhantízō","english":"sprinkled"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"Word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The armies which are in heaven followed him on white horses, clothed in white, pure, fine linen.","understanding":"καὶ τὰ στρατεύματα τὰ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ ἠκολούθει αὐτῷ ἐφ᾽ ἵπποις λευκοῖς, “The armies which are in heaven followed him on white horses” — στράτευμα: angels, and also the saints (17:14: ‘those who are with him are called chosen and faithful’). ἐνδεδυμένοι βύσσινον λευκὸν καθαρόν, “clothed in white, pure, fine linen” — the bride's dress (v. 8): they wear no armour and carry no weapons. The victory is His alone.","words":[{"strongs":"G4753","hebrew":"στράτευμα","translit":"stráteuma","english":"armies"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G190","hebrew":"ἀκολουθέω","translit":"akolouthéō","english":"followed"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G1746","hebrew":"ἐνδύω","translit":"endýō","english":"clothed"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G1039","hebrew":"βύσσινος","translit":"býssinos","english":"fine linen"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Out of his mouth proceeds a sharp, double-edged sword, that with it he should strike the nations. He will rule them with an iron rod. He treads the wine press of the fierceness of the wrath of God, the Almighty.","understanding":"καὶ ἐκ τοῦ στόματος αὐτοῦ ἐκπορεύεται ῥομφαία ὀξεῖα, “Out of his mouth proceeds a sharp, double-edged sword” — ῥομφαία, from the sword family: the long sword of 1:16, His word. ἵνα ἐν αὐτῇ πατάξῃ τὰ ἔθνη, “that with it he should strike the nations” — as Isaiah said the Messiah would strike the earth with the rod of His mouth. καὶ αὐτὸς ποιμανεῖ αὐτοὺς ἐν ῥάβδῳ σιδηρᾷ, “He will rule them with an iron rod” — Psalm 2 (2:27; 12:5). καὶ αὐτὸς πατεῖ τὴν ληνὸν τοῦ οἴνου τοῦ θυμοῦ τῆς ὀργῆς τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ παντοκράτορος, “He treads the wine press of the fierceness of the wrath of God, the Almighty” — θυμός and ὀργή, both from the wrath family, as in 16:19: the full force of God's settled wrath. The winepress of 14:19–20 is trodden by Christ Himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G1607","hebrew":"ἐκπορεύομαι","translit":"ekporeúomai","english":"proceeds"},{"strongs":"G4501","hebrew":"ῥομφαία","translit":"rhomphaía","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G3691","hebrew":"ὀξύς","translit":"oxýs","english":"sharp"},{"strongs":"G3960","hebrew":"πατάσσω","translit":"patássō","english":"strike"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4165","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","english":"rule"},{"strongs":"G4464","hebrew":"ῥάβδος","translit":"rhábdos","english":"rod"},{"strongs":"G4603","hebrew":"σιδήρεος","translit":"sidḗreos","english":"iron"},{"strongs":"G3961","hebrew":"πατέω","translit":"patéō","english":"treads"},{"strongs":"G3025","hebrew":"ληνός","translit":"lēnós","english":"wine press"},{"strongs":"G3631","hebrew":"οἶνος","translit":"oînos","english":"wine"},{"strongs":"G2372","hebrew":"θυμός","translit":"thymós","english":"fierceness"},{"strongs":"G3709","hebrew":"ὀργή","translit":"orgḗ","english":"wrath"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"}]},{"v":16,"text":"He has on his garment and on his thigh a name written, “KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS.”","understanding":"καὶ ἔχει ἐπὶ τὸ ἱμάτιον καὶ ἐπὶ τὸν μηρὸν αὐτοῦ ὄνομα γεγραμμένον, “He has on his garment and on his thigh a name written” — μηρός, thigh: where a horseman's sword hung, or on the part of the robe over the thigh, visible to all. Βασιλεὺς βασιλέων καὶ κύριος κυρίων, “KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS” — as in 17:14, in reverse order. The beast's heads bore blasphemous names; Christ's name is the truth.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"garment"},{"strongs":"G3382","hebrew":"μηρός","translit":"mērós","english":"thigh"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"KING"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"LORD"}]},{"v":17,"text":"I saw an angel standing in the sun. He cried with a loud voice, saying to all the birds that fly in the sky, “Come! Be gathered together to the great supper of God,","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἕνα ἄγγελον ἑστῶτα ἐν τῷ ἡλίῳ, “I saw an angel standing in the sun” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). καὶ ἔκραξεν φωνῇ μεγάλῃ λέγων πᾶσι τοῖς ὀρνέοις τοῖς πετομένοις ἐν μεσουρανήματι, “He cried with a loud voice, saying to all the birds that fly in the sky” — Δεῦτε συνάχθητε εἰς τὸ δεῖπνον τὸ μέγα τοῦ θεοῦ, “Come! Be gathered together to the great supper of God” — δεῖπνον: a terrible contrast with the marriage supper of the Lamb (v. 9). There are two suppers: one where you are the guest, one where you are the meal. From Ezekiel 39, where birds are summoned to feast on the armies of Gog.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G2896","hebrew":"κράζω","translit":"krázō","english":"cried"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3732","hebrew":"ὄρνεον","translit":"órneon","english":"birds"},{"strongs":"G4072","hebrew":"πέτομαι","translit":"pétomai","english":"fly"},{"strongs":"G3321","hebrew":"μεσουράνημα","translit":"mesouránēma","english":"sky"},{"strongs":"G4863","hebrew":"συνάγω","translit":"synágō","english":"gathered together"},{"strongs":"G1173","hebrew":"δεῖπνον","translit":"deîpnon","english":"supper"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":18,"text":"that you may eat the flesh of kings, the flesh of captains, the flesh of mighty men, and the flesh of horses and of those who sit on them, and the flesh of all men, both free and slave, small and great.”","understanding":"ἵνα φάγητε σάρκας βασιλέων καὶ σάρκας χιλιάρχων καὶ σάρκας ἰσχυρῶν, “that you may eat the flesh of kings, the flesh of captains, the flesh of mighty men” — ἰσχυρός, from the power family: the strong of 6:15, who hid in the caves. καὶ σάρκας ἵππων καὶ τῶν καθημένων ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν, καὶ σάρκας πάντων ἐλευθέρων τε καὶ δούλων καὶ μικρῶν καὶ μεγάλων, “and the flesh of horses and of those who sit on them, and the flesh of all men, both free and slave, small and great” — δοῦλος, from the servant family: the same categories who received the beast's mark (13:16) now share its fate.","words":[{"strongs":"G5315","hebrew":"φάγω","translit":"phágō","english":"eat"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G5506","hebrew":"χιλίαρχος","translit":"chilíarchos","english":"captains"},{"strongs":"G2478","hebrew":"ἰσχυρός","translit":"ischyrós","english":"mighty men"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horses"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sit"},{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"slave"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"small"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"}]},{"v":19,"text":"I saw the beast, and the kings of the earth, and their armies, gathered together to make war against him who sat on the horse, and against his army.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον τὸ θηρίον καὶ τοὺς βασιλεῖς τῆς γῆς καὶ τὰ στρατεύματα αὐτῶν συνηγμένα, “I saw the beast, and the kings of the earth, and their armies, gathered together” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); γῆ, from the world family. The armies gathered at Har-Magedon (16:14, 16). ποιῆσαι τὸν πόλεμον μετὰ τοῦ καθημένου ἐπὶ τοῦ ἵππου καὶ μετὰ τοῦ στρατεύματος αὐτοῦ, “to make war against him who sat on the horse, and against his army” — the war is announced; but no battle is described.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4753","hebrew":"στράτευμα","translit":"stráteuma","english":"armies"},{"strongs":"G4863","hebrew":"συνάγω","translit":"synágō","english":"gathered together"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"make"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"}]},{"v":20,"text":"The beast was taken, and with him the false prophet who worked the signs in his sight, with which he deceived those who had received the mark of the beast and those who worshiped his image. These two were thrown alive into the lake of fire that burns with sulfur.","understanding":"καὶ ἐπιάσθη τὸ θηρίον καὶ μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ ὁ ψευδοπροφήτης, “The beast was taken, and with him the false prophet” — πιάζω, to seize, arrest. No fighting: simply captured. ὁ ποιήσας τὰ σημεῖα ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ, ἐν οἷς ἐπλάνησεν τοὺς λαβόντας τὸ χάραγμα τοῦ θηρίου καὶ τοὺς προσκυνοῦντας τῇ εἰκόνι αὐτοῦ, “who worked the signs in his sight, with which he deceived those who had received the mark of the beast and those who worshiped his image” — σημεῖον, from the signs family; εἰκών, from the image family. ζῶντες ἐβλήθησαν οἱ δύο εἰς τὴν λίμνην τοῦ πυρὸς τῆς καιομένης ἐν θείῳ, “These two were thrown alive into the lake of fire that burns with sulfur” — ζάω, from the life family; λίμνη, a lake: the place of final punishment, first mentioned here (20:10, 14–15).","words":[{"strongs":"G4084","hebrew":"πιάζω","translit":"piázō","english":"taken"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G5578","hebrew":"ψευδοπροφήτης","translit":"pseudoprophḗtēs","english":"false prophet"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"worked"},{"strongs":"G4592","hebrew":"σημεῖον","translit":"sēmeîon","english":"signs"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"mark"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worshiped"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"alive"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown"},{"strongs":"G3041","hebrew":"λίμνη","translit":"límnē","english":"lake"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2545","hebrew":"καίω","translit":"kaíō","english":"burns"},{"strongs":"G2303","hebrew":"θεῖον","translit":"theîon","english":"sulfur"}]},{"v":21,"text":"The rest were killed with the sword of him who sat on the horse, the sword which came out of his mouth. All the birds were filled with their flesh.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ λοιποὶ ἀπεκτάνθησαν ἐν τῇ ῥομφαίᾳ τοῦ καθημένου ἐπὶ τοῦ ἵππου, “The rest were killed with the sword of him who sat on the horse” — ῥομφαία, from the sword family. τῇ ἐξελθούσῃ ἐκ τοῦ στόματος αὐτοῦ, “the sword which came out of his mouth” — Christ wins by His word alone, as Paul said: the Lord will destroy the lawless one ‘with the breath of his mouth’. καὶ πάντα τὰ ὄρνεα ἐχορτάσθησαν ἐκ τῶν σαρκῶν αὐτῶν, “All the birds were filled with their flesh.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G615","hebrew":"ἀποκτείνω","translit":"apokteínō","english":"killed"},{"strongs":"G4501","hebrew":"ῥομφαία","translit":"rhomphaía","english":"sword"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2462","hebrew":"ἵππος","translit":"híppos","english":"horse"},{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"came out"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"},{"strongs":"G3732","hebrew":"ὄρνεον","translit":"órneon","english":"birds"},{"strongs":"G5526","hebrew":"χορτάζω","translit":"chortázō","english":"filled"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Hallelujah Chorus","verseRef":"v. 1-6","image":"Picture a roar rising from heaven — a crowd beyond counting, like a waterfall, like rolling thunder — shouting one word again and again: ‘Hallelujah!’ The elders and living creatures fall on their faces: ‘Amen! Hallelujah!’ And a voice from the throne calls every servant of God, small and great, to join in.","meaning":"“Hallelujah! For the Lord our God, the Almighty, reigns!” (v. 6). Every injustice has been answered, every tear seen, every martyr vindicated. The only fitting response is praise."},{"title":"The Bride Made Ready","verseRef":"v. 7-9","image":"Picture a bride on her wedding day, in a dress of brilliant white linen, shining and pure. Look closer at the fabric: it is woven from countless acts of faithfulness — prayers, kindnesses, sacrifices, quiet obedience — all of them gifts of grace.","meaning":"“For the marriage of the Lamb has come, and his wife has made herself ready. It was given to her that she would array herself in bright, pure, fine linen: for the fine linen is the righteous acts of the saints” (vv. 7–8). “Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb” (v. 9)."},{"title":"Faithful and True","verseRef":"v. 11-16","image":"Picture heaven torn open, and a white horse charging out. Its Rider's eyes blaze like fire; many crowns sit on His head; His robe is soaked in blood; a sharp sword comes from His mouth; and across His thigh is written, ‘KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS.’ Behind Him ride the armies of heaven in spotless white.","meaning":"“He who sat on it is called Faithful and True. In righteousness he judges and makes war” (v. 11). “His name is called ‘The Word of God’” (v. 13). The Lamb who was slain returns as the conquering King."},{"title":"Two Suppers","verseRef":"v. 9, 17-18","image":"Picture two invitations. One, sealed with gold, invites you to the marriage supper of the Lamb. The other is called out by an angel standing in the sun to the vultures of the sky: ‘Come to the great supper of God’ — to feed on the armies of the beast.","meaning":"“Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb” (v. 9). “Come! Be gathered together to the great supper of God, that you may eat the flesh of kings” (vv. 17–18). Everyone will be at one supper or the other. Accept the Lamb's invitation."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Hallelujah over Babylon (v. 1-5)","body":"A great multitude in heaven cries ‘Hallelujah!’ because God's judgements are true and just: He has judged the great prostitute and avenged the blood of His servants; her smoke rises forever; the elders and creatures worship; a voice from the throne calls all God's servants to praise."},{"label":"The Marriage of the Lamb (v. 6-10)","body":"A multitude like many waters and mighty thunder cries ‘Hallelujah! The Lord our God Almighty reigns’; the marriage of the Lamb has come and His bride has made herself ready in fine linen, the righteous deeds of the saints; blessed are those invited to the marriage supper; John falls to worship the angel and is told, ‘Worship God, for the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.’"},{"label":"The Rider on the White Horse (v. 11-16)","body":"Heaven opens and a Rider on a white horse appears, called Faithful and True, who judges and makes war in righteousness; His eyes are like fire, He wears many crowns, His robe is dipped in blood, His name is the Word of God; the armies of heaven follow Him; a sharp sword comes from His mouth to strike the nations; He treads the winepress of God's wrath; His name is King of kings and Lord of lords."},{"label":"The Defeat of the Beast (v. 17-21)","body":"An angel standing in the sun summons the birds to the great supper of God; the beast and the kings of the earth gather to fight the Rider; the beast and the false prophet are captured and thrown alive into the lake of fire; the rest are killed by the sword from the Rider's mouth, and the birds gorge on their flesh."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Hallelujah!","paragraphs":["Earth mourned Babylon (ch. 18); heaven rejoices. Four times in verses 1–6 the multitude cries “Hallelujah!” — the only place this Hebrew word of praise appears in the New Testament. They praise God because “true and righteous are his judgments. For he has judged the great prostitute, who corrupted the earth with her sexual immorality, and he has avenged the blood of his servants at her hand” (v. 2). The prayer of the martyrs under the altar (6:10) is answered in full.","This is not a celebration of suffering. It is joy that God has finally put right what was wrong, and that evil will never again corrupt the earth. The elders and living creatures add their ‘Amen! Hallelujah!’ (v. 4), and a voice from the throne calls “all you his servants, you who fear him, the small and the great” (v. 5) to praise.","The climax comes with a sound “like the voice of many waters, and like the voice of mighty thunders, saying, ‘Hallelujah! For the Lord our God, the Almighty, reigns!’” (v. 6). The kingdom announced at the seventh trumpet (11:15) has arrived."]},{"heading":"The Marriage of the Lamb","paragraphs":["Now the true bride appears. “Let us rejoice and be exceedingly glad, and let us give the glory to him. For the marriage of the Lamb has come, and his wife has made herself ready” (v. 7). Throughout the Bible God's people are His bride: Israel in the prophets, the church in Paul. Paul wrote, “Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the assembly, and gave himself up for it... that he might present the assembly to himself gloriously, not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing” (Ephesians 5:25, 27).","The bride's dress is a gift and a work at the same time: “It was given to her that she would array herself in bright, pure, fine linen: for the fine linen is the righteous acts of the saints” (v. 8). It is ‘given’: no one earns a place at the wedding. Yet it consists of ‘the righteous acts of the saints’: the faithful lives of believers, which God Himself produces in them. The prostitute wore purple and scarlet and gold; the bride wears simple, shining linen.","“Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb” (v. 9). Jesus told parables of a wedding feast for a king's son, and at the Last Supper promised, “I will not drink of this fruit of the vine from now on, until that day when I drink it anew with you in my Father’s Kingdom” (Matthew 26:29). Overwhelmed, John falls at the angel's feet, and is told, “Look! Don’t do it! I am a fellow bondservant with you... Worship God, for the testimony of Jesus is the Spirit of Prophecy” (v. 10). Even angels must not be worshipped; all true prophecy points to Jesus."]},{"heading":"The Rider Called Faithful and True","paragraphs":["Then heaven opens, and Christ returns: “behold, a white horse, and he who sat on it is called Faithful and True. In righteousness he judges and makes war” (v. 11). Every detail recalls what we have already seen of Him: eyes like a flame of fire (1:14), a sharp sword from His mouth (1:16), the rod of iron (2:27; 12:5), the title ‘Faithful and True’ (3:14). He wears “many crowns” (v. 12) — more than the dragon or the beast.","“He is clothed in a garment sprinkled with blood” (v. 13). Some see the blood of His enemies, as in Isaiah 63, where God's garments are stained from the winepress. But His robe is bloodied before any battle is fought, and in Revelation the Lamb conquers by His own blood (5:9; 12:11). Many therefore see His own blood — the sign of the cross. Both truths stand together: the One who shed His blood for sinners is the One who judges those who reject it. “His name is called ‘The Word of God’” (v. 13), the title John gave Him in his Gospel: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God” (John 1:1).","The armies of heaven follow Him, “clothed in white, pure, fine linen” (v. 14) — the bride's dress. They carry no weapons and do no fighting. The only weapon is “a sharp, double-edged sword” from His mouth (v. 15). And on His robe and thigh is His name: “KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS” (v. 16)."]},{"heading":"The End of the Beast","paragraphs":["An angel standing in the sun calls the birds to “the great supper of God” (v. 17) — a grim counterpart to the marriage supper of the Lamb. Ezekiel used the same picture for the defeat of Gog's armies. There are two suppers in this chapter; every person will be at one of them.","“I saw the beast, and the kings of the earth, and their armies, gathered together to make war against him who sat on the horse, and against his army” (v. 19). This is the battle of Har-Magedon (16:16). But no battle is described. “The beast was taken, and with him the false prophet” (v. 20). They are simply seized, and “thrown alive into the lake of fire that burns with sulfur” (v. 20). The rest are killed “with the sword of him who sat on the horse, the sword which came out of his mouth” (v. 21).","Christ's victory is total and effortless. He speaks, and it is done. Paul said the same of the lawless one: “whom the Lord will kill with the breath of his mouth, and destroy by the manifestation of his coming” (2 Thessalonians 2:8). Whatever the details of the end, the outcome is certain: every power that has opposed God and oppressed His people will be overthrown by the word of the returning King."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Bridegroom and the Word","paragraphs":["John the Baptist said of Jesus, “He who has the bride is the bridegroom; but the friend of the bridegroom, who stands and hears him, rejoices greatly because of the bridegroom’s voice” (John 3:29). Jesus told of a king “who made a marriage feast for his son” (Matthew 22:2). Revelation 19 is that feast.","John's warning against worshipping the angel echoes Peter in Cornelius' house: when Cornelius “fell down at his feet, and worshiped him”, Peter raised him up, saying, “Stand up! I myself am also a man” (Acts 10:25–26).","And the Rider's victory by the word of His mouth fulfils Paul's description of Christ's coming: “Then the lawless one will be revealed, whom the Lord will kill with the breath of his mouth, and destroy by the manifestation of his coming” (2 Thessalonians 2:8)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Hallelujah","hebrew":"ἀλληλουϊα","translit":"allēlouïa","strongs":"G239 (Give praise αἰνέω, ainéō, G134)","verses":"v. 1, 3, 4, 6","insight":"Strong's: “praise ye Jah!, an adoring exclamation.” ἁλληλουϊά is a Hebrew word carried over into Greek: hallelu (praise!) and Yah (the short form of God's name, Yahweh). It opens and closes many psalms, especially Psalms 146–150, and the Jewish Hallel psalms sung at Passover. In the New Testament it appears only here, four times, as heaven celebrates the fall of Babylon and the reign of God. Jesus sang the Hallel with His disciples before going to the cross; here the redeemed sing it at His victory."},{"english":"Marriage","hebrew":"γάμος","translit":"gámos","strongs":"G1062 (supper δεῖπνον, deîpnon, G1173)","verses":"v. 7, 9","insight":"Strong's: “nuptials.” γάμος is a wedding and its celebration, which in the ancient world could last a week. Jesus' first sign was at a wedding in Cana; He called Himself the Bridegroom and told parables of wedding feasts. Paul called marriage a picture of ‘Christ and the assembly’. The whole of history ends in a wedding: the union of Christ and His people, with joy and feasting forever."},{"english":"The Word of God","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","strongs":"G3056 (sword ῥομφαία, rhomphaía, G4501)","verses":"v. 9, 13","insight":"Strong's: “something said (including the thought); by implication, a topic (subject of discourse), also reasoning (the mental faculty) or motive; by extension, a computation; specially, (with the article in John) the Divine Expression (i.e. Christ).” λόγος, from the word family, is used by John alone as a name for Christ: the Word who was with God and was God (John 1:1), the Word of life (1 John 1:1), and here ‘The Word of God’ riding to victory. He who spoke creation into being speaks the final word of judgement; the sword from His mouth is His word."},{"english":"Lake","hebrew":"λίμνη","translit":"límnē","strongs":"G3041 (sulfur θεῖον, theîon, G2303)","verses":"v. 20; 20:10, 14, 15; 21:8","insight":"Strong's: “a pond (large or small).” λίμνη is an ordinary word for a lake — Luke uses it of the Sea of Galilee. Revelation uses it five times for ‘the lake of fire’: the final destiny of the beast, the false prophet, the devil, Death and Hades, and all whose names are not in the book of life. It is ‘the second death’ (20:14). Its imagery of fire and sulfur recalls Sodom; its meaning is final separation from God."},{"english":"Supper","hebrew":"δεῖπνον","translit":"deîpnon","strongs":"G1173 (marriage γάμος, gámos, G1062)","verses":"v. 9, 17","insight":"Strong's: “dinner, i.e. the chief meal (usually in the evening).” That is why δεῖπνον was used for the Lord's Supper (1 Corinthians 11:20) and for great banquets. Chapter 19 sets two suppers side by side: ‘the marriage supper of the Lamb’ (v. 9), where the redeemed are guests, and ‘the great supper of God’ (v. 17), where the birds feast on the enemies of God. Jesus spread His table for sinners; those who refuse it face the other."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit fills your mouth with praise and your heart with hope.","items":[{"title":"Learn the Hallelujah","body":"Heaven's response to God's justice is “Hallelujah!” (vv. 1, 3, 4, 6). The Spirit invites you to practise that song now — in your trials as well as your blessings — because the Lord our God the Almighty reigns."},{"title":"Get Ready for the Wedding","body":"“His wife has made herself ready” (v. 7). The Spirit is preparing you for the marriage of the Lamb. Every act of faithful love and obedience is a thread in the bride's dress. What is He asking you to do today to get ready?"},{"title":"Worship Only God","body":"Even John was tempted to worship the angel (v. 10). The Spirit warns you against giving any creature — leader, preacher, celebrity, experience — the worship that belongs only to God. Let everything point you to Jesus."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Praise Check","body":"How often does ‘Hallelujah’ rise from your heart? What would help you praise God more freely (vv. 1–6)?"},{"title":"The Readiness Check","body":"If the marriage of the Lamb were tomorrow, what would you want to change about how you live today (vv. 7–8)?"},{"title":"The Worship Check","body":"Is there anyone or anything you admire so much that it borders on worship (v. 10)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Hallelujah Walk","body":"Take a walk this week and, at every turn or landmark, say ‘Hallelujah!’ and name one reason to praise God — His justice, His salvation, His reign, His love. End by reading Revelation 19:6–7 aloud."},{"title":"Weave the Linen","body":"Each day this week, do one ‘righteous act’ (v. 8) quietly for someone else — a kindness, a gift, a word of encouragement, a prayer. Offer each one to Christ as a thread in the bride's dress."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Hallelujah! Salvation and glory and power belong to our God, for His judgements are true and just. Hallelujah! The Lord our God the Almighty reigns! Thank You that You have judged the great prostitute and avenged the blood of Your servants. Let me rejoice and be glad and give You glory, for the marriage of the Lamb is coming. Lord Jesus, prepare me for that day. Clothe me in the fine linen of Your grace, and work in me the righteous deeds that please You. Thank You for inviting me to the marriage supper of the Lamb. Keep me from worshipping anyone but You. Faithful and True, Word of God, King of kings and Lord of lords, I worship You. Your eyes see everything; Your robe bears the blood You shed for me; Your word will conquer every enemy. Come, Lord Jesus, and reign. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Wedding at Cana","ref":"John 2:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 7-9","summary":"At a wedding in Cana of Galilee the wine ran out. At His mother's request, Jesus told the servants to fill six stone water jars and take some to the master of the feast. The water had become wine — the best wine, saved until last. John calls it the first of His signs, revealing His glory. Jesus began His ministry at a wedding feast, and Revelation shows history ending at one: the marriage supper of the Lamb, where the best wine has been kept until last."},{"title":"Gog's Armies and the Birds","ref":"Ezekiel 38–39","relatedVerses":"v. 17-21","summary":"Ezekiel prophesied that Gog, a great ruler from the north, would gather many nations to attack God's restored people. But God Himself would destroy them with fire and hail. Then He called the birds and wild animals to a great sacrificial feast: ‘You shall eat the flesh of the mighty and drink the blood of the princes of the earth.’ Revelation 19 uses Ezekiel's picture for the defeat of the beast's armies; chapter 20 names Gog and Magog directly."},{"title":"The Triumphal Entry Reversed","ref":"Zechariah 9:9-10; Matthew 21:1-11","relatedVerses":"v. 11-16","summary":"Zechariah prophesied that Zion's King would come: “He is righteous, and having salvation; lowly, and riding on a donkey” (Zechariah 9:9), and would speak peace to the nations. Jesus fulfilled this when He rode into Jerusalem on a donkey, on His way to the cross. In Revelation 19 the same King comes again — not on a donkey in humility, but on a white horse in glory, to judge and to reign. The One who came the first time to die comes the second time to rule."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":20,"chapterTitle":"The Thousand Years and the Great White Throne","themeTitle":"“Blessed and Holy Is He Who Has Part in the First Resurrection”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw an angel coming down out of heaven, having the key of the abyss and a great chain in his hand.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον ἄγγελον καταβαίνοντα ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, “I saw an angel coming down out of heaven” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). After the beast and false prophet (19:20), now the dragon. ἔχοντα τὴν κλεῖν τῆς ἀβύσσου καὶ ἅλυσιν μεγάλην ἐπὶ τὴν χεῖρα αὐτοῦ, “having the key of the abyss and a great chain in his hand” — κλείς; ἅλυσις, a manacle. Not even a mighty archangel is needed: an ordinary angel binds the devil. Christ holds the keys (1:18); His servant uses them.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"coming down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2807","hebrew":"κλείς","translit":"kleís","english":"key"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"},{"strongs":"G254","hebrew":"ἅλυσις","translit":"hálysis","english":"chain"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"}]},{"v":2,"text":"He seized the dragon, the old serpent, which is the devil and Satan, who deceives the whole inhabited earth, and bound him for a thousand years,","understanding":"καὶ ἐκράτησεν τὸν δράκοντα, ὁ ὄφις ὁ ἀρχαῖος, ὅς ἐστιν Διάβολος καὶ ὁ Σατανᾶς, “He seized the dragon, the old serpent, which is the devil and Satan” — κρατέω, from the hold family: to grip and overpower. The same four names as 12:9. ὁ πλανῶν τὴν οἰκουμένην ὅλην is added in some manuscripts, “who deceives the whole inhabited earth” — οἰκουμένη, from the world family. καὶ ἔδησεν αὐτὸν χίλια ἔτη, “and bound him for a thousand years” — δέω, to bind; ἔτος, from the time family: a year, as distinct from χρόνος, a span, and καιρός, a season. ‘A thousand years’ appears six times in verses 2–7.","words":[{"strongs":"G2902","hebrew":"κρατέω","translit":"kratéō","english":"seized"},{"strongs":"G1404","hebrew":"δράκων","translit":"drákōn","english":"dragon"},{"strongs":"G3789","hebrew":"ὄφις","translit":"óphis","english":"serpent"},{"strongs":"G744","hebrew":"ἀρχαῖος","translit":"archaîos","english":"old"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceives"},{"strongs":"G3625","hebrew":"οἰκουμένη","translit":"oikouménē","english":"inhabited earth"},{"strongs":"G1210","hebrew":"δέω","translit":"déō","english":"bound"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"}]},{"v":3,"text":"and cast him into the abyss, and shut it, and sealed it over him, that he should deceive the nations no more, until the thousand years were finished. After this, he must be freed for a short time.","understanding":"καὶ ἔβαλεν αὐτὸν εἰς τὴν ἄβυσσον καὶ ἔκλεισεν καὶ ἐσφράγισεν ἐπάνω αὐτοῦ, “and cast him into the abyss, and shut it, and sealed it over him” — three verbs: thrown, locked, sealed. ἵνα μὴ πλανήσῃ ἔτι τὰ ἔθνη, “that he should deceive the nations no more” — the purpose is specific: his power to deceive the nations is stopped. ἄχρι τελεσθῇ τὰ χίλια ἔτη, “until the thousand years were finished” — τελέω, from the perfect family; ἔτος, from the time family. μετὰ ταῦτα δεῖ λυθῆναι αὐτὸν μικρὸν χρόνον, “After this, he must be freed for a short time” — δεῖ, God's necessity; χρόνος, from the time family: a brief span.","words":[{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"cast"},{"strongs":"G12","hebrew":"ἄβυσσος","translit":"ábyssos","english":"abyss"},{"strongs":"G2808","hebrew":"κλείω","translit":"kleíō","english":"shut"},{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"sealed"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceive"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"must"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"freed"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"short"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"}]},{"v":4,"text":"I saw thrones, and they sat on them, and judgment was given to them. I saw the souls of those who had been beheaded for the testimony of Jesus, and for the word of God, and such as didn’t worship the beast nor his image, and didn’t receive the mark on their forehead and on their hand. They lived and reigned with Christ for a thousand years.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον θρόνους, καὶ ἐκάθισαν ἐπ᾽ αὐτούς, καὶ κρίμα ἐδόθη αὐτοῖς, “I saw thrones, and they sat on them, and judgment was given to them” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); κρίμα, from the judge family: the verdict is given in their favour, and authority to judge is given to them (Daniel 7:22). καὶ τὰς ψυχὰς τῶν πεπελεκισμένων διὰ τὴν μαρτυρίαν Ἰησοῦ καὶ διὰ τὸν λόγον τοῦ θεοῦ, “I saw the souls of those who had been beheaded for the testimony of Jesus, and for the word of God” — ψυχή, from the life family; πελεκίζω, beheaded with an axe; μαρτυρία, from the witness family; λόγος, from the word family. καὶ οἵτινες οὐ προσεκύνησαν τὸ θηρίον οὐδὲ τὴν εἰκόνα αὐτοῦ, “and such as didn’t worship the beast nor his image” — εἰκών, from the image family. καὶ ἔζησαν καὶ ἐβασίλευσαν μετὰ τοῦ Χριστοῦ χίλια ἔτη, “They lived and reigned with Christ for a thousand years” — ζάω, from the life family; ἔτος, from the time family. Those the beast ‘overcame’ (13:7) reign.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"thrones"},{"strongs":"G2523","hebrew":"καθίζω","translit":"kathízō","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G3990","hebrew":"πελεκίζω","translit":"pelekízō","english":"beheaded"},{"strongs":"G3141","hebrew":"μαρτυρία","translit":"martyría","english":"testimony"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G1504","hebrew":"εἰκών","translit":"eikṓn","english":"image"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G5480","hebrew":"χάραγμα","translit":"cháragma","english":"mark"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"forehead"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lived"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reigned"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"}]},{"v":5,"text":"The rest of the dead didn’t live until the thousand years were finished. This is the first resurrection.","understanding":"οἱ λοιποὶ τῶν νεκρῶν οὐκ ἔζησαν ἄχρι τελεσθῇ τὰ χίλια ἔτη, “The rest of the dead didn’t live until the thousand years were finished” — ζάω, from the life family; τελέω, from the perfect family; ἔτος, from the time family. αὕτη ἡ ἀνάστασις ἡ πρώτη, “This is the first resurrection” — ἀνάστασις, from the rise family: a standing up again. Some take it as a bodily resurrection of believers at Christ's return, before the thousand years; others as the new life believers receive when they die and go to reign with Christ (or are spiritually raised with Him now, Ephesians 2:6), with the bodily resurrection of all at the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"finished"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Blessed and holy is he who has part in the first resurrection. Over these, the second death has no power, but they will be priests of God and of Christ, and will reign with him one thousand years.","understanding":"μακάριος καὶ ἅγιος ὁ ἔχων μέρος ἐν τῇ ἀναστάσει τῇ πρώτῃ, “Blessed and holy is he who has part in the first resurrection” — the fifth blessing; ἅγιος, from the holy family; ἀνάστασις, from the rise family. ἐπὶ τούτων ὁ δεύτερος θάνατος οὐκ ἔχει ἐξουσίαν, “Over these, the second death has no power” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: the second death (v. 14) has no authority over them (2:11). ἀλλ᾽ ἔσονται ἱερεῖς τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τοῦ Χριστοῦ, καὶ βασιλεύσουσιν μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ χίλια ἔτη, “but they will be priests of God and of Christ, and will reign with him one thousand years” — ἔτος, from the time family. The promise of 1:6 and 5:10 fulfilled: a kingdom of priests.","words":[{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"has"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"part"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2409","hebrew":"ἱερεύς","translit":"hiereús","english":"priests"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reign"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"}]},{"v":7,"text":"And after the thousand years, Satan will be released from his prison,","understanding":"Καὶ ὅταν τελεσθῇ τὰ χίλια ἔτη, “And after the thousand years” — τελέω, from the perfect family; ἔτος, from the time family. λυθήσεται ὁ Σατανᾶς ἐκ τῆς φυλακῆς αὐτοῦ, “Satan will be released from his prison” — λύω; φυλακή. Why release him? Perhaps to show that even after a long reign of righteousness, the human heart apart from grace is still ready to rebel, and that God's final judgement is entirely just.","words":[{"strongs":"G5055","hebrew":"τελέω","translit":"teléō","english":"after"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"},{"strongs":"G4567","hebrew":"Σατανᾶς","translit":"Satanâs","english":"Satan"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"released"},{"strongs":"G5438","hebrew":"φυλακή","translit":"phylakḗ","english":"prison"}]},{"v":8,"text":"and he will come out to deceive the nations which are in the four corners of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them together to the war; the number of whom is as the sand of the sea.","understanding":"καὶ ἐξελεύσεται πλανῆσαι τὰ ἔθνη τὰ ἐν ταῖς τέσσαρσι γωνίαις τῆς γῆς, “and he will come out to deceive the nations which are in the four corners of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. τὸν Γὼγ καὶ Μαγώγ, “Gog and Magog” — in Ezekiel 38–39 Gog of the land of Magog leads the nations against God's people and is destroyed. Here they stand for all the nations in final rebellion. συναγαγεῖν αὐτοὺς εἰς τὸν πόλεμον, ὧν ὁ ἀριθμὸς αὐτῶν ὡς ἡ ἄμμος τῆς θαλάσσης, “to gather them together to the war; the number of whom is as the sand of the sea” — the same gathering ‘for the war’ as 16:14 and 19:19. Many see these as three views of one final battle.","words":[{"strongs":"G1831","hebrew":"ἐξέρχομαι","translit":"exérchomai","english":"come out"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceive"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G5064","hebrew":"τέσσαρες","translit":"téssares","english":"four"},{"strongs":"G1137","hebrew":"γωνία","translit":"gōnía","english":"corners"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1136","hebrew":"Γώγ","translit":"Gṓg","english":"Gog"},{"strongs":"G3098","hebrew":"Μαγώγ","translit":"Magṓg","english":"Magog"},{"strongs":"G4863","hebrew":"συνάγω","translit":"synágō","english":"gather"},{"strongs":"G4171","hebrew":"πόλεμος","translit":"pólemos","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G706","hebrew":"ἀριθμός","translit":"arithmós","english":"number"},{"strongs":"G285","hebrew":"ἄμμος","translit":"ámmos","english":"sand"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"}]},{"v":9,"text":"They went up over the width of the earth, and surrounded the camp of the saints, and the beloved city. Fire came down out of heaven from God and devoured them.","understanding":"καὶ ἀνέβησαν ἐπὶ τὸ πλάτος τῆς γῆς, “They went up over the width of the earth” — γῆ, from the world family. καὶ ἐκύκλευσαν τὴν παρεμβολὴν τῶν ἁγίων καὶ τὴν πόλιν τὴν ἠγαπημένην, “and surrounded the camp of the saints, and the beloved city” — παρεμβολή, a camp: God's people are still pilgrims; ἅγιος, from the holy family; ἀγαπάω, from the love family: the city God loves — His people. καὶ κατέβη πῦρ ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ καὶ κατέφαγεν αὐτούς, “Fire came down out of heaven from God and devoured them” — as on Gog in Ezekiel 38:22, and on Elijah's enemies. No battle; God simply ends it.","words":[{"strongs":"G305","hebrew":"ἀναβαίνω","translit":"anabaínō","english":"went up"},{"strongs":"G4114","hebrew":"πλάτος","translit":"plátos","english":"width"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2944","hebrew":"κυκλόω","translit":"kyklóō","english":"surrounded"},{"strongs":"G3925","hebrew":"παρεμβολή","translit":"parembolḗ","english":"camp"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"Fire"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"came down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2719","hebrew":"κατεσθίω","translit":"katesthíō","english":"devoured"}]},{"v":10,"text":"The devil who deceived them was thrown into the lake of fire and sulfur, where the beast and the false prophet are also. They will be tormented day and night forever and ever.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ διάβολος ὁ πλανῶν αὐτοὺς ἐβλήθη εἰς τὴν λίμνην τοῦ πυρὸς καὶ θείου, “The devil who deceived them was thrown into the lake of fire and sulfur” — διάβολος; λίμνη. The deceiver's career ends. ὅπου καὶ τὸ θηρίον καὶ ὁ ψευδοπροφήτης, “where the beast and the false prophet are also” — the whole unholy trinity together (19:20). καὶ βασανισθήσονται ἡμέρας καὶ νυκτὸς εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “They will be tormented day and night forever and ever” — ἡμέρα, from the time family; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. Satan's defeat is total and final.","words":[{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"deceived"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown"},{"strongs":"G3041","hebrew":"λίμνη","translit":"límnē","english":"lake"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2303","hebrew":"θεῖον","translit":"theîon","english":"sulfur"},{"strongs":"G2342","hebrew":"θηρίον","translit":"thēríon","english":"beast"},{"strongs":"G5578","hebrew":"ψευδοπροφήτης","translit":"pseudoprophḗtēs","english":"false prophet"},{"strongs":"G928","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","english":"tormented"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":11,"text":"I saw a great white throne, and him who sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away. There was found no place for them.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον θρόνον μέγαν λευκόν, “I saw a great white throne” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). Great in majesty; white in perfect purity and justice. καὶ τὸν καθήμενον ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν, “and him who sat on it” — God; and Jesus said the Father has given all judgement to the Son. οὗ ἀπὸ τοῦ προσώπου ἔφυγεν ἡ γῆ καὶ ὁ οὐρανός, καὶ τόπος οὐχ εὑρέθη αὐτοῖς, “from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away. There was found no place for them” — γῆ, from the world family. The old creation itself cannot stand before Him; there is nowhere to hide. Every person now stands before God alone.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3022","hebrew":"λευκός","translit":"leukós","english":"white"},{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sat"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G5343","hebrew":"φεύγω","translit":"pheúgō","english":"fled away"},{"strongs":"G5117","hebrew":"τόπος","translit":"tópos","english":"place"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"}]},{"v":12,"text":"I saw the dead, the great and the small, standing before the throne, and they opened books. Another book was opened, which is the book of life. The dead were judged out of the things which were written in the books, according to their works.","understanding":"καὶ εἶδον τοὺς νεκρούς, τοὺς μεγάλους καὶ τοὺς μικρούς, ἑστῶτας ἐνώπιον τοῦ θρόνου, “I saw the dead, the great and the small, standing before the throne” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family). No one is too great to escape or too small to be overlooked. καὶ βιβλία ἠνοίχθησαν, “and they opened books” — βιβλίον, from the book family: the records of every life (Daniel 7:10). καὶ ἄλλο βιβλίον ἠνοίχθη, ὅ ἐστιν τῆς ζωῆς, “Another book was opened, which is the book of life” — ἄλλος, from the another family; ζωή, from the life family. καὶ ἐκρίθησαν οἱ νεκροὶ ἐκ τῶν γεγραμμένων ἐν τοῖς βιβλίοις κατὰ τὰ ἔργα αὐτῶν, “The dead were judged out of the things which were written in the books, according to their works” — κρίνω, from the judge family; ἔργον, from the work family. Two kinds of book: the record of works, and the book of grace.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G3398","hebrew":"μικρός","translit":"mikrós","english":"small"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"standing"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"books"},{"strongs":"G455","hebrew":"ἀνοίγω","translit":"anoígō","english":"opened"},{"strongs":"G243","hebrew":"ἄλλος","translit":"állos","english":"Another"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":13,"text":"The sea gave up the dead who were in it. Death and Hades gave up the dead who were in them. They were judged, each one according to his works.","understanding":"καὶ ἔδωκεν ἡ θάλασσα τοὺς νεκροὺς τοὺς ἐν αὐτῇ, “The sea gave up the dead who were in it” — those lost without burial are not lost to God. καὶ ὁ θάνατος καὶ ὁ ᾅδης ἔδωκαν τοὺς νεκροὺς τοὺς ἐν αὐτοῖς, “Death and Hades gave up the dead who were in them” — Christ holds their keys (1:18). καὶ ἐκρίθησαν ἕκαστος κατὰ τὰ ἔργα αὐτῶν, “They were judged, each one according to his works” — κρίνω, from the judge family; ἔργον, from the work family: personally, individually, justly.","words":[{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"gave up"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G86","hebrew":"ᾅδης","translit":"háidēs","english":"Hades"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire. This is the second death, the lake of fire.","understanding":"καὶ ὁ θάνατος καὶ ὁ ᾅδης ἐβλήθησαν εἰς τὴν λίμνην τοῦ πυρός, “Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire” — the last enemy destroyed (1 Corinthians 15:26). Death itself dies. οὗτος ὁ θάνατος ὁ δεύτερός ἐστιν, ἡ λίμνη τοῦ πυρός, “This is the second death, the lake of fire” — the first death separates body and soul; the second separates the person from God forever.","words":[{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G86","hebrew":"ᾅδης","translit":"háidēs","english":"Hades"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"thrown"},{"strongs":"G3041","hebrew":"λίμνη","translit":"límnē","english":"lake"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"}]},{"v":15,"text":"If anyone was not found written in the book of life, he was cast into the lake of fire.","understanding":"καὶ εἴ τις οὐχ εὑρέθη ἐν τῇ βίβλῳ τῆς ζωῆς γεγραμμένος, “If anyone was not found written in the book of life” — βίβλος, from the book family: the register of citizens (3:5), as distinct from the βιβλία, the records of works (v. 12); ζωή, from the life family. ἐβλήθη εἰς τὴν λίμνην τοῦ πυρός, “he was cast into the lake of fire” — no one is saved by the record of their works; only by having their name in the Lamb's book of life (13:8; 21:27).","words":[{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G976","hebrew":"βίβλος","translit":"bíblos","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G906","hebrew":"βάλλω","translit":"bállō","english":"cast"},{"strongs":"G3041","hebrew":"λίμνη","translit":"límnē","english":"lake"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Dragon Chained","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture the dragon who once swept stars from the sky and stood over the woman's child. Now an ordinary angel comes down, grabs him, wraps a great chain around him, throws him into the pit, slams it shut, locks it and seals it.","meaning":"“He seized the dragon, the old serpent, which is the devil and Satan... and bound him for a thousand years, and cast him into the abyss” (vv. 2–3). However terrifying the enemy seems, he is no match for God. He can be bound by a single angel."},{"title":"The Martyrs Enthroned","verseRef":"v. 4-6","image":"Picture those who were beheaded in the arena for refusing the beast's image, their bodies thrown away in shame. Now see them seated on thrones, alive, reigning with Christ, priests of God, and the second death unable to touch them.","meaning":"“I saw the souls of those who had been beheaded for the testimony of Jesus... They lived and reigned with Christ for a thousand years” (v. 4). “Blessed and holy is he who has part in the first resurrection” (v. 6). The world's verdict is reversed."},{"title":"The Great White Throne","verseRef":"v. 11-12","image":"Picture heaven and earth fleeing away until nothing is left but a vast white throne and, before it, every human being who has ever lived — kings and beggars, famous and forgotten — standing alone. Books are opened. And one more book: the book of life.","meaning":"“I saw the dead, the great and the small, standing before the throne, and they opened books. Another book was opened, which is the book of life” (v. 12). Everyone will give account. The only safe place is to have your name in the Lamb's book."},{"title":"Death Thrown into the Fire","verseRef":"v. 13-14","image":"Picture the sea, the grave and the realm of the dead giving up every person they held. Then picture Death and Hades themselves — the old enemies that have taken every generation — seized and hurled into the lake of fire.","meaning":"“Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire” (v. 14). The last enemy is destroyed. For those in Christ, death will never touch them again."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Satan Bound (v. 1-3)","body":"An angel with the key of the abyss and a great chain seizes the dragon, binds him for a thousand years and seals him in the abyss so that he can no longer deceive the nations, until he is released for a short time."},{"label":"The Saints Reign (v. 4-6)","body":"John sees thrones given to those who judge, and the souls of those beheaded for the testimony of Jesus and who refused the beast; they come to life and reign with Christ a thousand years; this is the first resurrection; blessed and holy are those who share in it, for the second death has no power over them."},{"label":"Satan's Final Rebellion and Defeat (v. 7-10)","body":"After the thousand years Satan is released and deceives the nations, Gog and Magog, gathering them against the camp of the saints and the beloved city; fire from heaven consumes them; the devil is thrown into the lake of fire with the beast and false prophet, to be tormented forever."},{"label":"The Great White Throne (v. 11-15)","body":"John sees a great white throne, from whose presence earth and heaven flee; the dead, great and small, stand before it; books are opened, including the book of life, and all are judged by their works; sea, Death and Hades give up their dead; Death and Hades are thrown into the lake of fire, the second death, with anyone not found in the book of life."}],"sections":[{"heading":"The Thousand Years: How Christians Read It","paragraphs":["Revelation 20 is the only passage in the Bible that speaks of a thousand-year reign, and Christians have read it in three main ways. Premillennialists believe Christ returns (ch. 19) before (‘pre’) a literal or long period of reign on earth, during which Satan is bound and resurrected believers reign with Christ; then comes Satan's final rebellion and the last judgement. Amillennialists believe the thousand years is a symbolic description of the present age between Christ's first and second comings: Satan was bound at the cross so that he can no longer prevent the gospel from reaching the nations, and believers who die reign with Christ in heaven. Postmillennialists believe the gospel will gradually transform the world into a long era of peace and righteousness before Christ returns.","Each reading has serious arguments. Premillennialists point out that chapter 20 follows Christ's return in chapter 19 and speaks of a resurrection in which people ‘lived’. Amillennialists point out that Revelation often goes back over the same period from different angles; that Jesus spoke of binding ‘the strong man’ in His ministry: “how can one enter into the house of the strong man, and plunder his goods, unless he first bind the strong man?” (Matthew 12:29); that the binding is specifically so that Satan cannot ‘deceive the nations’ (v. 3); and that the gathering for war in verse 8 echoes 16:14 and 19:19, suggesting the same final battle.","Faithful, Bible-believing Christians hold each of these views, and this study will not pretend the question is settled. What all agree on is clear in the text: Satan's power is limited and his doom is certain; those who are faithful to Christ, even to death, will live and reign with Him; there will be a final judgement of all people; and death itself will be destroyed."]},{"heading":"Satan Bound, the Saints Enthroned","paragraphs":["An angel seizes “the dragon, the old serpent, which is the devil and Satan” and binds him for a thousand years, throwing him into the abyss “that he should deceive the nations no more, until the thousand years were finished” (vv. 2–3). The binding is specific: it stops him deceiving the nations. However one reads the timing, the picture is of Satan's power curbed by a single angel. The one who seemed so mighty in chapters 12–13 is helpless before God.","John then sees thrones, and “the souls of those who had been beheaded for the testimony of Jesus, and for the word of God, and such as didn’t worship the beast nor his image... They lived and reigned with Christ for a thousand years” (v. 4). Those whom the world condemned and executed now sit on thrones and share Christ's rule. Daniel saw that ‘judgment was given to the saints of the Most High’. The beast took their lives; Christ gives them life and a kingdom.","“Blessed and holy is he who has part in the first resurrection. Over these, the second death has no power, but they will be priests of God and of Christ, and will reign with him one thousand years” (v. 6). Whatever ‘the first resurrection’ means precisely, its promise is certain: those who belong to Christ will never face the second death. Jesus said, “he who hears my word, and believes him who sent me, has eternal life, and doesn’t come into judgment, but has passed out of death into life” (John 5:24)."]},{"heading":"The Last Rebellion","paragraphs":["After the thousand years, “Satan will be released from his prison, and he will come out to deceive the nations which are in the four corners of the earth, Gog and Magog” (vv. 7–8). The names come from Ezekiel 38–39, where Gog leads a vast coalition against God's restored people. Here they stand for all the nations in a final rebellion. They surround “the camp of the saints, and the beloved city” (v. 9) — God's people, still a pilgrim camp.","There is no battle. “Fire came down out of heaven from God and devoured them” (v. 9). And “The devil who deceived them was thrown into the lake of fire and sulfur, where the beast and the false prophet are also” (v. 10). The deceiver of Eden, the accuser of the brothers, the dragon of chapter 12 is finally and permanently defeated. Paul's promise is fulfilled: “And the God of peace will quickly crush Satan under your feet” (Romans 16:20).","Why would God release Satan at all? The text does not say, except that ‘he must’ (v. 3). Perhaps it shows that the human heart, even after the greatest blessings, still needs grace; and that God's final judgement falls on rebellion that is fully deliberate."]},{"heading":"The Great White Throne","paragraphs":["Then comes the final judgement. “I saw a great white throne, and him who sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away. There was found no place for them” (v. 11). There is nowhere left to hide. “I saw the dead, the great and the small, standing before the throne, and they opened books. Another book was opened, which is the book of life” (v. 12). Paul says, “For we must all be revealed before the judgment seat of Christ” (2 Corinthians 5:10).","There are two kinds of books. The ‘books’ record what each person has done: “The dead were judged out of the things which were written in the books, according to their works” (v. 12). God's judgement is fair; nothing is overlooked, and no one is judged for what they did not do. But there is also ‘the book of life’, the Lamb's register of those who belong to Him (13:8; 21:27). Salvation does not come from the record of works, which condemns us all, but from having one's name written in the book of life by grace. Those whose names are there are judged too — but their works are the fruit of faith, and their sins are covered by the Lamb's blood.","Everyone is raised: “The sea gave up the dead who were in it. Death and Hades gave up the dead who were in them” (v. 13). Then “Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire. This is the second death” (v. 14). Paul wrote, “The last enemy that will be abolished is death” (1 Corinthians 15:26). And the chapter ends with the most sobering sentence in the book: “If anyone was not found written in the book of life, he was cast into the lake of fire” (v. 15). The only question that finally matters is whether your name is written there."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Resurrection and Judgement","paragraphs":["Jesus taught a resurrection of all: “the hour comes, in which all that are in the tombs will hear his voice, and will come out; those who have done good, to the resurrection of life; and those who have done evil, to the resurrection of judgment” (John 5:28–29). He also spoke of a present, spiritual resurrection: “the hour comes, and now is, when the dead will hear the Son of God’s voice; and those who hear will live” (John 5:25).","Paul described the order of resurrection: “But each in his own order: Christ the first fruits, then those who are Christ’s, at his coming. Then the end comes, when he will deliver up the Kingdom to God” (1 Corinthians 15:23–24), and “For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet. The last enemy that will be abolished is death” (1 Corinthians 15:25–26).","He also said believers are already raised with Christ: God “raised us up with him, and made us to sit with him in the heavenly places in Christ Jesus” (Ephesians 2:6) — one reason some Christians see the reign of verse 4 as beginning now."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Thousand","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","strongs":"G5507 (years ἔτος, étos, G2094)","verses":"v. 2-7","insight":"Strong's: “a thousand.” χίλιοι is the ordinary Greek number. The phrase ‘a thousand years’ (χίλια ἔτη), with ἔτος from the time family, gives us the word ‘millennium’ (Latin mille, thousand). Revelation uses numbers symbolically again and again (seven, twelve, 144,000, 666), and 1,000 is 10 × 10 × 10, a number of fullness. Whether the thousand years is a literal or symbolic period, it stands in contrast to the ‘short time’ given to Satan: Christ's reign is long and complete; Satan's freedom is brief."},{"english":"Resurrection","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","strongs":"G386 (first πρῶτος, prōtos, G4413)","verses":"v. 5, 6","insight":"Strong's: “a standing up again, i.e. (literally) a resurrection from death (individual, genitive case or by implication, (its author)), or (figuratively) a (moral) recovery (of spiritual truth).” ἀνάστασις, from the rise family, is literally ‘standing up again’. Strong's notes both a literal and a figurative sense — which is exactly why Christians differ over ‘the first resurrection’: is it bodily resurrection at Christ's return, or the believer's new life in Christ that death cannot end? Either way, those who share in it are blessed, holy and safe from the second death."},{"english":"Chain","hebrew":"ἅλυσις","translit":"hálysis","strongs":"G254 (abyss ἄβυσσος, ábyssos, G12)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “a fetter or manacle.” ἅλυσις was used of the chains that bound prisoners — Peter in prison (Acts 12:6–7), Paul ‘an ambassador in chains’ (Ephesians 6:20), and the demon-possessed man who broke his chains (Mark 5:3–4). Satan, who has chained so many, is himself chained. The apostles' chains could not stop the gospel; Satan's chain stops him from deceiving the nations."},{"english":"Beheaded","hebrew":"πελεκίζω","translit":"pelekízō","strongs":"G3990 (testimony μαρτυρία, martyría, G3141)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to chop off (the head), i.e. truncate.” πελεκίζω, from πέλεκυς (an axe), refers to execution by beheading, the Roman punishment for citizens and the death of John the Baptist and, by tradition, of Paul. Those who refused the beast's image paid with their heads; they now receive thrones. Jesus said, ‘Don’t be afraid of those who kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul.’"},{"english":"Gog","hebrew":"Γώγ","translit":"Gṓg","strongs":"G1136 (Magog Μαγώγ, Magṓg, G3098)","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “Gog, a symbolic name for some future Antichrist.” In Ezekiel 38–39, Gog is the ruler of the land of Magog, who leads a vast alliance of nations from the far north against God's people and is destroyed by fire from heaven. In Revelation ‘Gog and Magog’ are not a ruler and his land but the nations themselves, ‘in the four corners of the earth’. Even Strong's calls the name ‘symbolic’: the last great coalition of the world against God."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you the end of Satan, death and judgement — and the only safe place to stand.","items":[{"title":"Satan Is on a Chain","body":"A single angel binds the devil (v. 2). The Spirit wants you to stop fearing the enemy as if he were God's equal. He is a defeated foe on a chain, and his end is the lake of fire. Resist him; he must flee."},{"title":"Faithfulness Is Rewarded","body":"Those who were beheaded for Jesus now reign with Him (v. 4). The Spirit wants you to know that no sacrifice for Christ is wasted. What the world takes from you for His sake, He will repay with life and a throne."},{"title":"Is Your Name in the Book?","body":"“If anyone was not found written in the book of life, he was cast into the lake of fire” (v. 15). The Spirit asks the most important question there is: is your name written in the Lamb's book of life? If you are not sure, come to Jesus today. If you are, tell someone else."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Book of Life Check","body":"On what basis do you believe your name is written in the book of life — your works, or the Lamb's grace (vv. 12, 15)?"},{"title":"The Accountability Check","body":"If the books of your life were opened today, what would you want to change (v. 12)?"},{"title":"The Fear Check","body":"Do you live in fear of death? How does the destruction of Death and Hades (v. 14) change that?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Settle the Question","body":"Read John 5:24 and Revelation 20:15 together. If you have never trusted Christ, pray now and ask Him to write your name in the book of life. If you have, write a prayer of thanks — and pray for three people by name whose names you long to see there."},{"title":"Live for the Throne","body":"Write down one costly act of faithfulness Christ is asking of you — forgiving, speaking up, giving, refusing a compromise. Do it this week, remembering that those faithful to death reign with Him (v. 4)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord God, You sit on the great white throne, and earth and heaven flee from Your face. Thank You that Satan is not Your equal, that a single angel can bind him, and that his end is the lake of fire. Thank You that those who were faithful to death, refusing the beast, now live and reign with Christ. Blessed and holy are those who share in the first resurrection. Thank You that the second death has no power over those who belong to You. Lord Jesus, thank You that my name is written in Your book of life — not because of my works, but because of Your blood. When the books are opened, let my works be the fruit of Your grace. Thank You that You will throw Death and Hades into the fire, and that death will never touch Your people again. Help me live today in the light of that day, and give me a burden for those whose names are not yet written. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Gog and Magog","ref":"Ezekiel 38–39","relatedVerses":"v. 7-10","summary":"Ezekiel prophesied that after Israel's restoration, Gog of the land of Magog would gather a vast army from many nations and come against God's people ‘like a storm’. But God would rain down fire and brimstone, and Gog's army would fall on the mountains of Israel. Then all the nations would know that God is the LORD. Revelation 20 uses these names for the final rebellion of the nations — and the same fire from heaven ends it."},{"title":"Daniel's Vision of Judgement","ref":"Daniel 7:9-10, 22-27","relatedVerses":"v. 4, 11-12","summary":"Daniel saw thrones set in place and the Ancient of Days take His seat; His throne was fiery flames, and thousands upon thousands stood before Him. ‘The judgment was set, and the books were opened.’ The beast was slain, and ‘judgment was given to the saints of the Most High, and the time came that the saints possessed the kingdom.’ Revelation 20 brings together both parts of Daniel's vision: thrones and judgement given to the saints, and the books opened before the great white throne."},{"title":"The Strong Man Bound","ref":"Mark 3:22-27","relatedVerses":"v. 1-3","summary":"When the scribes accused Jesus of casting out demons by Beelzebul, He answered that Satan cannot cast out Satan. ‘But no one can enter into the house of the strong man to plunder unless he first binds the strong man; and then he will plunder his house.’ Jesus' casting out of demons showed that He had come to bind Satan and set his captives free. Revelation 20 pictures the same binding of the strong man, so that the nations can no longer be held in his deception."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":21,"chapterTitle":"A New Heaven, a New Earth and the New Jerusalem","themeTitle":"“Behold, I Am Making All Things New”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth have passed away, and the sea is no more.","understanding":"Καὶ εἶδον οὐρανὸν καινὸν καὶ γῆν καινήν, “I saw a new heaven and a new earth” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); καινός, from the new family: new in kind and quality, as distinct from νέος, new in time or recently made. Not a different universe, but this one renewed and transformed, as Isaiah promised. γῆ, from the world family. ὁ γὰρ πρῶτος οὐρανὸς καὶ ἡ πρώτη γῆ ἀπῆλθαν, “for the first heaven and the first earth have passed away” — ἀπέρχομαι: the old order, scarred by sin and death, is gone. καὶ ἡ θάλασσα οὐκ ἔστιν ἔτι, “and the sea is no more” — the sea was the source of the beast (13:1), the place of chaos, and the barrier that kept John on Patmos. Nothing will separate God's people again.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"passed away"},{"strongs":"G2281","hebrew":"θάλασσα","translit":"thálassa","english":"sea"}]},{"v":2,"text":"I saw the holy city, New Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, prepared like a bride adorned for her husband.","understanding":"καὶ τὴν πόλιν τὴν ἁγίαν Ἰερουσαλὴμ καινὴν εἶδον καταβαίνουσαν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἀπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ, “I saw the holy city, New Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ἅγιος, from the holy family; καινός, from the new family. It comes down: it is God's gift, not humanity's achievement (contrast Babel, Genesis 11). ἡτοιμασμένην ὡς νύμφην κεκοσμημένην τῷ ἀνδρὶ αὐτῆς, “prepared like a bride adorned for her husband” — νύμφη, κοσμέω (from which ‘cosmetic’): the bride of 19:7 is also the city. The prostitute-city falls; the bride-city descends.","words":[{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G2419","hebrew":"Ἱερουσαλήμ","translit":"Hierousalḗm","english":"Jerusalem"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"New"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"coming down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2090","hebrew":"ἑτοιμάζω","translit":"hetoimázō","english":"prepared"},{"strongs":"G3565","hebrew":"νύμφη","translit":"nýmphē","english":"bride"},{"strongs":"G2885","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","english":"adorned"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husband"}]},{"v":3,"text":"I heard a loud voice out of heaven saying, “Behold, God’s dwelling is with people, and he will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God.","understanding":"καὶ ἤκουσα φωνῆς μεγάλης ἐκ τοῦ θρόνου λεγούσης, “I heard a loud voice out of heaven saying” — many manuscripts read ‘out of the throne’. Ἰδοὺ ἡ σκηνὴ τοῦ θεοῦ μετὰ τῶν ἀνθρώπων, “Behold, God’s dwelling is with people” — σκηνή, a tent: the tabernacle where God lived among Israel. καὶ σκηνώσει μετ᾽ αὐτῶν, “and he will dwell with them” — σκηνόω, the verb John used of the Word who ‘lived among us’. καὶ αὐτοὶ λαοὶ αὐτοῦ ἔσονται, καὶ αὐτὸς ὁ θεὸς μετ᾽ αὐτῶν ἔσται αὐτῶν θεός, “and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God” — the covenant promise repeated through the whole Bible, from Leviticus to Ezekiel, here fulfilled. ‘Peoples’ (plural) in many manuscripts: every nation.","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"loud"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G4633","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","english":"dwelling"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God’s"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G4637","hebrew":"σκηνόω","translit":"skēnóō","english":"dwell"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"}]},{"v":4,"text":"He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away.”","understanding":"καὶ ἐξαλείψει πᾶν δάκρυον ἐκ τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν αὐτῶν, “He will wipe away every tear from their eyes” — the promise of 7:17 and Isaiah 25:8. God Himself does it. καὶ ὁ θάνατος οὐκ ἔσται ἔτι, οὔτε πένθος οὔτε κραυγὴ οὔτε πόνος οὐκ ἔσται ἔτι, “Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more” — five things gone forever. Babylon was promised mourning (18:7–8); the new Jerusalem will never know it. ὅτι τὰ πρῶτα ἀπῆλθαν, “The first things have passed away” — πρῶτος: the old order of a fallen world.","words":[{"strongs":"G1813","hebrew":"ἐξαλείφω","translit":"exaleíphō","english":"wipe away"},{"strongs":"G1144","hebrew":"δάκρυ","translit":"dákry","english":"tear"},{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"Death"},{"strongs":"G3997","hebrew":"πένθος","translit":"pénthos","english":"mourning"},{"strongs":"G2906","hebrew":"κραυγή","translit":"kraugḗ","english":"crying"},{"strongs":"G4192","hebrew":"πόνος","translit":"pónos","english":"pain"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G565","hebrew":"ἀπέρχομαι","translit":"apérchomai","english":"passed away"}]},{"v":5,"text":"He who sits on the throne said, “Behold, I am making all things new.” He said, “Write, for these words of God are faithful and true.”","understanding":"Καὶ εἶπεν ὁ καθήμενος ἐπὶ τῷ θρόνῳ, “He who sits on the throne said” — God speaks directly, for only the second time in the book (1:8). Ἰδοὺ καινὰ ποιῶ πάντα, “Behold, I am making all things new” — καινός, from the new family: not ‘all new things’ but ‘all things new’ — renewal, not replacement; present tense: He is already doing it (2 Corinthians 5:17). καὶ λέγει· Γράψον, ὅτι οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι πιστοὶ καὶ ἀληθινοί εἰσιν, “He said, ‘Write, for these words of God are faithful and true’” — λόγος, from the word family: the titles of Christ (19:11) given to His words.","words":[{"strongs":"G2521","hebrew":"κάθημαι","translit":"káthēmai","english":"sits"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"making"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"Write"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"}]},{"v":6,"text":"He said to me, “I have become the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End. I will give freely to him who is thirsty from the spring of the water of life.","understanding":"καὶ εἶπέν μοι· Γέγοναν, “He said to me, ‘It is done!’” — γίνομαι, perfect tense, as at the seventh bowl (16:17); the WEB here reads ‘I have become’, following some manuscripts. Ἐγώ εἰμι τὸ Ἄλφα καὶ τὸ Ὦ, ἡ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ τέλος, “the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End” — ἀρχή, from the power family: the source and ruler; τέλος, from the perfect family: the goal and completion. God is where everything comes from and where everything is going. ἐγὼ τῷ διψῶντι δώσω ἐκ τῆς πηγῆς τοῦ ὕδατος τῆς ζωῆς δωρεάν, “I will give freely to him who is thirsty from the spring of the water of life” — ζωή, from the life family; δωρεάν, as a free gift, without cost (Isaiah 55:1).","words":[{"strongs":"G1096","hebrew":"γίνομαι","translit":"gínomai","english":"become"},{"strongs":"G1","hebrew":"Α","translit":"A","english":"Alpha"},{"strongs":"G5598","hebrew":"Ω","translit":"Ō","english":"Omega"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"Beginning"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"End"},{"strongs":"G1372","hebrew":"διψάω","translit":"dipsáō","english":"thirsty"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"spring"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1432","hebrew":"δωρεάν","translit":"dōreán","english":"freely"}]},{"v":7,"text":"He who overcomes, I will give him these things. I will be his God, and he will be my son.","understanding":"ὁ νικῶν κληρονομήσει ταῦτα, “He who overcomes, I will give him these things” — νικάω: the last of Revelation's promises to the overcomer (2:7–3:21), and the sum of them all; κληρονομέω, to inherit: the WEB renders it ‘I will give him’. καὶ ἔσομαι αὐτῷ θεὸς καὶ αὐτὸς ἔσται μοι υἱός, “I will be his God, and he will be my son” — the promise to David's son (2 Samuel 7:14), now given to every believer: they share the Son's place.","words":[{"strongs":"G3528","hebrew":"νικάω","translit":"nikáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But for the cowardly, unbelieving, sinners, abominable, murderers, sexually immoral, sorcerers, idolaters, and all liars, their part is in the lake that burns with fire and sulfur, which is the second death.”","understanding":"τοῖς δὲ δειλοῖς καὶ ἀπίστοις καὶ ἁμαρτωλοῖς, “But for the cowardly, unbelieving, sinners” — δειλός, the coward who denies Christ under pressure; ἄπιστος, the unbelieving or unfaithful; ἁμαρτωλός, from the sin family (found in some manuscripts). καὶ ἐβδελυγμένοις καὶ φονεῦσιν καὶ πόρνοις καὶ φαρμάκοις καὶ εἰδωλολάτραις καὶ πᾶσιν τοῖς ψευδέσιν, “abominable, murderers, sexually immoral, sorcerers, idolaters, and all liars” — the marks of Babylon and the beast's followers (9:21; 17:4; 22:15). τὸ μέρος αὐτῶν ἐν τῇ λίμνῃ τῇ καιομένῃ πυρὶ καὶ θείῳ, ὅ ἐστιν ὁ θάνατος ὁ δεύτερος, “their part is in the lake that burns with fire and sulfur, which is the second death” — λίμνη. A warning to the churches: those who persist in these, refusing grace, have no place in the city.","words":[{"strongs":"G1169","hebrew":"δειλός","translit":"deilós","english":"cowardly"},{"strongs":"G571","hebrew":"ἄπιστος","translit":"ápistos","english":"unbelieving"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinners"},{"strongs":"G948","hebrew":"βδελύσσω","translit":"bdelýssō","english":"abominable"},{"strongs":"G5406","hebrew":"φονεύς","translit":"phoneús","english":"murderers"},{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexually immoral"},{"strongs":"G5332","hebrew":"φαρμακεύς","translit":"pharmakeús","english":"sorcerers"},{"strongs":"G1496","hebrew":"εἰδωλολάτρης","translit":"eidōlolátrēs","english":"idolaters"},{"strongs":"G5571","hebrew":"ψευδής","translit":"pseudḗs","english":"liars"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"part"},{"strongs":"G3041","hebrew":"λίμνη","translit":"límnē","english":"lake"},{"strongs":"G2545","hebrew":"καίω","translit":"kaíō","english":"burns"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G2303","hebrew":"θεῖον","translit":"theîon","english":"sulfur"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G2288","hebrew":"θάνατος","translit":"thánatos","english":"death"}]},{"v":9,"text":"One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls, who were loaded with the seven last plagues came, and he spoke with me, saying, “Come here. I will show you the wife, the Lamb’s bride.”","understanding":"Καὶ ἦλθεν εἷς ἐκ τῶν ἑπτὰ ἀγγέλων τῶν ἐχόντων τὰς ἑπτὰ φιάλας, “One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls... came” — the same kind of angel that showed John the prostitute (17:1): a deliberate contrast. Δεῦρο, δείξω σοι τὴν νύμφην τὴν γυναῖκα τοῦ ἀρνίου, “Come here. I will show you the wife, the Lamb’s bride” — νύμφη; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2033","hebrew":"ἑπτά","translit":"heptá","english":"seven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G5357","hebrew":"φιάλη","translit":"phiálē","english":"bowls"},{"strongs":"G1073","hebrew":"γέμω","translit":"gémō","english":"loaded"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wife"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb’s"},{"strongs":"G3565","hebrew":"νύμφη","translit":"nýmphē","english":"bride"}]},{"v":10,"text":"He carried me away in the Spirit to a great and high mountain, and showed me the holy city, Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God,","understanding":"καὶ ἀπήνεγκέν με ἐν πνεύματι ἐπὶ ὄρος μέγα καὶ ὑψηλόν, “He carried me away in the Spirit to a great and high mountain” — Babylon was seen from a wilderness (17:3); the bride from a mountain, like Ezekiel's vision of the new temple. καὶ ἔδειξέν μοι τὴν πόλιν τὴν ἁγίαν Ἰερουσαλὴμ καταβαίνουσαν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἀπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ, “and showed me the holy city, Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God” — ἅγιος, from the holy family. The bride is a city: God's people living together in His presence.","words":[{"strongs":"G667","hebrew":"ἀποφέρω","translit":"apophérō","english":"carried"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G5308","hebrew":"ὑψηλός","translit":"hypsēlós","english":"high"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"showed"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G2419","hebrew":"Ἱερουσαλήμ","translit":"Hierousalḗm","english":"Jerusalem"},{"strongs":"G2597","hebrew":"καταβαίνω","translit":"katabaínō","english":"coming down"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":11,"text":"having the glory of God. Her light was like a most precious stone, as if it were a jasper stone, clear as crystal;","understanding":"ἔχουσαν τὴν δόξαν τοῦ θεοῦ, “having the glory of God” — the glory that filled the tabernacle and the temple now fills the whole city. ὁ φωστὴρ αὐτῆς ὅμοιος λίθῳ τιμιωτάτῳ, ὡς λίθῳ ἰάσπιδι κρυσταλλίζοντι, “Her light was like a most precious stone, as if it were a jasper stone, clear as crystal” — φωστήρ, a radiance; ἴασπις: the One on the throne looked like jasper (4:3). The city shines with God's own appearance.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"having"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5458","hebrew":"φωστήρ","translit":"phōstḗr","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stone"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G2393","hebrew":"ἴασπις","translit":"íaspis","english":"jasper"},{"strongs":"G2929","hebrew":"κρυσταλλίζω","translit":"krystallízō","english":"clear as crystal"}]},{"v":12,"text":"having a great and high wall; having twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels; and names written on them, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel.","understanding":"ἔχουσα τεῖχος μέγα καὶ ὑψηλόν, “having a great and high wall” — τεῖχος: security; nothing unclean can enter (v. 27). ἔχουσα πυλῶνας δώδεκα, καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖς πυλῶσιν ἀγγέλους δώδεκα, “having twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels” — πυλών, a gateway. καὶ ὀνόματα ἐπιγεγραμμένα, ἅ ἐστιν τῶν δώδεκα φυλῶν υἱῶν Ἰσραήλ, “and names written on them, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel” — as in Ezekiel's city (Ezekiel 48:31–34). The people of God of the Old Testament are its gates.","words":[{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"having"},{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"wall"},{"strongs":"G3173","hebrew":"μέγας","translit":"mégas","english":"great"},{"strongs":"G5308","hebrew":"ὑψηλός","translit":"hypsēlós","english":"high"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G4440","hebrew":"πυλών","translit":"pylṓn","english":"gates"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G1924","hebrew":"ἐπιγράφω","translit":"epigráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G5443","hebrew":"φυλή","translit":"phylḗ","english":"tribes"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G2474","hebrew":"Ἰσραήλ","translit":"Israḗl","english":"Israel"}]},{"v":13,"text":"On the east were three gates; and on the north three gates; and on the south three gates; and on the west three gates.","understanding":"ἀπὸ ἀνατολῆς πυλῶνες τρεῖς καὶ ἀπὸ βορρᾶ πυλῶνες τρεῖς καὶ ἀπὸ νότου πυλῶνες τρεῖς καὶ ἀπὸ δυσμῶν πυλῶνες τρεῖς, “On the east were three gates; and on the north three gates; and on the south three gates; and on the west three gates” — πυλών. Gates on every side: people come from every direction of the earth (Luke 13:29).","words":[{"strongs":"G395","hebrew":"ἀνατολή","translit":"anatolḗ","english":"east"},{"strongs":"G5140","hebrew":"τρεῖς","translit":"treîs","english":"three"},{"strongs":"G4440","hebrew":"πυλών","translit":"pylṓn","english":"gates"},{"strongs":"G1005","hebrew":"βοῤῥᾶς","translit":"borrhâs","english":"north"},{"strongs":"G3558","hebrew":"νότος","translit":"nótos","english":"south"},{"strongs":"G1424","hebrew":"δυσμή","translit":"dysmḗ","english":"west"}]},{"v":14,"text":"The wall of the city had twelve foundations, and on them twelve names of the twelve Apostles of the Lamb.","understanding":"καὶ τὸ τεῖχος τῆς πόλεως ἔχων θεμελίους δώδεκα, “The wall of the city had twelve foundations” — θεμέλιος: Abraham ‘looked for the city which has the foundations’ (Hebrews 11:10). καὶ ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν δώδεκα ὀνόματα τῶν δώδεκα ἀποστόλων τοῦ ἀρνίου, “and on them twelve names of the twelve Apostles of the Lamb” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. Twelve tribes on the gates, twelve apostles on the foundations: one people of God, Old and New Testament together (Ephesians 2:20).","words":[{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"wall"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"had"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundations"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"names"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"Apostles"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":15,"text":"He who spoke with me had for a measure a golden reed to measure the city, its gates, and its walls.","understanding":"Καὶ ὁ λαλῶν μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ εἶχεν μέτρον κάλαμον χρυσοῦν, “He who spoke with me had for a measure a golden reed” — κάλαμος, as in 11:1. ἵνα μετρήσῃ τὴν πόλιν καὶ τοὺς πυλῶνας αὐτῆς καὶ τὸ τεῖχος αὐτῆς, “to measure the city, its gates, and its walls” — μετρέω: measuring marks it as God's, protected and perfect, as in Ezekiel 40.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G2192","hebrew":"ἔχω","translit":"échō","english":"had"},{"strongs":"G3358","hebrew":"μέτρον","translit":"métron","english":"measure"},{"strongs":"G2563","hebrew":"κάλαμος","translit":"kálamos","english":"reed"},{"strongs":"G5552","hebrew":"χρύσεος","translit":"chrýseos","english":"golden"},{"strongs":"G3354","hebrew":"μετρέω","translit":"metréō","english":"measure"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G4440","hebrew":"πυλών","translit":"pylṓn","english":"gates"},{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"walls"}]},{"v":16,"text":"The city is square, and its length is as great as its width. He measured the city with the reed, twelve thousand twelve stadia. Its length, width, and height are equal.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ πόλις τετράγωνος κεῖται, “The city is square” — τετράγωνος. καὶ ἐμέτρησεν τὴν πόλιν τῷ καλάμῳ ἐπὶ σταδίων δώδεκα χιλιάδων, “He measured the city with the reed, twelve thousand twelve stadia” — about 2,200 kilometres: the size of the known world from Spain to India. τὸ μῆκος καὶ τὸ πλάτος καὶ τὸ ὕψος αὐτῆς ἴσα ἐστίν, “Its length, width, and height are equal” — a perfect cube, like the Most Holy Place in Solomon's temple (1 Kings 6:20). The whole city is the Holy of Holies: God's people live in His immediate presence.","words":[{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G5068","hebrew":"τετράγωνος","translit":"tetrágōnos","english":"square"},{"strongs":"G2749","hebrew":"κεῖμαι","translit":"keîmai","english":"is"},{"strongs":"G3372","hebrew":"μῆκος","translit":"mēkos","english":"length"},{"strongs":"G4114","hebrew":"πλάτος","translit":"plátos","english":"width"},{"strongs":"G3354","hebrew":"μετρέω","translit":"metréō","english":"measured"},{"strongs":"G2563","hebrew":"κάλαμος","translit":"kálamos","english":"reed"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G5505","hebrew":"χιλιάς","translit":"chiliás","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G4712","hebrew":"στάδιον","translit":"stádion","english":"stadia"},{"strongs":"G5311","hebrew":"ὕψος","translit":"hýpsos","english":"height"},{"strongs":"G2470","hebrew":"ἴσος","translit":"ísos","english":"equal"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Its wall is one hundred forty-four cubits, by the measure of a man, that is, of an angel.","understanding":"καὶ ἐμέτρησεν τὸ τεῖχος αὐτῆς ἑκατὸν τεσσεράκοντα τεσσάρων πηχῶν, “Its wall is one hundred forty-four cubits” — 12 × 12: the number of God's people (7:4). μέτρον ἀνθρώπου, ὅ ἐστιν ἀγγέλου, “by the measure of a man, that is, of an angel” — ordinary human measurements, used by the angel: the city is real, though beyond imagining.","words":[{"strongs":"G3354","hebrew":"μετρέω","translit":"metréō","english":"measured"},{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"wall"},{"strongs":"G1540","hebrew":"ἑκατόν","translit":"hekatón","english":"one hundred"},{"strongs":"G5062","hebrew":"τεσσαράκοντα","translit":"tessarákonta","english":"forty"},{"strongs":"G5064","hebrew":"τέσσαρες","translit":"téssares","english":"four"},{"strongs":"G4083","hebrew":"πῆχυς","translit":"pēchys","english":"cubits"},{"strongs":"G3358","hebrew":"μέτρον","translit":"métron","english":"measure"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"}]},{"v":18,"text":"The construction of its wall was jasper. The city was pure gold, like pure glass.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ ἐνδώμησις τοῦ τείχους αὐτῆς ἴασπις, “The construction of its wall was jasper” — the stone of God's appearance (4:3). καὶ ἡ πόλις χρυσίον καθαρὸν ὅμοιον ὑάλῳ καθαρῷ, “The city was pure gold, like pure glass” — the inside of the Most Holy Place was overlaid with pure gold. Gold so pure it is transparent. What Babylon wore on the outside (17:4), the new Jerusalem is all the way through.","words":[{"strongs":"G1739","hebrew":"ἐνδόμησις","translit":"endómēsis","english":"construction"},{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"wall"},{"strongs":"G2393","hebrew":"ἴασπις","translit":"íaspis","english":"jasper"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G3664","hebrew":"ὅμοιος","translit":"hómoios","english":"like"},{"strongs":"G5194","hebrew":"ὕαλος","translit":"hýalos","english":"glass"}]},{"v":19,"text":"The foundations of the city’s wall were adorned with all kinds of precious stones. The first foundation was jasper; the second, sapphire; the third, chalcedony; the fourth, emerald;","understanding":"οἱ θεμέλιοι τοῦ τείχους τῆς πόλεως παντὶ λίθῳ τιμίῳ κεκοσμημένοι, “The foundations of the city’s wall were adorned with all kinds of precious stones” — κοσμέω, as the bride was adorned (v. 2). The twelve stones recall the twelve stones on the high priest's breastplate, each engraved with a tribe's name. ὁ θεμέλιος ὁ πρῶτος ἴασπις, ὁ δεύτερος σάπφειρος, ὁ τρίτος χαλκηδών, ὁ τέταρτος σμάραγδος, “The first foundation was jasper; the second, sapphire; the third, chalcedony; the fourth, emerald” — the whole people of God, carried on the heart of their great High Priest.","words":[{"strongs":"G2310","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","english":"foundations"},{"strongs":"G5038","hebrew":"τεῖχος","translit":"teîchos","english":"wall"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G2885","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","english":"adorned"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stones"},{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"},{"strongs":"G2393","hebrew":"ἴασπις","translit":"íaspis","english":"jasper"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G4552","hebrew":"σάπφειρος","translit":"sáppheiros","english":"sapphire"},{"strongs":"G5154","hebrew":"τρίτος","translit":"trítos","english":"third"},{"strongs":"G5472","hebrew":"χαλκηδών","translit":"chalkēdṓn","english":"chalcedony"},{"strongs":"G5067","hebrew":"τέταρτος","translit":"tétartos","english":"fourth"},{"strongs":"G4665","hebrew":"σμάραγδος","translit":"smáragdos","english":"emerald"}]},{"v":20,"text":"the fifth, sardonyx; the sixth, sardius; the seventh, chrysolite; the eighth, beryl; the ninth, topaz; the tenth, chrysoprasus; the eleventh, jacinth; and the twelfth, amethyst.","understanding":"ὁ πέμπτος σαρδόνυξ, ὁ ἕκτος σάρδιον, ὁ ἕβδομος χρυσόλιθος, ὁ ὄγδοος βήρυλλος, “the fifth, sardonyx; the sixth, sardius; the seventh, chrysolite; the eighth, beryl” — ὁ ἔνατος τοπάζιον, ὁ δέκατος χρυσόπρασος, ὁ ἑνδέκατος ὑάκινθος, ὁ δωδέκατος ἀμέθυστος, “the ninth, topaz; the tenth, chrysoprasus; the eleventh, jacinth; and the twelfth, amethyst” — the exact identity of some ancient stones is uncertain; the point is dazzling variety and beauty, every colour of light.","words":[{"strongs":"G3991","hebrew":"πέμπτος","translit":"pémptos","english":"fifth"},{"strongs":"G4557","hebrew":"σαρδόνυξ","translit":"sardónyx","english":"sardonyx"},{"strongs":"G1623","hebrew":"ἕκτος","translit":"héktos","english":"sixth"},{"strongs":"G4556","hebrew":"σάρδιος","translit":"sárdios","english":"sardius"},{"strongs":"G1442","hebrew":"ἕβδομος","translit":"hébdomos","english":"seventh"},{"strongs":"G5555","hebrew":"χρυσόλιθος","translit":"chrysólithos","english":"chrysolite"},{"strongs":"G3590","hebrew":"ὄγδοος","translit":"ógdoos","english":"eighth"},{"strongs":"G969","hebrew":"βήρυλλος","translit":"bḗryllos","english":"beryl"},{"strongs":"G1766","hebrew":"ἔννατος","translit":"énnatos","english":"ninth"},{"strongs":"G5116","hebrew":"τοπάζιον","translit":"topázion","english":"topaz"},{"strongs":"G1182","hebrew":"δέκατος","translit":"dékatos","english":"tenth"},{"strongs":"G5556","hebrew":"χρυσόπρασος","translit":"chrysóprasos","english":"chrysoprasus"},{"strongs":"G1734","hebrew":"ἑνδέκατος","translit":"hendékatos","english":"eleventh"},{"strongs":"G5192","hebrew":"ὑάκινθος","translit":"hyákinthos","english":"jacinth"},{"strongs":"G1428","hebrew":"δωδέκατος","translit":"dōdékatos","english":"twelfth"},{"strongs":"G271","hebrew":"ἀμέθυστος","translit":"améthystos","english":"amethyst"}]},{"v":21,"text":"The twelve gates were twelve pearls. Each one of the gates was made of one pearl. The street of the city was pure gold, like transparent glass.","understanding":"καὶ οἱ δώδεκα πυλῶνες δώδεκα μαργαρῖται, “The twelve gates were twelve pearls” — μαργαρίτης: a pearl is formed through the oyster's suffering; Jesus told of a merchant who sold all for one pearl of great price. The prostitute wore pearls (17:4); the bride is entered through them. ἀνὰ εἷς ἕκαστος τῶν πυλώνων ἦν ἐξ ἑνὸς μαργαρίτου, “Each one of the gates was made of one pearl” — καὶ ἡ πλατεῖα τῆς πόλεως χρυσίον καθαρὸν ὡς ὕαλος διαυγής, “The street of the city was pure gold, like transparent glass” — what people most prize on earth, God's people walk on.","words":[{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G4440","hebrew":"πυλών","translit":"pylṓn","english":"gates"},{"strongs":"G3135","hebrew":"μαργαρίτης","translit":"margarítēs","english":"pearls"},{"strongs":"G4113","hebrew":"πλατεῖα","translit":"plateîa","english":"street"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G2513","hebrew":"καθαρός","translit":"katharós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G5194","hebrew":"ὕαλος","translit":"hýalos","english":"glass"},{"strongs":"G1307","hebrew":"διαφανής","translit":"diaphanḗs","english":"transparent"}]},{"v":22,"text":"I saw no temple in it, for the Lord God, the Almighty, and the Lamb, are its temple.","understanding":"Καὶ ναὸν οὐκ εἶδον ἐν αὐτῇ, “I saw no temple in it” — εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family); ναός. Startling to a Jewish reader: the city of God without a temple. ὁ γὰρ κύριος ὁ θεὸς ὁ παντοκράτωρ ναὸς αὐτῆς ἐστιν, καὶ τὸ ἀρνίον, “for the Lord God, the Almighty, and the Lamb, are its temple” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. No building is needed when God is present everywhere and directly. Jesus spoke of His body as the temple (John 2:19–21).","words":[{"strongs":"G3485","hebrew":"ναός","translit":"naós","english":"temple"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3841","hebrew":"παντοκράτωρ","translit":"pantokrátōr","english":"Almighty"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":23,"text":"The city has no need for the sun, neither of the moon, to shine, for the very glory of God illuminated it, and its lamp is the Lamb.","understanding":"καὶ ἡ πόλις οὐ χρείαν ἔχει τοῦ ἡλίου οὐδὲ τῆς σελήνης ἵνα φαίνωσιν αὐτῇ, “The city has no need for the sun, neither of the moon, to shine” — φαίνω, from the appear family: to give light. Isaiah: ‘The sun will be no more your light by day... but the LORD will be your everlasting light.’ ἡ γὰρ δόξα τοῦ θεοῦ ἐφώτισεν αὐτήν, καὶ ὁ λύχνος αὐτῆς τὸ ἀρνίον, “for the very glory of God illuminated it, and its lamp is the Lamb” — λύχνος, lamp; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The light that went out in Babylon (18:23) shines forever here.","words":[{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G4582","hebrew":"σελήνη","translit":"selḗnē","english":"moon"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"shine"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"illuminated"},{"strongs":"G3088","hebrew":"λύχνος","translit":"lýchnos","english":"lamp"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":24,"text":"The nations will walk in its light. The kings of the earth bring the glory and honor of the nations into it.","understanding":"καὶ περιπατήσουσιν τὰ ἔθνη διὰ τοῦ φωτὸς αὐτῆς, “The nations will walk in its light” — Isaiah 60: ‘Nations will come to your light, and kings to the brightness of your rising.’ καὶ οἱ βασιλεῖς τῆς γῆς φέρουσιν τὴν δόξαν αὐτῶν εἰς αὐτήν, “The kings of the earth bring the glory and honor of the nations into it” — γῆ, from the world family. The kings who once committed immorality with Babylon (17:2) now bring their glory to God's city. All that is good in the world's cultures is purified and brought into the new creation.","words":[{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"kings"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"bring"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"}]},{"v":25,"text":"Its gates will in no way be shut by day (for there will be no night there),","understanding":"καὶ οἱ πυλῶνες αὐτῆς οὐ μὴ κλεισθῶσιν ἡμέρας, “Its gates will in no way be shut by day” — πυλών; κλείω; ἡμέρα, from the time family. City gates were shut at night for safety; here there is no danger. νὺξ γὰρ οὐκ ἔσται ἐκεῖ, “(for there will be no night there)” — no darkness, no fear, no evil. The open gates proclaim welcome.","words":[{"strongs":"G4440","hebrew":"πυλών","translit":"pylṓn","english":"gates"},{"strongs":"G2808","hebrew":"κλείω","translit":"kleíō","english":"shut"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"}]},{"v":26,"text":"and they shall bring the glory and the honor of the nations into it so that they may enter.","understanding":"καὶ οἴσουσιν τὴν δόξαν καὶ τὴν τιμὴν τῶν ἐθνῶν εἰς αὐτήν, “and they shall bring the glory and the honor of the nations into it” — φέρω: the riches of every culture — art, music, learning, beauty — redeemed and offered to God. Nothing good is lost.","words":[{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"bring"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"}]},{"v":27,"text":"There will in no way enter into it anything profane, or one who causes an abomination or a lie, but only those who are written in the Lamb’s book of life.","understanding":"καὶ οὐ μὴ εἰσέλθῃ εἰς αὐτὴν πᾶν κοινὸν καὶ ὁ ποιῶν βδέλυγμα καὶ ψεῦδος, “There will in no way enter into it anything profane, or one who causes an abomination or a lie” — κοινός, common, unclean; βδέλυγμα, the abominations of Babylon's cup (17:4); ψεῦδος, the lie of the beast. εἰ μὴ οἱ γεγραμμένοι ἐν τῷ βιβλίῳ τῆς ζωῆς τοῦ ἀρνίου, “but only those who are written in the Lamb’s book of life” — βιβλίον, from the book family; ζωή, from the life family; ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. The gates are always open, but only those who belong to the Lamb will enter.","words":[{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G2839","hebrew":"κοινός","translit":"koinós","english":"profane"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"causes"},{"strongs":"G946","hebrew":"βδέλυγμα","translit":"bdélygma","english":"abomination"},{"strongs":"G5579","hebrew":"ψεῦδος","translit":"pseûdos","english":"lie"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb’s"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"All Things New","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture the old world — scarred by war, sickness, grief and death — dissolving like a morning mist. In its place, the same world made new: no sea to separate, no hospital, no cemetery. And from heaven a city descends, shining like a bride walking down the aisle toward her husband.","meaning":"“I saw a new heaven and a new earth” (v. 1). “Behold, God’s dwelling is with people, and he will dwell with them” (v. 3). “He who sits on the throne said, ‘Behold, I am making all things new’” (v. 5). The end of the story is not escape from the world but its renewal, with God at home among His people."},{"title":"Every Tear","verseRef":"v. 4","image":"Picture every grief you have ever carried: every funeral, every betrayal, every diagnosis, every tear shed alone at night. Then picture God Himself kneeling before you, taking your face in His hands, and gently wiping each tear away — for the last time.","meaning":"“He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away” (v. 4)."},{"title":"The City of Gold and Glass","verseRef":"v. 10-21","image":"Picture a city like a vast cube of light, its walls of jasper, its foundations glittering with twelve kinds of jewel, its streets of gold so pure you can see through them, its twelve gates each carved from a single pearl — and the names of Israel's tribes and the Lamb's apostles written on gates and foundations.","meaning":"“He... showed me the holy city, Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, having the glory of God” (vv. 10–11). Everything Babylon wore as a costume, the new Jerusalem is in reality. God's people, Old and New, are built together into a dwelling for Him."},{"title":"No Temple, No Sun","verseRef":"v. 22-25","image":"Picture a city without a single church building, yet everyone in it worshipping; without sun or moon, yet blazing with light; its gates standing open day and night because there is no night and nothing to fear — and the nations streaming in with their treasures.","meaning":"“I saw no temple in it, for the Lord God, the Almighty, and the Lamb, are its temple” (v. 22). “For the very glory of God illuminated it, and its lamp is the Lamb” (v. 23). Heaven is not a place with God in it; it is God's presence filling everything."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The New Creation (v. 1-8)","body":"John sees a new heaven and a new earth, the first having passed away and the sea gone; he sees the holy city, the new Jerusalem, coming down from God like a bride; a voice announces that God's dwelling is with people; He will wipe away every tear, and death, mourning, crying and pain will be gone; God declares, ‘I am making all things new’, promises the water of life freely to the thirsty and the inheritance to the overcomer, and warns that the cowardly, unbelieving and wicked will have their part in the lake of fire."},{"label":"The Bride, the City (v. 9-21)","body":"One of the bowl angels shows John the bride of the Lamb: the holy city Jerusalem coming down from heaven, shining with God's glory; it has a great high wall with twelve gates named for the twelve tribes and twelve foundations named for the twelve apostles; the angel measures it with a golden rod — a perfect cube, about 12,000 stadia each way; its wall is jasper, the city pure gold like glass, the foundations adorned with twelve precious stones, the gates twelve pearls, the street pure gold."},{"label":"God's Presence Is Everything (v. 22-27)","body":"There is no temple in the city, for God and the Lamb are its temple; it needs no sun or moon, for God's glory is its light and the Lamb its lamp; the nations walk by its light, and kings bring their glory into it; its gates are never shut, for there is no night; nothing unclean enters, but only those written in the Lamb's book of life."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A New Heaven and a New Earth","paragraphs":["After the last judgement, John sees the goal of all history: “I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth have passed away, and the sea is no more” (v. 1). The word for ‘new’ is καινός, new in kind and quality. God does not throw away His creation and start again; He renews it, as He renews a person in Christ: “Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. Behold, all things have become new” (2 Corinthians 5:17). Paul says creation itself “will be delivered from the bondage of decay into the liberty of the glory of the children of God” (Romans 8:21).","‘The sea is no more.’ In Revelation the sea is where the beast arose (13:1), the place of chaos and danger. For John on Patmos, it was also what separated him from the churches he loved. In the new creation nothing threatening, and nothing that separates, remains.","And at the centre is the great promise of the whole Bible: “Behold, God’s dwelling is with people, and he will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God” (v. 3). From the garden of Eden, through the tabernacle and temple, to the Word made flesh who ‘tabernacled’ among us, God has been moving toward this: living with His people, face to face, forever. Peter wrote, “we look for new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells” (2 Peter 3:13)."]},{"heading":"No More Tears","paragraphs":["“He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away” (v. 4). These are some of the most loved words in the Bible, and rightly so. Everything that has made life in this world painful — death, grief, cries of anguish, suffering — will be gone forever. And it is God Himself who wipes the tears away.","Then God speaks from the throne: “Behold, I am making all things new” (v. 5). It is not ‘all new things’ but ‘all things new’. And He adds, “I have become the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End. I will give freely to him who is thirsty from the spring of the water of life” (v. 6) — many manuscripts begin, ‘It is done!’ The God who began everything finishes everything; and the invitation is still open and still free. Jesus cried, “If anyone is thirsty, let him come to me and drink!” (John 7:37).","The promise to the overcomer sums up all seven letters: “He who overcomes, I will give him these things. I will be his God, and he will be my son” (v. 7). But there is also a warning, addressed above all to the churches: the cowardly who deny Christ under pressure, the unbelieving, the abominable, murderers, the immoral, sorcerers, idolaters and liars will have their part in the lake of fire (v. 8). The list describes those who persist in these things without repentance. Paul gives a similar list, and then adds the gospel: “Such were some of you, but you were washed. But you were sanctified. But you were justified in the name of the Lord Jesus” (1 Corinthians 6:11)."]},{"heading":"The Bride, the Lamb's Wife","paragraphs":["One of the bowl angels — the same kind who showed John the prostitute (17:1) — now says, “Come here. I will show you the wife, the Lamb’s bride” (v. 9). The contrast is deliberate. Babylon was seen from a wilderness; the bride from a great high mountain. Babylon sat on the beast; the bride comes down from God. Babylon wore gold, jewels and pearls as a costume; the new Jerusalem is built of them.","The city has “a great and high wall; having twelve gates... and names written on them, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel” (v. 12), and “twelve foundations, and on them twelve names of the twelve Apostles of the Lamb” (v. 14). The people of God of both Testaments are built into one city. Paul wrote that believers are “being built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the chief cornerstone; in whom the whole building, fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord” (Ephesians 2:20–21).","The angel measures the city: it is a perfect cube, about 12,000 stadia in each direction (v. 16) — roughly 2,200 kilometres, the size of the known world. The only perfect cube in the Old Testament was the Most Holy Place, where God's presence dwelt. The whole city is now the Holy of Holies: God's people live where only the high priest once entered, once a year. The numbers are all twelves and multiples of twelve — the number of God's people — and the jewels of the foundations recall the twelve stones on the high priest's breastplate."]},{"heading":"God and the Lamb Are Its Temple","paragraphs":["Then the most surprising feature: “I saw no temple in it, for the Lord God, the Almighty, and the Lamb, are its temple” (v. 22). The temple was always a sign of God's presence and a barrier to it — a place where only some could go, only at certain times. In the new Jerusalem the sign is replaced by the reality. God's presence fills everything, directly and openly.","“The city has no need for the sun, neither of the moon, to shine, for the very glory of God illuminated it, and its lamp is the Lamb” (v. 23). The light that went out in Babylon (18:23) shines forever here. And “The nations will walk in its light. The kings of the earth bring the glory and honor of the nations into it” (v. 24). The kings who once committed immorality with Babylon now bring their treasures to God. Whatever is good in human culture — beauty, skill, wisdom, art — will be purified and brought in.","“Its gates will in no way be shut by day (for there will be no night there)” (v. 25). The open gates speak of perfect safety and endless welcome. Yet “There will in no way enter into it anything profane, or one who causes an abomination or a lie, but only those who are written in the Lamb’s book of life” (v. 27). The door is wide open; the only way in is to belong to the Lamb. Abraham “looked for the city which has the foundations, whose builder and maker is God” (Hebrews 11:10). This is that city."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The City God Has Prepared","paragraphs":["Hebrews says of the faithful who died without receiving the promises: “But now they desire a better country, that is, a heavenly one. Therefore God is not ashamed of them, to be called their God, for he has prepared a city for them” (Hebrews 11:16). Paul calls it “the Jerusalem that is above is free, which is the mother of us all” (Galatians 4:26).","Jesus promised, “He who believes in me, as the Scripture has said, from within him will flow rivers of living water” (John 7:38) — a foretaste now of the spring of the water of life given freely in the new creation (v. 6).","And Paul describes the church as God's dwelling under construction: “in whom you also are built together for a habitation of God in the Spirit” (Ephesians 2:22). The new Jerusalem is that building complete."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"New","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","strongs":"G2537 (End τέλος, télos, G5056)","verses":"v. 1, 2, 5; 2:17; 3:12; 5:9; 14:3","insight":"Strong's: “new (especially in freshness; while G3501 (νέος) is properly so with respect to age.” καινός, from the new family, means new in kind or quality — fresh, renewed — while νέος means new in time, young, recent. Revelation always uses καινός: a new name, a new song, the new Jerusalem, a new heaven and a new earth, ‘all things new’. God does not discard His creation; He makes it new in quality, as He makes a sinner a new creation in Christ."},{"english":"Dwelling / tabernacle","hebrew":"σκηνή","translit":"skēnḗ","strongs":"G4633 (dwell σκηνόω, skēnóō, G4637)","verses":"v. 3; 13:6; 15:5","insight":"Strong's: “a tent or cloth hut (literally or figuratively).” σκηνή is a tent — the word used for the tabernacle in the wilderness, where God's presence dwelt among Israel. Its verb σκηνόω is used when ‘the Word became flesh, and lived (tabernacled) among us’ (John 1:14). Revelation 21:3 is the climax of this whole story: ‘God’s dwelling (σκηνή) is with people, and he will dwell (σκηνόω) with them.’ The tent of meeting becomes the eternal home."},{"english":"Foundations","hebrew":"θεμέλιος","translit":"themélios","strongs":"G2310 (Apostles ἀπόστολος, apóstolos, G652)","verses":"v. 14, 19","insight":"Strong's: “something put down, i.e. a substruction (of a building, etc.), (literally or figuratively).” θεμέλιος is the foundation that everything else rests on. The new Jerusalem has twelve foundations bearing the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb, each adorned with a precious stone. Paul says the church is built ‘on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Christ Jesus himself being the chief cornerstone’, and that ‘no one can lay any other foundation than that which has been laid, which is Jesus Christ’."},{"english":"Freely","hebrew":"δωρεάν","translit":"dōreán","strongs":"G1432 (spring πηγή, pēgḗ, G4077)","verses":"v. 6; 22:17","insight":"Strong's: “gratuitously (literally or figuratively).” δωρεάν, from δωρεά (a gift), means ‘as a gift, without payment’. Paul uses it of justification: ‘being justified freely by his grace’ (Romans 3:24). The water of life is given δωρεάν (21:6), and the book's last invitation repeats it: ‘He who desires, let him take the water of life freely’ (22:17). Babylon sold everything, even human souls; God gives the best thing of all for nothing."},{"english":"Profane","hebrew":"κοινός","translit":"koinós","strongs":"G2839 (abomination βδέλυγμα, bdélygma, G946)","verses":"v. 27","insight":"Strong's: “common, i.e. (literally) shared by all or several, or (ceremonially) profane.” κοινός means ‘common’ — shared by all, as in the early church holding all things ‘in common’ — but also ritually unclean, unfit for God's presence. Peter was told, ‘What God has cleansed, you must not call unclean (κοινός).’ Nothing unclean enters the new Jerusalem; but the Lamb has cleansed all whose names are in His book."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit shows you your true home and your future.","items":[{"title":"Your Tears Will End","body":"“He will wipe away every tear from their eyes” (v. 4). The Spirit wants you to carry this promise into every grief. Your sorrow is real, but it is temporary. God Himself will wipe your eyes, and death and pain will be gone forever."},{"title":"God Wants to Live with You","body":"“Behold, God’s dwelling is with people” (v. 3). The whole Bible has been moving toward this: God at home with His people. The Spirit invites you to begin living in that presence now — to know God not as a distant ruler, but as the One who dwells with you."},{"title":"Come and Drink Freely","body":"“I will give freely to him who is thirsty from the spring of the water of life” (v. 6). The Spirit offers you, today, the water of life — free. Whatever you are thirsty for, come to Jesus and drink."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Hope Check","body":"Do you think of your future mainly as ‘going to heaven’ or as living with God in a renewed creation (vv. 1–3)? How does that change how you treat the world now?"},{"title":"The Thirst Check","body":"What are you thirsty for? Are you trying to satisfy it in Babylon, or at the spring of the water of life (v. 6)?"},{"title":"The Book Check","body":"Is your name written in the Lamb's book of life (v. 27)? How do you know?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write a Letter from the New Creation","body":"Write a short letter to yourself as if from the new Jerusalem, describing what is gone (tears, pain, death) and what is there (God, the Lamb, the nations, light). Read it on a hard day this week."},{"title":"Bring Your Glory","body":"The kings bring their glory into the city (v. 24). Choose one gift or skill you have — music, cooking, craft, learning, hospitality — and use it this week as an offering of worship to God and a blessing to others."}]},"prayerReflection":"“God on the throne, thank You that You are making all things new. Thank You for the promise of a new heaven and a new earth, where the sea of chaos is no more. Thank You for the holy city coming down from heaven like a bride. Thank You that Your dwelling will be with Your people, and that You will be our God and we Your people. Lord, wipe away every tear. I long for the day when death, mourning, crying and pain are gone forever. You are the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End; You finish what You start. I am thirsty: give me the water of life freely. Help me overcome, and let me be Your child forever. Thank You that there will be no temple, because You and the Lamb are its temple, and no sun, because Your glory is its light. Thank You that my name is written in the Lamb's book of life. Bring the nations into Your light. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Abraham Looking for a City","ref":"Genesis 12:1-9; Hebrews 11:8-16","relatedVerses":"v. 2, 14","summary":"God called Abraham to leave his home in Ur and go to a land he did not know. He lived in tents in the land of promise, as a stranger, with Isaac and Jacob. Hebrews explains why: “For he looked for the city which has the foundations, whose builder and maker is God” (Hebrews 11:10). Abraham never saw that city in his lifetime. Revelation 21 shows it at last: the city with foundations, built by God, where Abraham and all his true children will live."},{"title":"Solomon's Most Holy Place","ref":"1 Kings 6:19-22","relatedVerses":"v. 16, 18","summary":"In the temple Solomon built, the inner sanctuary — the Most Holy Place, where the ark of the covenant stood — was a perfect cube, twenty cubits long, wide and high, and entirely overlaid with pure gold. Only the high priest could enter it, once a year, with blood. The new Jerusalem is a perfect cube of pure gold: the whole city is the Most Holy Place, and all God's people live there in His presence, forever."},{"title":"Ezekiel's City: ‘The LORD Is There’","ref":"Ezekiel 40–48","relatedVerses":"v. 10-16","summary":"In exile, Ezekiel was taken in a vision to a very high mountain and shown a new temple and city, measured by a man with a measuring reed. A river flowed from the temple, giving life wherever it went. The city had twelve gates named for the twelve tribes of Israel, and its name was ‘The LORD Is There’. Revelation 21–22 fulfils Ezekiel's vision — the high mountain, the measuring reed, the twelve gates, the river of life — and its name is still true: the Lord is there."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"Revelation","chapter":22,"chapterTitle":"The River of Life and the Final Invitation","themeTitle":"“Yes, I Come Quickly.” Amen! Yes, Come, Lord Jesus","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"He showed me a river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding out of the throne of God and of the Lamb,","understanding":"Καὶ ἔδειξέν μοι ποταμὸν ὕδατος ζωῆς λαμπρὸν ὡς κρύσταλλον, “He showed me a river of water of life, clear as crystal” — ζωή, from the life family: the life of God Himself. Eden had a river that watered the garden; Ezekiel saw a river flowing from the temple, giving life wherever it went. ἐκπορευόμενον ἐκ τοῦ θρόνου τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τοῦ ἀρνίου, “proceeding out of the throne of God and of the Lamb” — one throne, shared by God and the Lamb (ἀρνίον, from the lamb family). Life flows from God's rule. Jesus spoke of living water — the Holy Spirit (John 7:38–39).","words":[{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"showed"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"river"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2986","hebrew":"λαμπρός","translit":"lamprós","english":"clear"},{"strongs":"G2930","hebrew":"κρύσταλλος","translit":"krýstallos","english":"crystal"},{"strongs":"G1607","hebrew":"ἐκπορεύομαι","translit":"ekporeúomai","english":"proceeding"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"}]},{"v":2,"text":"in the middle of its street. On this side of the river and on that was the tree of life, bearing twelve kinds of fruits, yielding its fruit every month. The leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.","understanding":"ἐν μέσῳ τῆς πλατείας αὐτῆς, “in the middle of its street” — the river runs down the city's main street. καὶ τοῦ ποταμοῦ ἐντεῦθεν καὶ ἐκεῖθεν ξύλον ζωῆς, “On this side of the river and on that was the tree of life” — ξύλον; ζωή, from the life family: the tree that was barred after the fall (Genesis 3:24), promised to the overcomer (2:7), now open to all. ποιοῦν καρποὺς δώδεκα, κατὰ μῆνα ἕκαστον ἀποδιδοῦν τὸν καρπὸν αὐτοῦ, “bearing twelve kinds of fruits, yielding its fruit every month” — μήν, from the time family: endless abundance, no barren season. καὶ τὰ φύλλα τοῦ ξύλου εἰς θεραπείαν τῶν ἐθνῶν, “The leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations” — θεραπεία, healing (our ‘therapy’): the wounds of history between nations healed.","words":[{"strongs":"G3319","hebrew":"μέσος","translit":"mésos","english":"middle"},{"strongs":"G4113","hebrew":"πλατεῖα","translit":"plateîa","english":"street"},{"strongs":"G4215","hebrew":"ποταμός","translit":"potamós","english":"river"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"tree"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1427","hebrew":"δώδεκα","translit":"dṓdeka","english":"twelve"},{"strongs":"G2590","hebrew":"καρπός","translit":"karpós","english":"fruits"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"yielding"},{"strongs":"G3376","hebrew":"μήν","translit":"mḗn","english":"month"},{"strongs":"G5444","hebrew":"φύλλον","translit":"phýllon","english":"leaves"},{"strongs":"G2322","hebrew":"θεραπεία","translit":"therapeía","english":"healing"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"}]},{"v":3,"text":"There will be no curse any more. The throne of God and of the Lamb will be in it, and his servants will serve him.","understanding":"καὶ πᾶν κατάθεμα οὐκ ἔσται ἔτι, “There will be no curse any more” — κατάθεμα: the curse pronounced in Eden on the ground and on human life (Genesis 3:17) is lifted forever; Zechariah promised ‘there will be no more curse’ (Zechariah 14:11). καὶ ὁ θρόνος τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ τοῦ ἀρνίου ἐν αὐτῇ ἔσται, “The throne of God and of the Lamb will be in it” — ἀρνίον, from the lamb family. καὶ οἱ δοῦλοι αὐτοῦ λατρεύσουσιν αὐτῷ, “and his servants will serve him” — δοῦλος and λατρεύω, two members of the servant family: δοῦλος, a bondservant who belongs to his master; λατρεύω, priestly service in worship. God's slaves are His priests (7:15).","words":[{"strongs":"G2652","hebrew":"κατανάθεμα","translit":"katanáthema","english":"curse"},{"strongs":"G2362","hebrew":"θρόνος","translit":"thrónos","english":"throne"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G721","hebrew":"ἀρνίον","translit":"arníon","english":"Lamb"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servants"},{"strongs":"G3000","hebrew":"λατρεύω","translit":"latreúō","english":"serve"}]},{"v":4,"text":"They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads.","understanding":"καὶ ὄψονται τὸ πρόσωπον αὐτοῦ, “They will see his face” — ὁράω, from the see family. The deepest longing of the Bible: Moses was told no one could see God's face and live; the psalmist prayed to see it; Jesus promised the pure in heart ‘shall see God’. Now it is granted. καὶ τὸ ὄνομα αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τῶν μετώπων αὐτῶν, “and his name will be on their foreheads” — the seal of 7:3 and 14:1: they belong to Him wholly, like the high priest who wore ‘HOLY TO THE LORD’ on his forehead.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G3359","hebrew":"μέτωπον","translit":"métōpon","english":"foreheads"}]},{"v":5,"text":"There will be no night, and they need no lamp light; for the Lord God will illuminate them. They will reign forever and ever.","understanding":"καὶ νὺξ οὐκ ἔσται ἔτι, “There will be no night” — and therefore no fear and no darkness. καὶ οὐκ ἔχουσιν χρείαν φωτὸς λύχνου καὶ φωτὸς ἡλίου, ὅτι κύριος ὁ θεὸς φωτίσει ἐπ᾽ αὐτούς, “and they need no lamp light; for the Lord God will illuminate them” — φωτίζω: God Himself is their light. καὶ βασιλεύσουσιν εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “They will reign forever and ever” — βασιλεύω; αἰών, from the time family and the world family. The book that began with a persecuted church ends with God's servants reigning forever.","words":[{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G5532","hebrew":"χρεία","translit":"chreía","english":"need"},{"strongs":"G3088","hebrew":"λύχνος","translit":"lýchnos","english":"lamp"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"},{"strongs":"G2246","hebrew":"ἥλιος","translit":"hḗlios","english":"sun"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5461","hebrew":"φωτίζω","translit":"phōtízō","english":"illuminate"},{"strongs":"G936","hebrew":"βασιλεύω","translit":"basileúō","english":"reign"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":6,"text":"He said to me, “These words are faithful and true. The Lord God of the spirits of the prophets sent his angel to show to his bondservants the things which must happen soon.”","understanding":"Καὶ εἶπέν μοι· Οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι πιστοὶ καὶ ἀληθινοί, “He said to me, ‘These words are faithful and true’” — λόγος, from the word family: the epilogue (vv. 6–21) begins by affirming that everything in the book is trustworthy (21:5). καὶ ὁ κύριος ὁ θεὸς τῶν πνευμάτων τῶν προφητῶν ἀπέστειλεν τὸν ἄγγελον αὐτοῦ, “The Lord God of the spirits of the prophets sent his angel” — δεῖξαι τοῖς δούλοις αὐτοῦ ἃ δεῖ γενέσθαι ἐν τάχει, “to show to his bondservants the things which must happen soon” — δοῦλος, from the servant family; the words of 1:1, bringing the book full circle.","words":[{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G228","hebrew":"ἀληθινός","translit":"alēthinós","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G649","hebrew":"ἀποστέλλω","translit":"apostéllō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"show"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"},{"strongs":"G1163","hebrew":"δεῖ","translit":"deî","english":"must"},{"strongs":"G5034","hebrew":"τάχος","translit":"táchos","english":"soon"}]},{"v":7,"text":"“Behold, I come quickly. Blessed is he who keeps the words of the prophecy of this book.”","understanding":"καὶ ἰδοὺ ἔρχομαι ταχύ, “Behold, I come quickly” — ταχύ, soon, without delay, suddenly. The first of three ‘I come quickly’ in this chapter (vv. 7, 12, 20). Two thousand years later, ‘quickly’ reminds us that His coming is always near and always sudden, and that ‘one day is with the Lord as a thousand years’ (2 Peter 3:8). μακάριος ὁ τηρῶν τοὺς λόγους τῆς προφητείας τοῦ βιβλίου τούτου, “Blessed is he who keeps the words of the prophecy of this book” — the sixth blessing, echoing the first (1:3); τηρέω, from the hold family; λόγος, from the word family; βιβλίον, from the book family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"quickly"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keeps"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Now I, John, am the one who heard and saw these things. When I heard and saw, I fell down to worship before the feet of the angel who had shown me these things.","understanding":"Κἀγὼ Ἰωάννης ὁ ἀκούων καὶ βλέπων ταῦτα, “Now I, John, am the one who heard and saw these things” — βλέπω, from the see family: the eyewitness signature, like 1 John 1:1. καὶ ὅτε ἤκουσα καὶ ἔβλεψα, ἔπεσα προσκυνῆσαι ἔμπροσθεν τῶν ποδῶν τοῦ ἀγγέλου τοῦ δεικνύοντός μοι ταῦτα, “When I heard and saw, I fell down to worship before the feet of the angel who had shown me these things” — βλέπω again; the same mistake as 19:10. Even after all he has seen, John is tempted to worship the messenger rather than God.","words":[{"strongs":"G2491","hebrew":"Ἰωάννης","translit":"Iōánnēs","english":"John"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G991","hebrew":"βλέπω","translit":"blépō","english":"saw"},{"strongs":"G4098","hebrew":"πίπτω","translit":"píptō","english":"fell down"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"worship"},{"strongs":"G4228","hebrew":"πούς","translit":"poús","english":"feet"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G1166","hebrew":"δεικνύω","translit":"deiknýō","english":"shown"}]},{"v":9,"text":"He said to me, “See you don’t do it! I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers, the prophets, and with those who keep the words of this book. Worship God.”","understanding":"καὶ λέγει μοι· Ὅρα μή, “He said to me, ‘See you don’t do it!’” — ὁράω, from the see family. σύνδουλός σού εἰμι καὶ τῶν ἀδελφῶν σου τῶν προφητῶν καὶ τῶν τηρούντων τοὺς λόγους τοῦ βιβλίου τούτου, “I am a fellow bondservant with you and with your brothers, the prophets, and with those who keep the words of this book” — τηρέω, from the hold family; λόγος, from the word family; βιβλίον, from the book family. τῷ θεῷ προσκύνησον, “Worship God” — the message of the whole book in two words, repeated.","words":[{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"See"},{"strongs":"G4889","hebrew":"σύνδουλος","translit":"sýndoulos","english":"fellow bondservant"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4352","hebrew":"προσκυνέω","translit":"proskynéō","english":"Worship"}]},{"v":10,"text":"He said to me, “Don’t seal up the words of the prophecy of this book, for the time is at hand.","understanding":"Καὶ λέγει μοι· Μὴ σφραγίσῃς τοὺς λόγους τῆς προφητείας τοῦ βιβλίου τούτου, “He said to me, ‘Don’t seal up the words of the prophecy of this book’” — σφραγίζω; λόγος, from the word family; βιβλίον, from the book family. Daniel was told to seal his book until the time of the end (Daniel 12:4); John is told the opposite. The book is open, meant for the churches now. ὁ καιρὸς γὰρ ἐγγύς ἐστιν, “for the time is at hand” — καιρός, from the time family: the decisive season, as in 1:3.","words":[{"strongs":"G4972","hebrew":"σφραγίζω","translit":"sphragízō","english":"seal up"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G1451","hebrew":"ἐγγύς","translit":"engýs","english":"at hand"}]},{"v":11,"text":"He who acts unjustly, let him act unjustly still. He who is filthy, let him be filthy still. He who is righteous, let him do righteousness still. He who is holy, let him be holy still.”","understanding":"ὁ ἀδικῶν ἀδικησάτω ἔτι, καὶ ὁ ῥυπαρὸς ῥυπανθήτω ἔτι, “He who acts unjustly, let him act unjustly still. He who is filthy, let him be filthy still” — not an encouragement to sin, but a solemn warning: the time is short; character is becoming fixed. καὶ ὁ δίκαιος δικαιοσύνην ποιησάτω ἔτι, καὶ ὁ ἅγιος ἁγιασθήτω ἔτι, “He who is righteous, let him do righteousness still. He who is holy, let him be holy still” — ἅγιος and ἁγιάζω, two members of the holy family: holy in status, and being made holy in practice. As Daniel heard, ‘the wicked will do wickedly... but those who are wise will understand.’ Keep going in the way you are going — if it is the right way.","words":[{"strongs":"G91","hebrew":"ἀδικέω","translit":"adikéō","english":"acts unjustly"},{"strongs":"G4508","hebrew":"ῥυπαρός","translit":"rhyparós","english":"filthy"},{"strongs":"G4510","hebrew":"ῥυπόω","translit":"rhypóō","english":"be filthy"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"be holy"}]},{"v":12,"text":"“Behold, I come quickly. My reward is with me, to repay to each man according to his work.","understanding":"Ἰδοὺ ἔρχομαι ταχύ, “Behold, I come quickly” — ταχύ, the second time. καὶ ὁ μισθός μου μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ, “My reward is with me” — μισθός, wages: Isaiah's promise, ‘Behold, his reward is with him.’ ἀποδοῦναι ἑκάστῳ ὡς τὸ ἔργον ἐστὶν αὐτοῦ, “to repay to each man according to his work” — ἔργον, from the work family, singular: a person's whole life's work, the fruit of faith or unbelief (20:12).","words":[{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"quickly"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"reward"},{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"repay"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End.","understanding":"ἐγὼ τὸ Ἄλφα καὶ τὸ Ὦ, ὁ πρῶτος καὶ ὁ ἔσχατος, ἡ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ τέλος, “I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End” — Jesus now claims all three titles that God used of Himself (1:8; 21:6; Isaiah 44:6). ἀρχή, from the power family: the source and ruler; τέλος, from the perfect family: the goal and completion. The clearest statement in the book that Jesus is God.","words":[{"strongs":"G1","hebrew":"Α","translit":"A","english":"Alpha"},{"strongs":"G5598","hebrew":"Ω","translit":"Ō","english":"Omega"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"First"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"Last"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"Beginning"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"End"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Blessed are those who do his commandments, that they may have the right to the tree of life, and may enter in by the gates into the city.","understanding":"Μακάριοι οἱ ποιοῦντες τὰς ἐντολὰς αὐτοῦ, “Blessed are those who do his commandments” — the seventh and last blessing. The WEB follows manuscripts reading ‘do his commandments’; the oldest read πλύνοντες τὰς στολὰς αὐτῶν, ‘wash their robes’ (7:14) — in Greek the two phrases look and sound very alike. ἵνα ἔσται ἡ ἐξουσία αὐτῶν ἐπὶ τὸ ξύλον τῆς ζωῆς, “that they may have the right to the tree of life” — ἐξουσία, from the power family: the authority, the right of access; ζωή, from the life family. καὶ τοῖς πυλῶσιν εἰσέλθωσιν εἰς τὴν πόλιν, “and may enter in by the gates into the city” — the pearl gates (21:21), always open.","words":[{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandments"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"tree"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1525","hebrew":"εἰσέρχομαι","translit":"eisérchomai","english":"enter"},{"strongs":"G4440","hebrew":"πυλών","translit":"pylṓn","english":"gates"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Outside are the dogs, the sorcerers, the sexually immoral, the murderers, the idolaters, and everyone who loves and practices falsehood.","understanding":"ἔξω οἱ κύνες, “Outside are the dogs” — κύων: a term of contempt for the shamelessly impure (Philippians 3:2). καὶ οἱ φάρμακοι καὶ οἱ πόρνοι καὶ οἱ φονεῖς καὶ οἱ εἰδωλολάτραι, “the sorcerers, the sexually immoral, the murderers, the idolaters” — the list of 21:8. καὶ πᾶς φιλῶν καὶ ποιῶν ψεῦδος, “and everyone who loves and practices falsehood” — φιλέω, from the love family: to be fond of, to have a liking for, as distinct from ἀγαπάω, the love of commitment. Those who are fond of the lie — who love the beast's world — stay outside the city of truth. Even now, the gates are open to any who will wash their robes.","words":[{"strongs":"G1854","hebrew":"ἔξω","translit":"éxō","english":"Outside"},{"strongs":"G2965","hebrew":"κύων","translit":"kýōn","english":"dogs"},{"strongs":"G5333","hebrew":"φάρμακος","translit":"phármakos","english":"sorcerers"},{"strongs":"G4205","hebrew":"πόρνος","translit":"pórnos","english":"sexually immoral"},{"strongs":"G5406","hebrew":"φονεύς","translit":"phoneús","english":"murderers"},{"strongs":"G1496","hebrew":"εἰδωλολάτρης","translit":"eidōlolátrēs","english":"idolaters"},{"strongs":"G5368","hebrew":"φιλέω","translit":"philéō","english":"loves"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"practices"},{"strongs":"G5579","hebrew":"ψεῦδος","translit":"pseûdos","english":"falsehood"}]},{"v":16,"text":"I, Jesus, have sent my angel to testify these things to you for the assemblies. I am the root and the offspring of David; the Bright and Morning Star.”","understanding":"Ἐγὼ Ἰησοῦς ἔπεμψα τὸν ἄγγελόν μου μαρτυρῆσαι ὑμῖν ταῦτα ἐπὶ ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις, “I, Jesus, have sent my angel to testify these things to you for the assemblies” — μαρτυρέω, from the witness family. The whole book is for the churches. ἐγώ εἰμι ἡ ῥίζα καὶ τὸ γένος Δαυίδ, “I am the root and the offspring of David” — both the source of David's line (as God) and its descendant (as man). ὁ ἀστὴρ ὁ λαμπρὸς ὁ πρωϊνός, “the Bright and Morning Star” — ἀστήρ; πρωϊνός: the star that rises before dawn, announcing the new day. Balaam foresaw ‘a star out of Jacob’; Jesus promised to give the overcomer ‘the morning star’ (2:28) — Himself.","words":[{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G3992","hebrew":"πέμπω","translit":"pémpō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angel"},{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testify"},{"strongs":"G1577","hebrew":"ἐκκλησία","translit":"ekklēsía","english":"assemblies"},{"strongs":"G4491","hebrew":"ῥίζα","translit":"rhíza","english":"root"},{"strongs":"G1085","hebrew":"γένος","translit":"génos","english":"offspring"},{"strongs":"G1138","hebrew":"Δαβίδ","translit":"Dabíd","english":"David"},{"strongs":"G2986","hebrew":"λαμπρός","translit":"lamprós","english":"Bright"},{"strongs":"G4407","hebrew":"πρωϊνός","translit":"prōïnós","english":"Morning"},{"strongs":"G792","hebrew":"ἀστήρ","translit":"astḗr","english":"Star"}]},{"v":17,"text":"The Spirit and the bride say, “Come!” He who hears, let him say, “Come!” He who is thirsty, let him come. He who desires, let him take the water of life freely.","understanding":"Καὶ τὸ πνεῦμα καὶ ἡ νύμφη λέγουσιν· Ἔρχου, “The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’” — νύμφη: the church, moved by the Spirit, calls to Christ: ‘Come!’ καὶ ὁ ἀκούων εἰπάτω· Ἔρχου, “He who hears, let him say, ‘Come!’” — every hearer joins the prayer. καὶ ὁ διψῶν ἐρχέσθω, “He who is thirsty, let him come” — and the call turns outward to the world: Christ's invitation to everyone. ὁ θέλων λαβέτω ὕδωρ ζωῆς δωρεάν, “He who desires, let him take the water of life freely” — ζωή, from the life family; δωρεάν, as a free gift (21:6). The last invitation of the Bible is free and open to all.","words":[{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G3565","hebrew":"νύμφη","translit":"nýmphē","english":"bride"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"Come"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hears"},{"strongs":"G1372","hebrew":"διψάω","translit":"dipsáō","english":"thirsty"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"desires"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"take"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1432","hebrew":"δωρεάν","translit":"dōreán","english":"freely"}]},{"v":18,"text":"I testify to everyone who hears the words of the prophecy of this book, if anyone adds to them, may God add to him the plagues which are written in this book.","understanding":"Μαρτυρῶ ἐγὼ παντὶ τῷ ἀκούοντι τοὺς λόγους τῆς προφητείας τοῦ βιβλίου τούτου, “I testify to everyone who hears the words of the prophecy of this book” — μαρτυρέω, from the witness family; λόγος, from the word family; βιβλίον, from the book family. ἐάν τις ἐπιθῇ ἐπ᾽ αὐτά, ἐπιθήσει ὁ θεὸς ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸν τὰς πληγὰς τὰς γεγραμμένας ἐν τῷ βιβλίῳ τούτῳ, “if anyone adds to them, may God add to him the plagues which are written in this book” — ἐπιτίθημι; βιβλίον, from the book family. Like Moses' warning in Deuteronomy, it protects the message from distortion — the very sin of the false prophets.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testify"},{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"hears"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G2007","hebrew":"ἐπιτίθημι","translit":"epitíthēmi","english":"adds"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4127","hebrew":"πληγή","translit":"plēgḗ","english":"plagues"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"}]},{"v":19,"text":"If anyone takes away from the words of the book of this prophecy, may God take away his part from the tree of life, and out of the holy city, which are written in this book.","understanding":"καὶ ἐάν τις ἀφέλῃ ἀπὸ τῶν λόγων τοῦ βιβλίου τῆς προφητείας ταύτης, “If anyone takes away from the words of the book of this prophecy” — ἀφαιρέω, to remove; λόγος, from the word family; βιβλίον, from the book family. ἀφελεῖ ὁ θεὸς τὸ μέρος αὐτοῦ ἀπὸ τοῦ ξύλου τῆς ζωῆς καὶ ἐκ τῆς πόλεως τῆς ἁγίας, “may God take away his part from the tree of life, and out of the holy city” — ζωή, from the life family; ἅγιος, from the holy family. Some manuscripts read ‘the book of life’. The warning is first about this book, but it reflects how seriously God guards all His word.","words":[{"strongs":"G851","hebrew":"ἀφαιρέω","translit":"aphairéō","english":"takes away"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G975","hebrew":"βιβλίον","translit":"biblíon","english":"book"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"part"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"tree"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"city"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"}]},{"v":20,"text":"He who testifies these things says, “Yes, I come quickly.” Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus.","understanding":"Λέγει ὁ μαρτυρῶν ταῦτα, “He who testifies these things says” — μαρτυρέω, from the witness family: Jesus, the faithful witness (1:5). Ναί, ἔρχομαι ταχύ, “Yes, I come quickly” — ταχύ, the third and final time. Ἀμήν, ἔρχου κύριε Ἰησοῦ, “Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus” — the church's answer: the Aramaic prayer of the first Christians, ‘Maranatha’ (1 Corinthians 16:22). The last prayer of the Bible.","words":[{"strongs":"G3140","hebrew":"μαρτυρέω","translit":"martyréō","english":"testifies"},{"strongs":"G3483","hebrew":"ναί","translit":"naí","english":"Yes"},{"strongs":"G2064","hebrew":"ἔρχομαι","translit":"érchomai","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G5035","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","english":"quickly"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"}]},{"v":21,"text":"The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with all the saints. Amen.","understanding":"Ἡ χάρις τοῦ κυρίου Ἰησοῦ μετὰ πάντων τῶν ἁγίων, “The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with all the saints” — χάρις, grace; ἅγιος, from the holy family (many manuscripts read simply ‘with all’). A book of thunder, beasts and bowls ends like a letter, with grace. Ἀμήν, “Amen” — the last word of the Bible: so let it be.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2424","hebrew":"Ἰησοῦς","translit":"Iēsoûs","english":"Jesus"},{"strongs":"G5547","hebrew":"Χριστός","translit":"Christós","english":"Christ"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"saints"},{"strongs":"G281","hebrew":"ἀμήν","translit":"amḗn","english":"Amen"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The River and the Tree","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture a river as clear as crystal flowing from the throne of God and the Lamb, down the golden street of the city. On both banks grows the tree of life, heavy with twelve kinds of fruit, a new crop every month; and people from every nation rest under it, its leaves healing every old wound.","meaning":"“He showed me a river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding out of the throne of God and of the Lamb... On this side of the river and on that was the tree of life... The leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations” (vv. 1–2). The garden lost in Genesis is restored and surpassed in Revelation."},{"title":"They Will See His Face","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture a vast crowd of God's servants — no curse, no night, no lamps needed — and every face turned upward, looking at the One on the throne. For the first time, they see His face; and His name shines on their foreheads.","meaning":"“They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads. There will be no night... for the Lord God will illuminate them. They will reign forever and ever” (vv. 4–5). Every longing of the human heart is fulfilled in seeing God."},{"title":"The Morning Star","verseRef":"v. 16","image":"Picture the darkest hour of the night, just before dawn. Then, low on the horizon, one star appears, brighter than all the others, announcing that the night is almost over and the day is coming.","meaning":"“I, Jesus... I am the root and the offspring of David; the Bright and Morning Star” (v. 16). Whatever darkness you are in, Jesus is the sign that morning is coming."},{"title":"Come","verseRef":"v. 17, 20","image":"Picture the church, the bride, standing at the edge of history, looking toward the horizon, and the Spirit within her crying, ‘Come!’ Then she turns to the thirsty crowds of the world and calls to them too: ‘Come! Take the water of life — it's free.’ And from heaven comes the answer: ‘Yes, I come quickly.’","meaning":"“The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’... He who desires, let him take the water of life freely” (v. 17). “He who testifies these things says, ‘Yes, I come quickly.’ Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (v. 20)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"The River and the Tree (v. 1-5)","body":"The angel shows John the river of the water of life flowing from the throne of God and the Lamb down the city's street; the tree of life grows on both banks, bearing fruit every month, its leaves for the healing of the nations; there is no more curse; God's servants will worship Him, see His face and bear His name, with no night, for the Lord God is their light, and they will reign forever."},{"label":"These Words Are Faithful and True (v. 6-11)","body":"The angel affirms that the words are trustworthy; Jesus says, ‘I come quickly’ and blesses those who keep the prophecy; John falls to worship the angel and is told to worship God; he is told not to seal up the book, for the time is near; let the unjust and the righteous continue in their ways."},{"label":"Jesus' Final Words (v. 12-16)","body":"Jesus says He is coming soon with His reward for each person's work; He is the Alpha and Omega, the First and Last, the Beginning and End; blessed are those who wash their robes and may enter the city; outside are the dogs and all who love falsehood; Jesus has sent His angel to the churches; He is the root and offspring of David, the bright morning star."},{"label":"Invitation, Warning and Benediction (v. 17-21)","body":"The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come’; let the thirsty come and take the water of life freely; a warning against adding to or taking away from the prophecy; Jesus says, ‘Yes, I am coming quickly’; the church answers, ‘Amen. Come, Lord Jesus’; the grace of the Lord Jesus be with all."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Paradise Restored","paragraphs":["The Bible begins in a garden with a river and a tree of life, and ends in a city with a river and a tree of life. “He showed me a river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding out of the throne of God and of the Lamb, in the middle of its street. On this side of the river and on that was the tree of life” (vv. 1–2). What was lost in Genesis 3 is restored — and more than restored: a garden has become a garden-city, the home of a redeemed humanity from every nation.","The tree bears fruit every month, and “The leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations” (v. 2). The wounds between peoples — wars, hatreds, injustices — are healed. “There will be no curse any more” (v. 3). The curse that fell on the ground and on human life after the first sin is lifted forever.","And at the heart of it all: “The throne of God and of the Lamb will be in it, and his servants will serve him. They will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads” (vv. 3–4). Moses was told that no one could see God's face and live. Paul wrote, “For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face” (1 Corinthians 13:12). Jesus promised, “Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God” (Matthew 5:8). This is the beatific vision — the end and joy of all things. “They will reign forever and ever” (v. 5)."]},{"heading":"Faithful and True Words","paragraphs":["The book now closes as it began. “These words are faithful and true” (v. 6); the angel was sent “to show to his bondservants the things which must happen soon” (v. 6), the words of 1:1. “Behold, I come quickly. Blessed is he who keeps the words of the prophecy of this book” (v. 7) echoes the first blessing (1:3). The whole book is framed by blessing on those who keep it.","Once again John falls before the angel, and once again he is stopped: “See you don’t do it! I am a fellow bondservant with you... Worship God” (v. 9). If the beloved apostle, after seeing all this, could be tempted to worship the wrong thing, so can we. The whole message of Revelation could be summed up in those two words: Worship God.","Unlike Daniel, John is told, “Don’t seal up the words of the prophecy of this book, for the time is at hand” (v. 10). Then comes a sobering word: “He who acts unjustly, let him act unjustly still... He who is holy, let him be holy still” (v. 11). This is not permission to sin, but a warning that the time is short and character is becoming fixed. The direction you are going now is the direction you will go."]},{"heading":"The First and the Last","paragraphs":["Jesus speaks directly: “Behold, I come quickly. My reward is with me, to repay to each man according to his work. I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End” (vv. 12–13). He takes to Himself every title that God claimed in the book (1:8; 21:6). There is no clearer statement in Revelation that Jesus is fully God.","“Blessed are those who do his commandments, that they may have the right to the tree of life, and may enter in by the gates into the city” (v. 14). The oldest manuscripts read ‘Blessed are those who wash their robes’ — the robes washed white in the Lamb's blood (7:14). Either way, the way into the city is through the Lamb. “Outside are the dogs, the sorcerers, the sexually immoral, the murderers, the idolaters, and everyone who loves and practices falsehood” (v. 15). The gates are always open, but those who prefer the lie stay outside.","“I, Jesus, have sent my angel to testify these things to you for the assemblies. I am the root and the offspring of David; the Bright and Morning Star” (v. 16). He is both David's Lord and David's Son (Matthew 22:42–45). And He is the morning star that rises in the darkest hour before dawn. Peter wrote of the prophetic word as “a lamp shining in a dark place, until the day dawns, and the morning star arises in your hearts” (2 Peter 1:19)."]},{"heading":"Come","paragraphs":["The last invitation of the Bible: “The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’ He who hears, let him say, ‘Come!’ He who is thirsty, let him come. He who desires, let him take the water of life freely” (v. 17). The church, moved by the Spirit, cries to Christ to come; and then turns to the world and cries to everyone who is thirsty to come. The water of life is free. Jesus said to the Samaritan woman, “If you knew the gift of God, and who it is who says to you, ‘Give me a drink,’ you would have asked him, and he would have given you living water” (John 4:10).","Then a solemn warning against adding to or taking away from “the words of the prophecy of this book” (vv. 18–19). Like Moses' warning in Deuteronomy, it guards the message against the distortions of false prophets. It refers first to Revelation itself, but it shows how seriously God takes every word He has spoken.","And finally, “He who testifies these things says, ‘Yes, I come quickly.’ Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus” (v. 20). The church's last prayer is the prayer of the first Christians: Maranatha — ‘Come, Lord!’ (1 Corinthians 16:22). Paul speaks of “all those who have loved his appearing” (2 Timothy 4:8). The Bible ends not with a full stop but with a longing and a promise — and then with grace: “The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ be with all the saints. Amen” (v. 21)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Blessed Hope","paragraphs":["Paul calls Christ's return “the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13), and says the crown of righteousness is stored up “not to me only, but also to all those who have loved his appearing” (2 Timothy 4:8).","The face-to-face vision of verse 4 is the fulfilment of Paul's hope: “For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face. Now I know in part, but then I will know fully, even as I was also fully known” (1 Corinthians 13:12).","The church's prayer, ‘Come, Lord Jesus’, is the Greek form of the Aramaic Maranatha with which Paul closed his first letter to the Corinthians: “If any man doesn’t love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be accursed. Come, Lord!” (1 Corinthians 16:22). From the first generation to the last, the church has prayed it."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Healing","hebrew":"θεραπεία","translit":"therapeía","strongs":"G2322 (leaves φύλλον, phýllon, G5444)","verses":"v. 2","insight":"Strong's: “attendance (specially, medical, i.e. cure); figuratively and collectively, domestics.” θεραπεία, from θεραπεύω, gives us ‘therapy’. In the Gospels it describes Jesus' healing ministry. The leaves of the tree of life are ‘for the healing of the nations’: not because sickness exists in the new creation, but because all the wounds of history — between peoples, cultures and nations — will be fully and finally healed. Ezekiel's river had trees whose ‘leaf for healing’ never withered."},{"english":"Curse","hebrew":"κατανάθεμα","translit":"katanáthema","strongs":"G2652 (throne θρόνος, thrónos, G2362)","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “an imprecation.” κατανάθεμα (or κατάθεμα) is a curse, something devoted to destruction. After the first sin, God said, ‘Cursed is the ground for your sake.’ Paul says Christ redeemed us from the curse ‘having become a curse for us’ (Galatians 3:13). Because He bore the curse on the cross, ‘There will be no curse any more’ in the new creation. The story that began with a curse ends without one."},{"english":"Morning","hebrew":"πρωϊνός","translit":"prōïnós","strongs":"G4407 (Star ἀστήρ, astḗr, G792)","verses":"v. 16; 2:28","insight":"Strong's: “pertaining to the dawn, i.e. matutinal.” πρωϊνός describes the early morning, the first light. ‘The Bright and Morning Star’ is the planet Venus, which shines brightest just before sunrise, heralding the day. Jesus promised the overcomer in Thyatira, ‘I will give him the morning star’ (2:28); here He reveals that the star is Himself. In the darkest hour before dawn, He is the sign that the day is coming."},{"english":"Quickly","hebrew":"ταχύ","translit":"tachý","strongs":"G5035 (come ἔρχομαι, érchomai, G2064)","verses":"v. 7, 12, 20; 2:16; 3:11","insight":"Strong's: “shortly, i.e. without delay, soon, or (by surprise) suddenly, or (by implication, of ease) readily.” ταχύ appears three times in Jesus' final words: ‘I come quickly’ (vv. 7, 12, 20). Strong's notes both ‘soon’ and ‘suddenly’. Two thousand years later, His coming is still ‘soon’ in God's time (2 Peter 3:8) and will certainly be sudden, like a thief. Every generation is to live as though He may come today."},{"english":"Take away","hebrew":"ἀφαιρέω","translit":"aphairéō","strongs":"G851 (adds ἐπιτίθημι, epitíthēmi, G2007)","verses":"v. 19","insight":"Strong's: “to remove (literally or figuratively).” ἀφαιρέω means to take away or remove. Revelation ends with a warning modelled on Deuteronomy 4:2: ‘You shall not add to the word which I command you, neither shall you take away from it.’ Whoever adds to the book will receive its plagues; whoever takes away from it will lose their share in the tree of life. The book that reveals Jesus must be received whole — not edited to suit our tastes."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this final chapter the Holy Spirit and the bride join in one word: Come.","items":[{"title":"You Will See His Face","body":"“They will see his face” (v. 4). The Spirit wants this hope to shape your whole life. One day you will look into the face of God and the Lamb. Live today as someone who is going to see Him."},{"title":"Worship God","body":"Even at the end, John needed to be reminded: “Worship God” (v. 9). The Spirit asks you to keep returning to the centre: not visions, not experiences, not leaders, not even angels, but God alone."},{"title":"Say ‘Come’","body":"“The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’” (v. 17). The Spirit invites you to pray for Christ's return with longing — ‘Come, Lord Jesus’ — and to say ‘Come’ to every thirsty person you know, offering them the water of life freely."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Longing Check","body":"Do you long for Christ's return, or are you comfortable enough here that you would rather He waited (v. 20)?"},{"title":"The Invitation Check","body":"Who in your life is thirsty and needs to hear ‘Come’ (v. 17)? When will you tell them?"},{"title":"The Word Check","body":"Are there parts of God's word you would like to add to or take away from because they are uncomfortable (vv. 18–19)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Pray Maranatha","body":"Each morning and evening this week, pray the last prayer of the Bible: ‘Amen. Come, Lord Jesus.’ Then pray the last blessing for someone else: ‘The grace of the Lord Jesus be with you.’"},{"title":"Give Someone a Drink","body":"Invite one person this week to ‘come and drink’ — to read a Gospel with you, come to church, or hear your story of how Jesus satisfied your thirst. Offer it freely, as God offers it to you (v. 17)."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You are the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End, the root and offspring of David, the bright morning star. Thank You for the river of the water of life flowing from Your throne, and for the tree of life whose leaves will heal the nations. Thank You that there will be no more curse, because You became a curse for me. I long to see Your face. Put Your name on my forehead. Be my light, so that there is no night in me. Keep me faithful to the words of this book. Teach me to worship God alone. Thank You that the water of life is free; I come and drink, and I want to bring others to drink with me. Your grace has carried me through this whole book, from the throne to the river. The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come.’ I say it too: Amen. Come, Lord Jesus. The grace of the Lord Jesus be with all Your people. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Garden and the Tree of Life","ref":"Genesis 2:8-17; 3:22-24","relatedVerses":"v. 1-3","summary":"God planted a garden in Eden, with a river flowing out to water it and the tree of life in the middle. When Adam and Eve sinned, God drove them out and placed cherubim with a flaming sword to guard the way to the tree of life. For the rest of the Bible, the way back to the tree is closed — until Revelation 22, where the tree of life stands open on both sides of the river, in the middle of the city, and the curse is gone. The Bible's story ends where it began, but far better."},{"title":"Ezekiel's River","ref":"Ezekiel 47:1-12","relatedVerses":"v. 1-2","summary":"In his vision of the restored temple, Ezekiel saw water flowing out from under the threshold, growing from ankle-deep to knee-deep to a river too deep to cross. Wherever it flowed, it brought life: the Dead Sea became fresh and teemed with fish. On both banks grew trees whose leaves did not wither and whose fruit came every month, ‘and its leaf for healing’. Revelation 22 fulfils this vision in the new creation: the river of life from the throne, and the tree whose leaves heal the nations."},{"title":"Moses Asks to See God's Glory","ref":"Exodus 33:12-23","relatedVerses":"v. 4","summary":"After the golden calf, Moses pleaded with God, ‘Please show me your glory.’ God answered that He would make His goodness pass before him and proclaim His name, ‘but you cannot see my face, for man may not see me and live.’ He hid Moses in a cleft of the rock and covered him with His hand as He passed by. In Revelation 22, what Moses could not have is given to all God's servants: ‘They will see his face.’"}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"Revelation","titleEmojis":"👑🌅","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"The Lamb on the Throne Is Making All Things New","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of Revelation in one overarching thought, it is this: behind the turmoil of history stands the throne of God, and on that throne is the Lamb who was slain; He holds the scroll of history, He has already overcome the dragon by His blood, and He is coming soon to judge every evil, to bring down Babylon and the beast, and to make all things new — so His people must worship God alone and overcome as He did, by faithful witness even to death.","paragraphs":["Revelation was written to seven small churches in a vast empire. Some were persecuted, some compromised, some asleep, some lukewarm. The empire looked eternal; its emperor was worshipped as lord and god; its wealth and power seemed unstoppable. Into that world the risen Jesus sent a letter — an unveiling — that showed what was really happening.","First, He showed Himself: the Son of Man among the lampstands, with eyes like fire and the keys of death in His hand (ch. 1). Then He spoke to each church with perfect knowledge, calling them to repent and promising extravagant rewards to those who overcome (chs. 2–3). Then John was taken up to the throne room of heaven, where God reigns and the slain Lamb takes the scroll of history (chs. 4–5).","From the throne flow the judgements — seals, trumpets and bowls — growing in intensity, each a warning and a call to repentance (chs. 6–16). Behind them John saw the deeper conflict: the dragon, the beast and the false prophet against the woman, the Lamb and His witnesses (chs. 12–14). He saw the great prostitute Babylon in all her glamour, and her sudden fall (chs. 17–18). He saw heaven explode in Hallelujahs, the marriage of the Lamb, and the Rider called Faithful and True defeat every enemy by the sword of His mouth (ch. 19). He saw Satan bound and finally destroyed, and every person standing before the great white throne (ch. 20).","And at the end he saw what all of it was for: a new heaven and a new earth, the holy city coming down like a bride, God dwelling with His people, every tear wiped away, the river of life and the tree of life, and the servants of God seeing His face (chs. 21–22). The last words are an invitation and a prayer: ‘Come.’ ‘Amen! Yes, come, Lord Jesus.’ The book that seems so full of terror is, in the end, the book of the Bible's greatest hope."]},"map":{"intro":"Revelation is the twenty-seventh and last book of the New Testament, and the last book of the Bible. It gathers up the threads of the whole story — creation, fall, Israel, the prophets, the Gospel — and ties them together in the victory of the Lamb and the renewal of all things.","sections":[{"title":"The End That Answers the Beginning","body":"Revelation is the Bible's final chapter, and it deliberately mirrors the first. Genesis opens with the heavens and the earth; Revelation with a new heaven and a new earth. Genesis has a garden, a river and the tree of life; Revelation a garden-city, the river of life and the tree of life open to all. Genesis brings the serpent, the curse and death; Revelation the serpent thrown down, ‘no curse any more’ and ‘death will be no more’. In Genesis humanity is driven from God's presence; in Revelation ‘God’s dwelling is with people’ and ‘they will see his face’. And the Babylon that began at Babel ends in fire, replaced by the city of God."},{"title":"How This Study Has Read Revelation","body":"Christians have read Revelation in four main ways: as mostly fulfilled in the first centuries, as a map of church history, as mainly about the final events before Christ's return, or as a timeless picture of the battle between Christ and evil. This study has read each passage in the way its own words suggest: Revelation spoke first to seven real churches under Rome; it describes realities that recur in every age; and it looks forward to a real, personal return of Christ, a final judgement and a new creation. It speaks in symbols drawn from the Old Testament — and often tells us so (1:20; 11:8; 17:9, 18)."},{"title":"Hard Passages Read Honestly","body":"Several passages divide faithful readers. The 144,000 and the great multitude (ch. 7) are read by some as two groups, by others (as here) as one people seen two ways. The two witnesses (ch. 11) may be two end-time prophets or the witnessing church. The woman of chapter 12 is the people of God from whom the Messiah came, and Christians have also seen Mary in her. The beast and 666 (ch. 13) pointed John's readers to Nero and Rome, and also to every power that claims divine honour. Babylon (chs. 17–18) was Rome, and is every civilisation built on idolatry, greed and the blood of God's people. The thousand years (ch. 20) is read in three main ways — premillennial, amillennial and postmillennial — and this study has set them out without pretending the question is closed. What all agree on is the book's centre: the Lamb wins, and His people with Him."},{"title":"Word Families in Revelation","body":"Revelation added eight new families to the study: witness (μάρτυς, μαρτυρία, μαρτύριον, μαρτυρέω — the faithful witness who becomes a ‘martyr’); crown (στέφανος, the victor's wreath, against διάδημα, the royal crown claimed by the dragon and worn ‘many’ by Christ); book (βίβλος, βιβλίον, βιβλαρίδιον); lamb (ἀρνίον, Revelation's name for Christ, against ἀμνός and ἀρήν); wrath (ὀργή, settled anger, against θυμός, anger as passion, often side by side); sword (ῥομφαία from Christ's mouth against the beast's μάχαιρα); judge (κρίνω, κρίσις the act, κρίμα the verdict); and holy (ἅγιος, set apart, against ὅσιος, ‘you only are holy’). Familiar families recurred everywhere: καιρός and χρόνος in time, δύναμις, ἐξουσία, κράτος and ἰσχύς in power, and the four time-words of 9:15 — hour, day, month and year — in a single phrase."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: Revelation is the last book of the Bible, so the road ahead turns back to the beginning. The best way to understand Revelation's ending is to read the Bible's opening. The next two books are Genesis, the book of beginnings, which Revelation brings to completion; and Exodus, the story of redemption, whose plagues, Passover lamb, song by the sea and tabernacle shape Revelation on almost every page. (Genesis and Exodus are not yet in this app's Bible text, so they are described here in our own words.)","books":[{"book":"Genesis","connection":"Genesis is where every thread of Revelation begins. God creates the heavens and the earth and calls them good; He plants a garden with a river and the tree of life, and walks with the man and woman He made in His image. Then the serpent deceives, sin enters, the ground is cursed, death comes, and the way to the tree of life is guarded by cherubim with a flaming sword. God promises that the woman's offspring will crush the serpent's head. Humanity builds Babel to make a name for itself, and God scatters it. And God calls Abraham, promising that through him all the families of the earth will be blessed, and that his descendants will be as countless as the stars. Watch for each of these as you read: Revelation is Genesis answered — the serpent crushed, the curse lifted, Babylon fallen, the tree of life open, and a countless multitude from every nation blessed through Abraham's offspring, the Lamb."},{"book":"Exodus","connection":"Exodus gave Revelation its pictures of judgement and redemption. God hears His people crying under Pharaoh's oppression and sends plagues — water to blood, frogs, boils, hail, locusts, darkness — while Pharaoh hardens his heart. On the night of Passover, the blood of a lamb shelters God's people from judgement. They pass through the sea, and on the far shore Moses and Miriam sing of God's victory. At Sinai, with thunder, lightning, smoke and trumpet blast, God makes them ‘a kingdom of priests’, and He comes to dwell among them in the tabernacle, which His glory fills. Watch for each of these as you read: the trumpets and bowls of Revelation are the plagues on a world scale; the Lamb is the true Passover; the victors sing the song of Moses and the Lamb; and the tabernacle becomes the new Jerusalem, where God dwells with His people forever."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"Jesus, the First and the Last","anchorRef":"Revelation 1:17-18; 22:13","body":"Revelation is first ‘the Revelation of Jesus Christ’. He is the faithful witness, the firstborn of the dead, the ruler of the kings of the earth, who walks among His churches with eyes like fire: “I am the first and the last, and the Living one. I was dead, and behold, I am alive forever and ever” (Revelation 1:17–18). At the end He claims every title of God: “I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End” (Revelation 22:13)."},{"title":"Overcome: Christ's Word to the Churches","anchorRef":"Revelation 2–3","body":"Jesus knows every church — its love, its compromise, its suffering, its sleep, its lukewarmness — and calls each to repent or hold fast. Every letter ends with a promise to the one who overcomes, and the greatest is the last: “He who overcomes, I will give to him to sit down with me on my throne, as I also overcame, and sat down with my Father on his throne” (Revelation 3:21)."},{"title":"The Throne and the Lamb","anchorRef":"Revelation 4–5","body":"Behind all of history stands a throne, and it is not empty: “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God, the Almighty, who was and who is and who is to come!” (Revelation 4:8). At its centre is “a Lamb standing, as though it had been slain” (Revelation 5:6). God's power is revealed in the cross. The Lamb alone is worthy to open the scroll of history, because He “bought us for God with your blood, out of every tribe, language, people, and nation” (Revelation 5:9)."},{"title":"Judgements That Call to Repentance","anchorRef":"Revelation 6–9; 15–16","body":"The seals, trumpets and bowls show God's just judgements on a world in rebellion, released by the Lamb, limited by God, and answering the prayers of His suffering people. Yet again and again “They didn’t repent” (Revelation 9:20). Judgement alone does not change hearts; the witness of God's people does (11:13). And heaven confesses, “Yes, Lord God, the Almighty, true and righteous are your judgments” (Revelation 16:7)."},{"title":"The War Behind History","anchorRef":"Revelation 12–13","body":"The dragon, the beast and the false prophet wage war on God's people, demanding worship and controlling the marketplace. But the dragon has already been thrown down by the cross, and “They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death” (Revelation 12:11). The beast's power is ‘given’ and limited; “Here is the endurance and the faith of the saints” (Revelation 13:10)."},{"title":"Come Out of Babylon","anchorRef":"Revelation 17–18","body":"Babylon the great — the world-system of wealth, pleasure, power and idolatry, drunk on the blood of the saints — looks glorious but is already judged. Her fall comes in one hour. God's call to His people is urgent: “Come out of her, my people, that you have no participation in her sins, and that you don’t receive of her plagues” (Revelation 18:4)."},{"title":"All Things New","anchorRef":"Revelation 19–22","body":"Christ returns as King of kings and defeats every enemy; Satan, death and Hades are destroyed; every person is judged. Then God makes all things new: “Behold, God’s dwelling is with people, and he will dwell with them, and they will be his people, and God himself will be with them as their God. He will wipe away every tear from their eyes” (Revelation 21:3–4). And His servants “will see his face” (Revelation 22:4)."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of Revelation practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":"1","title":"Begin Every Day at the Throne","lesson":"The Lesson: “After these things I looked and saw a door opened in heaven, and the first voice that I heard, like a trumpet speaking with me, was one saying, ‘Come up here, and I will show you the things which must happen after this’” (Revelation 4:1).","practice":"The Practice: For the next month, before you check the news or your phone, read one hymn of worship from Revelation (4:8, 4:11, 5:9–10, 5:12–13, 7:10–12, 11:15–18, 15:3–4, 19:1–8) and pray it aloud. Then bring the day's fears and tasks to the One on the throne. Let worship, not worry, set the tone of your day."},{"step":"2","title":"Overcome as the Lamb Overcame","lesson":"The Lesson: “They overcame him because of the Lamb’s blood, and because of the word of their testimony. They didn’t love their life, even to death” (Revelation 12:11).","practice":"The Practice: Read the seven letters (chs. 2–3) and ask which church you most resemble. Write down one thing Jesus would praise and one thing He would correct. Then take three concrete steps this month: confess and repent of the compromise He names; share your testimony with one person; and choose one costly act of faithfulness — giving, forgiving, refusing a compromise at work — that shows you love Him more than your own comfort."},{"step":"3","title":"Come Out of Babylon and Say ‘Come’","lesson":"The Lesson: “The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’ He who hears, let him say, ‘Come!’ He who is thirsty, let him come. He who desires, let him take the water of life freely” (Revelation 22:17).","practice":"The Practice: Identify one way Babylon's values have a hold on you — in spending, entertainment, ambition or comfort — and take a clear step to come out of it. Then pray daily for three people who do not yet know Jesus, and invite at least one of them this month to ‘come and drink’. End each day with the Bible's last prayer: ‘Amen. Come, Lord Jesus.’"}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Lord Jesus Christ, I declare that You are the faithful witness, the firstborn of the dead and the ruler of the kings of the earth. You love me, You freed me from my sins by Your blood, and You made me part of Your kingdom and a priest to Your God and Father. To You be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. You walk among Your churches with eyes like fire; I invite You to search me, and I will repent where You show me, and hold fast what is good. I declare that the throne of heaven is occupied, and that the Lord God Almighty reigns. Holy, holy, holy is He. I declare that You, the Lamb who was slain, are worthy to take the scroll of history, for You bought me and people from every nation with Your blood. I will not fear the dragon, for he has been thrown down; I will overcome him by Your blood and by the word of my testimony, and I will not love my life more than You. I refuse the mark of the beast and the wine of Babylon; I will come out of her and keep myself for You, my Bridegroom. I declare that Your judgements are true and righteous, and that every wrong will be put right. I believe You are coming soon as King of kings and Lord of lords, that death will be destroyed, and that my name is written in the Lamb's book of life by Your grace alone. I look for a new heaven and a new earth, for the city where God dwells with His people, where every tear is wiped away and there is no more curse. I will see Your face. Until then I will worship God alone, keep the words of this book, and say to every thirsty soul, ‘Come, and take the water of life freely.’ Amen. Come, Lord Jesus. The grace of the Lord Jesus be with all the saints. Amen!”"}});
